[PlyCount "1"]
[GameId "2288537757061187"]

{Welcome to part 1 of my e2-e4 repertoire! This has been a long time coming, as it was clearly the biggest and last major missing piece in a complete Shankland opening encyclopedia. The idea had been floated to me for a while, but I basically just declined—it felt like too much work. It would be a much bigger project than d2-d4 was, and when I did d2-d4, the world was in lockdown, and Chessable did not have to compete for my time with my tournament schedule! Ultimately, I decided to do this project a bit over a year ago. My plan was to write the full repertoire—all 3 parts—keep the analysis to myself, play it in my own games and get some use out of it, and only release it a year later. Now that a year has passed, this is the result. The repertoire itself is aimed at getting very direct positions and playing for the initiative whenever possible. Nowadays, Black is equalizing with any serious opening, and I certainly can’t prove an advantage against the French or Caro-Kann. Nobody can. And anyone who claims otherwise is either massively naive or a swindling con artist. But I am very happy with what I managed to do. This repertoire covers all sidelines, the French, and the Caro-Kann. Part 2 will be the Sicilian, and Part 3 will be e2-e4 e7-e5. I think I showed a clear advantage in every single sideline Black can play, and in the French and Caro-Kann, I showed an advantage in a lot of spots, and new ideas in all of the big main lines that can make your opponents squirm. I want 3. Nc3 in both cases, aiming for a more open game where we can use the extra space to attack, and where we are quickly getting our pieces out. I have had very little chance to test this repertoire in my own practice, since whenever I play e2-e4, I almost always face ...c7-c5 or ...e7-e5. But, in the few cases where I did get to, the repertoire worked like a charm. I crushed both Gukesh and Rapport, and won a very nice game against Hungaski as well. The final section of illustrative games features “The repertoire in practice”. This shows games that were played specifically with this repertoire as the intended preparation. My 3 aforementioned games from above are included, as are games of a student and friend of mine who are both around 2200, showing how it plays out at a generally high level that is still some ways off from a top-tier professional/SuperGM. Those results were very encouraging as well! Best of luck to all who study this course! Sam Shankland} 1. Z0 *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
