1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... c5 $1 {This seems to be the best try, but is pretty rare. Black breaks up the center immediately, before White can consolidate it.} 6. Nc3 cxd4 7. Qxd4 {[%cal Gf3d4,Re7e5]} (7. Nxd4 {is running into} e5 {, so we must take with the queen.}) 7... Qa5 $1 {A move never once played, but it is the engine line. Just in case someone has this prepped.} 8. Qe5 $1 {Insisting on the queen trade.} Qxe5+ 9. Nxe5 Nbd7 10. Nd3 $14 {[%cal Gc3b5] I like keeping the pieces on. Black has some coordination issues, and I like the queenside majority for White.Nb5/Bf4 is on the radar. This is the best that Black can get, and it's still quite pleasant for us.} *

