[White "Quickstarter Guide: Sidelines"]
[Black "Scandinavian with 2...Qxd5 #2"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B01"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "17"]
[GameId "2288537760440395"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qa5 {[%csl Yc3][%cal Ya5e1,Rc7c6,Re7e6,Rc8f5,Gd2d4] Widely considered the main move. Black's idea is that the knight on c3 will be pinned down after d4 for a few turns, before Black eventually drops back with the queen via ...c7-c6/...Qc7. We need to realize that positionally speaking, Black is doing fantastic.The pieces develop quickly, and the c3-knight is misplaced, blocking the c-pawn. In the long run, Black will both have an incredibly solid London-like setup with the pawns on c6 and e6, and also plently of activity.This means that we must play concretely and force Black to make some concessions by exploiting the initiative and vulnerable queen position, otherwise Black will easily equalize. The key ideas for White are: ▷ Bd2 against some of the sidelines, targeting the queen on a5; ▷ Ne5+g2-g4/h2-h4 to track the bishop when it leaves the pawn chain; ▷ and Bc4 to induce an early ...e7-e6, locking the bishop outside.} 4. d4 Nf6 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gc8g4,Gc8f5] Black has to wait for Nf3 before deploying the queen bishop.} (4... c6 5. Nf3 {Black has nothing better than returning to the mainlines after} Nf6 ({,} 5... Bf5) ({or} 5... Bg4 {, all of which we can meet with} 6. Bc4 {.})) 5. Nf3 c6 6. Bc4 e6 {[%csl Ra7,Rc6][%cal Ge1g1,Ga2a3] ...Bf5 and ...Bg4 just transpose to the mainlines, so this is the only independent try. It doesn't make much sense to block the bishop in, though.Getting the bishop outside was the point of the whole opening.} (6... b5 $6 7. Bd3 b4 {A bunch of random one-movers that saddle Black with tons of weaknesses.} 8. Ne2 $16 {We will play a2-a3 later and split the queenside pawns.}) 7. O-O Be7 (7... Bd6 $6 {runs into} 8. Re1 O-O 9. Ne4 $1 Nxe4 10. Rxe4 {White has a big attack thanks to the rook lift. Black must be extremely careful not to get mated here.}) 8. Re1 O-O 9. Qe2 $16 {[%cal Gf3e5,Gc1f4] Black has a fairly wretched Rubinstein French nightmare ahead of them.} *

