1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 {[%csl Ge5]} dxe5 6. fxe5 {Now, ...Nc6 is surely Black's best move, and it has its own chapter.} Bf5 {If Black plays ...Nc6 later, then we will likely see a transposition to the ...Nc6 Be3-lines, so I will only examine what happens if Black plays for ...c7-c5 – it's the only reason to delay ...Nc6.} 7. Nc3 e6 8. Nf3 Bb4 {The critical line and point of no return. Black is adamant about their plan of ...c7-c5 and goes all in. We can be very direct now.} ({▷ Black is not ready for ...c7-c5 yet:} 8... c5 $2 9. d5 $18) ({▷} 8... Nc6 9. Be3 {should promptly take us back to the main line.}) 9. Bd3 $1 {Normally, we are not able to play Bd3 – Black's knight on c6 means that d4 is loose. Now, this is not the case.} c5 (9... Bxd3 $2 {Anything other than ...c7-c5 is busted} 10. Qxd3 Nc6 11. O-O $18 {Ng5 is a huge threat. After something like ...h7-h6 or ...Be7, we can keep building up with moves like Ne4, Be3, and Rad1. Black has no counterplay since ...f7-f6 is not viable anymore without the f5-bishop.}) 10. Bg5 {[%csl Rd8] Now, there is a long concrete variation that leads to a much better position for White:} Qd7 11. Bxf5 exf5 12. d5 Bxc3+ (12... Nxc4 $2 {Capturing the pawn too soon, either here or after ...Bxc3+, loses immediately} 13. Qe2 Nb6 14. e6 fxe6 15. Ne5 $18 {and Black is lost.}) 13. bxc3 O-O 14. O-O Nxc4 {Now that Black has castled, they can finally take the c4-pawn.} 15. Re1 $18 {[%cal Gd1d3,Gf3h4,Bg7g6,Bg5f6] Perhaps it doesn't look lost for Black at first glance, but once you look deeper, you will realize things are already critical. The biggest issue is that the b8-knight cannot develop properly, and in the time it takes for Black to get their pieces organized, we can create threats on the kingside. For example, Qd3-Nh4 was one plan that's suggested by the computer, with the idea that Black can't protect the pawn with ...g7-g6 since it allows Bf6.} *

