1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c6 4. h3 $1 {[%csl Gg4][%cal Gf2f4,Gg1f3,Rc8g4] In all these systems where Black is trying to blockade the light squares, a common theme is that White never wants to allow Bg4-Bxf3, trading off the bad bishop. Instead, we take a turn to prevent Black's best idea before proceeding with development.This move has the added benefit of not obstructing the f-pawn yet, as we will need this pawn to go to f4 to support e4-e5.} d6 5. f4 e5 {Against everything else, White has easy play using the three pawns in the center, similar to the Pirc sidelines. This is the only challenging try, exploiting the weakness of e1-h4 due to the h2-h3 move.} 6. dxe5 Qh4+ 7. Ke2 dxe5 8. Nf3 {[%csl Rh4]} Qe7 9. Nxe5 $1 {This is not checkers. After ...dxe5? Black is not obligated to take the pawn, and can instead play ...Nd7! with a good position since the dark-squared bishop can be maintained.With Nxe5, we now give Black the choice of playing pawn down or surrendering the bishop to win the pawn back, neither of which is particularly appealing.} Bxe5 {Comparatively better. At least this way, material parity is restored.} ({After} 9... Nf6 10. Kf2 $16 {consolidating the position is very easy. Black's pieces are just not adequately placed to take advantage of the exposed king.}) 10. fxe5 Nd7 11. Kf2 Nxe5 12. Be2 $16 {[%csl Rd6,Rf6,Rg7,Rh6][%cal Gh1f1,Gg1f1,Gf2g1] The king will get to safety and Black's dark squares are too weak.} *

