[PlyCount "23"]
[GameId "2288537760489556"]

1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} g6 6. g4 Bg7 7. g5 Nh5 {[%csl Rf4][%cal Gc1e3,Gd1d2,Ge1c1,Gd4d5] Once we're here, the development scheme is quite logical. Be3, Qd2 keeping the f4-square under watch, O-O-O. If Black doesn't capture on d4, then d4-d5 is one of the main ideas to seize space.} 8. Be3 O-O 9. Qd2 c6 10. O-O-O Qa5 11. Kb1 Re8 12. d5 $16 {Good move! Only after ...Re8, which prepares for ...exd4. The tension on c6 makes it hard for Black to do anything, and eventually, Black will have to resolve the tension to White's favor.} *

