1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {This was an old favorite of Karpov, and he won some nice games, but I think it is definitely the worst of Black's three moves.} 5. Nf3 Ngf6 {[%cal Gf6e4,Gd7f6,Gc8f5,Gf5g4] Black's point is obvious enough. They want to exchange off one pair of knights, bring their other knight to f6, and then get their bishop outside the pawn chain to either f5 or g4. If they pull this off, they should be totally fine. For some time, White was trying a quick 5.Bd3 to play Ne4-g5 and not allow h7-h6, or trying something like 5.Ng3 here. I have a different idea.} 6. Nc3 $5 {This move looks a bit weird, but it has some important strategic points.We avoid the knight exchange; this is obvious enough. White has more space, so avoiding piece exchanges should be in their favor. But I specifically like bringing the knight back to c3 rather than g3, which is played nearly 100 times more often!First of all, if Black wants to develop with ...g7-g6, our knight no longer looks ridiculous.Secondly, Black will have a much harder time achieving the ...c6-c5 break, which is how they would ideally like to break down the space advantage and activate their c8-bishop.} e6 $6 ({This is the most common move by a fairly wide margin, but I actually think it is not the best.Black is aiming to develop with} 6... e6 7. Z0 Be7 {,} 8. Z0 O-O {, and then push for} 9. Z0 c5 {later, this is all natural enough. However...}) 7. Bf4 $1 {This is definitely the best place to begin. White prevents Black from playing ...Bf8-d6, and they also are heavily discouraging ...c6-c5 with Nc3-b5 or d4-d5 always hanging over Black's head.} Be7 {This definitely feels the most natural to me, but I don't think it is best. Black does have other development schemes available to them.} 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Qe2 $1 {With the not so subtle intention of castling long. We are already in fresh territory.} b5 {This was the best I could find for Black, and it is a very human move considering that White is advertising that he wants to castle long. Still, I don't think Black will equalize.} 10. Ne5 $1 (10. a3 $5 a6 11. Rd1 Bb7 12. O-O {This is also decent for White.}) 10... Bb7 11. O-O-O $1 {Black now has to be absurdly precise to get a slightly worse position. It is very easy for them to totally collapse with normal-looking moves, either strategically in the center or violently on the kingside.} b4 $1 12. Ne4 c5 $1 {This is an incredibly scary move to play against a blitzing opponent. It feels like something should drop off in Black's position with all the knight tension and the d-file about to be opened, but concretely, there is no direct win.} (12... Nd5 $2 13. Bg3 $16 {White has the c5-break under control, and Black's position is miserable. Their light-squared bishop will not enter the game too easily.}) 13. Nxd7 Nxd7 14. dxc5 Bd5 $1 {Without this one, Black would be just about lost. Now, they are able to take on c5 next, and we cannot stop them.} 15. Kb1 Nxc5 16. Nxc5 Bxc5 17. Be4 $14 {Black ends up a little worse thanks to the isolani that will soon show up on d5. I have to believe their position is holdable, but they definitely cannot claim full equality, and it was not easy to get here!} *

