[Black "Caro-Kann with 4...Nf6 #1"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B16"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "23"]
[GameId "2288537760526425"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 $2 {A move that is the Yaacov Norowitz blitz special. It is a bad move, and Black is already quite a bit worse, but I do have to admire the fact that Yaacov plays it every single game regardless of how much damage modern engines can do.Over the years, it had some other adherents, but he has been so much more devoted to it that I think it is apt to name the line after him.} 6. Nf3 Bg4 (6... Rg8 7. g3 {It does not help Black at all, and even gives White's bishop access to the g2-square before castling short. The king will be totally safe, and the same c2-c4 and d4-d5 plan should be very powerful.}) 7. Be2 {Now, a couple of key points:  ▷ First off, Black's supposed kingside attack is a lot less scary than it might seem. They can often set up with ...Qc7, ...e7-e6, ...Bd6, ...Rg8; we don't care. Castle short anyway, you won't get mated.  ▷ Secondly, White's best plan is to slam forward with c2-c4 and d4-d5, blowing up the center and leaving Black's king in a tough spot. In general, I think Black should be trying to stop this from happening by playing with ...e7-e6, ...Qc7, ...Nd7, and ...O-O-O as quickly as possible, foregoing moves like ...Bd6 or ...Rg8 that don't do anything to stop d5.} e6 (7... Nd7 8. O-O {Black has nothing better than ...e7-e6 anyway.}) 8. O-O $1 {As mentioned, we don't care about any supposed attack Black might claim to have. Next up is c2-c4 and d4-d5.} Nd7 (8... Qc7 9. c4 $1 {As usual, we slam c2-c4. I don't see Black avoiding ...Rg8 or ...Nd7 here, which will promptly transpose to other move orders.}) 9. c4 $1 Qc7 {Now, ideally we would be able to play d4-d5 directly. It is not a bad move, but it doesn't work nearly as well as it does if Black had spent a move on something like ...Rg8 or ...Bd6. So, we can make one preparatory move first.} 10. Nh4 $1 (10. d5 $6 O-O-O {This one is not so convincing. Black is quite fast at making counterplay on the d-file.}) 10... Bxe2 11. Qxe2 {Now d4-d5 is very much on the cards.} O-O-O {What else?} 12. g3 $16 {[%cal Ge2h5,Gb2b4,Gb4b5,Gf1d1,Gd4d5]} (12. g3 Z0 13. Bf4 {, incoming.White's kingside is totally secure, and they have any number of good plans available:▷ Rd1 and d4-d5.▷ Just Qh5 to go after the f7-pawn.▷ b2-b4, and b4-b5.It's all terrible for Black.}) *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
