[GameId "2288537760567398"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} Qb6 $1 {Even though Black has spent a tempo on ...a7-a6, making it harder for them to attack the center quickly, I still think that trying to do so represents the best chance to equalize.Things get a bit messy here, and White is clearly better with easy moves after anything else Black can try. It should come as no surprise that this is Black's most common move by far.} 9. Qc1 {This is not the happiest square for the queen, but there was no choice.} f6 {Though the machine prefers taking on d4 and accepting a slightly worse position right off the bat, I definitely think that ...f7-f6 should be the most testing move. Black wants to contest our center directly.} 10. c3 Be7 11. h4 $1 {As usual, once we go Nce2, h2-h4 is our next move if we don't need to defend against a direct threat. It gains important kingside space, and it stops any hope of g5.Now, if we are allowed to play Kf2 next with Kg1 to follow, we will have a huge position.} (11. Kf2 $6 O-O 12. Kg1 {This would be the right plan for White, but unfortunately, Black is just in time to make counterplay with ...g7-g5.} g5) 11... O-O 12. h5 $1 {This move might look simple in its nature; White gains space on the kingside, what could be more natural? But it also has a hidden point. Now, Kf2 is a real idea.} (12. Kf2 $2 fxe5 13. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 {Black gets a lot of counterplay here.} 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Kg1 Ng4 16. c4) ({.} 12. Rh3 {This is a very strategically ambitious move. White is getting ready for Kf2, but Black is not in such bad shape.} Qa5 $1 {No more preparatory moves for you! Go to f2 right now, and then Black has just enough counterplay once they take on d4 and e5.} 13. Kf2 (13. Bd2 b5 14. Rg3 Qb6) 13... cxd4 $1 14. cxd4 fxe5 15. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 {Of course.} 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Kg1 Ng4 {, and now we see the point; White would win on the spot with Bd4, but here, Black has ...e6-e5 because the rook is loose on h3.} 18. Rg3 (18. Bd4 e5 $1) 18... e5 19. Rxg4 Bxg4 20. Nxe5 {.}) 12... h6 {This is best, and the most common move by far. We still cannot successfully play Kf2, because Black will once more sacrifice on e5 and we won't have h5-h6 hanging over the head.} 13. Rh3 {This is generally a very useful move.} ({Now, the normal response for Black would be to play} 13. Rh3 Qa5 {, forcing our king to f2 right away.}) 13... Rf7 {This is Black's most common move. It is sort of a waiting move, preemptively protecting f7.} (13... a5 14. Rg3 $1 Rf7 15. Qc2 $1 $14 {White is ready for} Z0 16. O-O-O {next, with a dangerous attacking setup on the kingside.}) 14. Kf2 $1 {The time is right. Now, Black is unable to sacrifice on e5 due to tactical reasons.} fxe5 15. fxe5 (15. dxe5 {This one is also quite testing, but I prefer the text.}) 15... Bg5 $1 {So far, this accurate move has only been played in one game out of four. The point is that Black has not necessarily given up on sacrificing on e5, but they want to get the bishop to g5 first. And because the b2-pawn is loose, we supposedly cannot play Kg1.} 16. Kg1 $1 {I will not be dissuaded so easily.} Bxe3+ 17. Qxe3 Qxb2 18. Re1 {Black has won a pawn, but the kingside attack will be very dangerous. They have to be precise here.} Ne7 $1 {Only move. Otherwise, Rg3 would cause massive problems.} 19. Nh4 $1 {So we renew the threat.} cxd4 $1 {This is far from an obvious move-allowing the knight to d4 accelerates White's development. Now, Black probably holds if it knows absolutely everything, but White has a lot of tries.} 20. Nxd4 {Of course, we take with the knight to bring more pieces into the game.} Nc5 $1 {Black cannot allow Bd3.} (20... Nf8 $2 21. Bd3 $16 {Black faces a vicious attack in the upcoming moves.}) 21. Rf3 $1 {We start trading off Black's defensive pieces.} Rxf3 22. gxf3 $1 {[%cal Ge1e2,Ge2g2] Re2-g2 is on the way. Perhaps Black can survive here, but it looks very scary to me.} *

