1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... Nxd4 11. Bxd4 a6 12. Qe3 $1 ({Nice and simple. White wants} 12. Qe3 Z0 13. Bd3 {next without losing a piece.}) 12... Qc7 13. Bd3 Bxd4 14. Qxd4 b5 15. Rhe1 $1 {White overprotects the e5-pawn, and is ready for f4-f5 next. The machine insists Black needs to trade queens here.} Qc5 (15... Rb8 $2 16. f5 $1 b4 17. f6 $1 {Black is mated.}) 16. Ne2 $14 {White has a typical structural edge for the Steinitz, and their rooks are much better placed than they often are. f4-f5 could come sooner or later.} *

