[White "Quickstarter Guide: 1...c6 & 1...e6"]
[Black "French Steinitz with 7...cxd4 #2"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "C11"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "49"]
[GameId "2288537760571496"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Qb6 {In modern times, this has become the main move. Black goes pawn-grabbing in the style of the Bg5 Najdorf, and hopes they can get away with it.For once, they will not have a strategically suspect position early on; we will, with the pawn less. But with energetic play, we will quickly gain the pawn back and get ahead on development.} 9. Qd2 $1 Qxb2 {Nothing else makes any sense.} 10. Rb1 Qa3 11. Bb5 $1 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 a6 13. Bxd7+ Bxd7 14. Rb3 $1 {Before we take the pawn back, we provoke Black's queen to a worse square.} Qe7 15. Rxb7 {Now, the battle lines are drawn. Black has the bishop pair, and their light-squared bishop is not nearly as bad as it is in a normal closed French structure. White has weaknesses down the c-file. If Black can complete the development and consolidate, they should be easily fine. But that is a big if!} Qh4+ {This is Black's most common move. They want to provoke some kind of concession before ultimately bringing the queen back home.} 16. Qf2 $1 {I think we can go for an endgame right away.} Qxf2+ 17. Kxf2 Be7 {Now, Black would be totally fine if they had one more move to play ...Bd8. They could then go ...Bc6 and castle. But we are too fast.} 18. Rhb1 $1 Bd8 19. Rb8 $1 {Black faces concrete issues on the back rank. Now, the position becomes very forced.} Rxb8 20. Rxb8 O-O 21. Rb7 $1 Bc6 22. Ra7 $1 {White wins a pawn.} Ba5 $1 23. Rxa6 Bxc3 24. Bxc3 Rc8 25. Ke3 $14 {This endgame is reached by force. I definitely think it should be holdable for Black, but they are a long way from securing the draw. They will not get their pawn back, and they cannot trade rooks; the resulting bishop endgame will win for White with the outside passer.In human practice, this position has been reached 8 times. Black held 6 of them, leaving White with 5.0/8. Of course, this is a small sample size, but scoring 62.5% is a lot better than you normally expect from move 1! Yes, the position should be held, but I think this is the best White can hope for, and Black still has some work to do.We'll see a game where even the recently crowned rapid World Champion went down with Black in a slow game, highlighting how the defense is not totally trivial.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
