1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({At long last, it is time to cover the main line; I consider this to be} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({here or} 6... Qc7 {. For the most part, these two moves will transpose. But each one has a couple of independent options that you should know how to handle. Understanding the resulting middlegame is extremely important, particularly with respect to when to play Qg4 and when not to.While you will need to memorize some specific moments, there are a few guidelines to help along the way.1. Black's best plan to meet Qg4 tends to be to immediately pressure the center. As such, if you ever see a move like ...b7-b6 or ...Bd7, Qg4 may well be the best move. You should not play it if Black is making moves that target the center.2. Black can aim to play ...b7-b6 and ...Ba6, trading off light-squared bishops at some point. If they do, it will be extremely important to have Qg4 available. Otherwise, you won't really be able to pressure the kingside.3. If Black ever plays ...Nc6, we can generally put the knight on f3 without too much concern. This rules out Qg4, but I don't think Qg4 is the right idea if Black is pressuring the center anyway. And, by playing ...Nc6, Black is showing that they will not be playing ...Bc8-a6 to trade light-squared bishops.4. Black tends to be fine if they can lock the position with ...c5-c4 and also prevent us from advancing a3-a4, clearing the a3-square for the bishop. In general, they would like to do this by playing Ba4 and not by playing Qa4. If they lock the position without stopping a3-a4, White should be better with a4 and Ba3.5. We are advancing h2-h4, h4-h5 to gain space. If Black ever allows h5-h6 to come safely, they are probably just going to lose. The weakness of the g5-square will be a big problem. But, be careful not to advance h5-h6 if Black's knight remains on g8. They will then be able to take on h6 with the knight, and their position in the resulting structure will be much safer than it would be if we got to keep our dark-squared bishop.})) 6... Ne7 7. h4 Qc7 8. h5 h6 {I consider this position to be the most critical tabiya of the Winawer.The machine gives White some edge, but things are not so simple. It often wants to go for some Qg4, and Qxg7, sacrificing the queenside. So, it starts with something like Ra1-b1, making sure that Qxc3 won't fork the rook on a1. It's definitely not that easy for White to show a clear edge, but I am happy with what I came up with.} 9. Rh3 $1 ({I like this flexible move a lot.First of all, by overprotecting the c3-square, White can play} 9. Rh3 Z0 10. Qg4 {much more easily.}) {And secondly, by not committing the f3-knight so soon, the queen still has access to g4. At this point, Black should make up their mind between Bd7 , Nbc6 and b6 .} 9... b6 {As is usually the case, whenever Black plays ...b7-b6, Qg4 becomes a much more attractive possibility since we are less concerned with direct counterplay on the queenside and in the center. I don't want to play it just yet, although I am glad to have it in reserve when the moment is right.} 10. a4 $1 ({This is a typical way to respond to ...b7-b6. White makes sure that they are ready to meet} 10. a4 Ba6 {with a check on b5,} 11. Bb5+ {, forcing Black to improve our structure if they want to get the bishops off the board.}) 10... Ba6 11. Bb5+ $1 Bxb5 12. axb5 {Black should be very precise here. I like that the human moves are not great.} a6 $6 ({This is somewhat better than} 12... a5 {here, but still ill-advised.}) 13. Qg4 $1 Rg8 14. c4 $1 {Black is ill prepared for the center to open like this.} dxc4 15. Ne2 $1 Qb7 16. Rha3 $14 {The position looks a bit artificial, but Black is in trouble. The e4-knight is not so far away from d6.} *

