1. e4 b6 {[%cal Gf2f4,Ge4e5,Gb1c3,Gg1f3,Gf1d3] I think 1...b6 is definitely worse than any other first move this course covers in its own chapter, but better than other first moves in the present section.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Bottom of the Barrel: Introduction & Model Game"]
[Black "Hracek, Zbynek vs. Blatny"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "89"]
[GameId "2288537760591982"]

1. e4 b6 {This game is a nice illustration of how to handle the hippo. I find that the people who play it fall into two extremes:▷ The total beginners looking for an easy setup they can memorize for every game.▷ The GMs who have really mastered the Black side and play it as their specialty line.For instance, Elshan was playing this at the US Open a few years ago with some success.} 2. d4 Bb7 3. Bd3 e6 4. Nf3 {You can train the line here.} Ne7 5. O-O g6 6. c4 Bg7 7. Nc3 d6 8. Be3 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4] The first step is always three pawns in the center. Perhaps the d3-bishop was better placed on e2, but since Black played it through the Owen move order, the bishop had to come here to protect the pawn.} Nd7 9. Qd2 h6 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Gd2h6] Setting up this battery here makes it hard for Black to get castled. Now Black is committed to playing ...g6-g5.} 10. Bc2 a6 11. b4 {[%csl Gc5][%cal Gc4c5] With three pawns instead of two, White is ready to break immediately. The wall falls apart quickly with c4-c5 incoming to blast open the position. Meanwhile, Black is still scrambling to castle.} g5 12. h3 Nf6 13. Rad1 g4 (13... O-O 14. e5 $1 {immediately causes Black's position to implode.}) 14. Nh2 gxh3 15. g3 h5 16. Qe2 Ng4 17. Bg5 f6 18. Bc1 Kf7 19. d5 e5 20. Nxg4 hxg4 21. Kh2 $1 {[%csl Yb7,Rg4,Rh3][%cal Gc4c5] Not capturing the pawn and instead leaving the kingside completely dead. The h3-pawn is the best defender for White!Now we have a characteristic structure for the Hippo; a KID where Black has played ...b7-b6 and ...Bb7. This should be hugely in White's favor, as these are moves Black normally tries to avoid in this structure.} Bc8 22. c5 Bh6 23. Bxh6 Rxh6 24. cxb6 cxb6 25. Rc1 Rg6 26. Nd1 Ra7 27. Ne3 Kg7 28. Bd3 Rg5 29. Rc3 a5 30. b5 Bd7 31. Rfc1 Rb7 32. Qc2 {If it wasn't evident by now, Black is totally lost. There is nothing to do on the kingside.} Rb8 33. Rc7 Kg6 34. a4 Kh5 35. Qd1 Kg6 36. Be2 f5 37. exf5+ Bxf5 38. Nxf5 Nxf5 39. Bxg4 Qf6 40. R1c6 e4 41. Bxf5+ Kxf5 42. Qc2 Re8 43. Rxb6 Qd4 44. Rxd6 Rf8 45. Rf7+ 1-0

