1. e4 g5 $2 ({If you are playing online, I would suspect this move is a mouseslip, as} 1... g6 {is a much more serious option.}) 2. d4 Bg7 {Some sort of reverse Grob. Given how bad the grob is, it should surprise no one that this is a dumpster fire.} ({▷} 2... h6 3. h4 {transposes to the 1...h7-h6 lines.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nc3 $18 {leaves Black searching for the meaning of life.}) 3. Bxg5 {This time, the pawn is safe to capture. We can stabilize the position quite easily.} c5 {[%cal Gg7b2]} 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Qb6 6. Nb5 $1 {[%cal Gb1c3,Rb6b2] Shielding the b2-pawn from the queen, while also floating the idea of Nc7+ at some point. Most importantly, it is preparing to move the other knight to c3, ending black's counterplay for good.} a6 {[%csl Rb5]} 7. Be3 {[%csl Rb6]} Qa5+ 8. N5c3 Qb4 {[%csl Rb2]} 9. Qc1 $18 {[%csl Gb2][%cal Gf1d3,Ga2a3,Ge1g1] With this last move, b2 and c3 are both overprotected. Black has run out of steam and is missing an important pawn for no compensation.} *

