1. e4 g5 $2 ({If you are playing online, I would suspect this move is a mouseslip, as} 1... g6 {is a much more serious option.}) 2. d4 Bg7 {Some sort of reverse Grob. Given how bad the grob is, it should surprise no one that this is a dumpster fire.} ({▷} 2... h6 3. h4 {transposes to the 1...h7-h6 lines.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nc3 $18 {leaves Black searching for the meaning of life.}) 3. Bxg5 {This time, the pawn is safe to capture. We can stabilize the position quite easily.} c5 {[%cal Gg7b2]} 4. Nf3 cxd4 5. Nxd4 Qb6 6. Nb5 $1 {[%cal Gb1c3,Rb6b2] Shielding the b2-pawn from the queen, while also floating the idea of Nc7+ at some point. Most importantly, it is preparing to move the other knight to c3, ending black's counterplay for good.} Bxb2 {This must be tried.} 7. N1c3 Bxa1 8. Qxa1 $18 {[%cal Gc3d5,Ga1h8,Gd5c7] Black has won the rook, but at a great cost: Nd5 will win the rook back, with huge interest.} *

