1. e4 a6 {The Saint George is not quite as terrible as it might look, but it is not dangerous at all. Black's best chances to fight involve immediately abandoning their plan on the second move and trying to transpose to a mainline opening where they have committed to ...a7-a6.} (1... a5 2. d4 $16 {Here, Black has tried everything under the sun, but the common pattern is that ...a7-a5 is much less useful than its counterpart, ...a7-a6. In fact, this move only serves to weaken the b5-square. Any transposition will lead to a better version of the opening being transposed to.We do three pawns in the center, develop to natural squares, and prepare to steamroll on the kingside.}) 2. d4 {Technically, there are a ton of 'okayish' options here for Black, but most of them transpose to other lines, so I will just cover the top 3 options that don't transpose for Black.} e6 {This is the better way to implement ...b7-b5, waiting for the knight to develop to c3 before playing ...b7-b5 so a2-a4 can be met with ...b5-b4.} 3. Be3 (3. Nc3 {transposes to the French against} d5 {, but 3. Be3 is a bit better in case Black tries to expand on the queenside with ...b7-b5 and ...c7-c5, since the knight will not be in the way. Thus, ...b7-b5 is still met with b2-b4, so Black has to find something else to do.}) 3... d5 (3... d6 {is a Hippo transposition.}) 4. Nd2 $14 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gf1d3,Ge1g1] This is some weird version of the Nd2-French, but Be3 is more useful than ...a7-a6, so we are doing fine here. Normal development should maintain the advantage.} *

