1. e4 a6 {The Saint George is not quite as terrible as it might look, but it is not dangerous at all. Black's best chances to fight involve immediately abandoning their plan on the second move and trying to transpose to a mainline opening where they have committed to ...a7-a6.} (1... a5 2. d4 $16 {Here, Black has tried everything under the sun, but the common pattern is that ...a7-a5 is much less useful than its counterpart, ...a7-a6. In fact, this move only serves to weaken the b5-square. Any transposition will lead to a better version of the opening being transposed to.We do three pawns in the center, develop to natural squares, and prepare to steamroll on the kingside.}) 2. d4 {Technically, there are a ton of 'okayish' options here for Black, but most of them transpose to other lines, so I will just cover the top 3 options that don't transpose for Black.} c5 $2 {[%cal Bc5d4]} 3. dxc5 $1 {It's not trivial to get this pawn back. That's why in the Sicilian, Black always initiates the capture.} Qa5+ $6 {[%csl Rc5]} 4. Nd2 Qxc5 5. Nc4 $18 {[%cal Gc1e3,Gc4b6] Believe it or not, Black is already busted. There is nothing to be done about Be3-Nb6.} *

