1. e4 a6 {The Saint George is not quite as terrible as it might look, but it is not dangerous at all. Black's best chances to fight involve immediately abandoning their plan on the second move and trying to transpose to a mainline opening where they have committed to ...a7-a6.} (1... a5 2. d4 $16 {Here, Black has tried everything under the sun, but the common pattern is that ...a7-a5 is much less useful than its counterpart, ...a7-a6. In fact, this move only serves to weaken the b5-square. Any transposition will lead to a better version of the opening being transposed to.We do three pawns in the center, develop to natural squares, and prepare to steamroll on the kingside.}) 2. d4 {Technically, there are a ton of 'okayish' options here for Black, but most of them transpose to other lines, so I will just cover the top 3 options that don't transpose for Black.} c5 $2 {[%cal Bc5d4]} 3. dxc5 $1 {It's not trivial to get this pawn back. That's why in the Sicilian, Black always initiates the capture.} e6 {[%cal Bf8c5]} 4. Be3 Qc7 {[%csl Rc5]} 5. Nc3 Bxc5 6. Bxc5 Qxc5 7. Qg4 $16 {[%csl Yb6,Rc8,Yd6,Yg7] Forcing the black king to either go uncastled or create more weaknesses with ...g7-g6. Clearly their position is suffering due to the absence of the dark-squared bishops.} *

