1. e4 a6 {The Saint George is not quite as terrible as it might look, but it is not dangerous at all. Black's best chances to fight involve immediately abandoning their plan on the second move and trying to transpose to a mainline opening where they have committed to ...a7-a6.} (1... a5 2. d4 $16 {Here, Black has tried everything under the sun, but the common pattern is that ...a7-a5 is much less useful than its counterpart, ...a7-a6. In fact, this move only serves to weaken the b5-square. Any transposition will lead to a better version of the opening being transposed to.We do three pawns in the center, develop to natural squares, and prepare to steamroll on the kingside.}) 2. d4 {Technically, there are a ton of 'okayish' options here for Black, but most of them transpose to other lines, so I will just cover the top 3 options that don't transpose for Black.} b5 {The most original way to play the position that doesn't go into a hippo or a worse version of some other opening. Unfortunately, it's also not very good.} ({▷} 2... d6 3. Nc3 {will either transpose to a modern or a hippo.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. Nc3 d5 4. Bd3 {transposes to the ...a7-a6 Caro sideline.}) 3. a4 $1 {Immediately busting down the standout pawn. Ideally, Black should have waited for us to place the knight on c3 before lunging out with ...b7-b5, because now ...b5-b4 is not as effective of a response.} b4 {[%csl Rb5]} ({▷} 3... Bb7 4. Nd2 $1 $16 {Patience. The problem of the b5-pawn still must be solved.}) ({▷} 3... bxa4 4. Rxa4 $16) 4. Nd2 $16 {[%csl Rc4] White is much better thanks to the extra space and hole on c4.} *

