1. e4 f5 $4 {The worst move Black can play on move one, and the only one where White is winning right off the bat. Therefore, we should try to do our worst.} 2. exf5 {[%cal Gd1h5]} Nf6 {Otherwise a check on h5 causes even more problems.} 3. d4 d5 {[%csl Rf5] I can't imagine anything else coming.} 4. Bd3 ({Black has three options: play} 4. Bd3 e6 ({immediately, play} 4... c5 {first, then ...e7-e6, or play}) (4... Nc6 {then ...e7-e6.})) 4... Nc6 {[%csl Rd4]} 5. Nf3 e6 (5... Ne4 6. Nh4 $1 $18 {White protects the f5-pawn and is ready for a check on h5. Black is dead lost.}) 6. fxe6 {As usual, after ...e7-e6, I just want to take it and keep things simple.} ({The computer thinks} 6. O-O {is even stronger, but I see a clean pawn up for nothing, and that's good enough for me.The following line shows a decent way for White to complete their development and coordinate their pieces.}) 6... Bxe6 7. O-O Be7 8. Re1 {[%csl Re6]} Bg4 {[%csl Rd4]} 9. c3 O-O 10. Nbd2 Qd7 11. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bh5 12. Qc2 {[%cal Gd3f5,Gf3e5] We can stop here. White has stopped all the threats Black may have had and now moves like Ne5 and Bf5 to exploit the weak light squares that are coming up.} *

