[Result "*"]
[ECO "B06"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "19"]
[GameId "2288537760710790"]

1. e4 Nh6 $2 {There are two ways Black usually plays this: The more traditional way is the plan of playing a system with ...g7-g6, ...f7-f6 and ...Nf7, and the more modern interpretation is trying to play ...d7-d5 and clear the f5-square for the knight.} 2. d4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Be3 f6 {This is one of the turtle systems, where Black tries to hunker down and frustrate White by making it hard to break through. However, all turtling systems essentially rely on the opponent either playing a suboptimal setup or attacking recklessly.The way to beat them is to actually play slowly and focus on gaining space and positional advantages. While we won't be able to crash through right, turtle setups lack any offensive or disruptive capacity, so we will not be able to oppose our buildup.} 5. h4 $1 {Now that Black has weakened their kingside with ...f7-f6, we can send the h-pawn to cause problems! Now it's actually best for Black if they ignore this since we are not actually threatening anything yet, but it is prophylaxis against ...Nf7.} Nf7 $6 {[%cal Gf1c4,Gd1d2,Gg1e2]} 6. h5 e6 {Ignoring the pawn is not an option, since it will leave the g6-pawn too weak.} 7. hxg6 hxg6 8. Rxh8+ Bxh8 9. Qg4 {[%csl Rg6]} g5 10. Nh3 $18 {[%cal Gf2f4] Preparing f2-f4. Black is lost for sure.} *

