1. e4 Nh6 $2 {There are two ways Black usually plays this: The more traditional way is the plan of playing a system with ...g7-g6, ...f7-f6 and ...Nf7, and the more modern interpretation is trying to play ...d7-d5 and clear the f5-square for the knight.} 2. d4 g6 3. Nc3 Bg7 4. Be3 f6 {This is one of the turtle systems, where Black tries to hunker down and frustrate White by making it hard to break through. However, all turtling systems essentially rely on the opponent either playing a suboptimal setup or attacking recklessly.The way to beat them is to actually play slowly and focus on gaining space and positional advantages. While we won't be able to crash through right, turtle setups lack any offensive or disruptive capacity, so we will not be able to oppose our buildup.} 5. h4 $1 {Now that Black has weakened their kingside with ...f7-f6, we can send the h-pawn to cause problems! Now it's actually best for Black if they ignore this since we are not actually threatening anything yet, but it is prophylaxis against ...Nf7.} Nf7 $6 {[%cal Gf1c4,Gd1d2,Gg1e2]} 6. h5 g5 7. h6 $1 {[%csl Rg7]} Bxh6 ({▷} 7... Bf8 8. Qh5 $18 {leaves Black nailed in a coffin. There is no room to develop anything. The h6-pawn is simply too cramped, on top of the domination of the center.}) ({▷} 7... Nxh6 $2 {loses on the spot} 8. Rxh6 $1 $18) 8. Bc4 e6 9. d5 $18 {[%csl Rb8,Rc8][%cal Gd1h5,Ge1c1,Gg1e2] Once again, White is not winning material or giving mate yet. But by seizing space, Black can't complete their development while we can develop slowly with Qh5, O-O-O, Nge2, etc.Soon enough, Black will collapse in the center.} *

