[PlyCount "21"]
[GameId "2288537760714888"]

1. e4 Na6 2. d4 c6 {[%cal Ga6c7,Gg7g6,Gf8g7,Gd7d5,Gh7h5,Gg8h6,Gh6f5,Ge4e5] The only way I've seen this opening played. The point is that Black will maneuver the knight to c7 and fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, then eventually set up some sort of blockade on the light squares with ...d7-d5, ...h7-h5, and ...Nh6. Not a totally stupid idea in theory, but like any plan that takes a long time, we know it's coming and can make the necessary preparations.} 3. Nc3 ({Do not fall for} 3. Bxa6 $6 Qa5+ $1 {, which allows Black to maintain the structure.}) 3... Nc7 4. Bd3 {Staying flexible with the g1-knight. You'll notice that in this course, the default response to the worst lines is two pawns in the center and then Nc3 & Bd3.} g6 5. h3 $1 ({If} 5. Nf3 {, Black is hoping for} d5 6. e5 Bg4 {, followed by trading the bishop and blockading the light squares. By preventing ...Bg4, we force Black to maintain the bad bishop, which will be impeded by their own blockading strategy,}) 5... Bg7 6. Nf3 d5 7. e5 f6 $1 {[%cal Gg8h6,Gh6f7,Gc3e2,Ge2f4,Gd1e2] The best try for Black. By creating tension, we cannot go sandbox mode.However, this move goes against Black's whole plan up until this point. Funnily enough, the other knight will be developed in the same manner to increase the pressure on e5. We should get the queen knight to the more effective f4-square before we need e2 for the queen:} 8. Ne2 Nh6 {[%cal Gh6f7]} 9. Nf4 Nf7 10. Qe2 fxe5 11. dxe5 $16 {[%cal Gc2c4,Ge1g1,Gf1d1] Our method of breaking open the center is going to be c2-c4. Black really does not want to play ...e7-e6 and kill the bishop, but if they don't, d5 will be constantly under pressure.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
