[White "1) Bottom of the Barrel"]
[Black "1I | 1...Na6 #2"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B12"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "19"]
[GameId "2288537760718985"]

1. e4 Na6 2. d4 c6 {[%cal Ga6c7,Gg7g6,Gf8g7,Gd7d5,Gh7h5,Gg8h6,Gh6f5,Ge4e5] The only way I've seen this opening played. The point is that Black will maneuver the knight to c7 and fianchetto the dark-squared bishop, then eventually set up some sort of blockade on the light squares with ...d7-d5, ...h7-h5, and ...Nh6. Not a totally stupid idea in theory, but like any plan that takes a long time, we know it's coming and can make the necessary preparations.} 3. Nc3 ({Do not fall for} 3. Bxa6 $6 Qa5+ $1 {, which allows Black to maintain the structure.}) 3... Nc7 4. Bd3 {Staying flexible with the g1-knight. You'll notice that in this course, the default response to the worst lines is two pawns in the center and then Nc3 & Bd3.} g6 5. h3 $1 ({If} 5. Nf3 {, Black is hoping for} d5 6. e5 Bg4 {, followed by trading the bishop and blockading the light squares. By preventing ...Bg4, we force Black to maintain the bad bishop, which will be impeded by their own blockading strategy,}) 5... Bg7 6. Nf3 d5 7. e5 h5 $6 {Consistent, but not viable anymore because Black failed to trade the bishop. Additionally, the Gurgenidze setup makes a lot less sense if we have not committed our pawn to the f4-square.} 8. O-O Nh6 9. Re1 O-O (9... Bf5 10. Bf1 $1 {No trade for you! Next up could be Ne2-g3. Black is more or less busted.}) 10. Nh4 $18 {[%cal Gc3e2,Ge2g3,Gc1e3,Gd1d2] Preventing Black from parking the knight on f5. Believe it or not, Black is already completely busted, with the computer claiming +3. Black can't generate any play before the gradual kingside buildup becomes fatal. For instance, the extremely slow plan is Ne2-g3, followed by Be3-Qd2, which will win material because ...Kh7 can now be met with Nxh5, and requires some major concessions to stop.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
