[Black "1J | 1...b6 #1"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "15"]
[GameId "2288537760718986"]

1. e4 b6 {[%cal Gf2f4,Ge4e5,Gb1c3,Gg1f3,Gf1d3] I had to cut off the bottom of the barrel somewhere, and ultimately, I think 1...b6 is a good place to do so. It is definitely worse than any other first move this course covers in its own chapter, but better than other first moves in the present section.} 2. d4 Bb7 {[%csl Re4] All you need to know about the Owen is to delay moving the knight to c3 since one of Black's main ideas is ...Bb4-Bxc3 to spoil the structure, and how to bust Bd3 f5?. Let's start with the latter.} 3. Bd3 f5 $2 {[%csl Re4] This gambit goes into all of the beginner books as an example of why moving the f-pawn early is bad, so that's already a poor sign for Black's prospects. I remember one of my first-ever chess classes when I was 10 years old featuring this position! The fictional players were 'Sneaky Sammy' vs. 'Foolish Freddy'.} 4. exf5 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} Bxg2 {This was the intended idea, but it fails concretely.} 5. Qh5+ g6 6. fxg6 Nf6 7. gxh7+ Nxh5 8. Bg6# {This was how Foolish Freddy found himself mated on move 8.} *

