[Black "1J | 1...b6 #2"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "21"]
[GameId "2288537760723083"]

1. e4 b6 {[%cal Gf2f4,Ge4e5,Gb1c3,Gg1f3,Gf1d3] I had to cut off the bottom of the barrel somewhere, and ultimately, I think 1...b6 is a good place to do so. It is definitely worse than any other first move this course covers in its own chapter, but better than other first moves in the present section.} 2. d4 Bb7 {[%csl Re4] All you need to know about the Owen is to delay moving the knight to c3 since one of Black's main ideas is ...Bb4-Bxc3 to spoil the structure, and how to bust Bd3 f5?. Let's start with the latter.} 3. Bd3 f5 $2 {[%csl Re4] This gambit goes into all of the beginner books as an example of why moving the f-pawn early is bad, so that's already a poor sign for Black's prospects. I remember one of my first-ever chess classes when I was 10 years old featuring this position! The fictional players were 'Sneaky Sammy' vs. 'Foolish Freddy'.} 4. exf5 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} Bxg2 {This was the intended idea, but it fails concretely.} 5. Qh5+ g6 6. fxg6 Bg7 {[%cal Rg6g7] Otherwise g6-g7+ would have been brutal.} 7. gxh7+ Kf8 8. Nh3 $1 {[%cal Gh3f4,Bh3g5,Gf4g6,Yh1g1] Creating three different threats, which can't all be prevented.} (8. hxg8=Q+ $2 Kxg8 9. Qg4 Bxh1 {gives Black some hope, because the king is at least not immediately mated.}) 8... Nf6 $2 {[%csl Rh5]} 9. Qg6 {The reason why Nh3 is better than Nf3. After Qg6, the bishop will be on the wrong square to block the b1-h7 diagonal after capturing the knight.} Bxh3 10. Rg1 $1 {[%csl Rg7]} Rxh7 11. Qg3 $18 {[%csl Rg7,Rh3,Rh7][%cal Gd3h7,Yc1h6,Yg1g8] A slick retreating move that leaves your opponent stewing in his own bad decisions. The rook hangs and can't move because the bishop needs protection.But if Black moves the bishop, White has Bh6+. The more you look at it, the worse it gets. After the rook gets captured, Black is lost for sure.} *

