1. e4 b6 {[%cal Gf2f4,Ge4e5,Gb1c3,Gg1f3,Gf1d3] I had to cut off the bottom of the barrel somewhere, and ultimately, I think 1...b6 is a good place to do so. It is definitely worse than any other first move this course covers in its own chapter, but better than other first moves in the present section.} 2. d4 Bb7 {[%csl Re4] All you need to know about the Owen is to delay moving the knight to c3 since one of Black's main ideas is ...Bb4-Bxc3 to spoil the structure, and how to bust Bd3 f5?. Let's start with the latter.} 3. Bd3 f5 $2 {[%csl Re4] This gambit goes into all of the beginner books as an example of why moving the f-pawn early is bad, so that's already a poor sign for Black's prospects. I remember one of my first-ever chess classes when I was 10 years old featuring this position! The fictional players were 'Sneaky Sammy' vs. 'Foolish Freddy'.} 4. exf5 $1 {What happens if I do it anyway?} Bxg2 {This was the intended idea, but it fails concretely.} 5. Qh5+ g6 6. fxg6 Bg7 {[%cal Rg6g7] Otherwise g6-g7+ would have been brutal.} 7. gxh7+ Kf8 8. Nh3 $1 {[%cal Gh3f4,Bh3g5,Gf4g6,Yh1g1] Creating three different threats, which can't all be prevented.} (8. hxg8=Q+ $2 Kxg8 9. Qg4 Bxh1 {gives Black some hope, because the king is at least not immediately mated.}) 8... Bxh1 {Black should at least try to take some material, but he is very lost.} 9. Ng5 Nh6 10. Nc3 $18 {[%csl Rf8,Rg7,Rh6][%cal Gc1d2,Ge1c1,Gb1c3,Gc1g1,Gg5e6] Slow and steady. It turns out that despite being down a rook, there is no rush to do something immediate because of the completely bare black king.Instead, we can afford to leisurely develop the pieces, get castled, and finally get the other rook to g1. Unfortunately, there's not much our opponent can do to interfere with this simple plan, even though they have a few moves to do so.} Nc6 {For instance, we can ignore.} 11. Bd2 Nxd4 {[%cal Gd8e8,Gd3g6]} (11... Qe8 {can always be met with} 12. Bg6 {.}) 12. O-O-O {[%csl Rh1]} Bc6 13. Ne6+ $1 {[%csl Rh6]} dxe6 14. Bxh6 {[%csl Rf8][%cal Gd1g1] The king is eventually mated, or Black will sustain catastrophic material losses.} *

