[PlyCount "19"]
[GameId "2288537760727181"]

1. e4 b6 {[%cal Gf2f4,Ge4e5,Gb1c3,Gg1f3,Gf1d3] I had to cut off the bottom of the barrel somewhere, and ultimately, I think 1...b6 is a good place to do so. It is definitely worse than any other first move this course covers in its own chapter, but better than other first moves in the present section.} 2. d4 Bb7 {[%csl Re4] All you need to know about the Owen is to delay moving the knight to c3 since one of Black's main ideas is ...Bb4-Bxc3 to spoil the structure, and how to bust Bd3 f5?. Let's start with the latter.} 3. Bd3 e6 ({▷} 3... Nf6 4. Qe2 $1 $16 {Protecting the pawn in the least committal way. Also importantly, avoiding Nc3 Bb4 ideas.}) ({▷ Against} 3... d5 {, we always want to play} 4. e5 $16 {, which gives Black a lousy French with this bishop developed to the wrong side.}) ({▷} 3... d6 4. c4 {is a poor King's Indian structure, where the bishop is on the wrong diagonal, and Black has created an additional hook on the queenside voluntarily.}) 4. Nf3 {I like this move early, since it rules out ...f7-f5 shenanigans for good.} d6 {Black's best bet is probably to play a Hippo, and I think this is his best move order to do so. But, they certainly will not equalize.} ({▷} 4... c5 5. c3 $16 {nothing too complicated. Black is not losing or anything, but they have given us free reign in the center. From here, we can eventually play e4-e5 and start something on the kingside, where we have the space advantage.If Black doesn't take on d4, we can consider initiating the capture with dxc5 and opening up the d-file to target the backward pawn on d7 and the weak d6-square.}) ({▷} 4... Nf6 {I gave Nc3, followed by ...Bb4 and Nd2 in this exact position in my 1. d4 repertoire. That's definitely good for White, but we can be even more direct.} 5. Qe2 $1 {No need to allow any ...Bb4. Black's best bet is probably to push ...d7-d5 at some point and end up in a really bad French.}) 5. c4 $1 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4] A good rule of thumb for playing against the Hippo is to put three pawns in the center. Not two, and not four. Three. Then you push the middle one, and when Black tries to close the position, you break with the outside one.Had White chosen a setup with d4, e4 and f4, the plan should be to play for e4-e5 and to meet ...d6-d5 with an f4-f5 break. In this case, we will instead aim to play d4-d5 and break with c4-c5.} Nd7 6. Nc3 g6 7. O-O Bg7 8. Be3 Ne7 9. Qd2 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Gd2h6]} O-O 10. Rad1 $16 {Black doesn't have much of a plan here since they can't ever hope to break with either ...c7-c5 or ...f7-f5. Sooner or later, d4-d5 and b2-b4 will come.} *

