1. e4 Nc6 $5 {The Nimzowitsch Defense. An offbeat opening for sure, but one that is actually quite robust. It is tough enough that I think it merits its own section and does not belong at the bottom of the barrel.} 2. d4 d5 {[%cal Bc8f5,Be7e6,Bf7f6,Bd8d7,Be8c8,Ge4e5,Gc2c3,Gf2f4] ...is the best move; Black plays a Caro/French structure but gets to develop both the knight and bishop actively, and will use ...f7-f6 as their main pawn break.} ({▷} 2... d6 $2 3. d5 {and}) ({▷} 2... e5 $2 3. d5 {are much worse, since we can directly attack the c6-knight. In these lines, Black is often coping with a severe lack of space.}) 3. e5 Bf5 4. c3 e6 {Black is quite solid and has good dynamics. In an ideal world, Black can get in ...Qd7, ...O-O-O, ...f7-f6, and be quite happy. We need to make sure things don't go this smoothly for Black, often through combinations of threatening g2-g4 and h2-h4, h4-h5, so Black has to worry about the security of the bishop on f5.} *

