1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d6 $6 {A few fanatic defenders of this line exist, like Kostya Kavutskiy, but if there is one thing I am going to drill into your mind, it's going to be the value of space. Modern engines all consider space as one of the most important factors.That's why most of these sidelines that dilly dally on the flanks are borderline unplayable.} 3. d5 $1 {[%csl Rc6] Usually, the downside with gaining space is that you are losing time, so you will fall behind in development and the pawns may become overextended. However, Black has conveniently fixed this problem for us by allowing us to gain time and space at the same time!} Nb8 4. c4 {[%cal Gc7c6,Ge7e6,Gc4d5,Ge4d5] You should make sure to keep the pawn on d5 defended twice so White can maintain a pawn here in case it is challenged by both the c- and e-pawns.} e5 5. Nc3 g6 6. h3 Bg7 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. g4 $16 {[%csl Rf5][%cal Gc1e3,Gd1c2,Gf3d2,Rf7f5] With this move, we reach a Makogonov King's Indian with an extra tempo.Black's position is terrible, and they lack any sources of counterplay as we slowly take over the whole board. If you're not a d4 player, then the basic development scheme is to play Be3/Qc2/Nd2.} *

