1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d6 $6 {A few fanatic defenders of this line exist, like Kostya Kavutskiy, but if there is one thing I am going to drill into your mind, it's going to be the value of space. Modern engines all consider space as one of the most important factors.That's why most of these sidelines that dilly dally on the flanks are borderline unplayable.} 3. d5 $1 {[%csl Rc6] Usually, the downside with gaining space is that you are losing time, so you will fall behind in development and the pawns may become overextended. However, Black has conveniently fixed this problem for us by allowing us to gain time and space at the same time!} Nb8 4. c4 {[%cal Gc7c6,Ge7e6,Gc4d5,Ge4d5] You should make sure to keep the pawn on d5 defended twice so White can maintain a pawn here in case it is challenged by both the c- and e-pawns.} e5 5. Nc3 Be7 6. h3 {Sticking with the h2-h3 idea.} Bg5 {[%csl Gc1,Gg5] Black has to try something, otherwise it is business as usual with Bd3, Nf3, and so on. Fortunately, we have a fun idea here:} 7. Bxg5 Qxg5 8. c5 $1 {[%cal Gc4c5,Gd5d6,Gc3b5,Gf1c4] Blowing open the position before Black can get their bearings.} Qd8 {Is the other option, maintaining the pawn.} 9. Nf3 Nf6 10. cxd6 cxd6 11. Be2 $16 {[%csl Rc7,Rd6][%cal Ga1c1,Gc3b5,Gb2b4,Gd1d2,Ge1g1] A very good version of the King's Indian mainlines. While it's true Black was able to get rid of the bad bishop, White has also boosted the queenside play by a few turns, while Black is a few turns behind getting f5. In fact, one idea we can float is playing g2-g4 ourselves to lock Black out of the kingside too.} *

