1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d6 $6 {A few fanatic defenders of this line exist, like Kostya Kavutskiy, but if there is one thing I am going to drill into your mind, it's going to be the value of space. Modern engines all consider space as one of the most important factors.That's why most of these sidelines that dilly dally on the flanks are borderline unplayable.} 3. d5 $1 {[%csl Rc6] Usually, the downside with gaining space is that you are losing time, so you will fall behind in development and the pawns may become overextended. However, Black has conveniently fixed this problem for us by allowing us to gain time and space at the same time!} Nb8 4. c4 {[%cal Gc7c6,Ge7e6,Gc4d5,Ge4d5] You should make sure to keep the pawn on d5 defended twice so White can maintain a pawn here in case it is challenged by both the c- and e-pawns.} e5 5. Nc3 Be7 6. h3 {Sticking with the h2-h3 idea.} Bg5 {[%csl Gc1,Gg5] Black has to try something, otherwise it is business as usual with Bd3, Nf3, and so on. Fortunately, we have a fun idea here:} 7. Bxg5 Qxg5 8. c5 $1 {[%cal Gc4c5,Gd5d6,Gc3b5,Gf1c4] Blowing open the position before Black can get their bearings.} dxc5 9. Nf3 {[%csl Re5,Rg5]} Qe7 {[%cal Be7e5] Black would love to play Qd8 instead, but the queen has to maintain contact with the e5-pawn.} 10. Nb5 $1 {[%cal Gd5d6]} Kf8 $7 {The only way not to get killed by d5-d6.} 11. Bc4 $16 {[%csl Rc7,Rf7][%cal Gd5d6,Gb2b4,Gb5c7,Gc4f7,Ga1c1,Gc1c7] Now Black needs to find ...Nh6 or else their position is close to losing, and in general, Black is living on the edge here.A few ideas for this position: we can consider playing b2-b4 to open up the c-file for the rook. d5-d6 is still on the cards, though it's less effective against ...Nh6 specifically, which defends f7 after the pawns are traded.Finally, in the event of ...a7-a6, we have the piece sacrifice Nxc7, followed up with d5-d6 and Nxe5, which leads to long term compensation.} *

