1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d6 $6 {A few fanatic defenders of this line exist, like Kostya Kavutskiy, but if there is one thing I am going to drill into your mind, it's going to be the value of space. Modern engines all consider space as one of the most important factors.That's why most of these sidelines that dilly dally on the flanks are borderline unplayable.} 3. d5 $1 {[%csl Rc6] Usually, the downside with gaining space is that you are losing time, so you will fall behind in development and the pawns may become overextended. However, Black has conveniently fixed this problem for us by allowing us to gain time and space at the same time!} Nb8 4. c4 {[%cal Gc7c6,Ge7e6,Gc4d5,Ge4d5] You should make sure to keep the pawn on d5 defended twice so White can maintain a pawn here in case it is challenged by both the c- and e-pawns.} g6 5. Nc3 Bg7 {Now we need to be a little accurate – pay attention to the move order Black is making. We need to ask ourselves why it might be the case that they are developing the bishop first.} (5... Nf6 {transposes to the main line.}) 6. Qc2 $1 {[%csl Rc3][%cal Gc2c3] Preventing the idea of ...Bxc3+!?, which would ruin the pawn structure and give something for Black to play for. Other moves are of the same evaluation, but why give Black extra options? Now they have to play ...Nf6 anyway, and go back to the main line positions.} (6. h3 $6 Bxc3+ $1 7. bxc3 {in the long run, Black can play for a dark-squared blockade. It is hard for us to use the unopposed bishop effectively, since the c3-pawn impedes the diagonal.}) 6... Nf6 7. Nf3 $16 {[%cal Gf1e2,Gh2h3,Ge1g1,Gc1e3] One small difference is that with the queen on c2 instead of d1, the f3-knight is not pinned by ...Bg4, and we might consider placing the bishop on e2 instead of d3. Otherwise, everything is the same as the main line.} *

