[PlyCount "15"]
[GameId "2288537760772248"]

1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d6 $6 {A few fanatic defenders of this line exist, like Kostya Kavutskiy, but if there is one thing I am going to drill into your mind, it's going to be the value of space. Modern engines all consider space as one of the most important factors.That's why most of these sidelines that dilly dally on the flanks are borderline unplayable.} 3. d5 $1 {[%csl Rc6] Usually, the downside with gaining space is that you are losing time, so you will fall behind in development and the pawns may become overextended. However, Black has conveniently fixed this problem for us by allowing us to gain time and space at the same time!} Nb8 4. c4 {[%cal Gc7c6,Ge7e6,Gc4d5,Ge4d5] You should make sure to keep the pawn on d5 defended twice so White can maintain a pawn here in case it is challenged by both the c- and e-pawns.} Nf6 {[%cal Gc2c4,Gd4d5] This is a Pirc down two tempi c2-c4 and d4-d5, a complete disaster for Black.} 5. Nc3 g6 {The best development scheme for Black. Any attempts to fight for the center at this point are too little, too late.} 6. h3 $1 {[%cal Gg1f3,Yc8g4] Ruling out ...Bg4 before we play Nf3. We want to make Black's position as claustrophobic as possible by not allowing a single piece trade!} Bg7 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 $16 {[%cal Ge1g1,Gc1e3,Ga1b1,Gb2b4] The optimal square for the bishop. Unlike the King's Indian, we do not have to watch over the d4-pawn.In the upcoming moves, castling and developing naturally will maintain our advantage into the middlegame.} *

