[Black "2B | 2...e5 #1"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "A41"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "17"]
[GameId "2288537760776345"]

1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 $6 {Another iteration of these lines where White gets to have e2-e4 and d4-d5 for nothing. All else being equal, the space advantage counts for a significant amount.} 3. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nce7 4. Nf3 d6 {[%csl Rf8] Black puts an end to any d5-d6 shenanigans, but at the cost of shutting in the f8-bishop. This also gives us time to develop the queen knight behind the pawn.} 5. c4 $1 {[%csl Gc4,Gd5,Ge4][%cal Gb1c3] Taking all the space. From here, Black can carry out one of three plans: ▷ ...f7-f5 immediately, ▷ ...f7-f5 with ...g7-g6 first, ▷ ...Nf6 and ...Ng6, like the main line.} f5 {Without the support of the g6-pawn, we can create a hole on e4.} 6. exf5 {[%csl Ge4]} Nxf5 7. Nc3 Nf6 8. Bd3 g6 9. h4 $16 {[%csl Rf5,Rg6,Rh7][%cal Gh4h5,Gd1c2,Gb1h7] Once the g-pawn sticks its neck out, we hammer away at this weakness. Following Qc2 and h4-h5, there will be enormous pressure along the b1-h7 diagonal, preventing Black from realizing their ultimate goal of ...Nd4.} *

