1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 $6 {Another iteration of these lines where White gets to have e2-e4 and d4-d5 for nothing. All else being equal, the space advantage counts for a significant amount.} 3. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nce7 4. Nf3 d6 {[%csl Rf8] Black puts an end to any d5-d6 shenanigans, but at the cost of shutting in the f8-bishop. This also gives us time to develop the queen knight behind the pawn.} 5. c4 $1 {[%csl Gc4,Gd5,Ge4][%cal Gb1c3] Taking all the space. From here, Black can carry out one of three plans: ▷ ...f7-f5 immediately ▷ ...f7-f5 with ...g7-g6 first, ▷ ...Nf6 and ...Ng6, like the main line.} g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 {The game seems to be heading towards mainline KID territory, but we have a trick up our sleeve:} 7. c5 $1 {Making the essential pawn break immediately without waiting for support with b2-b4 or Be3, and next trading the pawns earlier than usual, which gives White the leg up in the forthcoming race.} dxc5 $1 {Accepting the gambit is probably Black's best choice, rather than acquiescing to a worse KID.} 8. Be3 b6 9. Bc4 $44 {[%csl Yb5,Yc6,Yd5,Re7][%cal Gf3e5,Gg8f6,Gd5d6] White has excellent compensation for the pawn. The light squares are weak, the e7-knight is bad, and the other knight can't develop properly because it would obstruct the bishop's defense of e5.} *

