1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 $6 {Another iteration of these lines where White gets to have e2-e4 and d4-d5 for nothing. All else being equal, the space advantage counts for a significant amount.} 3. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nce7 4. Nf3 d6 {[%csl Rf8] Black puts an end to any d5-d6 shenanigans, but at the cost of shutting in the f8-bishop. This also gives us time to develop the queen knight behind the pawn.} 5. c4 $1 {[%csl Gc4,Gd5,Ge4][%cal Gb1c3] Taking all the space. From here, Black can carry out one of three plans: ▷ ...f7-f5 immediately ▷ ...f7-f5 with ...g7-g6 first, ▷ ...Nf6 and ...Ng6, like the main line.} g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 {The game seems to be heading towards mainline KID territory, but we have a trick up our sleeve:} 7. c5 $1 {Making the essential pawn break immediately without waiting for support with b2-b4 or Be3, and next trading the pawns earlier than usual, which gives White the leg up in the forthcoming race.} Nf6 8. cxd6 cxd6 9. Be2 $16 {[%csl Rd6][%cal Gf3d2,Ge1g1,Ga2a4,Gd2c4,Gc1e3] We have a KID with accelerated play.} *

