1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 $6 {Another iteration of these lines where White gets to have e2-e4 and d4-d5 for nothing. All else being equal, the space advantage counts for a significant amount.} 3. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nce7 4. Nf3 d6 {[%csl Rf8] Black puts an end to any d5-d6 shenanigans, but at the cost of shutting in the f8-bishop. This also gives us time to develop the queen knight behind the pawn.} 5. c4 $1 {[%csl Gc4,Gd5,Ge4][%cal Gb1c3] Taking all the space. From here, Black can carry out one of three plans: ▷ ...f7-f5 immediately ▷ ...f7-f5 with ...g7-g6 first, ▷ ...Nf6 and ...Ng6, like the main line.} Nf6 6. Nc3 Ng6 7. g3 Be7 8. Bg2 $16 {[%cal Ga2a4,Gh2h4,Gf3h2,Ga4a5] Grabbing all the space. The two knights on the kingside will be under control following h2-h4 and Nh2, while a2-a4, a4-a5 will stifle Black's queenside play.It is very difficult for Black to formulate active ideas without maneuvering space.} *

