1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 $6 {Another iteration of these lines where White gets to have e2-e4 and d4-d5 for nothing. All else being equal, the space advantage counts for a significant amount.} 3. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nce7 4. Nf3 Ng6 5. h4 h5 6. Bg5 {I believe this move order is slightly better, since it sets a small trap for Black.} Nf6 ({▷} 6... Be7 $2 {What Black would like to play, but the Shankland rule unfortunately does not apply here.} 7. d6 $1 cxd6 8. Nc3 $18 {Leaves Black in very bad shape. The doubled backward d-pawns are actively sabotaging the war effort.}) ({▷} 6... f6 $6 7. Be3 $16 {The knight can no longer go to f6, and the g6-square has been fatally weakened, all for a useless one-mover against the bishop.}) 7. Nc3 {Now, Black should choose a square to deploy the bishop to. Other moves don't make much sense.} Bc5 8. Nd2 {[%cal Gd2b3,Gd1f3,Ge1c1]} d6 9. Na4 $1 {[%csl Rc5] Hunting down the active bishop.} Bb6 ({If} 9... Bd4 {, we play} 10. c3 {, and Black has to give up the bishop anyway.}) 10. Nxb6 axb6 11. a3 $16 {[%csl Rf6][%cal Gc2c4,Gf1e2,Gd2b1,Gb1c3,Yg5d8] It's a bit troublesome for Black to get out of the pin. They will have to allow their pawns to be spoiled.} *

