1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 $6 {Another iteration of these lines where White gets to have e2-e4 and d4-d5 for nothing. All else being equal, the space advantage counts for a significant amount.} 3. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nce7 4. Nf3 Ng6 5. h4 h5 6. Bg5 {I believe this move order is slightly better, since it sets a small trap for Black.} Nf6 ({▷} 6... Be7 $2 {What Black would like to play, but the Shankland rule unfortunately does not apply here.} 7. d6 $1 cxd6 8. Nc3 $18 {Leaves Black in very bad shape. The doubled backward d-pawns are actively sabotaging the war effort.}) ({▷} 6... f6 $6 7. Be3 $16 {The knight can no longer go to f6, and the g6-square has been fatally weakened, all for a useless one-mover against the bishop.}) 7. Nc3 {Now, Black should choose a square to deploy the bishop to. Other moves don't make much sense.} Be7 $6 8. Bxf6 Bxf6 9. d6 $1 {[%csl Rc8] Once again utilizing this resource.} c6 10. g3 {Despite this pawn looking a bit overextended, Black doesn't have a good way to attack this pawn for instance, ...Re6 will be prevented by the bishop going to h3, while we will play Qd2 and have Rd1 in the pocket.} Nf8 11. Qd2 Ne6 12. Nd1 $16 {[%csl Re6][%cal Gd1e3,Gf1h3,Rh3e6,Ge1c1,Yg3g4] The next moves are Ne3, Bh3, and O-O-O. g3-g4 may eventually come to blow down the house.} *

