1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 e5 $6 {Another iteration of these lines where White gets to have e2-e4 and d4-d5 for nothing. All else being equal, the space advantage counts for a significant amount.} 3. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nce7 4. Nf3 Ng6 5. h4 h5 6. Bg5 {I believe this move order is slightly better, since it sets a small trap for Black.} Nf6 ({▷} 6... Be7 $2 {What Black would like to play, but the Shankland rule unfortunately does not apply here.} 7. d6 $1 cxd6 8. Nc3 $18 {Leaves Black in very bad shape. The doubled backward d-pawns are actively sabotaging the war effort.}) ({▷} 6... f6 $6 7. Be3 $16 {The knight can no longer go to f6, and the g6-square has been fatally weakened, all for a useless one-mover against the bishop.}) 7. Nc3 {Now, Black should choose a square to deploy the bishop to. Other moves don't make much sense.} Bb4 8. Qd3 {We absolutely do not want to allow our pawns to be doubled.} a6 9. Nd2 $1 ({By overprotecting the pawn, we now avoid the ...Nxe4 tactic, and threaten} 9. Nd2 Z0 10. a3 Ba5 11. d6 $1) 9... d6 10. g3 $14 {[%csl Gf4][%cal Gg3f4,Gf1e2,Ge1c1,Rf1g2,Re1g1,Yf2f4] Securing the f4-square. From here, White has two roughly equivalent options: ▷ Be2 and castling long, ▷ or Bg2 and castling short.In both cases, White pretty much maintains a free space advantage.f2-f4 will become a relevant idea soon. I don't think it's lost for Black, but certainly unpleasant to play against this annoying pin you can't get rid of.} *

