[White "3) Nimzowitsch with 2...d5"]
[Black "3A | 5...Qd7 #1"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B00"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "13"]
[GameId "2288537760792737"]

1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d5 {[%cal Bc8f5,Be7e6,Bf7f6,Bd8d7,Be8c8,Ge4e5,Gc2c3,Gf2f4]} ({By far the best continuation after 2.d4. The idea is that after} 2... d5 3. exd5 Qxd5 {, White is not in time for} 4. Nc3 {since d4 is under attack, and Black seizes the initiative in a vastly improved Scandi. The best continuation for us is 3.e5, grabbing space and getting a position similar to the Advance French and Caro lines.The advantage ...Nc6 gives over the other two is that the development of the bishop and knight are not compromised ...c7-c6 blocks the knight, ...e7-e6 blocks the bishop, and subsequently Black's piece development will be much more harmonious.However, it also comes with a major drawback: Black is no longer able to play ...c7-c5 to strike the base of the pawn chain. This means that to challenge the center, Black's plan will now be to attack the chain from the head with a plan revolving around ...f7-f6. Knowing this, we can determine that the optimal setup for Black will be ...Bf5, ...e7-e6, ...f7-f6, ...Qd7, and ...O-O-O. If we have to recapture on e5 with the d-pawn, then Black will coordinate with ...Bc5 and ...Nge7 with too much activity.Thus, White's best setups are all connected with f2-f4, in order to recapture on e5 with the f-pawn instead.}) 3. e5 Bf5 4. c3 e6 5. Nd2 {[%csl Re4][%cal Gd2f3,Yd2e4] Many players have tried Bd3 instead of Nd2, to trade off the active bishop. However, I believe White can try to exploit the f5-bishop's position with h2-h4 and g2-g4, so we should not jump to trade it off.Nd2 here takes control of the e4-square from the bishop, so we are ready for f2-f4 and g2-g4 without Be4 happening. Here, Black's most challenging try is to challenge the center immediately with f6. If they wait, White will seize the initiative on the kingside first.Only after Black commits to ...f7-f6, we will play Bd3, when the kingside has been slightly weakened, and our knight is in a better position. The other point of Nd2 is that we want to arrange our knights either Steinitz-style with Ndf3-Ne2 to bolster the pawn wedge or with the king knight coming out to h3, which leaves the bishop unblocked.Which one we choose depends on how Black proceeds.} Qd7 $6 6. h4 $1 {[%cal Gg2g4,Gh4h5] For instance, a slow move like 5...Qd7 runs into an attack on the kingside.} h5 ({Already here, Black is not in time for} 6... f6 7. g4 $1 Bg6 8. h5 Bf7 9. Ngf3 $16 {The pawn having reached h5 means that Black cannot hope to play something like ...Ne7-g6 to contest our e5-pawn, so it is there to stay. They really are not going to be able to challenge our central control. A nice point is that if they play} fxe5 {,} 10. Bb5 $1 {tends to be a good response.}) 7. Be2 $1 {It proves awkward for Black to defend the pawn. ...Bg6 will run into Nh3-f4, ...g7-g6 will mean that ...f7-f6 no longer becomes a serious idea thanks to the structure, and Black does not have enough counterplay to hope to sacrifice the h5-pawn as they often would in an advanced Caro-Kann.} *

