1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d5 {[%cal Bc8f5,Be7e6,Bf7f6,Bd8d7,Be8c8,Ge4e5,Gc2c3,Gf2f4]} ({By far the best continuation after 2.d4. The idea is that after} 2... d5 3. exd5 Qxd5 {, White is not in time for} 4. Nc3 {since d4 is under attack, and Black seizes the initiative in a vastly improved Scandi. The best continuation for us is 3.e5, grabbing space and getting a position similar to the Advance French and Caro lines.The advantage ...Nc6 gives over the other two is that the development of the bishop and knight are not compromised ...c7-c6 blocks the knight, ...e7-e6 blocks the bishop, and subsequently Black's piece development will be much more harmonious.However, it also comes with a major drawback: Black is no longer able to play ...c7-c5 to strike the base of the pawn chain. This means that to challenge the center, Black's plan will now be to attack the chain from the head with a plan revolving around ...f7-f6. Knowing this, we can determine that the optimal setup for Black will be ...Bf5, ...e7-e6, ...f7-f6, ...Qd7, and ...O-O-O. If we have to recapture on e5 with the d-pawn, then Black will coordinate with ...Bc5 and ...Nge7 with too much activity.Thus, White's best setups are all connected with f2-f4, in order to recapture on e5 with the f-pawn instead.}) 3. e5 Bf5 4. c3 e6 5. Nd2 {[%csl Re4][%cal Gd2f3,Yd2e4] Many players have tried Bd3 instead of Nd2, to trade off the active bishop. However, I believe White can try to exploit the f5-bishop's position with h2-h4 and g2-g4, so we should not jump to trade it off.Nd2 here takes control of the e4-square from the bishop, so we are ready for f2-f4 and g2-g4 without Be4 happening. Here, Black's most challenging try is to challenge the center immediately with f6. If they wait, White will seize the initiative on the kingside first.Only after Black commits to ...f7-f6, we will play Bd3, when the kingside has been slightly weakened, and our knight is in a better position. The other point of Nd2 is that we want to arrange our knights either Steinitz-style with Ndf3-Ne2 to bolster the pawn wedge or with the king knight coming out to h3, which leaves the bishop unblocked.Which one we choose depends on how Black proceeds.} f6 $7 6. f4 {Now that Black has created the inclusion of ...f7-f6, f2-f4, White no longer has as free a hand on the kingside thanks to the pawn tension. Here, Black has a few possibilities for how they want to set up.} a6 $6 {We aren't intending to go Bb5 in this setup, so ...a7-a6 is an unnecessary move. We can take this opportunity to prepare for ...g7-g5.} 7. Nh3 $1 {[%cal Gh3f2,Gd2f3] In general, White usually does not want to put his g1-knight on f3; that should be for the other knight. Here, the knight is quite fine on h3. Note that taking it will not butcher our structure, as there is a check on h5.} h5 {[%cal Gd2f3,Gf1d3,Ge1g1]} (7... Qd7 $6 {was Rapport's choice in Anand-Rapport, Bundesliga 2018. Here, White gains an advantage with the simple} 8. Nf2 $16) 8. Nf3 Nh6 9. Nf2 {[%csl Rh5][%cal Gf1e2,Ge1g1] Unlike the other lines, we stick the bishop on e2 instead, since Nh4 has become an option.} *

