1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d5 {[%cal Bc8f5,Be7e6,Bf7f6,Bd8d7,Be8c8,Ge4e5,Gc2c3,Gf2f4]} ({By far the best continuation after 2.d4. The idea is that after} 2... d5 3. exd5 Qxd5 {, White is not in time for} 4. Nc3 {since d4 is under attack, and Black seizes the initiative in a vastly improved Scandi. The best continuation for us is 3.e5, grabbing space and getting a position similar to the Advance French and Caro lines.The advantage ...Nc6 gives over the other two is that the development of the bishop and knight are not compromised ...c7-c6 blocks the knight, ...e7-e6 blocks the bishop, and subsequently Black's piece development will be much more harmonious.However, it also comes with a major drawback: Black is no longer able to play ...c7-c5 to strike the base of the pawn chain. This means that to challenge the center, Black's plan will now be to attack the chain from the head with a plan revolving around ...f7-f6. Knowing this, we can determine that the optimal setup for Black will be ...Bf5, ...e7-e6, ...f7-f6, ...Qd7, and ...O-O-O. If we have to recapture on e5 with the d-pawn, then Black will coordinate with ...Bc5 and ...Nge7 with too much activity.Thus, White's best setups are all connected with f2-f4, in order to recapture on e5 with the f-pawn instead.}) 3. e5 Bf5 4. c3 e6 5. Nd2 {[%csl Re4][%cal Gd2f3,Yd2e4] Many players have tried Bd3 instead of Nd2, to trade off the active bishop. However, I believe White can try to exploit the f5-bishop's position with h2-h4 and g2-g4, so we should not jump to trade it off.Nd2 here takes control of the e4-square from the bishop, so we are ready for f2-f4 and g2-g4 without Be4 happening. Here, Black's most challenging try is to challenge the center immediately with f6. If they wait, White will seize the initiative on the kingside first.Only after Black commits to ...f7-f6, we will play Bd3, when the kingside has been slightly weakened, and our knight is in a better position. The other point of Nd2 is that we want to arrange our knights either Steinitz-style with Ndf3-Ne2 to bolster the pawn wedge or with the king knight coming out to h3, which leaves the bishop unblocked.Which one we choose depends on how Black proceeds.} f6 $7 6. f4 {Now that Black has created the inclusion of ...f7-f6, f2-f4, White no longer has as free a hand on the kingside thanks to the pawn tension. Here, Black has a few possibilities for how they want to set up.} g5 $1 {[%csl Gb2,Gc3,Gd4,Ge5][%cal Gd2f3,Gf1d3,Gg1e2] In my opinion, the most challenging reply, continuing to challenge White's center and not allowing White to consolidate the space. Black plans to remove the f4-pawn first, so that we cannot recapture with f, then capture on e5 next, and make us take with the other pawn, favorably transforming the structure for Black.g3? to maintain the pawns isn't feasible, since after the pawn trades, the black queen will announce a check from h4. Thus, we have no choice but to allow the structure to be broken down.} ({▷} 6... fxe5 $2 7. fxe5 $16 {is exactly what we are looking for. We maintain the d4-pawn to lock out the f8-bishop, and the good light-squared bishop which will be traded off in short order with Bd3 is not enough to compensate for the space deficit.}) ({▷} 6... h5 $2 {Not helpful, since the Black knight will not land on f5.} 7. Be2 h4 8. Nh3 $16 {stopping the pawn in its tracks. We bring the other horse to f3 and castle.We never have to worry about Bxh3, since there is no way for Black to take advantage of either the doubled pawns or the newly-opened g-file.}) ({▷} 6... Be7 $2 {is a very bad move, which actually worsens Black's position by taking away the e7-square from the knight, which enables} 7. g4 Bg6 8. h4 {. h4-h5 must be stopped} h5 9. g5 $16 {Normally, Black would have ...Nge7-f5 to exploit the hole, but with the bishop in the way, the knight can't reach f5 in time. White is already threatening the plan of Bh3-Qe2 to pressure the pawn.Black may have to play ...f7-f5 and seal the kingside, but that will kill the g6-bishop. In any case, Black is much worse.}) 7. Ndf3 $1 {This will lead to a very harmonious development scheme.} fxe5 $2 8. Nxe5 $1 {[%csl Gd3][%cal Rd8h4,Yf3d2,Gf1d3]} (8. fxe5 $2 g4 $1 {is the intended idea, when the knight will have to block the queen's vision of the d3-square.}) 8... Nxe5 9. fxe5 g4 {This time, Black is too slow and we can play:} 10. Bd3 {...to eliminate the f5-bishop.} Bxd3 11. Qxd3 Qh4+ 12. g3 $18 {[%csl Rd5,Re6,Rg4][%cal Gg1e2,Ge2f4,Ge1g1,Gc3c4] Black's position, especially the light squares and f-file, is significantly weakened by the absence of their best minor piece.} *

