1. e4 Nc6 2. d4 d5 {[%cal Bc8f5,Be7e6,Bf7f6,Bd8d7,Be8c8,Ge4e5,Gc2c3,Gf2f4]} ({By far the best continuation after 2.d4. The idea is that after} 2... d5 3. exd5 Qxd5 {, White is not in time for} 4. Nc3 {since d4 is under attack, and Black seizes the initiative in a vastly improved Scandi. The best continuation for us is 3.e5, grabbing space and getting a position similar to the Advance French and Caro lines.The advantage ...Nc6 gives over the other two is that the development of the bishop and knight are not compromised ...c7-c6 blocks the knight, ...e7-e6 blocks the bishop, and subsequently Black's piece development will be much more harmonious.However, it also comes with a major drawback: Black is no longer able to play ...c7-c5 to strike the base of the pawn chain. This means that to challenge the center, Black's plan will now be to attack the chain from the head with a plan revolving around ...f7-f6. Knowing this, we can determine that the optimal setup for Black will be ...Bf5, ...e7-e6, ...f7-f6, ...Qd7, and ...O-O-O. If we have to recapture on e5 with the d-pawn, then Black will coordinate with ...Bc5 and ...Nge7 with too much activity.Thus, White's best setups are all connected with f2-f4, in order to recapture on e5 with the f-pawn instead.}) 3. e5 Bf5 4. c3 e6 5. Nd2 {[%csl Re4][%cal Gd2f3,Yd2e4] Many players have tried Bd3 instead of Nd2, to trade off the active bishop. However, I believe White can try to exploit the f5-bishop's position with h2-h4 and g2-g4, so we should not jump to trade it off.Nd2 here takes control of the e4-square from the bishop, so we are ready for f2-f4 and g2-g4 without Be4 happening. Here, Black's most challenging try is to challenge the center immediately with f6. If they wait, White will seize the initiative on the kingside first.Only after Black commits to ...f7-f6, we will play Bd3, when the kingside has been slightly weakened, and our knight is in a better position. The other point of Nd2 is that we want to arrange our knights either Steinitz-style with Ndf3-Ne2 to bolster the pawn wedge or with the king knight coming out to h3, which leaves the bishop unblocked.Which one we choose depends on how Black proceeds.} f6 $7 6. f4 {Now that Black has created the inclusion of ...f7-f6, f2-f4, White no longer has as free a hand on the kingside thanks to the pawn tension. Here, Black has a few possibilities for how they want to set up.} g5 $1 {[%csl Gb2,Gc3,Gd4,Ge5][%cal Gd2f3,Gf1d3,Gg1e2] In my opinion, the most challenging reply, continuing to challenge White's center and not allowing White to consolidate the space. Black plans to remove the f4-pawn first, so that we cannot recapture with f, then capture on e5 next, and make us take with the other pawn, favorably transforming the structure for Black.g3? to maintain the pawns isn't feasible, since after the pawn trades, the black queen will announce a check from h4. Thus, we have no choice but to allow the structure to be broken down.} ({▷} 6... fxe5 $2 7. fxe5 $16 {is exactly what we are looking for. We maintain the d4-pawn to lock out the f8-bishop, and the good light-squared bishop which will be traded off in short order with Bd3 is not enough to compensate for the space deficit.}) ({▷} 6... h5 $2 {Not helpful, since the Black knight will not land on f5.} 7. Be2 h4 8. Nh3 $16 {stopping the pawn in its tracks. We bring the other horse to f3 and castle.We never have to worry about Bxh3, since there is no way for Black to take advantage of either the doubled pawns or the newly-opened g-file.}) ({▷} 6... Be7 $2 {is a very bad move, which actually worsens Black's position by taking away the e7-square from the knight, which enables} 7. g4 Bg6 8. h4 {. h4-h5 must be stopped} h5 9. g5 $16 {Normally, Black would have ...Nge7-f5 to exploit the hole, but with the bishop in the way, the knight can't reach f5 in time. White is already threatening the plan of Bh3-Qe2 to pressure the pawn.Black may have to play ...f7-f5 and seal the kingside, but that will kill the g6-bishop. In any case, Black is much worse.}) 7. Ndf3 $1 {This will lead to a very harmonious development scheme.} gxf4 {[%csl Rf5,Rf6,Rg4][%cal Gh4f5,Re6f5]} (7... g4 $4 8. Nh4 $18 {is a disaster; Black will have a horrible pawn structure after we take the bishop.}) 8. Bd3 $1 {Now that we got the queen knight into position on f3, we can go for this bishop trade.} Bxd3 9. Qxd3 fxe5 {Easiest and most direct. However, Black may also try to find a way to keep the tension in the hopes that Black can break free of the spatial disadvantage.} ({▷} 9... Qd7 $6 {doesn't bring any benefit to Black. Once we get to take on e5 with a piece, White is doing fantastic.} 10. Bxf4 $16) ({▷} 9... f5 $4 10. Ne2 $18 {A strategic blunder, allowing White to keep the position closed, and thus giving White a free hand on the kingside. a2-a4/b2-b4 are coming, and the knight on f4 will kill all the play on the kingside.All of Black's minors are terrible and boxed out by the pawn structure.}) 10. dxe5 Nge7 {[%csl Re5][%cal Be7g6]} 11. Ne2 $1 (11. Bxf4 {would allow} Ng6 {, attacking the bishop, when we have to play} 12. Ne2 {. The knight does not want to babysit the bishop, and we'd rather have those knights traded so the bishop isn't in a compromised position.}) 11... Ng6 12. Nxf4 Nxf4 13. Bxf4 Qd7 14. O-O O-O-O 15. b4 $14 {[%csl Ra5][%cal Ra2a4,Rb4b5,Rc6a5,Ga1a2,Ga2f2,Gf3d4,Gh2h3,Gf4h2,Yf1f8] My verdict is that Black is almost OK here, but White is still a bit better objectively, and the position is imbalanced enough that we should have every chance to outplay our opponent.The plan is a little counterintuitive: we are not going to mate the black king, but instead use the threat of a pawn storm to sideline the black knight on a5 after b5, and instead move to play along the f-file. White's best plan seems to involve first clearing the f-file with Nd4/h2-h3/Bh2, followed by doubling the rooks via a4/Ra2-f2. Somewhere along the way, the king should go to h1 and step off the open file and diagonal. Against best play, Black should never be threatening to mate us, as long as we keep g2 secured. The machine wants g2-g3 sometimes, but I would strongly caution against that move, which provides a hook for the black pieces.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Scandinavian: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Introduction"]
[Result "*"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "6"]
[GameId "2288537760874671"]

1. e4 d5 {The Scandi is a tough cookie. It has a bad reputation, but Black has many different setups, and we can easily lose the advantage if we're not precise.  Black's idea is to essentially play a d4 structure against an e4 player, and make their position solid. In some ways, it's almost like Black's version of the London.  For e4 players, we need to know how to keep the dynamics alive and not allow Black to turtle up the position and play the game they want.} 2. exd5 {There are two main families, based on how Black intends to recapture the pawn: the lines with ...Qxd5 and the lines with ...Nf6-Nxd5. Our approach is a bit different in either case.} Qxd5 (2... Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 (3... Bg4 $2 {is the dubious Portuguese gambit, which might just lose by force the way most people play it.}) 4. c4 {The most direct way to play, and forces Black's knight to make a decision.} Nf6 {Returning home is the best option, and Black should now prepare to break with ...e7-e6 and ...c7-c5, reaching something that resembles an open Queen's Gambit.} (4... Nb4 $2 {is bad, misplacing the knight.}) (4... Nb6 {The knight is a bit awkward on this square. Black is going to develop with ...g7-g6 and eventually try for ...e7-e5 instead.})) 3. Nc3 Qa5 {[%csl Ye4][%cal Ra5e1,Gd2d4,Gc8f5,Yf5e4] The most popular move. This is the only line that enables Black to somewhat successfully develop the bishop outside the pawn chain, thanks to the c3-knight being pinned, allowing Be4 resources.} ({▷} 3... Qd6 {Attempts to keep the queen centralized and influencing the d4-square, while still vacating d8 for queenside castles. This line is tricky, but there are usually ways to exploit the queen's position here via Nc4 or Nb5 ideas.}) ({▷} 3... Qd8 {is the most solid but passive. Black is accepting a worse position in order to play this type of position against e4.}) ({▷} 3... Qe6+ $2) ({and} 3... Qe5+ $2 {are both garbage moves that play for tricks and lead to poor positions. They are rarely seen in practice.}) *

