1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. Nf3 (3. d4 {is my preferred move order, since we can go c2-c4 right away and cut down on Black's range of responses.}) 3... Nxd5 4. d4 g6 5. c4 Nb6 6. h3 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Gh3g4]} Bg7 7. Nc3 O-O 8. Be3 Nc6 9. Be2 e5 10. d5 Ne7 11. g4 $1 {[%csl Rf5] Continuing to play the game of area denial. You can train this line here.} Re8 $2 {[%csl Rb6,Re7]} ({Good or bad, Black should barge in with} 11... f5) ({or} 11... e4 {. His long-term prospects aren't so good, especially with the bad knights on b6 and e7.}) 12. Qb3 Nd7 13. c5 {Black is unfortunately not able to get organized in time before the threats begin to come.} b6 14. Ng5 Rf8 15. c6 Nc5 16. Bxc5 bxc5 17. Nge4 Kh8 18. O-O-O f5 19. gxf5 $2 (19. Nxc5 $18) 19... gxf5 $2 (19... Nxf5 {with the knight next sailing straight into d4. Black needs some activity!}) 20. Nxc5 e4 21. Ne6 Bxe6 22. dxe6 Qe8 23. Rd7 Rb8 24. Qc4 Qg6 25. Kb1 Qf6 26. Rc1 Rfc8 27. Qc5 Ng6 28. e7 Ne5 29. Ba6 Nxd7 30. cxd7 {[%csl Gd7,Ge7] Two pawns on the seventh rank is not something you see every day!} Qxa6 31. dxc8=Q+ Rxc8 32. Qxf5 Qd3+ 1-0

