1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 Qa5 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 c6 6. Bc4 Bf5 7. Ne5 e6 8. g4 Bg6 9. h4 Nbd7 10. Nxd7 Nxd7 11. h5 Be4 12. O-O Bd5 13. Nxd5 cxd5 14. Bd3 {I left things here, with a very promising position for White. In this game, Tahbaz tries to stir up some counterplay in the center, but against the bishop pair, it ended up backfiring badly.Black's best bet might be to sit around and defend, but no one likes to play that way!} Bd6 15. Bd2 {You can train the line here.} Qc7 16. Qf3 O-O 17. c3 Qc6 18. a4 e5 19. Bb5 Qc8 20. h6 $1 {Nicely calculated.} (20. Qxd5 $2 Nf6 {Black begins to confuse the issue. White should not allow this.}) 20... a6 (20... g6 {is the move Black wants to play, but Sethuraman calculated that this fails.} 21. Bxd7 Qxd7 22. Qf6 Qxg4+ {this time, it's just a check.} 23. Kh2 $18 {Black can give a few more checks, but will have to surrender the bishop to avert mate.}) 21. hxg7 (21. Be2 $1 {is more accurate. Now if} exd4 {there is} 22. Qxd5 $18) 21... Re8 22. Be2 exd4 23. cxd4 Re4 $2 (23... Nf6 $1 {, with the same idea of perpetual as a few moves ago, was the last chance. The e5-pawn has disappeared, which means the white king cannot flee via h2 and g3.}) 24. Rfc1 Qd8 25. Bd3 Qh4 (25... Rxd4 26. Bc3 {traps the rook.}) 26. Bxe4 dxe4 27. Qg2 $18 {Now White has a decisive material advantage.} Re8 28. Rc3 Qf6 29. Re3 Qg6 30. Rh3 Qxg7 31. Re1 Qxd4 32. Bc3 Qd5 33. Rh5 Be5 34. Rxe4 f6 35. Re1 Qf7 36. Qf3 Rc8 37. Rf5 Qg7 38. Re4 Qh6 39. Rh5 Qg7 40. Qf5 Bxc3 41. bxc3 Kh8 42. Rd4 Rc7 43. Rd6 h6 44. Rd3 Nf8 45. Rdh3 Kg8 46. Rxh6 1-0

