1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Bg4 $6 {[%csl Rd1] The Portuguese Gambit. It's tricky, but one of the openings that we can truly 'punish' by playing accurately! White does have to memorize some lines, but you can count on a huge position if you do.} 4. f3 $1 {[%csl Gd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gc4d5,Rf3g4] The first thing to know when playing against the Portuguese is that we shoud make Black work to recoup the d5-pawn. This means that we are immediately attacking this bishop, gaining a tempo so that we can defend the pawn with c2-c4 on the next move.} Bf5 {Other moves are playable in fact the computer says ...Bd7 and ...Bc8 are both significantly better but in practice this is the only move that is played, which makes our job preparing easier I suppose.  Now that Black cannot regain the pawn easily, they will try to make us weaken our position as much as possible to hold onto the pawn. This bishop move is obviously provoking g4.} 5. g4 $1 {We play it anyway. White and Black are both all-in on their respective strategies at this point; f2-f3 weakens the position for no reason unless we stick to defending the pawn.g2-g4 does bring us one important benefit, though: g4-g5 will be on the cards, which will remove the knight from influencing d5 in the future.} Bc8 6. c4 c6 {The best move in a bad position.} 7. dxc6 Nxc6 8. d5 Ne5 9. Qe2 $18 {[%cal Gb1c3,Gc1f4,Ge1c1] As usual, castling long puts Black in a dead lost position.} *

