[PlyCount "17"]
[GameId "2288537760927929"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Bg4 $6 {[%csl Rd1] The Portuguese Gambit. It's tricky, but one of the openings that we can truly 'punish' by playing accurately! White does have to memorize some lines, but you can count on a huge position if you do.} 4. f3 $1 {[%csl Gd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gc4d5,Rf3g4] The first thing to know when playing against the Portuguese is that we shoud make Black work to recoup the d5-pawn. This means that we are immediately attacking this bishop, gaining a tempo so that we can defend the pawn with c2-c4 on the next move.} Bf5 {Other moves are playable in fact the computer says ...Bd7 and ...Bc8 are both significantly better but in practice this is the only move that is played, which makes our job preparing easier I suppose.  Now that Black cannot regain the pawn easily, they will try to make us weaken our position as much as possible to hold onto the pawn. This bishop move is obviously provoking g4.} 5. g4 $1 {We play it anyway. White and Black are both all-in on their respective strategies at this point; f2-f3 weakens the position for no reason unless we stick to defending the pawn.g2-g4 does bring us one important benefit, though: g4-g5 will be on the cards, which will remove the knight from influencing d5 in the future.} Bg6 (5... Bd7 6. c4 $16 {is an improved version of the 4...Bd7-line. g2-g4 means that we will always have g4-g5 as a possibility for Black to be concerned with, and it helps us accelerate development with Bg2 and O-O. Additionally, we can go queenside in a pinch if we so desire.}) 6. c4 c6 {This is less effective, since it does not help develop the bishop.} 7. Nc3 cxd5 8. g5 {[%cal Rg5f6]} Nfd7 9. cxd5 $18 {[%cal Gd5e6,Rb8c6,Re7e6,Yg1e2,Yf1h3,Yd1b3,Yh2h4]} ({We capture with the pawn this time to make developing the bishop and knight more challenging, and to avoid losing a tempo after} 9. Nxd5 e6 {. From here, the plan is to develop with move such as Nge2 and Bh3 perhaps h2-h4 first to secure the g5-pawn. Black is already losing unfortunately, there is simply no compensation for the pawn.}) *

