1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Bg4 $6 {[%csl Rd1] The Portuguese Gambit. It's tricky, but one of the openings that we can truly 'punish' by playing accurately! White does have to memorize some lines, but you can count on a huge position if you do.} 4. f3 $1 {[%csl Gd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gc4d5,Rf3g4] The first thing to know when playing against the Portuguese is that we shoud make Black work to recoup the d5-pawn. This means that we are immediately attacking this bishop, gaining a tempo so that we can defend the pawn with c2-c4 on the next move.} Bf5 {Other moves are playable in fact the computer says ...Bd7 and ...Bc8 are both significantly better but in practice this is the only move that is played, which makes our job preparing easier I suppose.  Now that Black cannot regain the pawn easily, they will try to make us weaken our position as much as possible to hold onto the pawn. This bishop move is obviously provoking g4.} 5. g4 $1 {We play it anyway. White and Black are both all-in on their respective strategies at this point; f2-f3 weakens the position for no reason unless we stick to defending the pawn.g2-g4 does bring us one important benefit, though: g4-g5 will be on the cards, which will remove the knight from influencing d5 in the future.} Bg6 (5... Bd7 6. c4 $16 {is an improved version of the 4...Bd7-line. g2-g4 means that we will always have g4-g5 as a possibility for Black to be concerned with, and it helps us accelerate development with Bg2 and O-O. Additionally, we can go queenside in a pinch if we so desire.}) 6. c4 e6 7. Nc3 $1 {A waiting move. We want to play g4-g5 specifically in response to ...exd5, so we can potentially capture on d5 with the knight.One important thing you will learn throughout the rest of this chapter is that Black does not really have any ways to create threats since the space created by our pawns restrict Black's active ideas, and therefore Black is reliant on White to resolve the tension in the center to create activity.  In other words, it is not a dangerous gambit where our opponent can independently create threats regardless of what we do, as long as we can control the game, Black does not have any ideas at all!} (7. dxe6 $2 Nc6 8. exf7+ Kxf7 $44 {is what Black is hoping for. Now the position is too lose and something hanging is unavoidable.}) 7... c5 ({A general rule of thumb in high-level chess games is that players will only offer a trade that benefits them in some way, while beginners will trade for the sake of trading.It is important to note that a trade is by definition an equal exchange of material, so it brings no inherent benefits.You can use this information to your advantage – if a trade is being offered by a good player, they probably are hoping to gain something when you accept it.In this case, we have the opportunity to capture the c-pawn both ways, but they each help develop a black piece in some way,} 7... c5 8. dxc5 ({or} 8. dxc6 Nxc6 {. Therefore, we should resist the urge to take something and develop our own pieces instead!}) 8... Bxc5) 8. Be3 $1 exd5 {[%csl Rf6,Rf7][%cal Gc3d5] Now, unlike White, Black should probably take something because he is down a pawn and needs to get rid of some of White's pawns, which are preventing his development. However, the fact that Black is being forced to initiate the captures is a major strategic advantage. For instance, our main idea of g4-g5 is only effective given that Black has already made the capture exd5. If Black could delay capturing on d5, that would counter the g4-g5-plan, but unfortunately, the d5-pawn is preventing him from completing development with ...Nc6.} ({▷} 8... cxd4 9. Bxd4 Nbd7 ({If} 9... exd5 {then we have the typical} 10. Qe2+ {to ruin the coordination. Black is being forced to block with something, which allows us to take the knight and spoil the structure.} Be7 11. Bxf6) 10. h4) ({▷} 8... Nbd7 {Once again, we continue to leave the tension in the center.} 9. Nge2 $18) 9. g5 Nfd7 10. cxd5 $18 {[%cal Gh2h4,Gg1e2,Gd1d2] The usual development scheme of h2-h4, Nge2, and Qd2 leads to a winning position.} *

