1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Bg4 $6 {[%csl Rd1] The Portuguese Gambit. It's tricky, but one of the openings that we can truly 'punish' by playing accurately! White does have to memorize some lines, but you can count on a huge position if you do.} 4. f3 $1 {[%csl Gd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gc4d5,Rf3g4] The first thing to know when playing against the Portuguese is that we shoud make Black work to recoup the d5-pawn. This means that we are immediately attacking this bishop, gaining a tempo so that we can defend the pawn with c2-c4 on the next move.} Bf5 {Other moves are playable in fact the computer says ...Bd7 and ...Bc8 are both significantly better but in practice this is the only move that is played, which makes our job preparing easier I suppose.  Now that Black cannot regain the pawn easily, they will try to make us weaken our position as much as possible to hold onto the pawn. This bishop move is obviously provoking g4.} 5. g4 $1 {We play it anyway. White and Black are both all-in on their respective strategies at this point; f2-f3 weakens the position for no reason unless we stick to defending the pawn.g2-g4 does bring us one important benefit, though: g4-g5 will be on the cards, which will remove the knight from influencing d5 in the future.} Bg6 (5... Bd7 6. c4 $16 {is an improved version of the 4...Bd7-line. g2-g4 means that we will always have g4-g5 as a possibility for Black to be concerned with, and it helps us accelerate development with Bg2 and O-O. Additionally, we can go queenside in a pinch if we so desire.}) 6. c4 e6 7. Nc3 $1 {A waiting move. We want to play g4-g5 specifically in response to ...exd5, so we can potentially capture on d5 with the knight.One important thing you will learn throughout the rest of this chapter is that Black does not really have any ways to create threats since the space created by our pawns restrict Black's active ideas, and therefore Black is reliant on White to resolve the tension in the center to create activity.  In other words, it is not a dangerous gambit where our opponent can independently create threats regardless of what we do, as long as we can control the game, Black does not have any ideas at all!} (7. dxe6 $2 Nc6 8. exf7+ Kxf7 $44 {is what Black is hoping for. Now the position is too lose and something hanging is unavoidable.}) 7... Be7 8. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5]} h6 {Now that we have weakened the defense of the g6-bishop, the capture on e6 becomes possible, since we can follow up with Bd3.} 9. dxe6 Nc6 (9... fxe6 {is very cooperative} 10. Bd3 Bxd3 11. Qxd3 $18 {and thanks to the inclusion of h3-h4 and ...h7-h6, Qg6+ is immediately threatened, while ...O-O runs into g4-g5.}) 10. Be3 {[%csl Gd3][%cal Yg1e2,Gf1d3,Rc6b4,Rb4d3] Simple and direct – pawn is attacked, pawn is defended. Similar to the Alekhine lines, we are going to defend the pawn with the bishop first, although for a different reason: We want to keep the f1-bishop in contact with the d3-square, in case Black tries to jump in with ...Nb4.} ({Do not mess around with moves like} 10. exf7+ $6 {. Sure it might be objectively OK, but you are already up a pawn, and making Black waste a tempo to capture on e6 and block the e-file will make your job keeping the game under control much easier.}) 10... O-O 11. Bd3 $18 {At some point the pawn on e6 will have to be captured, which will leave White with a pawn up, more space, and the better structure. One tactical theme that comes up in these lines is that Qxd4/Nxd4 is not possible most of the time either because either Bxg6 is check directly or we have exf7+ ...Bxf7 Bh7+, creating a discovery against the queen on d4.} *

