1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Bg4 $6 {[%csl Rd1] The Portuguese Gambit. It's tricky, but one of the openings that we can truly 'punish' by playing accurately! White does have to memorize some lines, but you can count on a huge position if you do.} 4. f3 $1 {[%csl Gd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gc4d5,Rf3g4] The first thing to know when playing against the Portuguese is that we shoud make Black work to recoup the d5-pawn. This means that we are immediately attacking this bishop, gaining a tempo so that we can defend the pawn with c2-c4 on the next move.} Bf5 {Other moves are playable in fact the computer says ...Bd7 and ...Bc8 are both significantly better but in practice this is the only move that is played, which makes our job preparing easier I suppose.  Now that Black cannot regain the pawn easily, they will try to make us weaken our position as much as possible to hold onto the pawn. This bishop move is obviously provoking g4.} 5. g4 $1 {We play it anyway. White and Black are both all-in on their respective strategies at this point; f2-f3 weakens the position for no reason unless we stick to defending the pawn.g2-g4 does bring us one important benefit, though: g4-g5 will be on the cards, which will remove the knight from influencing d5 in the future.} Bg6 (5... Bd7 6. c4 $16 {is an improved version of the 4...Bd7-line. g2-g4 means that we will always have g4-g5 as a possibility for Black to be concerned with, and it helps us accelerate development with Bg2 and O-O. Additionally, we can go queenside in a pinch if we so desire.}) 6. c4 e6 7. Nc3 $1 {A waiting move. We want to play g4-g5 specifically in response to ...exd5, so we can potentially capture on d5 with the knight.One important thing you will learn throughout the rest of this chapter is that Black does not really have any ways to create threats since the space created by our pawns restrict Black's active ideas, and therefore Black is reliant on White to resolve the tension in the center to create activity.  In other words, it is not a dangerous gambit where our opponent can independently create threats regardless of what we do, as long as we can control the game, Black does not have any ideas at all!} (7. dxe6 $2 Nc6 8. exf7+ Kxf7 $44 {is what Black is hoping for. Now the position is too lose and something hanging is unavoidable.}) 7... exd5 8. g5 {[%cal Rg5f6]} Nh5 9. f4 $18 {[%csl Rh5][%cal Gf4f5,Gf1e2] This move creates serious problems. The black knight on h5 is trapped, and both f4-f5 and Be2 threaten to win it outright.As it turns out, there is no defense and black is simply lost already. There is a bunch of BS that Black can try, but it's all bells and whistles.None of it actually addresses the impending doom of the h5-knight. I'll show the most logical of Black's attempts, but if you get here in a game, you should look forward to an easy win.} Nc6 {Perhaps the best of a bad bunch.} ({▷} 9... dxc4 10. Be2 {traps the knight without any questions.}) ({▷} 9... Qd7 {Trying to stop f4-f5 but we simply play} 10. Bh3 {and f4-f5 is still happening.}) ({▷} 9... Qe7+ 10. Kf2 {We sidestep the check and Be2 is still a threat.}) 10. Be2 {[%csl Rh5]} Nb4 {[%cal Bb4c2]} 11. Bxh5 Nc2+ {This immediately also fails to impress:} 12. Kf2 Nxa1 13. Bg4 {[%cal Gf4f5,Gc3d5,Gg1f3,Gh1e1] Once again Black faces too many threats; Nxd5, f4-f5, Nf3-Re1, and so on.} *

