 it is not a dangerous gambit where our opponent can independently create threats regardless of what we do, as long as we can control the game, Black does not have any ideas at all!} (7. dxe6 $2 Nc6 8. exf7+ Kxf7 $44 {is what Black is hoping for. Now the position is too lose and something hanging is unavoidable.}) 7... exd5 8. g5 {[%cal Rg5f6]} Nh5 9. f4 $18 {[%csl Rh5][%cal Gf4f5,Gf1e2] This move creates serious problems. The black knight on h5 is trapped, and both f4-f5 and Be2 threaten to win it outright.As it turns out, there is no defense and black is simply lost already. There is a bunch of BS that Black can try, but it's all bells and whistles.None of it actually addresses the impending doom of the h5-knight. I'll show the most logical of Black's attempts, but if you get here in a game, you should look forward to an easy win.} Nc6 {Perhaps the best of a bad bunch.} ({▷} 9... dxc4 10. Be2 {traps the knight without any questions.}) ({▷} 9... Qd7 {Trying to stop f4-f5 but we simply play} 10. Bh3 {and f4-f5 is still happening.}) ({▷} 9... Qe7+ 10. Kf2 {We sidestep the check and Be2 is still a threat.}) 10. Be2 {[%csl Rh5]} Nb4 {[%cal Bb4c2]} 11. Bxh5 Bxh5 12. Qxh5 Nc2+ 13. Kf2 Nxa1 {[%csl Ra8,Rd8,Re8,Rf8,Rh8] Black won the rook but has lagged severely behind in the development department.} 14. Nf3 $18 {[%cal Gh1e1,Gf3e5,Gf4f5,Gc3d5] With an overwhelming advantage.} *

