 it is not a dangerous gambit where our opponent can independently create threats regardless of what we do, as long as we can control the game, Black does not have any ideas at all!} (7. dxe6 $2 Nc6 8. exf7+ Kxf7 $44 {is what Black is hoping for. Now the position is too lose and something hanging is unavoidable.}) 7... exd5 8. g5 {[%cal Rg5f6]} Nfd7 {The better of the knight retreats.} 9. Nxd5 $18 {[%csl Yg6][%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5,Gd1e2,Rf8d6,Rc4c5] Now this gets a bit complicated here, and White has to be precise, but in every variation White ends with a +2 advantage. If you face someone who's playing this, it's basically a memory test to see if you can remember all of the winning lines.The three key ideas to remember are... ▷ h4-h5 to harass the black bishop. ▷ Qe2+ to interrupt black's development and potentially force a queen trade. ▷ c4-c5 in response to ...Bd6 to force the bishop back to e7.} Nc6 {[%csl Re8][%cal Gg1e2,Gd1b3,Gc1d2] The other tries are all relatively easy to deal with.} ({▷ For instance,} 9... Bd6 10. c5 c6 11. Nf4 $1 Be7 12. h4 {No more tricks, we can calmy develop and convert the extra pawn and enormous space advantage.}) ({▷} 9... c6 $2 10. Qe2+ {spoils the day. We force a liquidation into an easily winning endgame.}) 10. h4 $1 {[%cal Gh4h5]} Be7 11. h5 Bf5 12. Ne3 g6 13. Nxf5 gxf5 14. d5 Bb4+ 15. Kf2 Ne7 16. f4 {[%csl Gc4,Gd5,Gf4,Gg5][%cal Gg1f3,Gc1e3,Gf1d3] It feels weird to make this many pawn moves, but at the same time these pawns severely restrict black's mobility, making it difficult for them to generate counterplay. Now we are finally ready to develop with moves such as Be3, Bd3, and Nf3.} *

