{Gaining a tempo by forcing the black rook away.} 14. Rxh7 Rxh7 15. gxh7 {is clearly not feasible.}) 14... Rf8 {Kf2 works less well here as the rook is already on f8, so we should go in for the d4-d5 plan instead. But first, we trade on f7, since Black can no longer recapture with the king – the rook on f8 blocks a vital retreat square in the event of Qh5+.} 15. gxf7+ Rxf7 16. d5 Nd4 17. Qe4 {[%cal Gh7h8,Ge4g6,Gf7f8] Rh8+ followed by Qg6+ is threatened, so there is no time to take the rook in the corner.} Qd6 {[%cal Gf1h3,Gg5g6] Covering g6 and preparing to castle, but it's not in time!} ({If} 17... Nbc2+ {, then} 18. Kf2 {.}) 18. Bh3 $18 {[%csl Rd4,Yg1][%cal Gh3c8,Gg5g6,Gh7h8,Yd6g3,Yf1g1,Ge1f1] Cutting off castling, and also more importantly, vacating the f1-square for the king in case of Qg3+. The position is still a bit messy, and there is definitely some work to be done, but the position is more than +4. In a classical game, you should have more than enough time to figure things out. There are various things Black can try: ...Nc2+, ...Qg3+, ...Qe5, and so on. But the long and short of it is that none of these moves solves the fundamental problems with Black's position, namely the unstable arrangement of the Black pieces and the weak light squares.Kf1 and g5-g6 are the two most immediate ideas.} (18. g6 $4 {Don't get Shankied!} Qg3+ {and there goes the knight.}) *

