1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Bg4 $6 {[%csl Rd1] The Portuguese Gambit. It's tricky, but one of the openings that we can truly 'punish' by playing accurately! White does have to memorize some lines, but you can count on a huge position if you do.} 4. f3 $1 {[%csl Gd5][%cal Gc2c4,Gc4d5,Rf3g4] The first thing to know when playing against the Portuguese is that we shoud make Black work to recoup the d5-pawn. This means that we are immediately attacking this bishop, gaining a tempo so that we can defend the pawn with c2-c4 on the next move.} Bf5 {Other moves are playable in fact the computer says ...Bd7 and ...Bc8 are both significantly better but in practice this is the only move that is played, which makes our job preparing easier I suppose.  Now that Black cannot regain the pawn easily, they will try to make us weaken our position as much as possible to hold onto the pawn. This bishop move is obviously provoking g4.} 5. g4 $1 {We play it anyway. White and Black are both all-in on their respective strategies at this point; f2-f3 weakens the position for no reason unless we stick to defending the pawn.g2-g4 does bring us one important benefit, though: g4-g5 will be on the cards, which will remove the knight from influencing d5 in the future.} Bg6 (5... Bd7 6. c4 $16 {is an improved version of the 4...Bd7-line. g2-g4 means that we will always have g4-g5 as a possibility for Black to be concerned with, and it helps us accelerate development with Bg2 and O-O. Additionally, we can go queenside in a pinch if we so desire.}) 6. c4 e6 7. Nc3 $1 {A waiting move. We want to play g4-g5 specifically in response to ...exd5, so we can potentially capture on d5 with the knight.One important thing you will learn throughout the rest of this chapter is that Black does not really have any ways to create threats since the space created by our pawns restrict Black's active ideas, and therefore Black is reliant on White to resolve the tension in the center to create activity.  In other words, it is not a dangerous gambit where our opponent can independently create threats regardless of what we do, as long as we can control the game, Black does not have any ideas at all!} (7. dxe6 $2 Nc6 8. exf7+ Kxf7 $44 {is what Black is hoping for. Now the position is too lose and something hanging is unavoidable.}) 7... exd5 8. g5 {[%cal Rg5f6]} Nfd7 {The better of the knight retreats.} 9. Nxd5 $18 {[%csl Yg6][%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5,Gd1e2,Rf8d6,Rc4c5] Now this gets a bit complicated here, and White has to be precise, but in every variation White ends with a +2 advantage. If you face someone who's playing this, it's basically a memory test to see if you can remember all of the winning lines.The three key ideas to remember are... ▷ h4-h5 to harass the black bishop. ▷ Qe2+ to interrupt black's development and potentially force a queen trade. ▷ c4-c5 in response to ...Bd6 to force the bishop back to e7.} Nc6 {[%csl Re8][%cal Gg1e2,Gd1b3,Gc1d2] The other tries are all relatively easy to deal with.} ({▷ For instance,} 9... Bd6 10. c5 c6 11. Nf4 $1 Be7 12. h4 {No more tricks, we can calmy develop and convert the extra pawn and enormous space advantage.}) ({▷} 9... c6 $2 10. Qe2+ {spoils the day. We force a liquidation into an easily winning endgame.}) 10. h4 $1 {[%cal Gh4h5]} Nb6 {[%csl Rb4] Our position is becoming critical since we are behind in development and have many weaknesses behind the advanced pawns. However, despite this, the computer finds concrete lines to directly refute Black's play.To start with, we need to throw in the queen check at this moment specifically.} (10... Bb4+ {achieves nothing spectacular} 11. Kf2 $18 {.}) 11. Qe2+ $1 Be7 {Timing is key. Now, we need to insert h4-h5 before capturing on e7.} 12. h5 $1 {[%csl Rg6]} (12. Nxe7 $2 {falls into} Nxd4 $1 $13 {which creates a huge mess.}) 12... Bf5 13. Nxe7 Nxd4 {Recapturing this knight is clearly not an option, so Black has to press on with 13...Nxd4.  But now we see the point behind h4-h5: because the bishop has been forced to a square where it is no longer defended, we can play the brilliant...} 14. Nxf5+ $3 {Sacrificing the queen!} Nxe2 15. Nxe2 $18 {[%csl Ra8,Rb6,Rd8,Yf5,Gg5,Yg7,Gh5,Rh8][%cal Yf5g7,Ge1f2,Gh1h4,Ge2g3,Gc1e3] Let's stop here. The smoke has cleared, and we are left with, on paper, material equality with three pieces against a queen.But there are a few developments that clearly favor White: all of Black's active minor pieces were traded off in the demolition derby, and the ones that remain are all undeveloped, the advanced pawns on the kingside provide the roots of a kingside attack, and the knight on f5 is already optimally placed and immediately threatening Nxg7+, which doesn't have a great way of being addressed. The game could proceed in many ways from here, but a few general ideas are that... ▷ The c4-pawn is not worth the time defending, and we should just let Black take it, ▷ White should not castle because the king is safest on the f2-square, and ▷ The h1-rook can be developed via the 4th rank.} *

