1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nb6 $6 {[%csl Yb6][%cal Rc7c5,Gd4c5,Gc5b6] A poor square. This knight belongs on f6, where it covers the kingside and does not interfere with the c4-c5 idea.With c4-c5 being more difficult to achieve since after we take, the knight is attacked, Black's main objective is now the ...e7-e5 break. Speaking of ...e7-e5, Black is in fact threatening to play this break right now. So, unlike many similar positions, we need to develop the king knight first to prevent this.} 5. Nf3 $1 Bg4 $6 {The move that we are normally afraid of runs into...} 6. c5 $1 {[%csl Rb6]} Bxf3 7. Qxf3 Nd5 8. Qb3 $1 {I insist on Qb3.} b6 $7 9. Nc3 $18 {[%csl Rb5,Rc6,Rd5] The light squares are way too weak, especially since we have an unopposed light-squared bishop. The d5-knight is also very unstable, whereas the other knight can't develop easily due to Bb5 or c5-c6.} *

