1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nb6 $6 {[%csl Yb6][%cal Rc7c5,Gd4c5,Gc5b6] A poor square. This knight belongs on f6, where it covers the kingside and does not interfere with the c4-c5 idea.With c4-c5 being more difficult to achieve since after we take, the knight is attacked, Black's main objective is now the ...e7-e5 break. Speaking of ...e7-e5, Black is in fact threatening to play this break right now. So, unlike many similar positions, we need to develop the king knight first to prevent this.} 5. Nf3 $1 g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8. h3 e5 $5 {I wanted to briefly mention this move despite never having played in the ~400 games from this position, since it's the engine's top choice. Black can apply the Shankland rule here what's the move you really want to play? and play execute main idea anyway.} 9. Bg5 $1 {[%csl Rd8] Connecting the rook with the queen, and simultaneously attacking the black queen. We are prepared to chop on e5 next turn, so Black must play...} f6 {[%csl Rg5]} 10. Be3 $16 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Ge1g1,Gd1b3,Gc4c5,Gd1c2,Gd4e5] White certainly has the better coordination, though it's kind of playable for Black. Practically, though, White's moves all come naturally, so I would not be too concerned with this.} *

