1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nb6 $6 {[%csl Yb6][%cal Rc7c5,Gd4c5,Gc5b6] A poor square. This knight belongs on f6, where it covers the kingside and does not interfere with the c4-c5 idea.With c4-c5 being more difficult to achieve since after we take, the knight is attacked, Black's main objective is now the ...e7-e5 break. Speaking of ...e7-e5, Black is in fact threatening to play this break right now. So, unlike many similar positions, we need to develop the king knight first to prevent this.} 5. Nf3 $1 g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8. h3 c5 9. d5 e6 10. Bg5 $16 {[%csl Rb6,Rb8,Rc8] Provoking ...f7-f6 again. Black's only option to keep the game going is the undesirable ...Bxc3+; the other moves like ...Qd7 or ...f7-f6 lead to a positionally lost middlegame.Black's minors are all poorly placed.} *

