1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nb6 $6 {[%csl Yb6][%cal Rc7c5,Gd4c5,Gc5b6] A poor square. This knight belongs on f6, where it covers the kingside and does not interfere with the c4-c5 idea.With c4-c5 being more difficult to achieve since after we take, the knight is attacked, Black's main objective is now the ...e7-e5 break. Speaking of ...e7-e5, Black is in fact threatening to play this break right now. So, unlike many similar positions, we need to develop the king knight first to prevent this.} 5. Nf3 $1 g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8. h3 c6 $6 {[%csl Rc6][%cal Yb8c6] Prophylaxis against d4-d5, but this move comes at the cost of stunting the development of the black knight.} 9. Be3 N8d7 {Black is almost in time for ...e7-e5, but...} 10. a4 $1 {[%cal Ga4a5,Ga5a6] Threatening to trap the knight. Black's only choice is...} a5 {[%csl Rb6][%cal Ge3b6,Re7e5,Bd4e5,Gd2d8,Bd7e5] After which, ...e7-e5 won't work because the b6-knight will hang after all the trades on e5 and d8.} 11. O-O $18 {It may seem extreme to give '+-' here, but the fact is Black is strategically lost, as both poor coordination and lack of space and counterplay are chronic issues.} *

