1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nb6 $6 {[%csl Yb6][%cal Rc7c5,Gd4c5,Gc5b6] A poor square. This knight belongs on f6, where it covers the kingside and does not interfere with the c4-c5 idea.With c4-c5 being more difficult to achieve since after we take, the knight is attacked, Black's main objective is now the ...e7-e5 break. Speaking of ...e7-e5, Black is in fact threatening to play this break right now. So, unlike many similar positions, we need to develop the king knight first to prevent this.} 5. Nf3 $1 g6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8. h3 Nc6 9. Be3 ({Don't be tempted by} 9. d5 $2 Na5 {when there will be a problem on c4.}) 9... e5 {Other moves don't make sense. We can castle and develop normally, and Black has no plan.} 10. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Ne7 {[%csl Re5][%cal Gg7a1]} (10... Na5 $2 {Black cannot win the c4-pawn, so the knight will end up stranded on a5.} 11. Nd2 c6 12. b4 $18 {with the Black pawn on e5 instead of e7, we can play b2-b4 here. White wins a piece and the game.}) 11. g4 $1 {[%csl Gf5] Domination. Taking away the pivot point for the black minors.} f5 {Seems dangerous, but it turns out neither of Black's threatened pawn pushes is of any concern. The main thing is that we prevented a piece from occupying f5.} (11... e4 {seems like desperation to me.} 12. Nxe4 f5 13. Nc5 Bxb2 14. Ng5 $1 $18) 12. Qd2 fxg4 13. hxg4 Rxf3 (13... Bxg4 14. Ng5 $18) 14. Bxf3 Nxc4 {[%csl Rd2]} 15. Qe2 Nxe3 16. Qxe3 {[%csl Yc8,Ye7,Yg7] Black does not have sufficient compensation, not by a long shot. Sacrificing the exchange did not fix the minors.} *

