1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... g6 $6 6. Nc3 Bg7 7. Be2 O-O 8. O-O c6 $6 9. h3 Nbd7 10. Bf4 $18 {[%cal Gf1e1,Gd1b3,Ga2a4,Ga4a5,Ga1d1] The issue with this setup for Black is that there are no pawn breaks available. White has a free hand to do whatever, while Black is being squeezed. Moves like Re1, a2-a4, and Qb3 will slowly increase the pressure.I think Black might straight up lose here.} *

