1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... Bg4 $6 6. Be2 {Don't go on autopilot here with Nc3: this time, ...Bxf3 is a real threat, so we need to unpin the horse.} e6 7. Qb3 $1 {[%csl Rb7] The typical response. Now that ...Bxf3 doesn't wreck the pawn structure, we put the b7-pawn under duress.} Qc8 {[%csl Ra6,Rb7,Rc6][%cal Gc1e3,Ge1c1,Gd4d5]} (7... b6 $6 {Weakens the position too severely.} 8. Nc3 $18) 8. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bh5 9. g4 {[%csl Rh5] ...Bf5 is the same thing.} Bg6 10. Nc3 {[%cal Gd4d5] We don't get to put the bishop on g2, but we are still much better. The d5-break is on the way.} *

