1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... Bg4 $6 6. Be2 {Don't go on autopilot here with Nc3: this time, ...Bxf3 is a real threat, so we need to unpin the horse.} c6 7. Nc3 e6 {This is a better way to go about it, so Black has ...Qb6 in the event of Qb3.} 8. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bh5 {[%cal Gc1e3,Gd1c2,Ge1c1]} (8... Bxf3 9. Bxf3 {is a typically advantageous position. We have the bishop pair, space advantage, and lead in development.As per usual, we go long and rush either g2-g4-g5 or d4-d5, depending on where the Black king ends up.}) 9. g4 {[%csl Rh5]} Bg6 10. Qb3 {[%csl Rb7]} Qb6 (10... Qc7 $2 11. Bf4 $1 {London players will know this trick!}) 11. c5 {[%csl Rb6]} Qxb3 12. axb3 $16 {[%csl Gb2,Gb3][%cal Gb2b5,Ra1a8] White has a fantastic endgame. The two b-pawns can be used as battering rams against Black's queenside. We should go b3-b4-b5 as soon as possible, before Black can get the a-pawn unpinned and control b5 with ...a7-a6.} *

