1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... c6 $6 {The wrong plan. Black needs to break with c5.} 6. Nc3 Bf5 7. h3 $1 {[%cal Gf5e4,Ra5e1] Preparing for g2-g4. Black is lacking the ...Be4 resources.} e6 8. g4 {[%csl Rf5]} Bg6 9. Bg2 $16 {[%cal Gc1f4,Gd1e2,Ge1g1] White has the easiest plan, and Black is probably going to be run over by all the pawns.} *

