1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... Bf5 $6 {Significantly worse than in the ...Qxd5-lines. The problem is that the white pawn on c4 makes Qb3 possible, which will cause problems for the loose b7-pawn.} 6. Nc3 {[%csl Rb7,Rf5][%cal Gd1b3,Gf3h4]} e6 7. Qb3 {[%csl Rb7] We probably won't take on b7 on the very next turn, but it is useful to have Qxb7 in the pocket. Black will have to worry about this move hanging over their head all the time.} Qc8 ({▷} 7... b6 8. g3 $16 {Thanks to the bishop being kept flexible, we can elect to place the bishop along the vulnerable long diagonal.}) ({▷} 7... Nc6 {Top engine recommendation. Most people elect ...Qc8 or ...b7-b6 in practice, because they want to immediately get rid of the Qxb7 idea.} 8. c5 $16 {Cutting off Nb4 so Qxb7 is really a threat now.} ({White also prepares Bb5 to pin down the poorly placed c6-knight.} 8. Qxb7 $2 Nb4 {is a problem.})) 8. Nh4 Bg6 9. Nxg6 hxg6 10. Bg5 $18 {[%cal Ge1c1,Gd4d5] Black was too cooperative, and White got everything they wanted. d4-d5 soon is going to blow up the center, and the kingside is not safe either due to h2-h4-h5.} *

