1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... c5 $1 {This seems to be the best try, but is pretty rare. Black breaks up the center immediately, before White can consolidate it.} 6. Nc3 cxd4 7. Qxd4 {[%cal Gf3d4,Re7e5]} (7. Nxd4 {is running into} e5 {, so we must take with the queen.}) 7... Qxd4 {This is what's played in practice.} 8. Nxd4 {[%cal Gd4b5] Ndb5 is the threat.} a6 9. Na4 $1 {[%csl Gb6,Gc5] Taking control over the dark squares. White will be looking to play on the queenside, using the pawn majority.} Nbd7 10. Be3 $14 {[%cal Ge3b6,Ga4b6,Ga4c5,Gb2b4,Gc4c5,Gf1e2] Not the most exciting position ever, but it is a better version of this structure than White usually gets. The main thing going for us is that we are ahead some tempi compared to normal.} *

