1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... e6 6. Nc3 c5 $1 {Again is the best.} 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 a6 9. g3 $14 {[%cal Gf1g2,Gd4b3,Ge1g1,Ga1c1] We get some sort of Pseudo-Maroczy/English setup. The bishops along the diagonals will exert annoying pressure against the black queenside.} *

