1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... e6 6. Nc3 b6 7. Bd3 Bb7 8. Qe2 Nbd7 9. Bc2 $1 {This time, ...Bb4 is a real idea since Black is better coordinated. Bc2 anticipates ...Bb4 and prepares to meet it with the simple Bd2.} Bb4 10. Bd2 $16 {[%cal Ga2a3] a2-a3 is next, and we can neutralize the bishop on b4.} *

