1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... e6 6. Nc3 Bb4 $2 {[%cal Bc7c5,Bb7b6,Bc8a6,Bb8c6,Bc6a5] We don't have to worry about the pawns being doubled. Black is quite a ways away from coordinating against c4 with ...c7-c5, ...b7-b6, ...Ba6, ...Nc6, and ...Na5. We should be able to get something going on the kingside or in the center far before the c4-pawn ever becomes an issue.} 7. a3 {[%csl Rb4]} Bxc3+ 8. bxc3 O-O 9. Bd3 $36 {[%cal Gc1g5,Ge1g1,Gf3e5,Gd1f3] Again, compared to typical versions of this structure, White is a few tempi ahead: that is the drawback of ...Nf6-xd5-f6. Typical attacking ideas on the kingside should lead to a strong initiative.} *

