1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... e6 6. Nc3 Be7 {By far the most popular. Black signals that he will develop first before breaking with c5.} 7. Bd3 {[%csl Gd3][%cal Rc8g4] In the early e6-lines, the bishop belongs on d3, since Bg4 is not a thing anymore. The bishop is more active here. Additionally, we prevent a potential queen trade along the d-file following ...c7-c5 dxc5.} O-O 8. O-O c5 {Black can try to break now, but it's already worse than a few moves ago.} 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. Qe2 $36 {[%cal Gf1d1,Gc1g5,Gc1f4,Ga2a3,Gb2b4] We do the Qe2 coordination and follow with rapid development. White has a big initiative.} *

