1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Nf6 3. d4 Nxd5 4. c4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {Taking command of the e5-square, and preventing any short-term ...e7-e5 shots. Black has many options here, and fortunately for us, most of the common tries are wrong.Black cannot play this like a ...Qxd5 Scandi – the key difference being the pawn on c4. This means that White has a much better long game, and Black's goal should be to break up this pawn duo rather than going for a London formation.} (5. Nc3 {like in the ...Nb6-line runs into} e5 $1 {.}) 5... e6 6. Nc3 Be7 {By far the most popular. Black signals that he will develop first before breaking with c5.} 7. Bd3 {[%csl Gd3][%cal Rc8g4] In the early e6-lines, the bishop belongs on d3, since Bg4 is not a thing anymore. The bishop is more active here. Additionally, we prevent a potential queen trade along the d-file following ...c7-c5 dxc5.} O-O 8. O-O b6 {[%csl Rc8] Black seems to be trying the fianchetto most of the time.} (8... c6 $2 {makes zero sense. How is Black going to get anything done with the c8-bishop still inside the pawn chain?}) 9. Qe2 Bb7 10. Bf4 $16 {[%csl Gc3,Rc7,Gd1,Gd3,Ge1,Ge2,Gf3,Gf4][%cal Gf1e1,Ga1d1,Rf3e5,Rc3b5,Rb5c7] We want to set up all the pieces in the center. Direct, simple play.With every passing move, it becomes harder and harder for Black to make ...c7-c5 happen. Already, moves like Ne5 and Nb5 are in the air.} *

