1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qe6+ $6 4. Be2 Qg6 {[%csl Rg2] The point of ...Qe6+, on the other hand, is the not-so-subtle plan to take the g2-pawn. A common error here is to bow to Black's threat and play Be2?, but after ...e7-e5, we become discoordinated.The best reaction to this is to just ignore it.} 5. Nf3 $1 Qxg2 {Otherwise, this whole operation was pointless. As the saying goes, 'in for a penny, in for a pound'.} 6. Rg1 {[%csl Rg2]} Qh3 7. d4 $18 {[%csl Rh3][%cal Gg1g3,Gf3e5,Gc1f4,Ge2c4,Ya8h8] If we think about this logically, Black has to be completely cooked: the only piece off the starting square is the wayward queen on h3. I trust that you can take this from here.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "5) Scandinavian with 2...Qxd5"]
[Black "5B | 3...Qe5+"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B01"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "21"]
[GameId "2288537761042651"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qe5+ $6 4. Be2 Bg4 {The main idea of ...Qe5+ – Black takes steps to prevent Nf3, and thus forcibly trades the light-squared bishops, which White has been trying to avoid. However, in this case, Black loses too much time.} (4... c6 {Black will drop the queen back to c7. Technically, Black isn't losing time compared to the ...Qa5-line by doing this; the queen probably would have had to move off a5 on its own accord eventually anyway.However, Black is losing flexibility: they don't get to choose when to play ...Qc7, and thus White can set up the pieces in a more optimal configuration. In addition, this also means Black cannot get ...Bf5/...Bg4 because they are lacking that ...Be4 resource.} 5. Nf3 Qc7 6. d4 $16 {As per usual, we stick the knight on e5. This time, we have the added option of Bf4 at some point.}) 5. d4 {[%csl Re5]} Bxe2 {Black has to trade now, as the pin can't be safely maintained.} (5... Qe6 $2 6. f3 Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. f4 $18 {The queen and bishop come under fire. Black has to resort to playing ...f7-f5 to avoid being down a piece here.}) 6. Ngxe2 Qa5 7. O-O Nf6 8. d5 {The computer likes b2-b4 more, but I like this move. When in doubt, stick with moves you understand! White makes sure that Black cannot play ...e7-e6 to continue the development.} g6 {This looks like the only sensible way to develop the bishop.} ({After} 8... c6 9. Nf4 $16 {, Black probably has to take on d5 sooner or later, but they will not enjoy our knight landing there and all the threats that will follow. Black's position is already critical.}) 9. b4 {Now I like this one because Black can't bring the queen to f5 or h5.} Qxb4 (9... Qa6 10. b5 Qd6 11. Bf4 $16 {White's development lead is growing, and Black is in real trouble.}) 10. Rb1 Qc4 11. Rxb7 $16 {[%cal Gc1f4] Black is badly lacking in development, and c7 will start to feel weak after Bf4 and/or Qf4.} *

