1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd8 {The most solid choice to avoid getting the queen in trouble, but also very passive.} 4. d4 Nf6 {Black can do something else on move 4, but ...Nf6 is more or less required in all of them, so I will cover all of Black's setups through the 4...Nf6 move order.} 5. Nf3 Bg4 6. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bxf3 7. Qxf3 {[%csl Rb7]} c6 8. Be3 {White could sacrifice the d4-pawn, but there's no need.} e6 9. Bd3 Nbd7 (9... Bb4 10. O-O $1 $16 {Only when ...Bb4 has been committed do we castle short. The bishop belongs on d6 in the short castles positions.}) 10. O-O-O Bb4 $1 {Black needs to drive away the knight from c3 so they can play ...Nd5.} ({▷} 10... Qa5 $2 11. Kb1 $18 {The attack died on the launch pad.}) ({▷} 10... Nb6 11. Kb1 Nbd5 12. Ne2 {is similar to the main line.}) 11. Ne2 Nd5 {Black has convinced us to forfeit the bishop, but that's not a problem.} 12. Kb1 Nxe3 13. fxe3 $16 {[%csl Yb2,Yc3,Yd4,Ye5,Rf7][%cal Ge3e4,Ge4e5,Gh1f1,Gc2c3,Rd3h7] This middlegame is an attacker's paradise. The opposite-colored bishops are essentially an extra piece for the attacker.White has the f-file and also the e3-e4, e4-e5 plan to deny Black the f6-square. The white bishop will be superior, as we can build a pawn chain to cut out the black bishop.} *

