1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} Nc6 {Black is trying to be as fast as possible in creating counterplay in the center. We need to take the opportunity to get c2-c4 in, so we have the option of playing d4-d5 in case the pressure against the d4-pawn becomes too great.} 6. Nb5 $1 {[%csl Rd6]} Qd8 7. c4 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4][%cal Gd4d5]} a6 8. Nc3 Bg4 9. d5 Ne5 10. Be3 $5 {Allowing the doubled pawns, but we save some time having to move our bishop back and forth between e2 and f3.} Nxf3+ (10... Bxf3 $6 11. gxf3 $16 {is much worse. The knight is a bad piece, and it will lose more time to f4.}) 11. gxf3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bf5 12. c5 $36 {[%cal Gd1a4,Gd1b3,Gf1c4] White has a pair of big pawns and Qb3 or Qa4+ will cause a nuisance on the queenside before Black is fully developed.} *

