1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} g6 6. Nb5 $1 {[%csl Rd6]} Qb6 ({After} 6... Qd8 7. c4 a6 8. Nc3 Bg7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O $16 {, we have a ...Qd8-Scandi with a suboptimal setup for Black. White has a clear edge thanks to the extra space, a plan for their pieces, and Black has no counterplay.}) 7. a4 c6 8. a5 Qd8 9. Nc3 $16 {[%cal Gf1c4,Ge1g1,Gf3e5,Gf1e1,Gc1f4] We've sort of transposed into a ...Qd8 Scandi, where ...c7-c6 and ...g7-g6 aren't the most productive moves for Black, while this a4-a5 advance is useful for us.} *

