1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} g6 6. Nb5 $1 {[%csl Rd6]} Qb6 ({After} 6... Qd8 7. c4 a6 8. Nc3 Bg7 9. Be2 O-O 10. O-O $16 {, we have a ...Qd8-Scandi with a suboptimal setup for Black. White has a clear edge thanks to the extra space, a plan for their pieces, and Black has no counterplay.}) 7. a4 a6 8. Na3 $1 {[%cal Ga3c4,Gc4e5] Routing the knight to e5, which will win a few tempi along the way if Black goes ...Bg4. Black's best hope is to trade off this knight instead of the one on f3, or else it will become too strong.} Be6 9. Nc4 {[%csl Rb6]} Bxc4 10. Bxc4 $16 Qb4+ {[%csl Rc4] Here Black has to play ...Qb4+ to disturb our coordination to stay in the game, after everything else, Black is screwed because White has too many positional advantages.} 11. Nd2 Bh6 {[%csl Rc4][%cal Bh6d2]} 12. Bd3 {[%csl Rh6][%cal Rb4d4,Rd2f3]} Bxd2+ (12... Qxd4 $4 {loses to} 13. Nf3 {.}) 13. Qxd2 {[%csl Gd4][%cal Gd2d4,Rd3b5,Rb4d4] Defending d4 indirectly.} Qxd2+ 14. Bxd2 $14 {[%csl Yb8,Gd2,Gd3,Yf6] White has a nice endgame with the bishops against the knights.} *

