1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} Bg4 $6 {We know what to do against this.} 6. h3 Bxf3 7. Qxf3 {[%csl Rb7]} Nc6 8. Bf4 $16 {[%csl Gf1,Gf4,Rd6][%cal Ge1c1,Gd4d5] This leads to an overwhelming advantage.} Nxd4 (8... Qxd4 $2 {is even easier:} 9. Nb5 Qxb2 10. Nxc7+ Kd8 11. Rd1+ $18 {.}) 9. Qxb7 Nxc2+ 10. Ke2 Nd4+ 11. Kd1 $1 {No repetition!} Qxf4 12. Qxa8+ $18 {[%csl Re8][%cal Re8d7] Black has insufficient compensation here, especially since their own king has to step forward.} *

