1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} Bg4 $6 {We know what to do against this.} 6. h3 Bh5 7. g4 Bg6 8. Bg2 $16 {[%csl Rg6][%cal Ge1g1,Gf3h4,Gf2f4,Gf4f5] In these types of positions where Black fails to gain counterplay against the d4-pawn, White is borderline winning. It's nice we got the bishop to this diagonal.On top of the normal Ne5 stuff, we also have the Nh4/f2-f4 option unlocked.} *

