1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} a6 {Preventing Nb5. A bit of an annoying move, since Black is now ready for ...b7-b5-b4 and also ...Nc6/...Bg4/...O-O-O. However, we have a move that will emerge as a theme in this line...} 6. g3 $1 {[%cal Gc1f4,Rb7b5,Yf1g2,Yg2a8] Developing the bishop this way comes with two benefits – we discourage ...b7-b5 and also set up Bf4.} b5 $6 7. Bg2 Bb7 8. O-O e6 ({If} 8... b4 {, then} 9. Na4 $18) 9. Ne5 Bxg2 10. Kxg2 $16 {[%cal Gd1f3,Gc1f4] The queen is coming to the diagonal with devastating effect.} *

