1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} a6 {Preventing Nb5. A bit of an annoying move, since Black is now ready for ...b7-b5-b4 and also ...Nc6/...Bg4/...O-O-O. However, we have a move that will emerge as a theme in this line...} 6. g3 $1 {[%cal Gc1f4,Rb7b5,Yf1g2,Yg2a8] Developing the bishop this way comes with two benefits – we discourage ...b7-b5 and also set up Bf4.} Bg4 {The downside is we can't break the pin with Be2 anymore, and the d4-pawn is weaker as a result.} (6... Nc6 $6 7. Bf4 $16 {is pretty easy.} Qb4 $2 {can be met with} 8. a3 Qxb2 $4 9. Na4 {, trapping the queen.}) 7. Bg2 Nc6 8. Be3 ({The simplest, keeping the pawn protected. After} 8. Bf4 {, we have to deal with} Qb4 {shenanigans.}) 8... e6 {Alternatively, Black can revert to castling kingside and play ...Rd8 instead. However, this plan is much slower and gives White more leeway to consolidate.} 9. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bh5 10. O-O Be7 11. Ne2 $1 O-O 12. c4 $16 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4] As we have seen a few times in this chapter already, when we get to arrange this pawn duo unopposed, we usually have a clear advantage.Black is active, but the c-pawn being blocked means it will be impossible to break down the center.} *

