1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} a6 {Preventing Nb5. A bit of an annoying move, since Black is now ready for ...b7-b5-b4 and also ...Nc6/...Bg4/...O-O-O. However, we have a move that will emerge as a theme in this line...} 6. g3 $1 {[%cal Gc1f4,Rb7b5,Yf1g2,Yg2a8] Developing the bishop this way comes with two benefits – we discourage ...b7-b5 and also set up Bf4.} Bg4 {The downside is we can't break the pin with Be2 anymore, and the d4-pawn is weaker as a result.} (6... Nc6 $6 7. Bf4 $16 {is pretty easy.} Qb4 $2 {can be met with} 8. a3 Qxb2 $4 9. Na4 {, trapping the queen.}) 7. Bg2 Nc6 8. Be3 ({The simplest, keeping the pawn protected. After} 8. Bf4 {, we have to deal with} Qb4 {shenanigans.}) 8... O-O-O {[%csl Rb7][%cal Ga1b1,Gb1b7,Gd4d5]} (8... Qb4 $2 {now is no good. After} 9. O-O Qxb2 10. Qd2 $18 {Black is in huge trouble, as there is no counterplay against the d4-pawn.}) 9. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bh5 10. Qe2 e6 ({▷} 10... e5 $4 {loses by force:} 11. dxe5 Nxe5 12. g4 Bg6 13. Rd1 Qe7 14. Rxd8+ Kxd8 15. Nxe5 Qxe5 16. f4 $18 {, and we trap the bishop.}) ({▷ On} 10... Qb4 $6 {there is} 11. O-O-O {I've carefully selected this specific move order so that we never have to gambit the b2-pawn in the event of ...Qb4.}) 11. O-O Qb4 {[%csl Rb2,Rd4]} 12. g4 {[%csl Rh5]} Bg6 13. Rfb1 $1 {[%cal Gb1b8,Ga1a8] Looks funny, but we need the a-pawn to be protected.} Nd5 {Required, otherwise a2-a3/b2-b4 and Black gets steamrolled.} 14. a3 {[%csl Rb4]} Qb6 (14... Nxc3 $4 {is a suicide mission.} 15. bxc3 Qxc3 16. Ra2 $1 $18 {Rxb7 is the threat, but even the slower plan to double up the rooks is decisive here.}) 15. Nxd5 exd5 16. b4 Qb5 17. Qd2 {[%csl Ga4][%cal Ga3a4] We have a3-a4 available.} Qa4 {Black is forced to play this move.} 18. Ne5 Nxe5 19. dxe5 $16 {[%csl Ra4][%cal Ge3g5,Gb1e1,Gf2f4,Gf4f5] White has a very promising position here. Black managed to stop the attack, but the queen is offside, and White has a big space and development lead.} ({f2-f4, f4-f5 are both incoming. It's important to note that Black can never take on c2:} 19. dxe5 Qxc2 {is met with} ({and} 19... Bxc2 {is met with} 20. Rc1 {, after which Black is getting mated.}) 20. Qd4) *

