1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} c6 ({This is the main move, but now I like a nice regrouping:} 5... Bf5 $2 6. Ne5 $18 {g2-g4 and Qf3 on the way. As we have seen numerous times, ...Bf5 should only be attempted with the queen on a5.}) 6. Ne5 $1 g6 7. Bf4 $1 Nh5 8. Be3 Nf6 {It is not a good sign for Black that this is what the machine wants to do.} 9. Qd2 $16 {[%cal Ge1c1,Gf1c4] White has a good attacking setup and can look to the future with confidence.} *

