1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qd6 {The point of moving the queen here is to stay on the d-file but allow potential queenside castling. The drawback is that the queen is vulnerable to both Nb5 and Ne4, both of which we will take advantage of.} 4. d4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {If allowed, we would like to play Nb5 next to improve our coordination. Black should prevent it with ...c7-c6 or ...a7-a6.} c6 ({This is the main move, but now I like a nice regrouping:} 5... Bf5 $2 6. Ne5 $18 {g2-g4 and Qf3 on the way. As we have seen numerous times, ...Bf5 should only be attempted with the queen on a5.}) 6. Ne5 $1 Nbd7 7. Nc4 $1 {[%csl Rd6]} (7. Bf4 $6 {is premature because of} Nd5 $1 {.}) 7... Qc7 8. a4 $1 {[%csl Gb5] Anchoring the knight on c4.} Nb6 9. Ne5 $16 {[%cal Ga4a5,Gc1f4,Gf1e2,Ge1g1,Gf1e1] Black can do a lot of roughly equivalent setups here, but against most of them we want to set up the pieces with a4-a5/Be2/Bf4/O-O/Re1.} Nbd7 $2 {Trying to do a repetition won't work because this time we have.} 10. Bf4 Nd5 {[%csl Rf4]} 11. Nxd5 $18 {[%cal Gf1b5] The queen is not there to recapture. After Bb5 next, Black is losing material.} *

