1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qa5 {[%csl Yc3][%cal Ya5e1,Rc7c6,Re7e6,Rc8f5,Gd2d4] Widely considered the main move. Black's idea is that the knight on c3 will be pinned down after d4 for a few turns, before Black eventually drops back with the queen via ...c7-c6/...Qc7. We need to realize that positionally speaking, Black is doing fantastic.The pieces develop quickly, and the c3-knight is misplaced, blocking the c-pawn. In the long run, Black will both have an incredibly solid London-like setup with the pawns on c6 and e6, and also plently of activity.This means that we must play concretely and force Black to make some concessions by exploiting the initiative and vulnerable queen position, otherwise Black will easily equalize. The key ideas for White are: ▷ Bd2 against some of the sidelines, targeting the queen on a5; ▷ Ne5+g2-g4/h2-h4 to track the bishop when it leaves the pawn chain; ▷ and Bc4 to induce an early ...e7-e6, locking the bishop outside.} 4. d4 Bf5 $6 {An imprecise move order, which we can punish.} 5. Bd2 {[%csl Re4][%cal Gd2a5,Gd1f3,Gf3b7,Gf3f5] Unpinning the knight, which takes away the e4-square from the black bishop, as well as creating potential for a future discovery. We are also preparing for Qf3-ideas, which will fork the bishop and b7-pawn.Black should wait for Nf3 before playing ...Bf5, as this resource would then be unavailable to White.} c6 6. g4 {[%csl Rf5]} Bg6 7. f4 $1 {[%cal Gf4f5] Another resource which is made available thanks to the absence of the knight on f3.} e6 8. Qe2 $18 {[%csl Rg6][%cal Gd4d5,Gf4f5,Gh2h4,Gh4h5,Gg1f3,Gf3e5,Ra8h8] Black is completely baked. There are too many ideas for White: d4-d5, f4-f5, h2-h4-h5, Nf3-Ne5 are all on the cards. The g6-bishop is a liability.} *

