1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qa5 {[%csl Yc3][%cal Ya5e1,Rc7c6,Re7e6,Rc8f5,Gd2d4] Widely considered the main move. Black's idea is that the knight on c3 will be pinned down after d4 for a few turns, before Black eventually drops back with the queen via ...c7-c6/...Qc7. We need to realize that positionally speaking, Black is doing fantastic.The pieces develop quickly, and the c3-knight is misplaced, blocking the c-pawn. In the long run, Black will both have an incredibly solid London-like setup with the pawns on c6 and e6, and also plently of activity.This means that we must play concretely and force Black to make some concessions by exploiting the initiative and vulnerable queen position, otherwise Black will easily equalize. The key ideas for White are: ▷ Bd2 against some of the sidelines, targeting the queen on a5; ▷ Ne5+g2-g4/h2-h4 to track the bishop when it leaves the pawn chain; ▷ and Bc4 to induce an early ...e7-e6, locking the bishop outside.} 4. d4 e5 $2 {Black is too far behind in development to hope this will work.} 5. Nf3 $1 exd4 ({▷} 5... Bg4 6. Bc4 $18 {Black is losing material everywhere:} Nc6 ({, If} 6... e4 {then} 7. Bd2 $1 {, followed by Nd5 after the exchange on f3.}) ({In case of} 6... Nf6 7. Bd2 {, and once more Nd5 can't be prevented.}) 7. Bxf7+) ({▷} 5... Bb4 6. Bd2 Bg4 7. a3 Bxc3 8. Bxc3 $18 {White simply wins a pawn.}) 6. Qxd4 Nc6 7. Bb5 $1 {White's pieces jump to life immediately, and Black is already more or less lost.} *

