[White "5) Scandinavian with 2...Qxd5"]
[Black "5E | 3...Qa5 #3"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B01"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "19"]
[GameId "2288537761091825"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qa5 {[%csl Yc3][%cal Ya5e1,Rc7c6,Re7e6,Rc8f5,Gd2d4] Widely considered the main move. Black's idea is that the knight on c3 will be pinned down after d4 for a few turns, before Black eventually drops back with the queen via ...c7-c6/...Qc7. We need to realize that positionally speaking, Black is doing fantastic.The pieces develop quickly, and the c3-knight is misplaced, blocking the c-pawn. In the long run, Black will both have an incredibly solid London-like setup with the pawns on c6 and e6, and also plently of activity.This means that we must play concretely and force Black to make some concessions by exploiting the initiative and vulnerable queen position, otherwise Black will easily equalize. The key ideas for White are: ▷ Bd2 against some of the sidelines, targeting the queen on a5; ▷ Ne5+g2-g4/h2-h4 to track the bishop when it leaves the pawn chain; ▷ and Bc4 to induce an early ...e7-e6, locking the bishop outside.} 4. d4 Nf6 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gc8g4,Gc8f5]} ({As we will see in the} 4... Bf5 $6 {variation , Black has to wait for Nf3 before deploying the queen bishop.}) (4... c6 5. Nf3 {Black has nothing better than returning to the mainlines after} Nf6 ({,} 5... Bf5) ({or} 5... Bg4 {, all of which we can meet with} 6. Bc4 {.})) 5. Nf3 Bg4 $2 {A thing to note is that Black rarely wants to pull the trigger on ...Bxf3, so this pin is a paper tiger – there is simply no threat connected with this move, and thus we should not allow ourselves to remain pinned.Our reaction to ...Bg4 will be the same each time.} 6. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bxf3 7. Qxf3 c6 8. Bd2 Nbd7 9. O-O-O e6 10. a3 {[%cal Gf1d3,Gg2g5,Gc3e4,Gh3h5] → White throws all the pawns at the kingside.GM Pantoja is the only practitioner of this line, and he hasn't won a single game yet! That should tell you all you need to know about this position.} *

