1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qa5 {[%csl Yc3][%cal Ya5e1,Rc7c6,Re7e6,Rc8f5,Gd2d4] Widely considered the main move. Black's idea is that the knight on c3 will be pinned down after d4 for a few turns, before Black eventually drops back with the queen via ...c7-c6/...Qc7. We need to realize that positionally speaking, Black is doing fantastic.The pieces develop quickly, and the c3-knight is misplaced, blocking the c-pawn. In the long run, Black will both have an incredibly solid London-like setup with the pawns on c6 and e6, and also plently of activity.This means that we must play concretely and force Black to make some concessions by exploiting the initiative and vulnerable queen position, otherwise Black will easily equalize. The key ideas for White are: ▷ Bd2 against some of the sidelines, targeting the queen on a5; ▷ Ne5+g2-g4/h2-h4 to track the bishop when it leaves the pawn chain; ▷ and Bc4 to induce an early ...e7-e6, locking the bishop outside.} 4. d4 Nf6 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gc8g4,Gc8f5]} ({As we will see in the} 4... Bf5 $6 {variation , Black has to wait for Nf3 before deploying the queen bishop.}) (4... c6 5. Nf3 {Black has nothing better than returning to the mainlines after} Nf6 ({,} 5... Bf5) ({or} 5... Bg4 {, all of which we can meet with} 6. Bc4 {.})) 5. Nf3 Bg4 $2 {A thing to note is that Black rarely wants to pull the trigger on ...Bxf3, so this pin is a paper tiger – there is simply no threat connected with this move, and thus we should not allow ourselves to remain pinned.Our reaction to ...Bg4 will be the same each time.} 6. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bh5 $6 {Black is already busted because they can't get ...e7-e6 and ...c7-c6 both in once we slam Ne5 down.} ({In case somebody tries this,} 6... Qh5 $4 {loses on the spot to} 7. hxg4 Qxh1 8. Ne2 $18 {and here they are resorting to desperate moves like ...g7-g5 to try and keep the queen alive.}) 7. g4 {[%csl Rh5]} Bg6 8. Ne5 {In the main line, Black already has ...c7-c6 and can play ...e7-e6 here. However, now Black has to make the devil's choice.} e6 9. Nc4 {[%csl Ra5]} Qa6 10. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5,Gh1h3]} Bb4 {Black needs to keep this knight pinned down. Everything else is totally gone.A key idea after we've played h3-h4 is to play Rh3 at some point to get off the open diagonal and support the knight laterally.} ({▷} 10... Nbd7 {for instance} 11. h5 Be4 12. Rh3 $18) ({▷} 10... Qc6 {, for whatever reason, has been played eleven times.} 11. Rh3 $18) 11. h5 {[%csl Rg6]} Be4 {[%csl Rh1]} 12. Rh3 $18 {[%csl Gg4,Gh5][%cal Gh3c3,Gc1d2] White is not immediately breaking through or winning material, but the game is positionally won. The queen on a6 is misplaced, and White has grabbed all the space on the kingside.} *

