[Black "5E | 3...Qa5 #7"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B01"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "17"]
[GameId "2288537761104117"]

1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qa5 {[%csl Yc3][%cal Ya5e1,Rc7c6,Re7e6,Rc8f5,Gd2d4] Widely considered the main move. Black's idea is that the knight on c3 will be pinned down after d4 for a few turns, before Black eventually drops back with the queen via ...c7-c6/...Qc7. We need to realize that positionally speaking, Black is doing fantastic.The pieces develop quickly, and the c3-knight is misplaced, blocking the c-pawn. In the long run, Black will both have an incredibly solid London-like setup with the pawns on c6 and e6, and also plently of activity.This means that we must play concretely and force Black to make some concessions by exploiting the initiative and vulnerable queen position, otherwise Black will easily equalize. The key ideas for White are: ▷ Bd2 against some of the sidelines, targeting the queen on a5; ▷ Ne5+g2-g4/h2-h4 to track the bishop when it leaves the pawn chain; ▷ and Bc4 to induce an early ...e7-e6, locking the bishop outside.} 4. d4 Nf6 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gc8g4,Gc8f5]} ({As we will see in the} 4... Bf5 $6 {variation , Black has to wait for Nf3 before deploying the queen bishop.}) (4... c6 5. Nf3 {Black has nothing better than returning to the mainlines after} Nf6 ({,} 5... Bf5) ({or} 5... Bg4 {, all of which we can meet with} 6. Bc4 {.})) 5. Nf3 Nc6 $6 {[%cal Bd4d5,Rc7c6] The knight does not belong here in the long run, where it is vulnerable to d4-d5 pushes and prevents ...c7-c6, which would make Black's position much more secure. The only way to justify this is if Black can either get a quick ...e7-e5 in, or rush to put pressure on the d4-pawn.} 6. Bd2 $1 {The easiest. We unpin the knight, and now the queen cannot go back to c7, so when the knight moves, there will be a big problem.You do need to memorize an important idea if Black goes ...Bg4, but otherwise, it should be easy enough.} a6 {This is best, but not scary.} 7. Bc4 {[%cal Gc3d5] Preparing Nd5. The queen must flee.} Qh5 {[%csl Ge7,Gf7,Gg7,Gh7][%cal Gd1e2,Ge1c1]} (7... Nxd4 8. Nxd4 Qc5 9. Bxf7+ $1 Kxf7 10. Nf3 $16 {Black's structure is horrible.}) 8. h3 Bg4 {[%cal Bh5h1]} 9. Be2 $16 {[%cal Ge2f3,Gf3c6] We managed to contain the counterplay.After we castle, Black will have no choice but to chop the knight on f3, after which we can take the black knight on c6 to ruin their structure and follow up with Qf3.} *

