1. e4 d5 2. exd5 Qxd5 3. Nc3 {First, let's get these two ridiculous queen checks out of the way. They aren't difficult to bust, as the play is very linear into bad positions for Black.} Qa5 {[%csl Yc3][%cal Ya5e1,Rc7c6,Re7e6,Rc8f5,Gd2d4] Widely considered the main move. Black's idea is that the knight on c3 will be pinned down after d4 for a few turns, before Black eventually drops back with the queen via ...c7-c6/...Qc7. We need to realize that positionally speaking, Black is doing fantastic.The pieces develop quickly, and the c3-knight is misplaced, blocking the c-pawn. In the long run, Black will both have an incredibly solid London-like setup with the pawns on c6 and e6, and also plently of activity.This means that we must play concretely and force Black to make some concessions by exploiting the initiative and vulnerable queen position, otherwise Black will easily equalize. The key ideas for White are: ▷ Bd2 against some of the sidelines, targeting the queen on a5; ▷ Ne5+g2-g4/h2-h4 to track the bishop when it leaves the pawn chain; ▷ and Bc4 to induce an early ...e7-e6, locking the bishop outside.} 4. d4 Nf6 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gc8g4,Gc8f5]} ({As we will see in the} 4... Bf5 $6 {variation , Black has to wait for Nf3 before deploying the queen bishop.}) (4... c6 5. Nf3 {Black has nothing better than returning to the mainlines after} Nf6 ({,} 5... Bf5) ({or} 5... Bg4 {, all of which we can meet with} 6. Bc4 {.})) 5. Nf3 Bf5 6. Ne5 c6 {White was threatening some ideas connected with Nc4.} ({For instance, if} 6... e6 $2 7. g4 Bg6 8. Nc4 Qa6 9. h4 $36 {and the pieces do not coordinate for Black. If Black tries} Be4 {at any point, we react with} 10. Rh3 {to activate the rook along the third rank.}) 7. Bc4 {[%csl Rf7]} e6 8. g4 $1 {[%csl Rf5]} Bg6 (8... Be4 $2 {Too soon.} 9. O-O $1 Bd5 10. Bd3 $18 {The bishop is in limbo. White will play f2-f4, securing the center, followed by potentially f4-f5 or g4-g5. The queen goes to e2, X-raying the black king.}) 9. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] Aggressively going after the bishop.} Nbd7 (9... Bb4 $6 10. Bd2 Ne4 11. Rh3 $1 Nxd2 12. Qxd2 $18 {Black is busted. They can't develop the knight without having to give up castling rights, and h4-h5 is still a big threat.}) 10. Nxd7 Nxd7 11. h5 {[%csl Rg6]} Be4 {[%csl Rh1][%cal Ga5e1] Thanks to this resource, Black is still in the game.} 12. O-O $1 {[%csl Re4]} Bd5 13. Nxd5 cxd5 14. Bd3 Bd6 15. Bd2 $16 {[%csl Gg4,Gh5][%cal Ga2a4,Gc2c3,Gd1f3,Gg1g2] White has a fantastic position here, with control over every sector of the board. These advanced pawns are a big asset.We control the bishop pair, so Black cannot take advantage of the open air in front of the king. The most important thing to understand from here on is that this is not a racing position; we are better, but Black is still solid.We need to play slowly and gradually increase our advantage by improving moves like a2-a4, c2-c3, Qf3, and Kg2. Make sure to keep the game under control and don't let Black get active! You can follow a model game here.} *

