1. e4 Nf6 {The Alekhine is an opening where Black loses time now to provoke White's pawns forward and then trade them off later, gaining the time back by liquidating the pawns, which took many moves also to advance.} 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 {I recommend the four pawns attack, the most direct way to play. We keep the pawn center alive.Another benefit is that it reduces Black's possible variations. For the most part, the 'four pawns' is very linear, and there's not much room for Black to deviate from the script without getting into trouble. The main position of interest begins after...} dxe5 {Black can't avoid trading these pawns, so they should do it now and make sure White is recapturing with the f-pawn.} 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 Bf5 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 {...where White has space and Black needs to prove that they have some counterplay or else be worse.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
