1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. c4 Nb6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 Bf5 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Qd7 10. Be2 O-O-O 11. O-O f6 12. d5 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 fxe5 14. a4 $1 {[%cal Ga4a5] It turns out that the b6-knight becomes a massive liability when the king castles queenside.Train the line here.} a5 15. Nb5 Bb4 16. d6 $1 {The key idea. Everything falls apart after this.} Na8 17. c5 $18 {[%csl Ra8,Gc5,Gd6] Now Kotronias simply consolidates the gains. Black isn't immediately mated, but will be suffocated over the next few moves as all of Black's pieces are in bad shape, especially the knight, which was driven into the corner.} Kb8 18. Bf3 c6 19. Na3 e4 20. Be2 b6 21. cxb6 Qxd6 22. Qxd6+ ({Good enough, I would have preferred to keep the queens on with} 22. Qb3 {instead. Smerdon remarks that the majority of swindles occur in the endgame, so there is that to keep in mind.}) 22... Rxd6 23. Rad1 $6 Rhd8 (23... Rxd1 $1 24. Rxd1 e5 {could have prolonged Black's resistance.}) 24. Rxd6 Rxd6 25. g4 Bd2 26. Bf2 Kb7 27. Nc4 Rd5 28. gxf5 exf5 29. Rd1 Bb4 30. Be3 g6 31. Kf2 Rxd1 32. Bxd1 c5 33. Bb3 Nxb6 {At last, Black is rid of this wretched pawn two moves before resignation!} 34. Nxb6 Kxb6 35. Bg8 1-0

