1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. c4 Nb6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 Bf5 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Bg4 10. Be2 Bxf3 11. gxf3 Qh4+ 12. Bf2 Qf4 13. c5 Nd7 14. Qc1 {Train the line here.} Qf5 15. Qb1 $5 {Chandra insists on the queen trade, which is positionally not a bad idea. The queen is the only active piece for Black, and it also allows White to play with low risk in a stable, clearly better endgame.} Qxb1+ 16. Rxb1 O-O-O 17. f4 g6 18. Bf3 Ne7 19. Bh4 {Trying to trade into an opposite colored bishops scenario, but with the weak pawns on dark squares, it destabilizes White's position.} (19. b4 {is more to the point.} Bh6 20. Ne2 Nd5 {seems like an issue, but it turns out this trade is far better:} 21. Bxd5 $1 exd5 22. b5 $18 {And not only did we close the d-file, but also the f2-bishop serves as the anchor for our position.}) 19... Re8 (19... h6 $1 {with the idea of ...g6-g5 could have activated the rook.}) 20. Bxe7 Rxe7 21. c6 $6 {[%cal Gf8b4] Opens up the diagonal for the f8-bishop to develop. White is not yet ready to exploit the opening of the king position.} (21. b4 {is more consistent with ...Bxe7. White must contain the unopposed bishop. Black cannot play ...c7-c6, since after b4-b5 it would lead to a catastrophe.c5-c6 can be played whenever White is ready; there is no need to rush it when Black's pieces are a long way away from generating counterplay.}) 21... bxc6 22. Bxc6 Bh6 23. O-O Rd8 24. Rbd1 f6 (24... Nb8 $1 25. Bg2 c6 {gives the king and rook some breathing room.}) 25. Kg2 Nb8 26. Ba4 fxe5 27. fxe5 Kb7 28. Rf3 a5 29. Ne4 Ka7 30. Nc5 Na6 31. Nb3 Rb8 32. a3 $1 {Nicely executed prophylaxis. Lenderman has run out of luck, and the king's position now becomes untenable.} Rb6 33. Rf2 Ka8 34. Nxa5 c5 35. dxc5 Nxc5 36. Rd8+ Rb8 37. Rxb8+ Kxb8 38. Nc6+ Kc7 39. Nxe7 $18 {Black has avoided mate, but at too high a cost; the exchange.} Nxa4 40. Rf7 Kd8 41. Rxh7 1-0

