1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. d4 d6 4. c4 Nb6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Bf5 7. Nc3 e6 8. Nf3 Nc6 9. Be2 Be7 10. O-O O-O 11. Be3 {If you want to train this line, you can go here.} f6 12. exf6 Bxf6 13. Qd2 Qe7 14. Rad1 Rad8 15. Kh1 Kh8 16. h3 h6 17. Bg1 Bg6 18. Qe3 Qb4 {All smoke and mirrors. There is no follow-up connected with this one-mover.} 19. b3 {[%csl Ga2,Gb3,Gc4] Everything is under control.} Qa5 20. Ne4 Ne7 21. g4 Nd7 22. Bh2 Rde8 23. Nxf6 Nxf6 24. Ne5 Bc2 25. Qd2 {Kollars prefers the endgame.} (25. Rd2 Be4+ 26. Kg1 $18 {looks pretty decisive to me. Rxf6 is always looming, and something will hang. Too many weak squares. The queen on a5 is out of play.}) 25... Be4+ 26. Kg1 Qxd2 27. Rxd2 Rd8 28. b4 a6 29. a4 Kg8 30. a5 c6 31. Re1 Nc8 32. Bf1 Nd6 33. Rde2 Bh7 34. Bg2 Nc8 35. Rd2 Nd6 36. Rde2 Nc8 37. b5 cxb5 38. cxb5 Rxd4 39. Bxb7 axb5 40. Nc6 Rd7 41. a6 Be4 42. Rxe4 Nxe4 43. Ne5 Rd2 44. Bxe4 $18 {Movesian does a good job of making the win not so easy. The game shouldn't have lasted so long after this point.} Ra2 45. Bb7 Rff2 46. Bg3 Rfc2 47. Rd1 Nd6 48. Kf1 Nf7 49. Nc6 Rc3 50. Be1 (50. Bf2 $1 Rcc2 (50... Rf3 51. Ne7+) 51. Bd4 {does the trick.}) 50... Rf3+ 51. Kg1 Ng5 {Now there are real swindling chances. White allowed too much counterplay for no reason.} 52. Rd2 Ra1 53. Kg2 Rfa3 54. a7 Rxe1 55. a8=Q+ Rxa8 56. Bxa8 Re3 57. h4 Ne4 58. Rd4 Nf6 59. Kf2 Ra3 60. Rd8+ $4 Kh7 (60... Kf7 $1 {, and White cannot actually hold the g4-pawn.} 61. Ne5+ Ke7 62. Rc8 Kd6 $10) 61. Ne5 g5 62. h5 Kg7 63. Bf3 Ra2+ 64. Ke3 Ra3+ 65. Ke2 Ra2+ 66. Ke3 Ra3+ 67. Kd4 Ra4+ 68. Kc5 Ra3 69. Rb8 Nd7+ 70. Nxd7 Rxf3 71. Kd6 Rf4 72. Rb7 1-0

