1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 {[%csl Ge5]} dxe5 6. fxe5 {Now, ...Nc6 is surely Black's best move, and it has its own chapter. In the present section, we will examine the other options.} Bf5 {If Black plays ...Nc6 later, then we will likely see a transposition to the ...Nc6, Be3-lines, so I will only examine what happens if Black plays for ...c7-c5 – it's the only reason to delay ...Nc6.} 7. Nc3 e6 8. Nf3 Bb4 {The critical line and point of no return. Black is adamant about their plan of ...c7-c5 and goes all in. We can be very direct now.} ({▷ Black is not ready for ...c7-c5 yet:} 8... c5 $2 9. d5 $18) ({▷} 8... Nc6 9. Be3 {should promptly take us back to the main line.}) 9. Bd3 $1 {Normally, we are not able to play Bd3 – Black's knight on c6 means that d4 is loose. Now, this is not the case.} c5 (9... Bxd3 $2 {Anything other than ...c7-c5 is busted} 10. Qxd3 Nc6 11. O-O $18 {Ng5 is a huge threat. After something like ...h7-h6 or ...Be7, we can keep building up with moves like Ne4, Be3, and Rad1. Black has no counterplay since ...f7-f6 is not viable anymore without the f5-bishop.}) 10. Bg5 {[%csl Rd8] Now, there is a long concrete variation that leads to a much better position for White:} Qd7 11. Bxf5 cxd4 12. Nxd4 exf5 13. Nxf5 $1 {[%csl Rd8][%cal Rd7f5,Rd1d8]} Qxd1+ 14. Rxd1 O-O {It's true that Black managed to escape to an endgame, but the difference in piece activity is still a major issue.} 15. O-O Bxc3 $6 {Tempting, but only exacerbates Black's difficulties. The bishop was the only active piece.An example of how quickly this can go downhill is illustrated in the following line:} 16. bxc3 Nxc4 $2 17. e6 $1 {[%csl Rf8][%cal Rf7e6,Rf5e7,Rf1f8]} f6 {[%csl Rg5]} 18. Ne7+ Kh8 19. Rxf6 $1 {[%cal Gd1d8,Rg7f6,Rf8f6] And it's over.} *

