1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Bb4 $6 {Doesn't accomplish much. Rarely is it worth it to trade this bishop for the knight to double white's pawns, and the c3-knight guards nothing of importance.} 10. Be2 {Ignoring the bishop's antics and proceeding as normal.} Na5 {[%cal Ra5c4] A lame one-mover.} 11. c5 {[%csl Rb6]} Nd5 {[%csl Re3]} 12. Bd2 {[%csl Yb4,Gc5,Gd4,Ge5] Sooner or later, the bishop, running low on squares, will have to trade itself for the knight, leaving White with an enormous space advantage.} *

