1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Bb4 $6 {Doesn't accomplish much. Rarely is it worth it to trade this bishop for the knight to double white's pawns, and the c3-knight guards nothing of importance.} 10. Be2 {Ignoring the bishop's antics and proceeding as normal.} O-O 11. O-O Na5 {[%csl Rc4]} 12. c5 {[%csl Rb6] This time, Black can try jumping with the knight, as Qa4+ is not a thing anymore.} Nbc4 {[%csl Re3]} 13. Bg5 {[%csl Rd8]} Qd7 14. Qe1 $1 {[%cal Ga2a3] Preparing a2-a3.} b5 15. a3 {[%csl Rb4]} Bxc3 16. Qxc3 $18 {[%cal Gb2b3] But now there is a different issue: What is going to be done about b2-b3?} Nc6 $7 17. Rad1 $18 {[%cal Gf3h4,Gf1f4,Gf4g4,Gc3g3] In a few moves, the dark-squared bishop is going to be sorely missed – Nh4 marks the start of a brutal attack.} (17. b3 $4 Nxa3 $1 {Don't get Shankied!}) *

