1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Bg4 {Is a possible idea. While Black is losing a tempo to play his strongest idea, white cannot immediately exploit the slow play.} 10. Be2 Bxf3 11. gxf3 {[%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yf3,Rf4] The point: we have to recapture with the pawn, otherwise d4 hangs.This transformation is a nuisance for us, but we did gain a bishop pair and a few extra tempi.} Qh4+ 12. Bf2 {[%csl Rh4]} Qf4 13. c5 {[%csl Rb6]} Nd5 {Gives exactly what we want. We can permanently fix the issue of the backward d-pawn.} 14. Nxd5 exd5 15. Qd2 (15. O-O {is technically better, but White has to make some weird moves and sacrifice the f-pawn. Qd2 worked just fine against GM DeFirmian, so I figured that one would be easier to play.}) 15... Qxd2+ 16. Kxd2 $16 {This is a pleasantly better endgame, and the plans are easy to remember. How the game might continue:} g6 {[%cal Gf8h6] Trying to get to h6.} 17. Be3 Ne7 18. Bd3 {[%cal Gb2b4,Ga2a4,Gh2h4,Gf3f4] White tries to expand everywhere, all at once. Space makes space!} *

