1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Bg4 {Is a possible idea. While Black is losing a tempo to play his strongest idea, white cannot immediately exploit the slow play.} 10. Be2 Bxf3 11. gxf3 {[%csl Yd4,Ye5,Yf3,Rf4] The point: we have to recapture with the pawn, otherwise d4 hangs.This transformation is a nuisance for us, but we did gain a bishop pair and a few extra tempi.} Qh4+ 12. Bf2 {[%csl Rh4]} Qf4 13. c5 {[%csl Rb6]} Nd7 14. Qc1 {Keep it simple! Black's queen is their most active piece. The rest of the troops are all bound by the c5-d4-e5 constellation.} Qxc1+ 15. Rxc1 $16 {[%csl Yc5,Rc6,Yd4,Rd7,Ye5,Rf8][%cal Gb2b4,Ga2a4,Gc1b1,Ge2c4,Gc3e4,Rd7b6,Rd7f6,Rf8c5] While we still have the backward d-pawn, the placement of the d7-knight makes that fact awkward to exploit. White can look forward to queenside expansion, with a nice advantage.} *

