1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Nb4 $2 {[%cal Gb4c2] Easy to deal with.} 10. Rc1 c5 {The only logical follow-up, since the knight is about to be kicked back to square one after a2-a3. However, you probably noticed that the ...c7-c5-break is almost always the wrong plan for Black in the Alekhine.} 11. Qb3 $1 {[%csl Rb4][%cal Gd4c5] Does two things: Removes the queen from the d-file to avoid the impending queen trade, and threatens dxc5, winning a piece.} cxd4 12. Nxd4 {[%cal Gc4c5]} (12. Nxd4 Z0 13. c5 {still is a threat, since} Bxc5 14. Nxf5 {again wins a piece.}) 12... Bd3 (12... Nd3+ 13. Bxd3 Bxd3 14. Ncb5 $18 {White is winning thanks to the overwhelming lead in development. Castling and rushing the c-pawn should mop things up.}) 13. Rd1 Bxf1 14. Rxf1 $18 {[%csl Ra8,Rh8][%cal Gf1f8,Gd1d8,Gc4c5,Gd4b5] Every single white troop is participating while the black rooks are sleeping in the corners. As the younger generation would say had to add this first part in, Black is 'cooked'.} *

