1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Qd7 {The plan is to first bring a rook to the d-file before playing ...Bg4, when Black will have enough attackers to actually threaten capturing the d4-pawn.  There are two ways Black can achieve this: by castling long, or playing ...Rd8 directly. Our response is more or less the same against both.} 10. Be2 Rd8 11. O-O Bg4 12. c5 {[%csl Rb6]} ({I actually prefer the more direct} 12. Ng5 {but I think it's not worth it to recommend many lines to memorize in this already obscure variation.}) 12... Nd5 {[%csl Re3]} 13. Nxd5 Qxd5 {Here, the difference is that Qa4 is no longer effective with the king not being on that side. Instead, we should come up with a different plan.} 14. Qe1 $1 {[%csl Gb4,Gd4][%cal Ge1c3,Gb2b4] Preparing to bring the queen to c3.} Be7 15. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Bh5 16. Qc3 $14 {[%csl Ra2][%cal Rd5a2,Ya1d1] White is solidly ahead thanks to the extra space. Starting from here, both sides have many options, but one tip to remember is not to accidentally blunder the a2-pawn with Rad1. The d4-pawn is protected as long as the queen stays on d5 technically, Black has the chance to play ...Bxf3 and ...Nxd4 because of a fork on e2, but that leads to a better endgame for White as well. That's why we should play Kh2 next turn to take away this option from Black. Once the queen goes back to d7 and is no longer attacking the a2-pawn, THEN we play Rad1.} *

