1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Qd7 {The plan is to first bring a rook to the d-file before playing ...Bg4, when Black will have enough attackers to actually threaten capturing the d4-pawn.  There are two ways Black can achieve this: by castling long, or playing ...Rd8 directly. Our response is more or less the same against both.} 10. Be2 O-O-O (10... Bg4 {transposes after} 11. O-O {to 10...Rd8 or 10...O-O-O.}) 11. O-O f6 $6 {Now is the time to strike!} 12. d5 $1 Nxe5 13. Nxe5 fxe5 14. a4 $18 {[%csl Rb6][%cal Ga4a5] We can stop here. Immediately, the a-pawn is coming to remove the knight, after which the a7-pawn hangs.} ({▷ If Black plays} 14. a4 Kb8 {, keeping the structure together, then} ({▷ If Black instead plays} 14... a5 {, then} 15. Nb5 {, threatening d5-d6, is dangerous.Overall, the piece coordination lines up very poorly for Black, who will have a hard time getting out of the bind.}) 15. a5 Nc8 16. Qb3 {leads to a strong attack.}) *

