1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Qd7 {The plan is to first bring a rook to the d-file before playing ...Bg4, when Black will have enough attackers to actually threaten capturing the d4-pawn.  There are two ways Black can achieve this: by castling long, or playing ...Rd8 directly. Our response is more or less the same against both.} 10. Be2 O-O-O (10... Bg4 {transposes after} 11. O-O {to 10...Rd8 or 10...O-O-O.}) 11. O-O Bg4 $6 {The intended plan, but falls short:} 12. c5 $1 {Interrupting black before he can successfully eliminate the d4-pawn. Now, unless the knight wants to go time-out in the corner, Nd5 is forced.} Nd5 13. Nxd5 Qxd5 14. Kh1 $1 {Calmly stepping off the diagonal. Since we lured the queen to d5, ...Bxf3 is no longer an imminent problem.The onus is on the opponent to come up with something spectacular to compensate for the severe lack of space.} f6 {Pretty much the only thing that has been attempted, but other tries are equally good or bad, depending on how you look at it.} 15. Qa4 $18 {[%csl Ra7,Yd4,Ye5][%cal Gb2b4,Gb4b5,Ga1e1,Ge2c4] A great multi-purpose move, facilitating b2-b4 and Rad1, while also protecting the e5-pawn indirectly by tying the c6-knight to the a-pawn. White's position looks a bit loose, but everything is tactically defended.Coming up: counterintuitively, the rook probably wants to go to e1 instead of d1, since one of Black's ideas is ...Qe4. b2-b4 and Bc4 are both in the air.Even the prophylactic Bg1 can be considered. It turns out that the threats to the d4-pawn are all smoke and mirrors, and the e5-pawn is not hanging either, so Black's whole plan was a failure. For instance, the line...} Qe4 16. Qa3 fxe5 $2 {...fails tactically to...} 17. Ng5 {[%csl Rg4] ...and the bishop drops out of the game.} *

