[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "31"]
[GameId "2288537761218836"]

1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Qd7 {The plan is to first bring a rook to the d-file before playing ...Bg4, when Black will have enough attackers to actually threaten capturing the d4-pawn.  There are two ways Black can achieve this: by castling long, or playing ...Rd8 directly. Our response is more or less the same against both.} 10. Be2 O-O-O (10... Bg4 {transposes after} 11. O-O {to 10...Rd8 or 10...O-O-O.}) 11. O-O Kb8 $1 {Taking it slow before following through with ...Bg4. It is the best move, but people rarely play it. Logically, though, it's rarely a poor idea to follow up on ...O-O-O with ...Kb8.} 12. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5] Should be a familiar pattern by now – Aaron the a-pawn comes to kick the horse!} Bb4 $2 {Whenever your opponent 'prevents' you from doing something, the first thing you should ask yourself is 'what if I do it anyway?'. This is the Shankland rule, and this is the first thing I tell all my students!} (12... a5 13. Nb5 $16 {Thanks to this outpost, the d4-pawn will never become compromised. The machine suggests many slow plans, such as Qc1/Kh1/Bg1 or Qe1/Rc1. If Black ever plays ...f7-f6, we can play exf6 and Nh4, which is very annoying.}) 13. a5 $1 {Anyway!} Nxa5 {[%cal Gf1d1,Gd4d5]} (13... Bxa5 14. Qb3 $18 {Rfd1 and d4-d5 are coming next, and the black minors cannot find stable squares.}) 14. c5 {[%csl Rb6]} Nbc4 15. Bc1 {[%cal Ga1a4] The cluster of minor pieces is left in a very precarious spot. Ra4 is the immediate threat.} Bxc3 16. bxc3 $18 {[%csl Ra5,Rc4][%cal Ra5c4,Rc4a5,Ga1a5,Ge2c4] Ok, Black is up a pawn, but they are going to have to compromise the king's position to defend one of those knights with the b-pawn once they come under fire. Like a married couple, the two knights are handcuffed together.In the meantime, there's no meaningful counterplay that Black can drum up in the center.} *

