1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Be7 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O f6 {The only real plan for Black, otherwise it's too cramped!} 12. exf6 Bxf6 13. Qd2 Qe7 14. Rad1 Rad8 {[%cal Gb2b3,Gd2c1,Gg1h1,Gh2h3,Ge3g1] We have reached the tabiya of the whole line. There are a few things to understand about the position here: ▷ The first is that both sides more or less have their pieces placed in the best positions, which means that any subsequent improvements will be very minor on both sides such as the Kh1-Bg1 plan, which only marginally improves the position of the bishop. ▷ But since this is true of both sides, you have time to make these minor adjustments, which brings me to the second point: the structural transformations c4-c5 and d4-d5 for White, ...e6-e5 for Black, at least immediately, are not favorable for either side.These two points mean that, on the whole, this position is very slow, and we should not be rushing to do something, but rather slowly make incremental improvements and either wait for a misstep from Black, or become ready for a pawn break. Some moves that might be good in the upcoming turns are b2-b3, Qc1, Kh1, h2-h3, and Bg1.The evaluation of the whole four-pawn line hinges on whether or not White has something here, and I think White has some opportunities. At least, there is no way for Black to immediately liquidate the game.} 15. Kh1 $1 {[%cal Ge3g1] Prophylaxis, and removing the king from the long diagonal. At a later point, the bishop may retreat to g1. From here, there are a few plans Black can choose from.} (15. Qc1 $6 {, the old mainline, allows} e5 $1 16. d5 Nd4 {, and Black has too much activity.}) 15... Bg4 $6 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Gg4f3] Trying to knock out the d4-pawn's defender, but the move is slightly suboptimal – releases control over the e4-square, which we can immediately pounce on...} 16. Ne4 $1 Bxf3 17. Nxf6+ Rxf6 18. gxf3 $16 {[%csl Yc4,Yd4,Re4][%cal Gf1g1,Gg1g3,Gd1g1,Rd2c2,Re2d3,Rd3e4] White is somewhere between tangibly and solidly better, and the two black knights do not inspire confidence. White has some options to continue improving the position.One plan is Qc2, Bd3, and Be4 to fix the bishop, another plan is Rg1-Rg3-Rdg1 to put pressure against the g-pawn. Regardless of what plan is chosen, make sure not to blunder one of the two central pawns!} *

