1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Be7 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O f6 {The only real plan for Black, otherwise it's too cramped!} 12. exf6 Bxf6 13. Qd2 Qe7 14. Rad1 Rad8 {[%cal Gb2b3,Gd2c1,Gg1h1,Gh2h3,Ge3g1] We have reached the tabiya of the whole line. There are a few things to understand about the position here: ▷ The first is that both sides more or less have their pieces placed in the best positions, which means that any subsequent improvements will be very minor on both sides such as the Kh1-Bg1 plan, which only marginally improves the position of the bishop. ▷ But since this is true of both sides, you have time to make these minor adjustments, which brings me to the second point: the structural transformations c4-c5 and d4-d5 for White, ...e6-e5 for Black, at least immediately, are not favorable for either side.These two points mean that, on the whole, this position is very slow, and we should not be rushing to do something, but rather slowly make incremental improvements and either wait for a misstep from Black, or become ready for a pawn break. Some moves that might be good in the upcoming turns are b2-b3, Qc1, Kh1, h2-h3, and Bg1.The evaluation of the whole four-pawn line hinges on whether or not White has something here, and I think White has some opportunities. At least, there is no way for Black to immediately liquidate the game.} 15. Kh1 $1 {[%cal Ge3g1] Prophylaxis, and removing the king from the long diagonal. At a later point, the bishop may retreat to g1. From here, there are a few plans Black can choose from.} (15. Qc1 $6 {, the old mainline, allows} e5 $1 16. d5 Nd4 {, and Black has too much activity.}) 15... h6 (15... Kh8 {will likely transpose to the ...h7-h6 lines. It's a sign that Black understands how to play the position properly, so it's unlikely Black will deviate from the ...h7-h6 plan.}) 16. Qc1 Kh8 17. h3 Bh7 (17... Rd7 {immediately is possible, but our response will be the same as after ...Bh7 first.}) 18. Bg1 e5 $5 {Only played once, but Stockfish and Leela both see it as an option to try to break first.} 19. d5 Nb4 (19... e4) ({and} 19... Nd4 {both lose a pawn for insufficient compensation, so this is the only try.}) 20. b3 $1 {[%csl Gc4][%cal Gb3c4] To understand why b2-b3 is the best, we need to understand what happens after a2-a3.} (20. a3 e4 $1 21. axb4 exf3 {, and here White would like to capture with the bishop, but can't because the c4-pawn is en prise. So by playing b2-b3 first, we are preparing to enter this variation and be able to recapture on f3 with the bishop!}) 20... Bc2 {Otherwise a2-a3 comes and ...Nb4 was pointless.} 21. a3 $5 {[%csl Rb4][%cal Gc2d1] Shankland rule! Gifting our opponent this rook. But this sacrifice is completely sound: once this bishop is gone, you will notice that Black has been playing checkers with their remaining pieces, and their light squares will be 'cooked'!} Bxd1 22. Bxd1 Nd3 23. Qe3 Nf4 24. Ne4 $14 {[%csl Ge4,Rf6][%cal Gd1c2,Ge3e4,Ga3a4,Ga4a5,Gh1h2] Later down the line, a battery along the b1-h7 diagonal could prove fatal for Black. But for now, we maintain the blockade of the light squares and continue improving our position.} *

