ws} e5 $1 16. d5 Nd4 {, and Black has too much activity.}) 15... h6 (15... Kh8 {will likely transpose to the ...h7-h6 lines. It's a sign that Black understands how to play the position properly, so it's unlikely Black will deviate from the ...h7-h6 plan.}) 16. Qc1 Kh8 17. h3 Bh7 (17... Rd7 {immediately is possible, but our response will be the same as after ...Bh7 first.}) 18. Bg1 Rd7 {Now that the king is off the diagonal, this plan is viable.} 19. Rfe1 {[%csl Re7][%cal Ge1e7,Gd4d5] Interrupting the plan before Black gets ...Rfd8 in.} Rfd8 $2 {Unfortunately, the Shankland rule does not have a 100% success rate unless you are me!.} 20. d5 $1 {Busting through the defenses.} exd5 21. cxd5 {[%csl Re7][%cal Ge2b5,Ge2c4,Re1e7]} Bxc3 {Everything else is losing material to some discovered attack.} 22. bxc3 Nxd5 23. Bb5 {[%csl Re7]} Qf6 {But wait, this doesn't look that bad?} 24. Bxc6 bxc6 25. Bc5 {[%csl Yd6,Rd7,Ye7][%cal Gf3e5,Yc5f8,Gd1d8,Ge1e8] ...but sometimes looks can be deceiving. In fact, this position is almost +3. How exactly is Black stopping Ne5, winning the exchange, without allowing c3-c4? Black has too many back rank issues, d-file issues, and bad coordination to stave off all the threats, even though at first glance it looks like a normal position.} *

