1. e4 Nf6 2. e5 Nd5 3. c4 Nb6 4. d4 d6 5. f4 dxe5 6. fxe5 Nc6 7. Be3 {[%csl Yd4][%cal Rc8g4,Gg1f3,Rg4d1] The key thing to remember in this opening is to delay the development of the g1-knight as much as possible until black moves the c8-bishop; that way, we avoid the ...Bg4 pin, which will seriously undermine the d4-pawn.} Bf5 {Now Black has to play this move now, since they need the bishop out of the way before playing ...e7-e6.} 8. Nc3 e6 9. Nf3 Be7 10. Be2 O-O 11. O-O f6 {The only real plan for Black, otherwise it's too cramped!} 12. exf6 Bxf6 13. Qd2 Qe7 14. Rad1 Rad8 {[%cal Gb2b3,Gd2c1,Gg1h1,Gh2h3,Ge3g1] We have reached the tabiya of the whole line. There are a few things to understand about the position here: ▷ The first is that both sides more or less have their pieces placed in the best positions, which means that any subsequent improvements will be very minor on both sides such as the Kh1-Bg1 plan, which only marginally improves the position of the bishop. ▷ But since this is true of both sides, you have time to make these minor adjustments, which brings me to the second point: the structural transformations c4-c5 and d4-d5 for White, ...e6-e5 for Black, at least immediately, are not favorable for either side.These two points mean that, on the whole, this position is very slow, and we should not be rushing to do something, but rather slowly make incremental improvements and either wait for a misstep from Black, or become ready for a pawn break. Some moves that might be good in the upcoming turns are b2-b3, Qc1, Kh1, h2-h3, and Bg1.The evaluation of the whole four-pawn line hinges on whether or not White has something here, and I think White has some opportunities. At least, there is no way for Black to immediately liquidate the game.} 15. Kh1 $1 {[%cal Ge3g1] Prophylaxis, and removing the king from the long diagonal. At a later point, the bishop may retreat to g1. From here, there are a few plans Black can choose from.} (15. Qc1 $6 {, the old mainline, allows} e5 $1 16. d5 Nd4 {, and Black has too much activity.}) 15... h6 (15... Kh8 {will likely transpose to the ...h7-h6 lines. It's a sign that Black understands how to play the position properly, so it's unlikely Black will deviate from the ...h7-h6 plan.}) 16. Qc1 Kh8 17. h3 Bh7 (17... Rd7 {immediately is possible, but our response will be the same as after ...Bh7 first.}) 18. Bg1 Rd7 {Now that the king is off the diagonal, this plan is viable.} 19. Rfe1 {[%csl Re7][%cal Ge1e7,Gd4d5] Interrupting the plan before Black gets ...Rfd8 in.} Qd8 {[%csl Gc1,Gd1,Ge1,Gf1,Gg1,Gh1] Black was smart and didn't allow d4-d5, so we finally have to admit defeat and protect this pawn.} (19... Qf7 20. Bf1 $16 {This is where I will cut it. An amazing sight to behold – every single piece being effective at its maximum range! From here, White has very easy play, and there is nothing for Black to strike at with all our pieces so far away. Ne4, c4-c5, and b2-b3 are some candidates for the next move.}) 20. Qe3 Nb4 {[%cal Bb4c2] Black can cause some temporary problems, but once the knight is driven back, we can resume.} (20... Bf5 {To be honest, Black is sort of running out of active ideas. Against something like ...Bf5 or ...Re8, we can continue the slow constrictor strategy.} 21. b3 $16 {We still don't fear ...Nb4, so we do not have to play a2-a3 and weaken the b3-square unnecessarily.}) 21. Rf1 Nc2 {[%csl Re3]} 22. Qc1 (22. Qc1 Nxd4 {doesn't work thanks to the timely appearance of the rook to f1, which will overload the queen on d8 at the end of all the trades. Black has run out of tricks and has to retreat.}) 22... Nb4 23. a3 {[%csl Rb4]} Nc6 24. b4 $16 {[%csl Gb4,Rb6,Gc4,Rc6,Gd4][%cal Yb4b5,Yc4c5,Ge2c4] Funny how all the pieces ended up back in their original spots. However, through careful sequencing, we have suppressed all of the counterplay, and now we are rewarded with this nice, superior middlegame.b4-b5 and c4-c5 cannot be prevented, then we clear the c4-square for the bishop. White still should take it slow and advance all the pawns and pieces methodically until all the life in Black's position is squeezed out!} *

