1. e4 g6 {The Modern and Pirc are two related openings, with their own benefits and drawbacks.In the Pirc, Black can elect to develop the bishop on e7 and avoid any e5 shenanigans, but commits to fighting for the center.In the Modern, Black is not committed to putting a pawn in the center, but has to play with the fianchetto, so we can target it via h2-h4, h4-h5, or Be3-Qd2-Bh6.  Black often wants to keep us at arm's length and not allow pawn tension, very similar to the g3-English setups for White. Black gives up space in the center in hopes that eventually the counterplay on the queenside will amount to something, or the g7-bishop will become a powerhouse.} 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 {Black has several tries, as their opening choice has been flexible.} d6 {...d7-d6 with ...a7-a6 and ...b7-b5 is the most popular.} ({▷} 3... a6 {, followed immediately by ...b7-b5 runs into some issues with Nf3-Ng5, since Black is late to playing ...Bg4.}) ({▷} 3... c5 $2 {is the Pterodactyl Gambit. Read my advice on playing openings named after animals, extinct, fictional, or otherwise!}) ({▷} 3... d5 $5 {is the Mittenberger Gambit, which is much stronger than it appears at first glance. It's a serious move which needs attention.}) ({▷} 3... c6 {is a system where Black tries to blockade the light squares with ...d7-d5+...h7-h5/...Nh6, but this can be prevented if we are smart about what move order we choose. Black is generally doing poorly in these positions if they can't blockade, as their lack of space and counterplay, and poor g7-bishop, are all factors.}) ({▷} 3... e6 $2 {is trying to reach a Hippo, but that will not be possible. Black should use a different move order.}) 4. Be3 a6 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Rd1d2,Rd2h6,Re4e5,Gg8h6,Yg8f6] Unlike the Pirc, Black is intentionally delaying ...Nf6 and instead focuses on making advances on the queenside with ...a7-a6 and ...b7-b5. This serves two purposes: it avoids e4-e5 with tempo, and keeps h6 protected in the event of Bh6. However, it does come with one drawback...} 5. g4 $5 {[%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Gf1g2,Yh2h4,Yh7h5,Rg8f6,Rg8h6,Rg4g5] This move has a few purposes, but the most obvious ones are not the most important ones. g4-g5 hanging over Black's head means that it will be harder to develop their knight, and Bg2 being in the air makes it harder to play ...Bb7, or at least to cause any real trouble on the long diagonal.But the biggest point, by far, is much more blunt. We want to play h2-h4-h5, and if Black responds with ...h7-h5 themselves, we will be able to take it.} (5. h4 {If Black had played ...b7-b5 instead of ...d7-d6, I think this move is very convincing. As is, it is the main move and definitely challenging, but it is not as easy. The reason is that after} h5 6. Nf3 (6. Qd2 {White should prefer this move, and they can still fight for an edge, but it is outside the scope of our repertoire.}) 6... Nf6 $1 {We are missing the e4-e5 resource. Black should be okay.}) 5... b5 6. Bg2 *

