1. d4 g6 2. e4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d5 4. Nxd5 c6 5. Ne3 Qxd4 6. Qxd4 Bxd4 7. a4 Nd7 8. Bc4 $6 {[%csl Gh3][%cal Gf1g2,Gf1h3]} ({Imprecise. We know from the course that we are supposed to go} 8. a5 {if we are allowed to, to gain space.This bishop usually does not actually want to develop, and instead should remain flexible on f1. There are many times the bishop can come out via g2 or h3 instead, or is needed to support a knight developing to h3.}) 8... Nc5 9. f3 Be6 10. Ne2 Be5 11. a5 $1 {Sarana corrects his mistake, and now we reach something similar to what we see in the course. White has some enduring edge, but Black is solid enough.} Nf6 12. Bxe6 Nxe6 13. Nc4 Bc7 14. Be3 Nh5 15. g3 f5 16. e5 (16. exf5 $1 {was the way, spoiling the structure.}) 16... f4 $1 {White was ready to play f3-f4 next and lock Black out of the game, but this move is well spotted.This shows that we need to keep in mind Black's ideas and counterplay when handling this from the White side.} 17. Bd2 fxg3 18. hxg3 O-O 19. f4 b5 20. Ne3 Rad8 21. O-O-O Nd4 22. Nxd4 Rxd4 23. Be1 Rfd8 24. b3 a6 25. Rxd4 Rxd4 26. Bf2 Rd7 27. b4 Ng7 28. Rd1 Rxd1+ 29. Nxd1 Ne6 30. Nc3 g5 31. Be3 gxf4 32. gxf4 h5 $2 {[%csl Rh5][%cal Gc1h4,Gh4h5] Up until here, Black was fine, but this move voluntarily makes this pawn a target. If Black had just moved the king up to f5, there is no way White can ever target the h-pawn.} 33. Kd1 Kf7 34. Ne4 Kg6 35. Ke2 Kf5 36. Kf3 h4 37. c3 $2 {White does the same mistake, and voluntarily creates a target in their camp for no reason. Perhaps White was afraid of ...Nd4+ at some point, but the bishop firmly guards this square.} (37. Nc3 $1 {prepares to relocate the knight to e2 to collect h4. Black unfortunately can't really guard this pawn, as it has become overextended.}) 37... Bb8 38. Nf2 Nc7 39. Nd3 Nd5 40. Bd4 h3 41. Kg3 h2 42. Kxh2 Nxf4 43. Nc5 Bxe5 44. Nxa6 Ne6+ 45. Bxe5 Kxe5 46. Nc5 Nc7 47. Kg3 Kd5 48. Kf4 Kc4 49. Ne4 Nd5+ 50. Kf5 Kd3 51. a6 Nc7 52. a7 Ke3 $4 {An unfortunate error, removing the king from attacking the c3-pawn. White can use this moment to reposition the knight. Black was close to making the draw.} ({After} 52... Na8 $10 {, how does White make progress?}) 53. Nc5 Kd2 54. Na6 Na8 55. Ke6 {[%cal Ge6c8,Gc8a8]} Kxc3 56. Kd7 c5 57. bxc5 b4 58. Nxb4 Kxb4 59. c6 Nb6+ 60. Kd8 1-0

