1. d4 g6 2. e4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c5 4. dxc5 Qa5 5. Bd2 Qxc5 6. Nd5 Na6 7. Nf3 {You can follow the line here.} e6 {[%csl Rd6,Rf6,Rh6] I mentioned that we should pay attention to this swiss cheese structure. In this game, Black gets schooled on the dark squares.} 8. Bc3 Bxc3+ 9. Nxc3 d6 (9... Nc7 10. Qd2 e5 {some formation with ...e6-e5 and ...Ne6 has to be attempted, not completely to surrender the dark squares.}) 10. Qd2 Nf6 11. O-O-O {Already, the d6-pawn cannot be reinforced.} O-O 12. Qxd6 Qxf2 13. Qf4 Kg7 14. Bxa6 $1 {The knight is chopped off, leaving Black with the crappy bishop against White's knights. This is a common strategy when targeting a specific color complex.} bxa6 15. Qe5 Qb6 16. Rhf1 Qb8 17. Qd4 Qb6 18. Qe5 Qb8 {White repeats once to gain some time, but obviously will not be making a draw here in response to Black's desperate attempts to trade queens.Funnily enough, Black is probably still screwed even if the queens come off, since the fundamental issue of the bishop being bad, and the weak dark squares, were never solved.} 19. Rd6 Kg8 20. Qd4 Ne8 21. Rd8 Qb6 22. Qxb6 {Now, after the rook invades the back rank, White agrees to trade queens.} axb6 23. Ne5 $18 {[%cal Gd8h8,Gd8a8] Black's pieces are all lined up like bowling pins on the last rank.} Bb7 (23... Ng7 24. Rxf8+ Kxf8 25. Rxf7+ {does not inspire confidence either.}) 24. Rxa8 Bxa8 25. Nd7 {White goes up an exchange. The rest is trivial.} Nd6 26. Rd1 Nb7 27. Nxf8 Kxf8 28. Rd7 Ke8 29. Rc7 Nd8 30. a4 h5 31. h4 f5 32. exf5 gxf5 33. g3 e5 34. Kd2 f4 35. gxf4 exf4 36. Rc4 f3 37. Ke3 Kd7 38. Rd4+ Ke8 39. Rb4 Kd7 40. Rxb6 Nf7 41. Rxa6 Bc6 42. Ra7+ Ke6 43. Nb5 Kd5 1-0

