{An oldie but a goodie. This famous game featured in 'My 60 Memorable Games' is known for being a perfect illustration of the tactical idea of interference. Fischer demonstrates surgical precision in his handling of the middlegame position.} 1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. f4 Nf6 5. Nf3 {[%cal Rc7c5,Gf1d3]} ({Fischer plays through the} 5. Nf3 {move order, though we would be playing}) (5. Bd3) ({to avoid} 5. Nf3 c5 {.}) 5... O-O 6. Bd3 Bg4 7. h3 Bxf3 8. Qxf3 Nc6 9. Be3 e5 10. dxe5 dxe5 11. f5 {This is the position we reached from the course.  Benko opts to resolve the tension immediately. It's not ideal to open up so many lines on the kingside, but on the other hand, if Black waits too long, White arrives with g2-g4, g4-g5 very fast.} gxf5 (11... Nd4 12. Qf2 b5 {is the plan the computer prefers:} 13. g4 c5 14. g5 c4 $1 15. gxf6 Bxf6 16. O-O-O cxd3 17. Rxd3 b4 18. Nd5 $16 {, but White retains a big initiative well into the middlegame.}) 12. Qxf5 Nd4 13. Qf2 ({Even} 13. Qxe5 Ng4 14. Qxg7+ $3 Kxg7 15. hxg4 {wins for White}) 13... Ne8 14. O-O Nd6 {The knight is not really more effective here than it was on f6, but it was hard to suggest a move for Black that accomplishes anything.} 15. Qg3 $1 {[%cal Gf2g3,Gg3g4,Gg4h5] Executing the 'queen creep'. The queen heads towards the optimal attacking square one step at a time.} Kh8 16. Qg4 c6 17. Qh5 Qe8 $4 (17... Ne6 {keeps Black's position on life support, since Bxd4 is prevented.}) 18. Bxd4 exd4 19. Rf6 $1 {Benko got Shankied. Fischer prevents the intended ...f7-f5 resource.} Kg8 20. e5 h6 21. Ne2 {[%csl Rd6][%cal Rf6d6,Yd6f5,Yh5f5] Benko throws in the towel; the knight can't move without allowing Qf5.} 1-0

