1. e4 g6 2. d4 c6 3. Nc3 d5 4. e5 Bg7 5. h3 Nh6 6. g4 f6 7. f4 {You can train this position here.} Nf7 8. Be3 Qb6 9. Na4 {The typical reaction to ...Qb6 in many openings; attack and defense.Likewise, Black's usual continuation is to give us a check and argue that the a4-knight becomes misplaced. However, we can usually get it back into the game with b2-b4, Nb2, Nd3, and in the process, we have gained space and some tempi against the queen.} Qa5+ 10. c3 Na6 11. b4 Nxb4 $5 {True to her style, Gaprindashvili dives right in for a piece sac. Practically, this might not be the worst decision, since retreating the queen leads to a very undesireable position.} (11... Qc7 12. Bxa6 bxa6 13. Nf3 {Black is going to be handicapped by these pawns.}) 12. cxb4 Qxb4+ 13. Kf2 fxe5 14. fxe5 O-O {Here, White needs to be a bit cautious.} 15. Nf3 (15. Kg2 $2 Nxe5 16. dxe5 $4 Qe4+ {for instance is a catastrophe!}) 15... Bxe5 16. dxe5 Nxe5 {Black is now down two pieces for four pawns, which is a strange material imbalance for sure.} 17. Bg2 Bxg4 $1 {Third piece sacrificed, removing the remaining pawns!} 18. hxg4 Nxg4+ 19. Ke2 Qe4 $2 {Allows White to stabilize the position.} (19... Nxe3 20. Kxe3 e5 {Ok, White is winning, but in a practical game, who knows. The king is naked, and the pawns are coming!}) 20. Qd4 Rf4 21. Nc3 Qc2+ 22. Qd2 Nxe3 23. Qxc2 (23. Kxe3 $1) 23... Nxc2 24. Rac1 Na3 25. Ne5 Rf5 26. Nd3 e5 $4 {A tactical oversight. White now seizes the opportunity to wake up her pieces.} 27. Nxd5 $1 cxd5 28. Bxd5+ Kg7 29. Rc7+ Kf6 30. Rxb7 Rc8 {White should focus now on finishing off the black king. Materially, Black is not doing too badly, so munching a few extra pawns won't really help White.} 31. Rxa7 Nb5 32. Rf7+ Kg5 33. Rg1+ Kh6 34. Rxf5 gxf5 35. Bb3 $4 {The bishop has to stay alive to have any winning chances.} Nd4+ 36. Kd1 Nxb3 37. axb3 Rc3 38. Nc1 e4 39. Kd2 Rf3 40. b4 f4 41. Re1 Rf2+ 42. Re2 e3+ 43. Kc3 Rf1 44. Nd3 Kg5 45. b5 Ra1 46. b6 Ra8 47. Rg2+ Kf5 48. Rg7 Rb8 49. b7 Kf6 50. Rc7 f3 51. Nb4 f2 52. Nd5+ Ke5 53. Nxe3 Kd6 54. Rf7 Kc6 55. Nc4 f1=Q 56. Rxf1 Kxb7 57. Rf7+ Kc6 58. Rxh7 Rb1 59. Rh6+ Kc5 60. Rh5+ Kc6 61. Ne3 Kd6 62. Kd4 Ke6 63. Rh6+ Kf7 64. Nf5 Re1 65. Ng3 Rg1 66. Nh5 Re1 67. Ng3 Rg1 68. Ne4 Ra1 69. Kd5 Ra5+ 70. Nc5 Ra1 71. Ke5 Re1+ 72. Ne4 Kg7 73. Rb6 Kf7 74. Rf6+ Kg7 75. Rf4 Kg6 76. Ke6 Re3 77. Rg4+ Kh5 78. Rg8 Kh6 79. Kf5 Rf3+ 80. Ke5 Re3 81. Rg1 Ra3 82. Nd6 Ra5+ 83. Kf6 Kh5 84. Nf7 Ra2 85. Nd6 Kh4 86. Ke5 Kh3 87. Ne4 Ra8 88. Rg6 Ra1 89. Nf2+ Kh4 90. Rg2 Ra5+ 91. Kf4 Kh5 92. Ne4 Kh6 93. Nd6 Ra1 94. Ke5 Re1+ 95. Kf6 Kh5 96. Nf5 Ra1 $4 {A mysterious move. The draw was right in front of her!} (96... Re6+ $10 {One last move to end the game.}) 97. Rg5# 1-0

