1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 5. g4 Nc6 ({I had forgotten my analysis here that suggested I can slam} 5... Nc6 6. d5 {right away – once more, I had not reviewed the Modern for this specific game. But the way I played was not bad either.}) 6. h3 {Now, if allowed, next move I can play d4-d5 and meet Ne5 with f2-f4, reaching the same kind of positions as the course gives. Richard found the only way to avoid it and create independent value, but it did not help him.} e5 7. dxe5 $1 {A very good choice, especially given the circumstances – I had already won game 1 with Black, and only needed a draw here. Taking back ...dxe5 will allow an unpleasant endgame with no real chance at counterplay, but as the game showed, the alternative was much worse.} Nxe5 $2 (7... dxe5 {This was needed, and Black might have saved the game, but he would not have saved the match.} 8. Qxd8+ Nxd8 9. Nd5 {Black would be absolutely fine here if his a6-pawn were back on a7. He would go ...Ne6 and ...c7-c6 to kick away my active knight, and all would be well in the world. As is, ...c7-c6 is never going to be a thing because of Nb6, and Black faces nagging pressure with no counterplay. After} Ne6 10. O-O-O Nf6 11. Nf3 $14 {, moves like Bc4 and Ng5 could be on the agenda, and Black is just a bit worse with no chance to imbalance the game.}) 8. f4 $1 Nd7 9. Qd2 $18 {Nine moves in, and the game is basically over. I can castle long next, set up with Nge2 and Bg2, and Black will never have a single active idea. Eventually, he will be hit by either e4-e5 or h4-h5.} Ngf6 10. O-O-O O-O 11. Bg2 Re8 12. Nge2 Rb8 13. Ba7 Ra8 14. Be3 {A bit cheeky – White is totally winning, but a draw was enough for me to win the match and advance to the sweet 16, so I repeated moves to see if Richard wanted to save some face. True to his style, he decided not to. But he really is just lost.  I can do something like Ng3 next, when g4-g5 becomes a huge problem. But what can Black do? ...b7-b5 loses on the spot to e4-e5, moving the d7-knight also gets hit with e4-e5...} h5 15. Bf3 {Keeping it simple. Now gxh5, next would tear open Black's kingside.} (15. e5 dxe5 16. f5 {The computer claims this crushes. And it definitely does. But my move was also more than good enough.}) 15... hxg4 (15... h4 {This would have kept the kingside closed temporarily, but Black is still dead lost.} 16. g5 Nh7 17. Qe1 {Among other moves, this looks the simplest to me. Just grab h4, come back, and shove h3-h4, h4-h5.}) 16. hxg4 Nb6 17. Bxb6 $1 cxb6 18. g5 Nh7 (18... Ng4 19. Rh4 {Black's knight is immediately trapped.}) 19. Qxd6 {I could have played for mate, but this seemed easy enough.} Qxd6 20. Rxd6 b5 21. Nd5 $1 {It looks like I just trapped my own rook to ...Bf8, but I had seen Rxh7 in response.} Rb8 (21... Bf8 22. Rxh7 $1) 22. Nf6+ Nxf6 {Richard offered me a draw here, which would clinch me the match. But... I somehow could not bring myself to take it. I saw that once I go gxf6 and e4-e5, Black is just mated on the spot by Rd2-h2.} 23. gxf6 Bh8 24. e5 Be6 25. Rd2 {[%cal Ge2d4,Gd2h2] With Nd4 and Rdh2 coming next, Black had seen enough.} 1-0

