1. e4 g6 2. d4 Nf6 {I refuse to take the Norwegian Rat seriously. I believe the line I gave in my 1. d4 repertoire blows it out of the water, and I think a simple copy and paste will work just fine.} 3. e5 Nh5 4. Be2 {[%csl Rh5] White threatens to take on h5. Black has one trick, but as long as White doesn't fall for it, they will be much better.} d6 {Now, it is important not to get too materialistic and take on h5, as this will give Black some counterplay. Instead, White should simply bolster their center.} 5. f4 $1 {White makes sure that they can take back on e5 with the f-pawn. The position resembles an Austrian Attack in the Pirc defense, but a very bad version for Black, thanks to the poorly placed knight on h5.} dxe5 {This is probably Black's best option, just trying to make some trouble in the center, but it comes up short.} ({▷ After} 5... Nc6 6. d5 $18 {The knight is booted away, Bxh5 is coming, and Black is already dead lost.}) ({▷} 5... Ng7 {has been the most common move, although White is obviously much better after} 6. Nf3 {with O-O to follow, and a lead in development plus a big space advantage.}) 6. fxe5 c5 (6... Nc6 7. c3 $18 {Black is already lost. They have no way to challenge White's center with their c-pawn blocked.}) 7. d5 e6 8. Nc3 {This is one of the rare moments in my 1. d4 repertoire where I recommend blocking the c-pawn with Nb1-c3, but the position looks much more like a 1. e4 game. Black is in a very bad way.White is always ready to meet ...exd5 with Qxd5, Black will struggle to play ...Bg7 as then after Nf3, the knight is in danger of being trapped with g2-g4, and the position looks like a trainwreck.} *

