1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c5 $6 {This opening is called the 'Pterodactyl Defense' or something ridiculous like that. Here's a little nugget of chess advice: don't play any openings that are named after animals!The reason why Black plays ...c7-c5 on move one to reach the Sicilian is so that they can initiate the capture on d4. If White is allowed to initiate the capture, it's actually quite bothersome to recoup the c5-pawn. Thus, the next move naturally is:} 4. dxc5 $1 Bxc3+ {Not that ridiculous, it does triple our pawns.} 5. bxc3 {[%csl Rc2,Rc3,Rc5] At first glance, these pawns may seem very bad. However, there are a few factors in our favor:▷ One is that we still have our dark-squared bishop and Black doesn't, so the c5-pawn can be defended via Be3.▷ Another is that the c5-pawn itself exerts a cramping influence on the black queenside, making it difficult for Black to complete development without eventually having to play either ...b7-b6 or ...d7-d6, which trades off one of our weaknesses for us.▷ Finally, we have access to the maneuver Qd4-Qb4, which will remove the black queen from a5.} Nf6 {[%csl Re4]} 6. e5 {[%csl Rf6]} Ne4 {Unlike the ...Qa5-line, here we are unfortunately losing one of the triplets. However, Black is losing lots of time dancing the knight around.} 7. Bc4 (7. Qd4 $2 Qa5 $1 {Don't get shankied!}) 7... Nxc5 8. Qd5 {[%csl Rc5,Rf7]} Ne6 9. Nf3 $16 {[%csl Gc1,Gc4,Ye5] While Black has a structural advantage, White has space, development, and the bishop pair going for them.} *

