1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c5 $6 {This opening is called the 'Pterodactyl Defense' or something ridiculous like that. Here's a little nugget of chess advice: don't play any openings that are named after animals!The reason why Black plays ...c7-c5 on move one to reach the Sicilian is so that they can initiate the capture on d4. If White is allowed to initiate the capture, it's actually quite bothersome to recoup the c5-pawn. Thus, the next move naturally is:} 4. dxc5 $1 Bxc3+ {Not that ridiculous, it does triple our pawns.} 5. bxc3 {[%csl Rc2,Rc3,Rc5] At first glance, these pawns may seem very bad. However, there are a few factors in our favor:▷ One is that we still have our dark-squared bishop and Black doesn't, so the c5-pawn can be defended via Be3.▷ Another is that the c5-pawn itself exerts a cramping influence on the black queenside, making it difficult for Black to complete development without eventually having to play either ...b7-b6 or ...d7-d6, which trades off one of our weaknesses for us.▷ Finally, we have access to the maneuver Qd4-Qb4, which will remove the black queen from a5.} Qa5 6. Qd4 {[%cal Gd4c5,Gd4c3,Rd4h8] First, we protect these pawns while simultaneously attacking the rook in the corner. Black has to play ...Nf6 and is not in time to cover the b4-square.} Nf6 7. Qb4 $1 {Once again coming with tempo. Here, Black has two choices: retreat the queen and hope to get the tempo back against the b4-queen later, or play ...Nc6 immediately and save time but allow the queen trade.} Nc6 {[%csl Rb4]} 8. Qxa5 Nxa5 9. Bd3 {[%csl Ra5,Rc8,Rf6][%cal Ga8c8] Once the queens are gone, it turns out that Black simply has no way to access the c5-pawn, and thus has no choice but to admit defeat and play ...b7-b6 in order to complete development.} b6 $7 10. cxb6 axb6 11. Bh6 $16 {[%cal Re8g8] Causing one last headache before ending the line – we prevent castles. The position is imbalanced, but with most of them being in White's favor.} *

