1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c5 $6 {This opening is called the 'Pterodactyl Defense' or something ridiculous like that. Here's a little nugget of chess advice: don't play any openings that are named after animals!The reason why Black plays ...c7-c5 on move one to reach the Sicilian is so that they can initiate the capture on d4. If White is allowed to initiate the capture, it's actually quite bothersome to recoup the c5-pawn. Thus, the next move naturally is:} 4. dxc5 $1 Qa5 5. Bd2 {[%csl Gc3][%cal Gd2c3,Ra5e1]} Qxc5 6. Nd5 $1 {[%cal Gd2b4] A silent killer. Bb4 is threatened, when Black has nowhere to run with the queen to continue covering c7 c6 doesn't count: Bb5! invites the queen to a very forkable square.} Na6 7. Nf3 e6 {[%csl Rd5]} 8. Nc3 $16 {[%csl Ya6,Rd6,Rf6][%cal Gd7e6,Ge6f7,Gf7g6,Gg6h7] The knight returns after completing its mission of inducing ...Na6 and ...e7-e6, which are both detrimental to the integrity of Black's position.} *

