[White "8) Modern Sidelines"]
[Black "8B | 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 c6 #2"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B15"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "17"]
[GameId "2288537761329453"]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c6 4. h3 $1 {[%csl Gg4][%cal Gf2f4,Gg1f3,Rc8g4] In all these systems where Black is trying to blockade the light squares, a common theme is that White never wants to allow Bg4-Bxf3, trading off the bad bishop. Instead, we take a turn to prevent Black's best idea before proceeding with development.This move has the added benefit of not obstructing the f-pawn yet, as we will need this pawn to go to f4 to support e4-e5.} d5 5. e5 h5 $2 {The blockade won't function without ...Bg4.} 6. Nf3 Nh6 7. Bd3 Bf5 8. O-O Nd7 9. Re1 $18 {[%csl Rg7][%cal Gc3e2,Ge2f4] The g7-bishop does not belong there, and h5 seriously weakens the kingside and kills any chance of playing ...f7-f6 down the line.There is no need to rush in with e5-e6 just yet; the advantage is long-term. A good plan is to first bring the worst minor piece to f4 with Ne2! to prepare e5-e6 later on.} *

