1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 c6 4. h3 $1 {[%csl Gg4][%cal Gf2f4,Gg1f3,Rc8g4] In all these systems where Black is trying to blockade the light squares, a common theme is that White never wants to allow Bg4-Bxf3, trading off the bad bishop. Instead, we take a turn to prevent Black's best idea before proceeding with development.This move has the added benefit of not obstructing the f-pawn yet, as we will need this pawn to go to f4 to support e4-e5.} d5 5. e5 f6 6. f4 Nh6 7. g4 $1 {[%csl Gf5,Rh6][%cal Gg4f5] Boxing out the knight.} O-O 8. Nf3 fxe5 9. dxe5 Nf7 (9... e6 $4 {The most natural move, but unfortunately also a losing one! The main issue is the c8-bishop loses contact with the g4-square, which means we can go for...} 10. h4 $1 {Advancing against the compromised black kingside.Unfortunately, Black lacks any counterplay in the center or queenside to bother us, so this slow-looking move will eventually result in the destruction of the black kingside structure.} Nxg4 11. h5 $18 {Once we trade on g6 and begin attacking the vulnerable g4-knight, things will get very ugly very fast.}) 10. Qe2 $16 {[%csl Ge5][%cal Rg6g5,Ge2e5,Gc1d2,Gf1g2,Ge1c1] Bolstering e5. The takeaway from these c6-d5 Modern lines is that Black's entire position is not cohesive at all, and there are too many clashing ideas for it to work. After developing the bishops and castling long, Black is struggling to come up with a plan, and also can never play ...e7-e6 because h3-h4 will come knocking down the kingside.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "8) Modern Sidelines"]
[Black "8B | 2...Bg7 3.Nc3 d5 #1"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B06"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "19"]
[GameId "2288537761333551"]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d5 $5 {The Mittenberger Gambit: Surprisingly, not such a terrible option for Black. In fact, the machines think it is the best. The key points are...  ▷ that Black is essentially trying to get the ...c7-c6+...d7-d5 line but with the c-pawn able to go to c5 in one move. This means that e5 is weaker since we do not have the time to play both h2-h3 and f2-f4 because of ...c7-c5, destabilizing the center, and  ▷ White can't hold onto the pawn, no matter how they capture, so Black is not committing to a true gambit. If Nxd5, then ...c7-c6 wins back the pawn directly, and after exd5, Black will hunt down the pawn with ...Nbd7-b6, and the c3-knight obstructs c2-c4.} 4. Nxd5 $1 {This one was quite challenging to prove an advantage, and after careful consideration, I've concluded that we should go for the endgame.  Initially, I was skeptical of White's chances because the resulting structure is symmetric, but it turns out that Black has some concrete problems, and it's not a walk in the park to coordinate their position.} c6 5. Ne3 {[%csl Gc4][%cal Ge3c4] Other knight moves are possible, but this square is the most challenging since the c4 jump becomes available at a later time.} Qxd4 ({First of all, let's get} 5... Bxd4 $2 {out of the way. With the queens on, this is incredibly dangerous. White is ahead in development and can coordinate an attack.} 6. Bc4 $1 $36 {, holding off on Nf3.  The point is that the bishop has to go to b6 after Nf3 in view of Bxf7+. Against pretty much every move, we play} Z0 7. Nf3 Bb6 8. Qe2 {, misplacing the bishop and avoiding the queen trade. White is much better here.}) 6. Qxd4 Bxd4 7. a4 {[%csl Gc1,Yc6,Yc8,Rd4,Ye4,Yg6][%cal Ga4a5,Ge3c4,Gc1e3,Re7e5,Rg8f6,Gf2f3] a2-a4 makes sense to take the space, and we can adjust our setup based on how Black reacts to the threat of a4-a5.Here, Black is facing some concrete issues, despite the symmetrical pawn structures. Foremost is the poor position of the d4-bishop, which makes it hard for Black to play ...e7-e5 or ...Nf6. The bishop likely has to waste time retreating to g7.Another issue is that this ...c7-c6 move has weakened the dark squares around the queenside, especially d6 and b6. This discourages ...a7-a5 or ...e7-e5, which means that Black cannot contest the space.Finally, the pawn structure means that our queen bishop is significantly stronger than its black counterpart. Following moves like Nc4 and Be3, we can already begin to target the weaknesses on the queenside.The coordination we are looking for is a4-a5 on the next move, if Black allows it, and f2-f3/Nc4/Be3. Black has many moves, the order of which may be interchangeable. I will give an overview of a few possible ways for Black to proceed, and some general plans for White.The general conclusion is that White is perpetually going to retain the edge.} a5 {[%csl Rb6] Prevents our pawn from advancing further, but weakens the b6-square. We continue with the f2-f3/Ne2/Nc4/Be3 plan.} 8. f3 Na6 {To keep the bishop unblocked is an alternative way to deploy the knight.} 9. Ne2 {[%csl Rd4]} Bc5 10. c3 $14 {[%csl Ra6] Controlling the a6-knight.} *

