 hard for Black to play ...e7-e5 or ...Nf6. The bishop likely has to waste time retreating to g7.Another issue is that this ...c7-c6 move has weakened the dark squares around the queenside, especially d6 and b6. This discourages ...a7-a5 or ...e7-e5, which means that Black cannot contest the space.Finally, the pawn structure means that our queen bishop is significantly stronger than its black counterpart. Following moves like Nc4 and Be3, we can already begin to target the weaknesses on the queenside.The coordination we are looking for is a4-a5 on the next move, if Black allows it, and f2-f3/Nc4/Be3. Black has many moves, the order of which may be interchangeable. I will give an overview of a few possible ways for Black to proceed, and some general plans for White.The general conclusion is that White is perpetually going to retain the edge.} Bg7 {Pre-emptively retreats the bishop.} 8. a5 Nf6 {[%csl Re4]} (8... e5 $2 {is never good for Black. It only creates weaknesses and kills the bishop.}) 9. f3 O-O 10. Nc4 Be6 11. Be3 Nbd7 12. g3 $14 {[%cal Gg1h3,Gh3f4,Ge1c1] The knight is on the way to f4 we do not want the pawns to be doubled after Bxh3, hence g2-g3 first. White might castle queenside to complete development.} *

