1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d5 $5 {The Mittenberger Gambit: Surprisingly, not such a terrible option for Black. In fact, the machines think it is the best. The key points are...  ▷ that Black is essentially trying to get the ...c7-c6+...d7-d5 line but with the c-pawn able to go to c5 in one move. This means that e5 is weaker since we do not have the time to play both h2-h3 and f2-f4 because of ...c7-c5, destabilizing the center, and  ▷ White can't hold onto the pawn, no matter how they capture, so Black is not committing to a true gambit. If Nxd5, then ...c7-c6 wins back the pawn directly, and after exd5, Black will hunt down the pawn with ...Nbd7-b6, and the c3-knight obstructs c2-c4.} 4. Nxd5 $1 {This one was quite challenging to prove an advantage, and after careful consideration, I've concluded that we should go for the endgame.  Initially, I was skeptical of White's chances because the resulting structure is symmetric, but it turns out that Black has some concrete problems, and it's not a walk in the park to coordinate their position.} c6 5. Ne3 {[%csl Gc4][%cal Ge3c4] Other knight moves are possible, but this square is the most challenging since the c4 jump becomes available at a later time.} Qxd4 ({First of all, let's get} 5... Bxd4 $2 {out of the way. With the queens on, this is incredibly dangerous. White is ahead in development and can coordinate an attack.} 6. Bc4 $1 $36 {, holding off on Nf3.  The point is that the bishop has to go to b6 after Nf3 in view of Bxf7+. Against pretty much every move, we play} Z0 7. Nf3 Bb6 8. Qe2 {, misplacing the bishop and avoiding the queen trade. White is much better here.}) 6. Qxd4 Bxd4 7. a4 {[%csl Gc1,Yc6,Yc8,Rd4,Ye4,Yg6][%cal Ga4a5,Ge3c4,Gc1e3,Re7e5,Rg8f6,Gf2f3] a2-a4 makes sense to take the space, and we can adjust our setup based on how Black reacts to the threat of a4-a5.Here, Black is facing some concrete issues, despite the symmetrical pawn structures. Foremost is the poor position of the d4-bishop, which makes it hard for Black to play ...e7-e5 or ...Nf6. The bishop likely has to waste time retreating to g7.Another issue is that this ...c7-c6 move has weakened the dark squares around the queenside, especially d6 and b6. This discourages ...a7-a5 or ...e7-e5, which means that Black cannot contest the space.Finally, the pawn structure means that our queen bishop is significantly stronger than its black counterpart. Following moves like Nc4 and Be3, we can already begin to target the weaknesses on the queenside.The coordination we are looking for is a4-a5 on the next move, if Black allows it, and f2-f3/Nc4/Be3. Black has many moves, the order of which may be interchangeable. I will give an overview of a few possible ways for Black to proceed, and some general plans for White.The general conclusion is that White is perpetually going to retain the edge.} Nd7 $6 8. a5 Nc5 9. f3 Be6 {Black plays for activity, but we are too solid. Black will be pushed back.} 10. Nd1 $1 {[%csl Rb2][%cal Gc1e3,Gg1e2] Overprotecting b2. Now we are ready for Ne2/Be3.} Bg7 11. Be3 Nd7 12. Ne2 $16 {[%cal Ge2f4,Gf4e6] I believe that aside from the space advantage, the main factor here is the asymmetry of the two bishops on e3 and e6; while the e3-bishop is keeping the a8-rook on lockdown and complementing the f3-e4 pawn formation, the e6-bishop is hampered by its own pawns and doesn't serve any purpose here. Nf4 already threatens to trap this bishop and wreck the pawn structure.} *

