 hard for Black to play ...e7-e5 or ...Nf6. The bishop likely has to waste time retreating to g7.Another issue is that this ...c7-c6 move has weakened the dark squares around the queenside, especially d6 and b6. This discourages ...a7-a5 or ...e7-e5, which means that Black cannot contest the space.Finally, the pawn structure means that our queen bishop is significantly stronger than its black counterpart. Following moves like Nc4 and Be3, we can already begin to target the weaknesses on the queenside.The coordination we are looking for is a4-a5 on the next move, if Black allows it, and f2-f3/Nc4/Be3. Black has many moves, the order of which may be interchangeable. I will give an overview of a few possible ways for Black to proceed, and some general plans for White.The general conclusion is that White is perpetually going to retain the edge.} Be6 $6 {Commits the bishop too soon. This move is targeted against Nc4 to prevent Ne2, but here we have not gone Nc4 yet, and thus, Be6 does nothing.} 8. Ne2 {[%csl Rd4]} Be5 {[%cal Gf2f4,Ge4e5] Otherwise Nf4 comes with tempo. With the bishop here, the f2-f4/e4-e5 plan becomes available.} 9. g3 {[%cal Gf1g2,Gf2f4,Ge4e5]} Nf6 10. Bg2 $16 {We are ready to zipper Black down the middle, giving us a concrete advantage. This pawn chain will restrict the black minor pieces.} *

