1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 a6 $5 {Black is trying to go ...b7-b5 before ...d7-d6, which is not an entirely stupid idea. However, there are some drawbacks, which we will soon see.} 4. Be3 b5 (4... d6 {transposes back to the main line.}) 5. h4 $1 {In the main line, I recommended g2-g4, which is easier to play than h2-h4, but here we have a favorable version of the h2-h4 mainline. Because Black does not have ...d7-d6 yet, Black's main idea against what we're about to do does not work.} h5 {In general, this would be the move I would want to avoid and the reason we go for g2-g4 first in the main line. But here, there is an important difference on display.} 6. Nf3 $1 {[%cal Gf3g5] Rushing to g5.} Nf6 {[%cal Gf6g4] If Black's pawn were on d6 here and their b-pawn was back on b7, this move would be quite annoying. As is, because we have e4-e5 as a possibility, it is not nearly as convincing.} 7. e5 $1 {[%csl Rf6]} Ng4 8. Ng5 $1 {[%cal Gd1f3] Without the pawn on d6, Black does not have pressure against e5 and on top of that the diagonal is wide open, facilitating Qf3.} d5 (8... O-O 9. Be2 Nxe3 (9... d6 10. e6 $1) 10. fxe3 {Bxh5 is coming, and Black won't last long.}) 9. Bd3 $16 {[%csl Ra6,Rb5,Rd5,Rf7,Rg6,Rh5] Clearly, this was not the structure Black was hoping for.} *

