1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} Nd7 {With this move, Black is remaining flexible with the pawns, and intends to choose their structure based on what we do. But any move that is flexible in one aspect will be commital in another, and when preparing openings, we should try and figure out what flexibility was lost with this particular move order.} 5. g4 $1 {[%cal Rc8g4] It turns out that our setup in the main line is strengthened by this move ...Nd7: the knight obstructs the bishop's vision towards g4. The ideas are almost identical – we have prophylaxis against ...Nf6 due to g4-g5, and also prevent ...h7-h5 due to gxh5.The only difference is that since Black is not committed with the bishop to b7 yet, we should hold off on developing our own bishop to g2 too quickly. The bishop may become useful on the other diagonal.Black can respond in several ways, which I will briefly cover.} a6 ({▷} 5... c5 6. Qd2 $14 {is a very strong move that should lead White to an excellent version of the Dragon. Normally, it is ill-advised to allow Black to play ...cxd4 and to be unable to recapture with the knight, as then ...Nc6 will win a tempo.But, because the knight is committed to d7 already, this is not the case.}) ({▷} 5... c6 6. h4 {leads to a typical scenario where Black has to figure out what to do about the unpleasant threat of h5-h6.}) ({▷} 5... e5 $2 6. Qd2 $16 {is very bad. Black gets stuck without a good way to proceed, since ...exd4 is not desirable, and there is not enough pressure on d4 to make White flinch first.}) 6. h4 $16 {[%cal Gh4h6] This transposes directly to the 4...a6 mainline, covered in the next chapter.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
