1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} c6 {I believe this is an inaccurate move order, as Black is missing the threat of ...Ng4 for this one turn. ...Nf6 followed by ...c7-c6 is a more precise way to play.} 5. f4 $1 {Now we get the Austrian setup against the modern too!} b5 6. Bd3 $16 {This time, there is no ...e7-e5, so we play this one instead to have the bishop out before the knight hems it in. ...Nf6 can always be responded to with e4-e5, and ...b5-b4 with Nce2. When Black goes ...Nd7, we play Nf3 to avoid the ...Bg4 pin.} *

