1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} c6 {I believe this is an inaccurate move order, as Black is missing the threat of ...Ng4 for this one turn. ...Nf6 followed by ...c7-c6 is a more precise way to play.} 5. f4 $1 {Now we get the Austrian setup against the modern too!} Qb6 6. a3 $1 {[%csl Ra3][%cal Rb6b2,Gc3a4,Rb2a3]} ({A slick way to defend the b2-pawn. This is a common idea against ...Qb6 in many openings; the point is that} 6. a3 Qxb2 7. Na4 {now traps the queen, since the a3-square is no longer available.}) 6... Nf6 7. Nf3 O-O 8. Bd3 $16 {[%csl Rb6] The queen on b6 is kind of purposeless, since ...Qxb2 is never a real threat.} *

