1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} c6 {I believe this is an inaccurate move order, as Black is missing the threat of ...Ng4 for this one turn. ...Nf6 followed by ...c7-c6 is a more precise way to play.} 5. f4 $1 {Now we get the Austrian setup against the modern too!} d5 6. e5 h5 {This takes us to a version of the Gurgenidze setup, but quite a good version for White.Black really needs to manage to trade off their light-squared bishop to make this work, or at least be able to meet h2-h3 with ...h5-h4. Now, this will not happen.} 7. h3 Bf5 8. Nf3 Nh6 9. Be2 Nd7 10. O-O $16 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Gf3g5,Bg2g4] The knight is really bad on h6. Black's setup like this only ever makes sense if they can trade their light-squared bishop, and here, this will not happen. Next up could be Ng5 to eyeball some weak squares, and eventually g2-g4 will come.} *

