1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} c6 {I believe this is an inaccurate move order, as Black is missing the threat of ...Ng4 for this one turn. ...Nf6 followed by ...c7-c6 is a more precise way to play.} 5. f4 $1 {Now we get the Austrian setup against the modern too!} d5 6. e5 h5 {This takes us to a version of the Gurgenidze setup, but quite a good version for White.Black really needs to manage to trade off their light-squared bishop to make this work, or at least be able to meet h2-h3 with ...h5-h4. Now, this will not happen.} 7. h3 Nh6 8. Bf2 $1 {Dodging ...Nf5. The issue now is that Black has two pieces they want to occupy the f5-square with, but only one gets to go there. Black also cannot get into ...h5-h4. This blockade will not hold.} Bf5 (8... Nf5 9. g3 $1 {, preventing ...h5-h4. The knight will be pushed off of f5 after further preparations.}) 9. Be2 $16 {[%cal Gg2g4] g2-g4 is on the way. If ...Qd7, then Nf3-Nh4 will track down the bishop.} *

