1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} c6 {I believe this is an inaccurate move order, as Black is missing the threat of ...Ng4 for this one turn. ...Nf6 followed by ...c7-c6 is a more precise way to play.} 5. f4 $1 {Now we get the Austrian setup against the modern too!} Nf6 6. h3 {[%csl Gg4] Taking control over the g4-square.} O-O 7. Nf3 b5 8. e5 $1 {[%csl Rf6] A good reaction to ...b7-b5, as we will see in the Pirc chapter.} Nd5 9. Nxd5 cxd5 10. Qd2 $16 {[%csl Rb5,Gd4,Ge5,Gf4][%cal Gf1d3,Ge1g1] White has a significant advantage thanks to the structural transformation and the trade of the c3-knight, which often becomes a target. The e5-pawn is an unassailable beachhead into the black camp, while the b5-pawn looks ridiculous now.} *

