1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} Nc6 $6 {This move is usually pretty bad because it blocks the c-pawn, so there is no ...c7-c6 or ...c7-c5. In addition to that, ...e7-e5 is now harder to achieve because of d4-d5, which gains a tempo. ...Nc6 only makes sense if Black can follow it up with a quick ...e7-e5 d4-d5 ...Nd4, planting the knight in the middle, but this will not happen.} 5. Qd2 {[%cal Gd2c3,Rd4d5,Yg7c3] Not d4-d5 just yet, as ...Bxc3+ could be annoying.} Nf6 6. f3 e5 7. Nge2 {[%cal Be1c1,Bg2g4,Bh2h4] There is nothing wrong with d4-d5, but I like keeping a bit more tension. We castle long and throw the kingside pawns.} *

