[PlyCount "23"]
[GameId "2288537761390919"]

1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} Nc6 $6 {This move is usually pretty bad because it blocks the c-pawn, so there is no ...c7-c6 or ...c7-c5. In addition to that, ...e7-e5 is now harder to achieve because of d4-d5, which gains a tempo. ...Nc6 only makes sense if Black can follow it up with a quick ...e7-e5 d4-d5 ...Nd4, planting the knight in the middle, but this will not happen.} 5. Qd2 {[%cal Gd2c3,Rd4d5,Yg7c3] Not d4-d5 just yet, as ...Bxc3+ could be annoying.} e5 {The only move that makes sense. Against everything else, White can go f2-f3 and h2-h4 and run Black over.} 6. d5 {[%csl Rc6]} Nd4 {This is the move Black would like to work, but unfortunately, Black is not in time to play ...c7-c5.} 7. Nge2 c5 8. dxc6 $18 {This is the worst version of a Saemisch pawn sacrifice I could possibly imagine. Black is very, very busted.} bxc6 9. Nxd4 exd4 10. Bxd4 Nf6 {Normally, we would get this position with White having burned a tempo on c2-c4, which weakens their queenside, possibly burning two more tempos on Ne2-c1-b3 before taking on d4, and even then, White still fights for an edge. Here, it is all over.} 11. O-O-O O-O 12. Bc4 $18 {White is a clean pawn up, facing no counterplay, and is already dead winning.} *

