1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} Nf6 {[%csl Ge3][%cal Rf2f4] With ...Nf6, Black is transposing into a Pirc, but one where White has already committed to the English attack instead of the Austrian. This is somewhat annoying because it means that we have to play something different from my main recommendation.Fortunately, there is a simple and direct way for White to play here to secure the edge.} 5. Qd2 Ng4 {This is somewhat annoying initially, but Black's play will go nowhere.} 6. Bg5 h6 {[%csl Rg5]} 7. Bh4 g5 {Other moves are possible, but then we just boot the knight on g4 with f2-f3 or h2-h3.} 8. Bg3 e5 9. d5 f5 {[%cal Bf5f4]} 10. exf5 Bxf5 11. f3 {[%csl Rg4]} Nf6 12. O-O-O $16 {[%csl Ge4,Rg7][%cal Gg3f2,Gg1e2,Ge2g3,Gg3e4,Gf1d3] We know this structure is terrible for Black. The bishop is totally dead after we occupy the e4-square.} *

