1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} Nf6 {[%csl Ge3][%cal Rf2f4] With ...Nf6, Black is transposing into a Pirc, but one where White has already committed to the English attack instead of the Austrian. This is somewhat annoying because it means that we have to play something different from my main recommendation.Fortunately, there is a simple and direct way for White to play here to secure the edge.} 5. Qd2 c6 {The most popular choice. Black uses the c-pawn to support ...b7-b5 instead of the a-pawn.} 6. Bh6 {[%csl Rg7]} Bxh6 $1 {Of course, Black does not castle into it.} 7. Qxh6 b5 8. e5 $1 dxe5 9. dxe5 Nd5 10. O-O-O $16 {As usual, we respond to ...b7-b5 with e4-e5 immediately, without giving Black a chance to drive the knight from c3.Now ...b5-b4 is useless, as the knight can capture on d5 without losing time.} *

