1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 {Now, the main move, by a wide margin, is ...a7-a6, which will be covered in its own chapter. But Black has other moves they can try, too, that we must be ready for.} Nf6 {[%csl Ge3][%cal Rf2f4] With ...Nf6, Black is transposing into a Pirc, but one where White has already committed to the English attack instead of the Austrian. This is somewhat annoying because it means that we have to play something different from my main recommendation.Fortunately, there is a simple and direct way for White to play here to secure the edge.} 5. Qd2 c6 {The most popular choice. Black uses the c-pawn to support ...b7-b5 instead of the a-pawn.} 6. Bh6 {[%csl Rg7]} Bxh6 $1 {Of course, Black does not castle into it.} 7. Qxh6 Qa5 {[%csl Re4] This is most common, but also quite bad for Black.} 8. Bd3 {[%cal Bb8d7,Be7e5,Bg1f3,Gc6c5,Gd4d5,Ya5h5,Yh6h5,Rf6g4,Rh6g7]} ({A few pointers on how to proceed from here: ▷ If} 8. Bd3 Nbd7 ({or} 8... e5 {, then} 9. Nf3 {is good as we saw in previous lines.}) ({▷ If} 8... c5 {, then} 9. d5 {.}) ({▷ If} 8... Qh5 {, we trade queens with a better endgame.}) ({▷} 8... Ng4 {can always be met with} 9. Qg7 {, winning the h-pawn.}) ({▷ Perhaps best is} 8... Na6 {with the idea of ...Nb4, but we can just ignore this. ...Nxd3 cxd3 only strengthens White's structure in the center.})) 8... b5 $2 {This is the second most common, but Black is already lost.} (8... e5 9. Qg7 $18 {White wins a piece.}) 9. Nf3 $1 b4 {[%csl Rc3]} 10. Ne2 {e4-e5 is on the way, and it is not going to feel good for Black when it happens.} Nbd7 11. O-O Qc7 12. Ng3 $18 {[%cal Gf1e1,Ge4e5] White has achieved the ideal coordination. Rfe1, followed by e4-e5 is on the way, and the king can't castle because the queen cannot be removed from h6. Black is in big trouble.} *

