1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Rd1d2,Rd2h6,Re4e5,Gg8h6,Yg8f6] In general, I consider this to be Black's main move in the Modern, often named for Tiger Hillarp Persson:▷ Black is hoping to play with ...Nbd7 and ...b7-b5.▷ White has quite a few setups available that should be able to show an edge, but the positions can be a bit tricky.I like playing in a very blunt manner that avoids a lot of the subtleties that Black is hoping to take advantage of.} 5. g4 {[%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Gf1g2,Yh2h4,Yh7h5,Rg8f6,Rg8h6,Rg4g5] This move has a few purposes, but the most obvious ones are not the most important ones. g4-g5 hanging over Black's head means that it will be harder to develop their knight, and Bg2 being in the air makes it harder to play ...Bb7, or at least to cause any real trouble on the long diagonal.But the biggest point, by far, is much more blunt. We want to play h2-h4, h4-h5, and if Black responds with ...h7-h5 themselves, we will be able to take it.} (5. h4 {If Black had played ...b7-b5 instead of ...d7-d6, I think this move is very convincing. As is, it is the main move and definitely challenging, but it is not as easy. The reason is that after} h5 6. Nf3 (6. Qd2 {White should prefer this move, and they can still fight for an edge, but it is outside the scope of our repertoire.}) 6... Nf6 $1 {We are missing the e4-e5 resource. Black should be okay.}) 5... Nd7 6. h4 $1 {Of course.} h5 7. gxh5 $1 {As usual, we are happy to take on h5.} Rxh5 8. Be2 {[%csl Rh5]} Rh8 9. Nf3 Ngf6 10. Ng5 {[%cal Gh4h5] h4-h5 is coming, and Black is in trouble.} *

