1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Rd1d2,Rd2h6,Re4e5,Gg8h6,Yg8f6] In general, I consider this to be Black's main move in the Modern, often named for Tiger Hillarp Persson:▷ Black is hoping to play with ...Nbd7 and ...b7-b5.▷ White has quite a few setups available that should be able to show an edge, but the positions can be a bit tricky.I like playing in a very blunt manner that avoids a lot of the subtleties that Black is hoping to take advantage of.} 5. g4 {[%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Gf1g2,Yh2h4,Yh7h5,Rg8f6,Rg8h6,Rg4g5] This move has a few purposes, but the most obvious ones are not the most important ones. g4-g5 hanging over Black's head means that it will be harder to develop their knight, and Bg2 being in the air makes it harder to play ...Bb7, or at least to cause any real trouble on the long diagonal.But the biggest point, by far, is much more blunt. We want to play h2-h4, h4-h5, and if Black responds with ...h7-h5 themselves, we will be able to take it.} (5. h4 {If Black had played ...b7-b5 instead of ...d7-d6, I think this move is very convincing. As is, it is the main move and definitely challenging, but it is not as easy. The reason is that after} h5 6. Nf3 (6. Qd2 {White should prefer this move, and they can still fight for an edge, but it is outside the scope of our repertoire.}) 6... Nf6 $1 {We are missing the e4-e5 resource. Black should be okay.}) 5... b5 {This is the main move, and the most consistent one with Black's general plan in the Tiger Modern.} 6. Bg2 Bb7 7. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] The alternatives to ...h7-h6 here are all much worse, since Black must be ready to meet h4-h5 with ...g6-g5 to keep the kingside closed. If we get h4-h5, then Black will be prevented from playing ...h7-h6 due to hxg6, and h5-h6 ourselves on the next move will be a huge pain to deal with. If Black captures in response to h4-h5, then we have the thematic idea g4-g5!, preventing the development of the g8-knight, and the queen will zoom out to recoup the pawn on h5.} h5 $2 {Even worse than on move 5. This time, we are really going to force through h5.} 8. gxh5 $1 {The point of White's play is on clear display.} Rxh5 9. Bf3 $1 {[%csl Rh5]} Rh8 10. h5 $1 {Black is not able to keep the kingside closed.} gxh5 11. e5 $18 {[%csl Rf7,Yg8,Rh5][%cal Gh1a8,Gh1h5,Ge5e6] White is completely winning. e5-e6 ideas are already in the air.The position is opening up, and Black isn't developed and furthermore, can't develop!. The good news for us is that there is a real possibility that people will play this in a real game, which will be a free point.} *

