n h5.} h6 {Black must prevent h4-h5-h6 or else the bishop will be banished to Narnia. As we will see, the alternatives are not good.} ({▷} 7... Nf6 $2 {walks right into it.} 8. h5 $16 {Thanks to the Bg2, ...b5-b4 is never a concern because we have Nd5.}) ({▷} 7... e6 $2 {is not helpful in any way.} 8. h5 $16) 8. g5 $1 hxg5 (8... e6 $2 9. h5 gxh5 10. Rxh5 $16 {It might be awkward to capture on h6. Qg4 first is also an idea, to prepare g5-g6 or gxh6.}) 9. h5 $1 {[%csl Rg5][%cal Gg6g5] Because Black has captured on g5 first, Black no longer has the ability to keep the kingside closed with ...g6-g5 because the other pawn is blocking it.} b4 {[%csl Rc3]} 10. Nd5 e6 {[%csl Rd5]} 11. Nxb4 gxh5 12. Rxh5 Nf6 {[%csl Rh5,Re4]} 13. Rxh8+ Bxh8 14. Bxg5 c5 {[%csl Rb4]} 15. dxc5 Qa5 16. c3 Qxc5 17. Be3 $14 {[%csl Ga2,Ra6,Gb2,Gc3,Rd6,Ge4,Re6,Gf2,Rf7] Computer thinks Black is OK, but we have a pawn, and I don't see Black's compensation.For some guidance on how to win the position from here, you can check out my book Converting an Extra Pawn in Chess. 🙂} *

