1. e4 g6 2. d4 Bg7 3. Nc3 d6 4. Be3 a6 {[%csl Gh6][%cal Rd1d2,Rd2h6,Re4e5,Gg8h6,Yg8f6] In general, I consider this to be Black's main move in the Modern, often named for Tiger Hillarp Persson:▷ Black is hoping to play with ...Nbd7 and ...b7-b5.▷ White has quite a few setups available that should be able to show an edge, but the positions can be a bit tricky.I like playing in a very blunt manner that avoids a lot of the subtleties that Black is hoping to take advantage of.} 5. g4 {[%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Gf1g2,Yh2h4,Yh7h5,Rg8f6,Rg8h6,Rg4g5] This move has a few purposes, but the most obvious ones are not the most important ones. g4-g5 hanging over Black's head means that it will be harder to develop their knight, and Bg2 being in the air makes it harder to play ...Bb7, or at least to cause any real trouble on the long diagonal.But the biggest point, by far, is much more blunt. We want to play h2-h4, h4-h5, and if Black responds with ...h7-h5 themselves, we will be able to take it.} (5. h4 {If Black had played ...b7-b5 instead of ...d7-d6, I think this move is very convincing. As is, it is the main move and definitely challenging, but it is not as easy. The reason is that after} h5 6. Nf3 (6. Qd2 {White should prefer this move, and they can still fight for an edge, but it is outside the scope of our repertoire.}) 6... Nf6 $1 {We are missing the e4-e5 resource. Black should be okay.}) 5... b5 {This is the main move, and the most consistent one with Black's general plan in the Tiger Modern.} 6. Bg2 Bb7 7. h4 {[%cal Gh4h5] The alternatives to ...h7-h6 here are all much worse, since Black must be ready to meet h4-h5 with ...g6-g5 to keep the kingside closed. If we get h4-h5, then Black will be prevented from playing ...h7-h6 due to hxg6, and h5-h6 ourselves on the next move will be a huge pain to deal with. If Black captures in response to h4-h5, then we have the thematic idea g4-g5!, preventing the development of the g8-knight, and the queen will zoom out to recoup the pawn on h5.} h6 {Black must prevent h4-h5-h6 or else the bishop will be banished to Narnia. As we will see, the alternatives are not good.} ({▷} 7... Nf6 $2 {walks right into it.} 8. h5 $16 {Thanks to the Bg2, ...b5-b4 is never a concern because we have Nd5.}) ({▷} 7... e6 $2 {is not helpful in any way.} 8. h5 $16) 8. g5 $1 hxg5 (8... e6 $2 9. h5 gxh5 10. Rxh5 $16 {It might be awkward to capture on h6. Qg4 first is also an idea, to prepare g5-g6 or gxh6.}) 9. h5 $1 {[%csl Rg5][%cal Gg6g5] Because Black has captured on g5 first, Black no longer has the ability to keep the kingside closed with ...g6-g5 because the other pawn is blocking it.} b4 {[%csl Rc3]} 10. Nd5 e6 {[%csl Rd5]} 11. Nxb4 g4 $1 (11... c5 $4 12. dxc5 $18 {is not the way to go, as we will see in the example games.}) 12. hxg6 Rxh1 13. Bxh1 {[%cal Gd1g4,Bg1e2]} Qh4 $1 {[%csl Rh1] Everything else allows White to consolidate the position with Ne2 or Qxg4.} 14. Bg2 Nd7 15. Qe2 $13 {White is better in this double-edged position, but care should be taken by both sides.After this move, we are finally prepared to take on f7 against everything except for ...Qh2.} ({If Black plays} 15. Qe2 Qh2 {, we should hold off on gxf7+ since it gives Black an extra tempo to open up the f-file towards the king, which will have to go to f1 to defend the bishop.Otherwise, we can castle queenside and argue that our pawn structure is superior and Black's king doesn't have a home.}) *

