1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 {The second move sides are covered briefly – they are rare and generally transpose back to the mainlines anyways.} 3. Nc3 g6 ({▷} 3... Nbd7 $6 {attempts to reach a Black Lion via this move order, but as we will see, it won't work as intended.}) ({▷} 3... c6 $6 {is the 'small center' system. Potentially ...Qa5 is an idea. However, these lines give White too much of a free hand in the center and are generally not too great.}) ({▷} 3... e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 {is the Black Lion defense. This might be an exception to the animal name rule, since it lives up to its name – the Black Lion is certainly a ferocious opening, where there will be complications. White needs to be energetic to prove an advantage here.}) 4. f4 {The Austrian Attack, but with a twist:} Bg7 5. Bd3 $5 {The old mainlines with the crazy attacking plan of e4-e5 and h2-h4, h4-h5 have been refuted. This is the most accurate move order, since Nf3 allows Black the additional option of playing ...c7-c5, and the Bb5+ line is also fine for Black.  Since the direct tries don't quite work out, White should approach this with a bit of subtlety.} O-O {This leads back to the Austrian with Bd3, but we have avoided ...c7-c5.} (5... Nc6 {is the other main try, the idea being to try and get} 6. Nf3 Bg4 {in instead of castling. Besides these two moves, Black has a few other tries as well, which I have covered here.}) *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
