1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 c6 4. f4 Qa5 5. Bd3 e5 6. Nf3 exd4 7. Nxd4 g6 8. O-O Bg7 9. Kh1 O-O 10. f5 Nbd7 11. Qe1 {Follow the line here.} Nc5 $2 ({Black cannot afford to dilly-dally on the queenside while White's pieces are breathing down the king's neck. Black needs} 11... Ne5 {to bring more defenders towards his majesty.}) 12. Qh4 {[%csl Ra5,Rc5][%cal Gc1h6,Gd4f3,Gf3g5] With the Black pieces offside, White prepares the 'deathstar' setup with Bh6 and Nf3/g5, very similar to the system employed in the Grand Prix attack.} Qb4 13. Nf3 Nxd3 14. cxd3 Nd7 15. Bh6 f6 16. Bxg7 Kxg7 17. d4 Nb6 18. Rae1 Bd7 19. fxg6 hxg6 20. Qg3 Rae8 21. Ne2 Rh8 22. Nf4 Rh6 23. e5 fxe5 24. dxe5 dxe5 25. Nxe5 Qd6 26. Qc3 $2 (26. Nxd7 $1 Qxd7 27. Rxe8 Qxe8 28. Re1 $18 {wins on the spot. Black is no shape to defend their king, and mate will soon follow after Ne6+.}) 26... Na4 27. Nc4+ Nxc3 28. Nxd6 Rxe1 29. Rxe1 Kf8 30. Nxb7 $4 ({Leinier must've miscounted the material here, because this one allows Black to equalize the pawns} 30. bxc3 $1 g5 31. Nxb7 gxf4 {leaves White up a pawn and still with the win in grasp.}) 30... Nxa2 31. Nc5 Bf5 32. Ra1 Nb4 33. Ra4 Nd5 34. Nxd5 cxd5 35. Rxa7 Rh4 36. Ra4 Rxa4 37. Nxa4 Bc2 38. Nb6 Bb3 $2 (38... d4 $1 {seems obvious enough. Keep the pawn on a dark square, then bring the king to the center.}) 39. Nd7+ Ke7 40. Ne5 Bc2 41. Kg1 Kd6 42. Nf3 Kc5 43. Kf2 d4 44. Nd2 Ba4 45. g4 Bc6 46. Kg3 Bb5 47. Kf4 Be2 48. h3 Kd5 49. h4 Ke6 50. Kg5 Kf7 51. Nb3 d3 52. Nd2 Bd1 53. Kf4 Ke6 54. Nf3 Kd5 55. Ke3 Kc4 56. Ne5+ Kd5 57. Nxg6 Bxg4 58. Nf4+ Kc4 59. Nxd3 Kb3 60. Kf4 Bd1 61. Kg5 Bc2 62. Ne5 Bd1 63. Ng4 Kxb2 64. h5 Kc3 65. h6 Bc2 66. Ne5 Bh7 67. Kf6 Kd4 68. Kg7 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
