[White "Pirc: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Emms, John vs. Pein"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B07"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "49"]
[GameId "2288537761493348"]

1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. Be3 Bg7 5. Qd2 a6 6. Bh6 {Train the line here.} O-O 7. O-O-O b5 8. f3 Nc6 9. h4 e5 10. d5 Ne7 {So far, so good. White should have a winning attack if everything goes according to plan.} 11. Nge2 $2 {A natural move, but an instructive mistake. The way White played this game was very intuitive, but understanding why the natural moves aren't correct is important.The reason we want to avoid Nge2 is that we actually need this square for the other knight in case Black goes ...b5-b4.The g1-knight can be developed to g5 via h3 instead, or we can move Nce2-g3 and then N1e2. Just about every other move was good here, as long as it didn't commit the knight.} ({▷} 11. h5 {is best, sacrificing the pawn to open up the file, but...}) ({▷} 11. g4) ({▷} 11. Bd3) ({▷} 11. Kb1 {...are all fine as well.}) 11... Bxh6 12. Qxh6 Kh8 13. Qd2 $2 {White wants to bring the queen back so that g2-g4, g4-g5 can be played without trapping the queen. But this is actually the wrong plan! White should be rushing to push the h-pawn instead. Furthermore, Black gave us an opportunity to do something about ...b5-b4.} (13. Ng3 $1 {is a strong utility move, clearing the e2-square for the other knight and preparing h4-h5. Now Black has to retreat one of the knights back to g8 to remove the queen.}) 13... h5 $1 {Now h4-h5 is physically impossible.} 14. g4 Kg7 $2 (14... b4 {might as well flick this in.} 15. Nb1 hxg4 $1 {No fear! There is no mate.} 16. h5 g5 $1 17. Qxg5 Nh7 18. Qe3 f5 {To use the words of Josiah Stearman, 'Black is taking over'.}) 15. g5 Nd7 16. f4 {Now White is back in business.} exf4 17. Nxf4 Ne5 18. Nd3 Bg4 19. Be2 Nc4 20. Qf4 b4 21. Nxb4 Bxe2 22. Nxe2 Nxb2 {Desperation. Black shortly threw in the towel.} 23. Qf6+ Kg8 24. Qxb2 Rb8 25. Qc3 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Pirc: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Lagarde, Maxime vs. Barbot"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C41"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "49"]
[GameId "2288537761497445"]

1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 c6 6. g4 {To train this line, you can go here.} g6 7. g5 Nh5 8. Be3 b5 9. a4 $2 (9. a3 {like I recommended is better, White should not invite ...b5-b4.}) 9... b4 10. Ne2 Bg7 11. Qd2 O-O 12. dxe5 Nxe5 (12... c5 $3 {was the way to equalize, denying the use of the d4-square. Either capture on d6 fails tactically... ▷} 13. exd6 ({▷} 13. Qxd6 $4 Nxe5 {.}) 13... Bxb2 {.}) 13. Nxe5 Bxe5 14. O-O-O Qa5 15. b3 $1 {[%cal Gh8a1] The open long diagonal looks scary, but the queen cannot form a battery with the bishop, and even if it could, we always have Bd4 to contest the diagonal. What's more important is that the queenside remains closed.} Ba6 16. f4 Bg7 17. Ng3 Nxg3 18. hxg3 Rfe8 19. Bg2 $4 (19. Bxa6 Qxa6 20. Qxd6 Rxe4 21. Bd4 $18 {was clean, eliminating the counterplay. In the resulting heavy piece endgame. White's queen dominates because it is centralized. Black also has more structural weaknesses.}) 19... Rad8 20. Bf3 c5 (20... d5 21. e5 d4 $13 {The queen's path to h2 becomes interrupted.}) 21. Rh1 Bb7 22. f5 Bxe4 23. Bxe4 Rxe4 24. f6 Bf8 25. Rxh7 $1 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Pirc: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Shankland, Samuel vs. Pancevski"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C41"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "55"]
[GameId "2288537761501542"]

1. d4 {To conclude the illustrative games section, I'd like to show an example from my own tournament practice.} d6 2. e4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 exd4 5. Nxd4 Be7 6. Bf4 O-O 7. Qd2 {You can train this position here.} c6 8. O-O-O {I always remember this game as an example of why Black should avoid this line. The pressure in this setup is very unpleasant. White's active and centralized pieces make it hard for Black to maneuver.} b5 9. f3 b4 10. Na4 {The c6-pawn hanging will make it a challenge to develop the b8-knight. Perhaps Pancevski should've just bit the bullet and played...} Bd7 11. b3 $1 {[%csl Gb3] Again we see this b3-idea. It's more important to secure the queenside than the potential holes this creates, which cannot be effectively exploited by Black.} d5 (11... c5 12. Nb5 Qa5 13. Nxd6 $1 {We see the point of b2-b3: my knight is not loose here.} Bxa4 14. bxa4 Qxa4 15. Bc4 $18 {Material is equal, but piece activity most definitely is not.}) 12. e5 {Not the best understanding on my part. This releases a lot of the pressure against his position. ...dxe4 is never really anything to worry about, since it gives up the c4-square and also opens up the d-file against the vulnerable d7-bishop and queen behind it.} ({Instead, I should have just continued with my kingside play with} 12. g4 $1 dxe4 13. fxe4 $18 {The pawns are really irrelevant here; what matters is that lines are opening up, and clearly this does not benefit Black.} Bxg4 14. Be2 {Black will not enjoy the open d- and g-files.}) 12... Nh5 13. Be3 {What's the move we really want to play?} g6 $2 {Definitely not this!} (13... c5 $1 {Shankland rule.} 14. Nxc5 Bxc5 15. g4 {This was my plan and I thought White vis much better, but it turns out I was overestimating my chances.} Nf6 16. exf6 Qxf6 17. h4 {White is a little more comfortable, but the game goes on. This might not be fun for Black, but it is a lot better than what he ended up getting.}) 14. g4 Ng7 {The fianchettoed knight is not a great defensive piece!} 15. Ne2 $1 {Nice and simple, prophylaxis against ...c6-c5. Next up, I can tear open the kingside.} (15. h4 {The computer is unfazed and wants to smash right away, but I was not sure about allowing the ...c6-c5 and ...d5-d4 advances.} c5 16. Nb5 d4 17. Bh6 Bc6 {...Nd7-b6 or ...Qd7 can come soon, and this did not look so clean to me.}) 15... Qa5 16. h4 c5 17. Nf4 $1 d4 (17... Bxa4 18. Nxd5 {.}) 18. Nd5 $1 {This is totally gone.} dxe3 ({▷} 18... Nc6 19. Bg5 $18) ({▷} 18... Bd8 19. Bg5 Bxa4 20. Bf6 $1 {everything wins, but this one was really nice, with Qh6 coming next. Unfortunately, I did not get to play it.}) 19. Nxe7+ Kh8 20. Qd6 {[%csl Rb8][%cal Ge7g6,Rd6f8] The poor knight still can't get out!} Re8 21. h5 $1 {Black's king will not survive the coming onslaught.} gxh5 22. Qh6 c4 23. Nd5 Bxg4 24. fxg4 Nd7 25. Nf6 Nf8 26. Rd5 Qc7 27. gxh5 cxb3 28. axb3 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Pirc: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Shankland, Samuel vs. Gukesh"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C41"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "123"]
[GameId "2288537761501543"]

1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {My first time getting to test out the Rg1-idea I found while writing this course came against none other than the current world champion. And it worked like a charm.} c6 6. g4 h6 7. Be3 b5 8. a3 Qc7 9. Qd2 (9. g5 {This was the best move and the one I recommend in the course, though the one I played is not bad either.I had not expected the Philidor from Gukesh, and did not review it before the game. But, even with my imperfect memory, I was able to get a huge position.}) 9... exd4 $2 {This was too optimistic.} (9... Be7 {Black should have preferred a slower developing move. They are undoubtedly worse, but the game goes on. For example:} 10. g5 $1 hxg5 11. Nxg5 Rxh2 12. O-O-O {White has a very powerful initiative, and can look for Be2 next with Rh1 and/or f2-f4 to follow. But at least Black has a pawn for their suffering.}) 10. Nxd4 $1 Ne5 {I suspect what happened here is that Gukesh saw that he was winning a pawn on g4. If I defend it, I will walk into ...c6-c5, followed by Nf3. But White has a number of ways to grow their initiative, and I found the best one.} 11. f4 $18 {[%csl Re5][%cal Ge4e5] Eleven moves into the game, and I had the World Champion dead to rights. I daresay this was a successful debut for the Rg1-idea!} Nc4 {A sad necessity.} (11... Nexg4 12. Nxc6 $1 {Well spotted. The machine claims White is also winning with O-O-O, but this is not obvious at first glance, and energetic play will be needed to prove it. Here, I equalize material right away, b5 is loose, and Black's position is a trainwreck as early as move 12.}) 12. Bxc4 bxc4 13. Nf5 {In human terms, I think this is a very good decision. I won't have to give up any material, and Bd4 with O-O-O next looks very convincing. Black can hardly complete their development.} (13. O-O-O {There was nothing wrong with castling, but I was not in the mood to give up g4 unnecessarily.}) 13... Rb8 (13... h5 14. g5 $1 Nxe4 15. Nxg7+ $1 {This resource immediately ends the discussion, and I had to see it before going Nf5.} Bxg7 16. Nxe4 {, O-O-O and Bd4 will end the game very quickly.}) 14. Bd4 Be6 15. e5 $1 {The most direct.} dxe5 16. Bxe5 Qb6 17. Bd4 Qa5 18. O-O-O $1 {This one took some bravery since it allows ...Rxb2, but I trusted my calculation. White crashes through in the center first.} Rxb2 19. Bxf6 $1 (19. Kxb2 $2 Bxa3+ {This would quickly see the tables turn.}) 19... gxf6 20. Rge1 $1 {Qd7 is not a small threat.} Rb7 21. Nd6+ $1 Bxd6 22. Qxd6 ({Black's king is stuck in the center, ...Qxa3 has been prevented,} 22. Qxd6 Qxc3 {is met with mate, and 23. f5 is not goofing around.}) 22... Qb6 ({A calm move like} 22... Qb6 23. Rd2 {was surely good enough here, but once more, I trusted my calculation.}) (22... Qc7 23. f5 $18 {When in doubt, keep things simple. White easily wins with an extra piece.}) 23. Rxe6+ $1 fxe6 24. Qxe6+ Re7 (24... Kf8 {Now White wins directly.} 25. Qxf6+ Ke8 (25... Kg8 26. Rd8+) 26. Qxh8+) 25. Qc8+ Kf7 26. Qxh8 Qe3+ 27. Rd2 $1 Qxf4 {Now I have to be careful not to allow ...Re1+ Nd1 and ...Rxd1, with mate on f1. But if I don't miss that, it's all over.} (27... Qxc3 28. Qh7+ $1 {This is the only move not to lose, but it also mates in 4.} Ke6 {I did not actually see Qg8-c8 with mate, but Re2 is quite enough.} (28... Kf8 29. Rd8+) (28... Ke8 29. Qg8#) 29. Re2+ {Winning the rook, and the game.}) 28. Qh7+ Kf8 29. Qf5 Re1+ 30. Nd1 Qxf5 31. gxf5 {The rest was easy. A piece is a piece.} Ke7 32. Kb2 Re5 33. Rf2 Kd6 34. Nc3 Re3 35. Rf4 Ke5 36. Rh4 Kxf5 37. Rxh6 Kg5 38. Rh7 a5 39. Rg7+ Kh5 40. Rg3 Re5 41. Nd1 f5 42. Kc3 f4 43. Rg8 Re2 44. h3 Re1 45. Kd2 Rh1 46. Nf2 Rf1 47. Ne4 Ra1 48. Rf8 Rxa3 49. Rxf4 Rxh3 50. Nc3 Rh2+ 51. Kc1 Rh4 52. Rf5+ Kg4 53. Rxa5 Kf3 54. Re5 Rd4 55. Re6 Rh4 56. Kb2 Rg4 57. Rxc6 Ke3 58. Rd6 Rg8 59. Nd5+ Ke2 60. Kc3 Rg4 61. Re6+ Kf2 62. Nb6 1-0

