1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nd7 {The most popular independent try, but this is bad because it allows...} 3. f4 $1 {[%csl Yb1,Gb2,Gc3,Gd4,Ge5,Gf4][%cal Ge4e5,Gc2c3,Gc7c5] Taking control over e5 and indirectly preventing ...Nf6. Unlike the mainlines where the knight is on c3, we retain the option to solidify the center in response to ...c7-c5 with c2-c3 once the pawn reaches e5. In general, we should only play e4-e5 if Black plays ...Nf6.} e5 {Unavoidable, since otherwise the development can't be completed.} 4. Nc3 {Now that e5 has been committed to, we can play Nc3 without fearing ...c7-c5. The problem with committing the knight to d7 too early for Black is that we can now recapture on d4 with the queen and not worry about ...Nc6.} exd4 5. Qxd4 {[%cal Rb8c6]} Ngf6 6. Be3 g6 7. O-O-O $14 {[%csl Gd4] We have a slightly bigger advantage than usual, thanks to being able to recapture on d4 with the queen.} *

