1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} c6 {[%cal Gd8a5,Ga5e5,Ge7e5,Rf6e4,Ra5e1] Black wants to be tricky by playing ...Qa5 and pinning the knight, while simultaneously supporting the e5-pawn break.} 4. f4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4,Gf4] Grabbing as much space as possible. Note that the common way to play against the Pirc in many different lines is to increase the space advantage as much as possible, whether it's through f2-f4, g2-g4, or a2-a4. Space creates space.} Qa5 {The only reason to play ...c7-c6; other moves transpose into lousy versions of other Pirc lines.} 5. Bd3 {[%cal Gc1e3]} ({The better way to defend the e4-pawn.} 5. Bd2 $6 {leaves the d4-pawn and later the knight that will recapture this pawn more vulnerable. That bishop belongs on e3.}) (5. e5 $6 {is unnecessary. We should try and hold the space we have. A common misconception people have is that advanced pawns = aggressive play. In fact, often it's quite the opposite: space is a long-term advantage, and recklessly throwing the pawns against the opponent can leave weaknesses. Instead, the player with more space should try and consolidate the space they already control, while methodically increasing the space advantage slowly until it becomes overwhelming.}) 5... d5 $6 6. e5 Ne4 {In for a penny, in for a pound.} 7. Bxe4 dxe4 8. Bd2 {[%csl Re4] In the long run, the e4-pawn is a goner. Black will have to pull a ton of strings just to not be down a pawn.} Qb6 9. Nge2 Bf5 10. Be3 $16 {[%csl Re4][%cal Ge2g3] Someday Ng3 is going to happen and win the e4-pawn back. Black can take the b2-pawn in exchange, but that will leave White with a gigantic space advantage that should be decisive.} *

