1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} c6 {[%cal Gd8a5,Ga5e5,Ge7e5,Rf6e4,Ra5e1] Black wants to be tricky by playing ...Qa5 and pinning the knight, while simultaneously supporting the e5-pawn break.} 4. f4 {[%csl Gd4,Ge4,Gf4] Grabbing as much space as possible. Note that the common way to play against the Pirc in many different lines is to increase the space advantage as much as possible, whether it's through f2-f4, g2-g4, or a2-a4. Space creates space.} Qa5 {The only reason to play ...c7-c6; other moves transpose into lousy versions of other Pirc lines.} 5. Bd3 {[%cal Gc1e3]} ({The better way to defend the e4-pawn.} 5. Bd2 $6 {leaves the d4-pawn and later the knight that will recapture this pawn more vulnerable. That bishop belongs on e3.}) (5. e5 $6 {is unnecessary. We should try and hold the space we have. A common misconception people have is that advanced pawns = aggressive play. In fact, often it's quite the opposite: space is a long-term advantage, and recklessly throwing the pawns against the opponent can leave weaknesses. Instead, the player with more space should try and consolidate the space they already control, while methodically increasing the space advantage slowly until it becomes overwhelming.}) 5... e5 {The most logical follow-up.} 6. Nf3 exd4 {This is gradually becoming the main move.} 7. Nxd4 g6 8. O-O Bg7 9. Kh1 $1 {[%cal Ra7g1] Take it slow! Getting off the g1-a7 diagonal and avoiding any pin.} O-O 10. f5 Nbd7 11. Qe1 Ng4 {[%csl Rd4]} 12. Qh4 $1 {[%csl Rg4] Counterattacking the knight. This trade will stop us from losing time to retreat the knight.} Bxd4 (12... Nge5 13. Be2 {transposes to the ...Nbd7-line where Black plays ...Nfd7. Make sure not to blunder ...Nxd3!}) 13. Qxg4 {[%cal Ge4e5] We are threatening e4-e5!, forcing Black to occupy the e5-square with a piece that's not the knight, and clearing the e4-square and b1-h7 diagonal for our bishop. Black should retreat the bishop so that the bishop is not hanging after e4-e5.} Bg7 14. Bg5 {[%csl Rd6,Rf8][%cal Gg5e7] → While we have given up the e5-square, the f5-pawn cramps the bishop on c8, while the further advance of f5-f6 is always hanging over Black's head. White's setup is extremely solid, and the control over the center is strong, meaning we can largely play around the e5-knight. For instance, we can bring the queen to g3 to cut out some possible knight jumps, while also pressuring the d6-pawn.} *

