1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} Nbd7 {Black is hoping for us to play Nf3 so they can transpose to the Black Lion. But we won't let that happen!} 4. g4 $1 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gg4g5,Rf6d7,Yf1g2,Yg1e2,Ye2g3] White takes advantage of the obstructed black bishop to gain space on the kingside. The g-pawn comes to give the black knight a kick.In the future, the white bishop can develop to g2 and the white knight can develop to e2, then g3, which will strengthen White's control over the kingside.} e5 $2 5. g5 {[%csl Rf6]} Ng8 6. Be3 $16 {[%csl Rf8,Rg8] Leaves Black without a good way to complete their development, as the bishop and knight will be fighting over the e7-square. White's advantage should already be decisive.} *

