1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} Nbd7 {Black is hoping for us to play Nf3 so they can transpose to the Black Lion. But we won't let that happen!} 4. g4 $1 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gg4g5,Rf6d7,Yf1g2,Yg1e2,Ye2g3] White takes advantage of the obstructed black bishop to gain space on the kingside. The g-pawn comes to give the black knight a kick.In the future, the white bishop can develop to g2 and the white knight can develop to e2, then g3, which will strengthen White's control over the kingside.} h6 $7 {If Black plays any move that allows us to play g4-g5 and boot the knight back to g8, white will gain a decisive space advantage. The other knight's development to d7 prevents the f6-knight from using that square as an escape path.} 5. h3 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Gg1e2,Rd1g4,Yh2h3,Yh3g4,Gg1f3] Now that Black had to waste a move stopping g4-g5, we can now focus on consolidating the center and kingside space. The main purpose of this move is to allow the knight to develop without interrupting the queen's defense of the g4-pawn. We know that Black's plan revolves around ...c7-c6 and ...b7-b5, which will limit the scope of the bishop along the f1-a6 diagonal, which is why this bishop will be better placed on g2.} c6 $5 6. f4 e5 {Black is forced to go for this, as otherwise ...e7-e5 is going to run them over.} 7. Nf3 {If Black does nothing to disturb us, we are going to want to stick the bishop on d3 and queen on e2.} Be7 (7... exd4 $2 8. Nxd4 $18 {Black should wait for White to clarify the position of their pieces before initiating this exchange.}) 8. Bd3 {Black prevented us from taking twice on e5, so we proceed with our development scheme. Now that the bishop is on d3, it makes more sense to take on d4, since Black now has ...Nc5, and the knight on d4 is unprotected.} exd4 9. Nxd4 Nc5 {The move order isn't so important, but sooner or later, Black has to go take the bishop to trade some pieces off and get rid of the bishop pair. One thing Black needs to ensure is that we don't get to castle queenside; if we can do so successfully, it's very obvious that our attack is crashing through first, especially with the hook on h6.} 10. Qe2 Qb6 {We respond to with...} 11. Nb3 Nxd3+ {...and this time, since the knight already had to move off of d4, it's more beneficial to play...} 12. cxd3 $14 {Since our king is a bit weaker, we should castle short instead. Later on, we can reroute this b3-knight around to f3 or c4 via d2.} *

