1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} Nbd7 {Black is hoping for us to play Nf3 so they can transpose to the Black Lion. But we won't let that happen!} 4. g4 $1 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gg4g5,Rf6d7,Yf1g2,Yg1e2,Ye2g3] White takes advantage of the obstructed black bishop to gain space on the kingside. The g-pawn comes to give the black knight a kick.In the future, the white bishop can develop to g2 and the white knight can develop to e2, then g3, which will strengthen White's control over the kingside.} h6 $7 {If Black plays any move that allows us to play g4-g5 and boot the knight back to g8, white will gain a decisive space advantage. The other knight's development to d7 prevents the f6-knight from using that square as an escape path.} 5. h3 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Gg1e2,Rd1g4,Yh2h3,Yh3g4,Gg1f3] Now that Black had to waste a move stopping g4-g5, we can now focus on consolidating the center and kingside space. The main purpose of this move is to allow the knight to develop without interrupting the queen's defense of the g4-pawn. We know that Black's plan revolves around ...c7-c6 and ...b7-b5, which will limit the scope of the bishop along the f1-a6 diagonal, which is why this bishop will be better placed on g2.} e5 6. Nge2 Be7 7. Ng3 O-O {[%cal Gd4h8,Gd4a7]} (7... exd4 $2 {A big error by Black.} 8. Qxd4 $16 {A common misconception is that you should not recapture in the center with your queen. But the queen on d4 is only an issue if Black can play ...Nc6 or ...Bc5 to attack it; otherwise, like every other piece, the queen is more powerful in the center than on the rim. From here, the queen exerts influence everywhere on the board.}) 8. Be3 d5 $1 {Black must break free before they are crushed beneath the weight of White's massive space advantage.} (8... c6 $6 {too slow} 9. d5 $1 $16 {cementing the space advantage. If Black tries to increase the pressure with ...Nb6, we can run the a-pawn to harass the knight.}) *

