1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} Nbd7 {Black is hoping for us to play Nf3 so they can transpose to the Black Lion. But we won't let that happen!} 4. g4 $1 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gg4g5,Rf6d7,Yf1g2,Yg1e2,Ye2g3] White takes advantage of the obstructed black bishop to gain space on the kingside. The g-pawn comes to give the black knight a kick.In the future, the white bishop can develop to g2 and the white knight can develop to e2, then g3, which will strengthen White's control over the kingside.} h6 $7 {If Black plays any move that allows us to play g4-g5 and boot the knight back to g8, white will gain a decisive space advantage. The other knight's development to d7 prevents the f6-knight from using that square as an escape path.} 5. h3 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Gg1e2,Rd1g4,Yh2h3,Yh3g4,Gg1f3] Now that Black had to waste a move stopping g4-g5, we can now focus on consolidating the center and kingside space. The main purpose of this move is to allow the knight to develop without interrupting the queen's defense of the g4-pawn. We know that Black's plan revolves around ...c7-c6 and ...b7-b5, which will limit the scope of the bishop along the f1-a6 diagonal, which is why this bishop will be better placed on g2.} e5 6. Nge2 c6 7. Be3 b5 (7... Be7 8. Ng3 O-O {transposes to 6...Be7.}) 8. a3 a5 {...is probably the most direct. Black is going to slam b4 and immediately cause problems, however, we can strike first with:} 9. d5 {I hope you're noticing a theme here: we are waiting for our opponent to do something on the side before taking action in the center, so that when the position opens up, those advances end up becoming weaknesses.} ({In this case, it is the b5-pawn that has become fatally weakened by the a5-advance. Black cannot Shankland rule this:} 9. d5 b4 {loses a pawn after} 10. dxc6 {.}) 9... cxd5 10. Nxd5 {Both captures are good, but this one is more straightforward.} Nxd5 ({▷} 10... Nxe4 $2 {cannot work. Black is getting killed on the diagonal.} 11. Bg2 Nec5 {Probably the best try. Now Nc7+ is an advantage, but we can do better:} 12. Nec3 Rb8 13. b4 $1 Na4 14. Nxa4 bxa4 15. c4 {White will recoup the pawn with an overwhelming positional advantage.}) ({▷} 10... Rb8 11. Ng3 {transposes to the main line, as Black has nothing better than ...Nxd5 here.}) 11. Qxd5 Rb8 12. Ng3 $16 {A very bad version of the Najdorf structure. White has many weaknesses to work against.} *

