1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} Nbd7 {Black is hoping for us to play Nf3 so they can transpose to the Black Lion. But we won't let that happen!} 4. g4 $1 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gg4g5,Rf6d7,Yf1g2,Yg1e2,Ye2g3] White takes advantage of the obstructed black bishop to gain space on the kingside. The g-pawn comes to give the black knight a kick.In the future, the white bishop can develop to g2 and the white knight can develop to e2, then g3, which will strengthen White's control over the kingside.} h6 $7 {If Black plays any move that allows us to play g4-g5 and boot the knight back to g8, white will gain a decisive space advantage. The other knight's development to d7 prevents the f6-knight from using that square as an escape path.} 5. h3 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Gg1e2,Rd1g4,Yh2h3,Yh3g4,Gg1f3] Now that Black had to waste a move stopping g4-g5, we can now focus on consolidating the center and kingside space. The main purpose of this move is to allow the knight to develop without interrupting the queen's defense of the g4-pawn. We know that Black's plan revolves around ...c7-c6 and ...b7-b5, which will limit the scope of the bishop along the f1-a6 diagonal, which is why this bishop will be better placed on g2.} e5 6. Nge2 c6 7. Be3 b5 (7... Be7 8. Ng3 O-O {transposes to 6...Be7.}) 8. a3 {Our typical reaction to ...b7-b5. Now Black has to choose between a few different plans.} Be7 {Probably the best way to go. Black is rushing to castle and reorganizing with ...Re8/Bf8, after which they will be totally fine. Therefore, we need to try to disrupt this plan.} 9. Ng3 exd4 {Since we have not committed to Nf5 yet, and thus e4 is still defended well, we can recapture with the bishop instead.} ({If we recapture with the queen,} 9... exd4 10. Qxd4 {, then Black doesn't necessarily have to castle,} O-O {, and transpose to the mainline; there are other options, such as} (10... a5 {.})) 10. Bxd4 O-O 11. h4 {The queen still being on d1 means that we can now go for this h3-h4, g4-g5 plan.} c5 {Else Black is crushed on the kingside.} 12. Be3 Ne5 13. g5 Nfg4 14. Nd5 $14 {The position is sharp, and White should play accurately to keep the position under control.The key things that are giving us the advantage are control over the light squares f5 and d5, and this pawn wedge on g5. If we can maintain these, then we should be better.} (14. Nd5 Nxe3 {is not a big concern, as our bishop isn't that strong anymore: it's served its purpose in supporting g5, and we can allow it to be traded off.}) *

