1. e4 d6 2. d4 {There is a lot of overlap between the 2nd move Pirc sides with other lines, with many transpositions. As a result, there's not too much to cover here.} Nf6 {[%csl Gc4,Gd4,Ge4]} ({▷} 2... g6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Modern chapter.}) ({▷} 2... e5 3. Nf3 {transposes to the Philidor, which will be covered in a future course.}) ({▷} 2... e6 3. Nf3 {is one move order to reach the Hedgehog, which is covered in its own file.}) ({▷} 2... c6 3. c4 $16 {taking all the space.}) ({▷} 2... Nc6 $6 {transposes to 2...d6 in the Nimzowitsch chapter.}) 3. Nc3 {Now the main moves, by far, are e5 and g6, both of which are covered in their own sections. The present chapter only addresses Black's sidelines.} Nbd7 {Black is hoping for us to play Nf3 so they can transpose to the Black Lion. But we won't let that happen!} 4. g4 $1 {[%csl Rd7][%cal Gg4g5,Rf6d7,Yf1g2,Yg1e2,Ye2g3] White takes advantage of the obstructed black bishop to gain space on the kingside. The g-pawn comes to give the black knight a kick.In the future, the white bishop can develop to g2 and the white knight can develop to e2, then g3, which will strengthen White's control over the kingside.} h6 $7 {If Black plays any move that allows us to play g4-g5 and boot the knight back to g8, white will gain a decisive space advantage. The other knight's development to d7 prevents the f6-knight from using that square as an escape path.} 5. h3 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Gg1e2,Rd1g4,Yh2h3,Yh3g4,Gg1f3] Now that Black had to waste a move stopping g4-g5, we can now focus on consolidating the center and kingside space. The main purpose of this move is to allow the knight to develop without interrupting the queen's defense of the g4-pawn. We know that Black's plan revolves around ...c7-c6 and ...b7-b5, which will limit the scope of the bishop along the f1-a6 diagonal, which is why this bishop will be better placed on g2.} e5 6. Nge2 c6 7. Be3 b5 (7... Be7 8. Ng3 O-O {transposes to 6...Be7.}) 8. a3 {Our typical reaction to ...b7-b5. Now Black has to choose between a few different plans.} Be7 {Probably the best way to go. Black is rushing to castle and reorganizing with ...Re8/Bf8, after which they will be totally fine. Therefore, we need to try to disrupt this plan.} 9. Ng3 {In some lines, we might take on e7, but the knight on f5 itself is very powerful.} O-O {Black should continue along with the plan of castling as soon as possible.} ({▷} 9... Nb6 {tries to cover f5 with the bishop; however, the opening of the a4-e8 diagonal gives us the perfect opportunity to play} 10. d5 $16 {Taking on d5 now loses a pawn, so Black is forced to allow dxc6, after which we will have a fantastic Najdorf structure.}) ({▷} 9... g6 $6 10. Qd2 $16 {This is way too weakening. The h6-pawn will be a permanent liability.}) 10. Nf5 {With the knight here, White is threatening ideas with Rg1 and g4-g5, so Black needs to act quickly to avoid being flattened like a pancake.} exd4 ({▷} 10... d5 $6 {can be ignored.} 11. Bg2 Nxe4 12. Nxe4 dxe4 13. d5 cxd5 14. Qxd5 $16 {Black's pieces were not ready for the opening of the center. White will get the e4-pawn back, and various sacrifices on h6 are in the air.}) ({▷} 10... Re8 $2 {wrong order.} 11. d5 $1 {Re8 is now a wasted move, since the e-file will stay closed. Unfortunately, after this slip-up, g4-g5 is going to be inevitable when the kingside gets blown open.}) 11. Qxd4 c5 {There is no time to waste!} 12. Qd1 Bb7 13. Bg2 b4 14. Nd5 Nxd5 15. exd5 $14 {Black successfully avoided a catastrophe, but White still has positional advantages. The position is very comfortable, especially with the strong knight on f5, which cannot be removed.} *

