[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "15"]
[GameId "2288537761620352"]

1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} h5 $5 {Arguing that Rg1 is more detrimental to White's position than ...h7-h5 is to Black's, which has some merit. This is an interesting idea recommended by Maxime Lagarde.} 6. h3 c6 7. Be3 b5 8. a3 $14 {[%csl Gg4][%cal Gg2g4] We have a more standard Pirc position with Rg1 and ...h7-h5 included. Eventually, though, we can still play g2-g4 and claim the edge.} *

