1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} Be7 6. g4 c6 7. g5 Nh5 8. Be3 {[%csl Re5][%cal Rf3e5]} (8. Be3 Z0 9. Nxe5 {is a big threat.}) 8... g6 {[%csl Gh5][%cal Gg6h5]} 9. Qd2 b5 10. a3 O-O 11. h4 $16 {[%csl Ra7,Rb5,Rc6][%cal Gd4d5,Ge1c1] With this last move, White tightens the position and is ready for offensive action. White's main idea is to continue with d4-d5, which will expose the weaknesses on Black's queenside.} *

