1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} exd4 $6 {Waiting for Rg1 is actually worse for Black, as the black knight should go to c6, while White is intending to castle with the other rook anyway.} 6. Nxd4 g6 7. Bg5 $1 {Provoking ...h7-h6, since Black has played ...g7-g6.} h6 {[%csl Rg5]} 8. Bf4 Bg7 9. Qd2 $16 {[%csl Rd7,Rh6][%cal Re8g8] The opportunity cost of playing Rg1 is low, since we're going to castle on the other side anyways. On the other hand, the knight being on d7 and the pawn being on h6 are very unfavorable.} *

