1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} g6 {This move is a bit tricky, but if we know what we are doing, White ends up with a nice position.} 6. Be3 $1 (6. g4 {The automatic move is actually inaccurate.} exd4 $1 7. Nxd4 Bg7 {It's an issue that} 8. g5 {can be met with} Nxe4 {here. White might be better, but I find it less convincing and practical than the mainline with 6. Be3.}) 6... Bg7 7. dxe5 $1 dxe5 (7... Nxe5 8. Nxe5 dxe5 9. Qxd8+ Kxd8 10. O-O-O+ {The typical symmetrical endgame is really terrible for Black now because of the horribly placed bishop on g7. The plan is simple: Bc4, Rd3, Rgd1.}) 8. g4 O-O 9. Qd2 $14 {White is ready to castle long next, with a very pleasant position.} *

