1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} c6 6. g4 h6 {Ignoring the threat of g4-g5 will transpose into one of the earlier sidelines, but in the ...c7-c6 mainline, Black does play ...h7-h6 the majority of the time.} 7. Be3 b5 {[%cal Gb7b5,Ga2a3] Whenever ...b7-b5 happens, we respond with a2-a3.} 8. a3 Qc7 9. g5 {[%csl Rf6]} hxg5 10. Nxg5 $14 {[%cal Gd4d5,Gf2f4] The knight can't be kicked out of g5. For the most part, the h2-pawn is inconsequential; it's more important to prioritize getting castled and busting open the center with f2-f4 or d4-d5.} *

