1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} c6 6. g4 h6 {Ignoring the threat of g4-g5 will transpose into one of the earlier sidelines, but in the ...c7-c6 mainline, Black does play ...h7-h6 the majority of the time.} 7. Be3 Qa5 8. Nd2 $1 {[%cal Gd2b3,Gd2c4] Typical response to ...Qa5 ideas: we unpin the knight on c3, while also preparing Nb3/Nc4 ideas.} Be7 9. d5 $1 cxd5 10. Nb3 Qd8 11. exd5 $14 {We have a structure resembling the Najdorf, but with a few key differences: namely, Black hasn't played ...a7-a6 yet and will have to burn a tempo to do that, while we have already achieved g2-g4, something that is usually prevented by ...h7-h5 in the Najdorf.} *

