1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} c6 6. g4 h6 {Ignoring the threat of g4-g5 will transpose into one of the earlier sidelines, but in the ...c7-c6 mainline, Black does play ...h7-h6 the majority of the time.} 7. Be3 Be7 {I believe this is the most challenging option. Black simply develops the bishop and waits for White to do something first.} 8. a3 $1 {As is often the case, if our opponent is playing the waiting game... we play the waiting game too!White finds the most useful non-commital move just like Black has, and that move is a2-a3. Before, I said we play this move whenever Black goes ...b7-b5, but since Black is playing it slowly, we can take a time out to discourage ...b7-b5 since it now threatens nothing. Black should, from here on out, avoid playing ...b7-b5 for this reason.} ({For instance, after} 8. Qd2 exd4 9. Nxd4 {,} Ne5 $5 {now becomes an option. Now Black has a few options, and this time, how we respond is highly dependent on what they do. Switching the knight to g6 is Black's strongest idea.Here we have to understand that g4-g5 is the move we want to make work, but Black has annoying ideas connected with capturing, then playing ...Ng4 if we do it right now. Therefore, we either must wait for Black to do something to make ...Ng4 less strong, or prevent ...Ng4 directly before throwing g4-g5.}) 8... Qc7 $6 {[%csl Rg5][%cal Gd8c7,Rd8g5] This removes a defender of the g5-square, which thus makes ...Ng4 less effective since our knight is not hanging anymore. Therefore...} 9. g5 {[%csl Rf6]} hxg5 10. Nxg5 {[%cal Gf1c4] Something must be done about the looming threat of Bc4.} exd4 $6 (10... b5 11. f4 $16 {The position is complicated and the middlegame will be difficult to play for both sides, but White clearly has the objective upper hand here thanks to the space and progress already made on the kingside. One tip is that the computer is always finding a way to get the queen to g2 in many lines.}) 11. Bxd4 {[%cal Gf1c4]} Ne5 12. Bxe5 $1 dxe5 13. Bc4 $16 {[%cal Gd1f3,Ge1c1] White has given up the bishop pair, but the remaining bishop wakes up. Combined with the knight on g5 and the f7-pawn feeling the heat.The enemy king will likely be stuck in the center, since castling either side is not feasible due to tactical issues with the f7-pawn.} *

