1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} c6 6. g4 h6 {Ignoring the threat of g4-g5 will transpose into one of the earlier sidelines, but in the ...c7-c6 mainline, Black does play ...h7-h6 the majority of the time.} 7. Be3 Be7 {I believe this is the most challenging option. Black simply develops the bishop and waits for White to do something first.} 8. a3 $1 {As is often the case, if our opponent is playing the waiting game... we play the waiting game too!White finds the most useful non-commital move just like Black has, and that move is a2-a3. Before, I said we play this move whenever Black goes ...b7-b5, but since Black is playing it slowly, we can take a time out to discourage ...b7-b5 since it now threatens nothing. Black should, from here on out, avoid playing ...b7-b5 for this reason.} ({For instance, after} 8. Qd2 exd4 9. Nxd4 {,} Ne5 $5 {now becomes an option. Now Black has a few options, and this time, how we respond is highly dependent on what they do. Switching the knight to g6 is Black's strongest idea.Here we have to understand that g4-g5 is the move we want to make work, but Black has annoying ideas connected with capturing, then playing ...Ng4 if we do it right now. Therefore, we either must wait for Black to do something to make ...Ng4 less strong, or prevent ...Ng4 directly before throwing g4-g5.}) 8... Qa5 {This is a serious move. Black argues that we are wasting time preventing a move that hasn't been played and swiftly switches gears.However, Black is playing on razor-thin margins in this line and must be precise to attempt to equalize.} 9. Nd2 $1 {[%csl Ga5][%cal Gd2b3,Ra5e1,Gb3a5,Rf6e4] The typical reaction. Although Black has a slightly better version of it here.} exd4 (9... Nf8 10. h4 h5 $1 {This move was seen in the recent 2024 game Gupta-Kadric.}) 10. Nb3 {[%csl Ra5]} Qb6 $1 {[%csl Rb2][%cal Yb3d4,Gb6b2] As Lenderman puts it, 'disturbing the harmony'. We are prevented from recapturing with the knight. If the queen went somewhere else, Nxd4 leads to a good position for White.} 11. Bxd4 {[%csl Rb6]} c5 {[%csl Rd4]} 12. Be3 Ne5 13. Bb5+ Kf8 $7 {[%cal Ge1c1,Gf2f4,Gh2h4,Gg4g5] Black has to move the king. All the counterplay is coming from the hanging g4-pawn, which will no longer be hanging if Black allows their own harmony to be disturbed. In fact, Black is almost lost after any of the interpositions.} (13... Bd7 $4 14. Bxd7+ Nexd7 15. Qe2 $18 {for instance is totally lost.}) 14. Be2 {[%csl Gg4][%cal Ge2g4,Bf2f4] Protecting the pawn. We are ready for f2-f4 now.} g5 $7 {[%csl Rf4][%cal Gf2f4,Rg5f4]} (14... Be6 $2 15. f4 $36 {Walks straight into the advancing pawns. h2-h4/g4-g5 and f4-f5 are all serious ideas now.}) 15. Qd2 Bxg4 $7 {[%cal Gc3d5,Rf6d5] Again, 'only move'. If one of the knights takes the pawn, Nd5 will kill Black.} (15... Nexg4 16. Bxg4 Nxg4 17. Nd5 $16 {surrenders the d5-square for no reason; it's better to take the other way.}) 16. f4 Nf3+ $1 (16... gxf4 $4 17. Bxf4 {The bishop will enter on the h6-square with devastating effect.}) 17. Bxf3 Bxf3 18. fxg5 hxg5 19. Bxg5 $13 {[%cal Bh8h3] I'll stop this line here, but Black continues to have to find 'only moves' to maintain rough equality, the next one being ...Rh3!. If this is what Black signed up for, all the power to them!} *

