[PlyCount "45"]
[GameId "2288537761644937"]

1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 e5 4. Nf3 Nbd7 5. Rg1 {We have to approach this a bit differently than ...Nbd7 on the third move – since we already played Nf3, we cannot play g2-g4 directly.  Instead, we have to choose which way to prepare g2-g4: Rg1 or h2-h3. I believe Rg1 is slightly better, since the rook may become useful if the g-file opens up after a potential ...h7-h6. We give up castling rights on the kingside, but most of the time we wouldn't castle kingside anyway, so it's a very minor downside.In a lot of positions, we can combine g2-g4, g4-g5 with d4-d5, maximizing space gain.} c6 6. g4 h6 {Ignoring the threat of g4-g5 will transpose into one of the earlier sidelines, but in the ...c7-c6 mainline, Black does play ...h7-h6 the majority of the time.} 7. Be3 Be7 {I believe this is the most challenging option. Black simply develops the bishop and waits for White to do something first.} 8. a3 $1 {As is often the case, if our opponent is playing the waiting game... we play the waiting game too!White finds the most useful non-commital move just like Black has, and that move is a2-a3. Before, I said we play this move whenever Black goes ...b7-b5, but since Black is playing it slowly, we can take a time out to discourage ...b7-b5 since it now threatens nothing. Black should, from here on out, avoid playing ...b7-b5 for this reason.} ({For instance, after} 8. Qd2 exd4 9. Nxd4 {,} Ne5 $5 {now becomes an option. Now Black has a few options, and this time, how we respond is highly dependent on what they do. Switching the knight to g6 is Black's strongest idea.Here we have to understand that g4-g5 is the move we want to make work, but Black has annoying ideas connected with capturing, then playing ...Ng4 if we do it right now. Therefore, we either must wait for Black to do something to make ...Ng4 less strong, or prevent ...Ng4 directly before throwing g4-g5.}) 8... Nf8 {[%cal Gf8g6] To the point and best.} ({▷} 8... b5 $6 {is inaccurate, since we spent the last move preparing for this. We cannot go g4-g5 yet since there are still two pieces on the g5-square, but since the g4-pawn is itself not hanging, we can play} 9. h4 $16 {intending to recapture on g5 with a pawn. White is clearly better already.}) ({▷} 8... a5 $2 {has been tried a few times, but I don't see the point of this move. We weren't trying to play b2-b4 anyway.} 9. Qd2 $16 {Simple enough. White benefits greatly from the inclusion of a2-a3 and ...a7-a5.}) 9. h3 {[%csl Rg4][%cal Gc8g4,Gf6g4] The pawn was hanging.} Ng6 10. g5 {[%csl Gd8,Ge7,Rg5][%cal Gd8g5,Yf3g5,Rf6g4] Now we're not in the best position to play g4-g5 since Black still has ...Ng4-lined up, but Black was threatening to play ...Nh7 next and prevent g4-g5 for good. I tried to make Qe2 work there, but the blockade ended up being too hard to break. I believe we have to go for the complications or lose the chance to play g4-g5 altogether.} hxg5 11. Nxg5 {If we had one more move to get Qd2 in, we would be doing fantastic. However...} Ng4 $1 {[%csl Rg5,Re3] After this, the following moves are forced:} (11... d5 $2 {was played in the only game that reached this position, but it's an awful move!} 12. dxe5 Nxe4 (12... Nxe5 13. Qd4 $18) 13. Ncxe4 dxe4 14. Qxd8+ Bxd8 15. Nxf7 $1 $18) 12. Rxg4 {[%csl Gg5][%cal Gg4g5]} exd4 13. Nxf7 $1 Kxf7 14. Bc4+ d5 15. exd5 {[%cal Gd5d6] Now, Black needs to take something in lieu of the threat of d5-d6+ lording over them.} dxe3 (15... dxc3 $4 {is the losing option:} 16. dxc6+ Ke8 17. Qxd8+ Bxd8 18. cxb7 Bxb7 19. Rxg6 $18 {, and we will be up a few pawns.} cxb2 {leads to a dangerous attack after} 20. Re6+ {, and we're still up a pawn.}) 16. Qf3+ {[%cal Re7f6,Rd5d6]} Ke8 17. Rxg6 exf2+ {Otherwise Qe4 is lethal.} 18. Ke2 Qd7 $7 {[%csl Gf5][%cal Gd7f5] Black needs to get the queens of or be crushed.} 19. Rxg7 Qf5 20. Qe4 Qxe4+ 21. Nxe4 cxd5 22. Bxd5 Bxh3 23. Kxf2 $14 {[%cal Ga1g1,Gc2c4,Gb2b4,Gc4c5] Okay, Black should survive here, but we have a pawn and some activity. It's not over yet!} *

