1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 c5 $6 {[%csl Ge4][%cal Gd3e4] The whole point of starting with 5.Bd3. ...c7-c5 does not work as well as it does against 5.Nf3 because the e4-pawn is defended, which takes the sting out of ...Qa5. Here we can take it, and Black will be left with a lousy structure.} 6. dxc5 (6. dxc5 Qa5 $2 {threatens nothing now, so we can take on} 7. cxd6 {to avoid ...Qxc5.}) 6... O-O 7. Nf3 {[%cal Rd6c5] We should leave the pawn on c5, since our goal was to make Black recapture unfavorably. It's not worth it to start pawn-grabbing here.} dxc5 {Sooner or later, this capture must be made, or eventually White will actually be ready to take on d6.} 8. e5 {[%csl Rf6]} Nd5 9. Nxd5 Qxd5 10. Qe2 $16 {[%cal Gd3e4] We have transposed to a line in 5...O-O, which is much better for White.} *

