1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 O-O 6. Nf3 c6 $6 {If you haven't noticed by now, this move is typically bad when White has three pawns advanced – when White plays e4-e5 to boot the knight on f6, Black must burn a tempo to play ...c6-c5 again to attack the center....c7-c6 can only be justified if White isn't imminently threatening e4-e5, and Black has time to follow up with both ...b7-b5, ...b5-b4.} 7. O-O Bg4 8. Be3 e5 {White reacts with the typical response:} 9. dxe5 dxe5 10. f5 $16 {[%csl Gf5,Rg4] The bishop being stranded in front of the pawns means that we can force a trade with h2-h3 next, and the remaining black bishop on g7 isn't too spectacular.} *

