1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 O-O 6. Nf3 c6 $6 {If you haven't noticed by now, this move is typically bad when White has three pawns advanced – when White plays e4-e5 to boot the knight on f6, Black must burn a tempo to play ...c6-c5 again to attack the center....c7-c6 can only be justified if White isn't imminently threatening e4-e5, and Black has time to follow up with both ...b7-b5, ...b5-b4.} 7. O-O b5 {Now that Black has played ...b7-b5, it's time to strike!} (7... Nbd7 $2 {walks straight onto the rake. ...c7-c6 and ...Nbd7 is the perfect invitation to e4-e5.} 8. e5 $18) 8. e5 $18 {[%csl Rf6] Good move! The best square for the knight, d5, is now unavailable since b5 hangs at the end. The knight will have to go to a suboptimal square, leaving White with a free hand to attack on the kingside.} *

