1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 O-O 6. Nf3 c5 $6 {Does not work with the bishop on d3. The e4-square is secured, which stops ...Qa5 ...Nxe4 shenanigans} 7. dxc5 dxc5 {The structural change is poor, but unfortunately, it must be played.} (7... Qa5 $2 8. cxd6 $18 {...Nxe4 does not work, so Black is down a pawn for baloney.}) 8. e5 {[%csl Rf6]} Nd5 9. Nxd5 Qxd5 10. Qe2 {[%cal Gd3e4]} Bf5 {Against anything else, we will play Be4, Be3, and O-O, with dominant piece centralization.} 11. Bxf5 gxf5 12. Be3 $14 {[%csl Rf5,Rf7,Yg7][%cal Yf4e5,Ye5f4,Ga1d1,Gc2c4] Black managed to avoid losing time with Be4, but Black's position still has serious issues. The g7-bishop is garbage, the pawn structure is permanently crippled, and White still has the suffocating space advantage.} *

