1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 O-O 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. O-O e5 8. d5 (8. dxe5 $6 {In this case, it is not as effective.} dxe5 9. f5 gxf5 10. exf5 e4 $10 {After the smoke clears, Black is doing alright.}) 8... Nd4 9. f5 $1 {[%csl Gf5][%cal Gc8g4] Cutting out the bishop and wedging the pawn into the heart of Black's position.} c6 {Trying to counter us by breaking in the center. We have to consolidate the light squares to maintain our pawns.} (9... gxf5 $2 {is almost always a bad idea: it opens up too many lines towards the kingside.} 10. exf5 Nxf5 {; otherwise, there was no point capturing this pawn.} 11. Ng5) 10. Bg5 {[%cal Gd1d2] We stop the threat of ...cxd5 and prepare for Qd2 next, so Black has to play ...h7-h6.} h6 {[%csl Rg5]} 11. Bxf6 Bxf6 12. Nd2 $14 {[%csl Rd4,Yd5,Ye4,Yf5][%cal Ga2a4,Gg1h1,Rc3e2] While it's true we unfortunately had to trade off the bishop pair, we managed to maintain the two beachheads on f5 and d5.Next up, we can play moves like Kh1 and a2-a4 to consolidate the light squares and slowly increase the pressure against Black's position. Eventually, we will probably have to play Ne2 and get rid of the annoying knight on d4.} *

