1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 Nc6 {The main and best alternative.} 6. e5 $5 {[%csl Rf6]} dxe5 (6... Nd7 7. Nf3 $14 {is not critical. None of our pawns are in any danger, as all of Black’s firepower is concentrated on the well-defended e-pawn.}) 7. fxe5 {[%csl Rf6]} Nd5 (7... Nd7 8. Nf3 Nb6 {transposes to ...Nb6 on move 8, and otherwise the knight shouldn't remain on [d7] as it blocks the bishop.}) 8. Nf3 {In the upcoming positions, the bishop may go to e4 to increase the pressure against d5 and get out of the way of any ...Nb4 ideas. The knight will backtrack to e2 and prepare c2-c3 to stabilize the d4-pawn, which is currently the point of weakness in our position.Black ideally does not want to play ...f7-f6-fxe5, since it would seriously weaken their position. But this means that the g7-bishop will be eternally bad. Black's main sources of counterplay will be either connected with ...c7-c5 or ...f7-f5.} Be6 $6 {Not a great square for the bishop.} 9. Na4 $1 {[%cal Gc2c3,Ga4c5] Preparing Nc5 to bother the bishop and also c2-c3 to solidify the pawn chain.} O-O 10. Nc5 Bg4 11. c3 $16 {[%csl Gb2,Gc3,Gd4,Ge5][%cal Gc5e4,Ge1g1,Gh2h3] We were able to use this Na4 resource to coordinate them, and now Black's only viable pawn break is the weakening f6. At some point, the knight can be brought back to e4 after the b7-pawn is defended.} *

