1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 Nc6 {The main and best alternative.} 6. e5 $5 {[%csl Rf6]} dxe5 (6... Nd7 7. Nf3 $14 {is not critical. None of our pawns are in any danger, as all of Black’s firepower is concentrated on the well-defended e-pawn.}) 7. fxe5 {[%csl Rf6]} Nd5 (7... Nd7 8. Nf3 Nb6 {transposes to ...Nb6 on move 8, and otherwise the knight shouldn't remain on [d7] as it blocks the bishop.}) 8. Nf3 {In the upcoming positions, the bishop may go to e4 to increase the pressure against d5 and get out of the way of any ...Nb4 ideas. The knight will backtrack to e2 and prepare c2-c3 to stabilize the d4-pawn, which is currently the point of weakness in our position.Black ideally does not want to play ...f7-f6-fxe5, since it would seriously weaken their position. But this means that the g7-bishop will be eternally bad. Black's main sources of counterplay will be either connected with ...c7-c5 or ...f7-f5.} Nb6 9. Ne2 Nb4 10. Be4 {We have to draw some concessions from Black before giving up the bishop, so we come to e4 to provoke ...f7-f5. Black has to do something direct, or c2-c3 will flush them back to square one.} c5 11. c3 {[%csl Rb4]} Nc6 12. a4 {[%cal Ga4a5]} a5 {Our opponent can absolutely not allow a4-a5 to happen.} 13. O-O cxd4 14. Nexd4 Nxd4 15. cxd4 $14 {[%csl Rb6] The b6-knight is very poor, and the centralized bishop exerts pressure against the b7-pawn. ...Nd5, which would ideally fix these issues, is running into tactical issues with Qb3/Ng5.} *

