1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 Nc6 {The main and best alternative.} 6. e5 $5 {[%csl Rf6]} dxe5 (6... Nd7 7. Nf3 $14 {is not critical. None of our pawns are in any danger, as all of Black’s firepower is concentrated on the well-defended e-pawn.}) 7. fxe5 {[%csl Rf6]} Nd5 (7... Nd7 8. Nf3 Nb6 {transposes to ...Nb6 on move 8, and otherwise the knight shouldn't remain on [d7] as it blocks the bishop.}) 8. Nf3 {In the upcoming positions, the bishop may go to e4 to increase the pressure against d5 and get out of the way of any ...Nb4 ideas. The knight will backtrack to e2 and prepare c2-c3 to stabilize the d4-pawn, which is currently the point of weakness in our position.Black ideally does not want to play ...f7-f6-fxe5, since it would seriously weaken their position. But this means that the g7-bishop will be eternally bad. Black's main sources of counterplay will be either connected with ...c7-c5 or ...f7-f5.} Nb6 9. Ne2 Nb4 10. Be4 {We have to draw some concessions from Black before giving up the bishop, so we come to e4 to provoke ...f7-f5. Black has to do something direct, or c2-c3 will flush them back to square one.} f5 11. Bd3 Be6 12. Nf4 Nxd3+ 13. Qxd3 Bc4 14. Qd1 $16 {[%cal Gb2b3,Ge1g1] The c4-bishop is a bit short on squares; likely having to take the route of trading for the f3-knight via d5.The pawn on f5 likely means we aren't crashing through on the kingside, but it does leave the d4-e5 chain unchallenged and the e6-square constantly weak, giving White a permanent advantage.} *

