1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 Nc6 {The main and best alternative.} 6. e5 $5 {[%csl Rf6]} dxe5 (6... Nd7 7. Nf3 $14 {is not critical. None of our pawns are in any danger, as all of Black’s firepower is concentrated on the well-defended e-pawn.}) 7. fxe5 {[%csl Rf6]} Nd5 (7... Nd7 8. Nf3 Nb6 {transposes to ...Nb6 on move 8, and otherwise the knight shouldn't remain on [d7] as it blocks the bishop.}) 8. Nf3 {In the upcoming positions, the bishop may go to e4 to increase the pressure against d5 and get out of the way of any ...Nb4 ideas. The knight will backtrack to e2 and prepare c2-c3 to stabilize the d4-pawn, which is currently the point of weakness in our position.Black ideally does not want to play ...f7-f6-fxe5, since it would seriously weaken their position. But this means that the g7-bishop will be eternally bad. Black's main sources of counterplay will be either connected with ...c7-c5 or ...f7-f5.} Ncb4 9. Be4 f5 10. Bxd5 Nxd5 11. Ne2 b5 $7 {Otherwise c2-c4 will come.} 12. c3 {[%cal Gh2h4,Gh4h5,Ga2a4] White maintains the edge, with the best plans being to use the rook pawns to open up some lines on the flanks.} *

