1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 Nc6 {The main and best alternative.} 6. e5 $5 {[%csl Rf6]} dxe5 (6... Nd7 7. Nf3 $14 {is not critical. None of our pawns are in any danger, as all of Black’s firepower is concentrated on the well-defended e-pawn.}) 7. fxe5 {[%csl Rf6]} Nd5 (7... Nd7 8. Nf3 Nb6 {transposes to ...Nb6 on move 8, and otherwise the knight shouldn't remain on [d7] as it blocks the bishop.}) 8. Nf3 {In the upcoming positions, the bishop may go to e4 to increase the pressure against d5 and get out of the way of any ...Nb4 ideas. The knight will backtrack to e2 and prepare c2-c3 to stabilize the d4-pawn, which is currently the point of weakness in our position.Black ideally does not want to play ...f7-f6-fxe5, since it would seriously weaken their position. But this means that the g7-bishop will be eternally bad. Black's main sources of counterplay will be either connected with ...c7-c5 or ...f7-f5.} Bg4 (8... Nxc3 $2 9. bxc3 $18 {A major positional error. Bringing the pawn to c3 significantly strengthens the center, and with the knight on c6, there is no ...c7-c5 to challenge it.O-O followed by Qe1-h4 and Ng5/Bh6 will lead to a dangerous attack.}) 9. Be4 {[%csl Rd5]} e6 $1 {Black really does not want to be capturing on c3.} 10. O-O Qd7 11. Bxd5 $1 exd5 12. h3 {[%csl Rg4]} Be6 ({▷} 12... Bf5 $5 {, provoking Nh4, misplacing the knight. We don't take the bait!} 13. a4) ({▷} 12... Bxf3 $2 13. Rxf3 {leaves f7 without a protector. White is going to double all the rooks on the f-file, and there are going to be big problems.}) 13. a4 $14 {[%csl Rf6,Rh6][%cal Gc1g5,Gd1d2] Discouraging ...O-O-O, which will run into a4-a5. Black should castle kingside instead. White is just marginally better here.We have two options for how to proceed – we can either try to exploit the dark squares, or we can maneuver the bad knight to f4.This is a slow strategy, where we will try to outplay and grind Black down with positional advantages. I don't think White is at any risk of losing here – Black doesn't have a plan of their own.} *

