1. e4 d6 2. d4 Nf6 3. Nc3 g6 4. f4 Bg7 5. Bd3 e5 (5... c6 $6 6. Nf3 $16 {, h2-h3 then e4-e5 is coming up, as always, ...c7-c6 does not belong against the three pawns setup.}) 6. dxe5 dxe5 {[%cal Yf4f5,Yg6f5,Ye4f5,Re5e4]} 7. Nf3 ({Here,} 7. f5 {is running into} gxf5 8. exf5 e4 $1 {. So instead, we must allow the capture on f4.}) 7... exf4 8. Bxf4 $16 {[%csl Gc3,Gd3,Gf3,Gf4][%cal Gf1f8,Gd1d8,Ge1c1,Rc1h6,Rh2h4,Rh4h5,Re4e5] White does have an IKP Isolated King's Pawn, but also has phenomenal development and open files to work with. White will castle queenside and go for an attack on the kingside with ideas such as Qd2/Bh6 and h2-h4, h4-h5. The e-pawn can become a battering ram as well with e4-e5, removing the Black knight from f6.} *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
