[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "8"]
[GameId "2288537761698207"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 ({For a very long time,} 2... d5 3. Nc3 {was considered to be, by far, White's best attempt to fight for an advantage.} ({In recent years, it has almost completely given way to either the 'Advance' with} 3. e5 {, or a sideline with}) (3. exd5 {or some alternative to 2.d4 .}) ({I am definitely guilty of contributing to this trend myself–I have played} 3. Nc3 {exactly one time in the last 15 years. Instead, I have mostly relied on the 'Advance', and I have a rather massive score with White.}) ({But I never really got why} 3. Nc3 {went out of fashion.}) ({And now that} 3. e5 {has been so topical for so long, Black not only has a ton of different options to fight against both the 'Short' variation and the 'Tal' variation, but they also seem to be equalizing if they play the most direct moves.Upon writing this course, I think it is absolutely worth giving the old mainline a try again. Undoubtedly, Black can equalize; they always will in a mainline if they are well prepared. But, I think I can put their position under a lot of pressure, test their knowledge in ways that have not been seen yet, and ultimately reach very playable positions even if equality is ultimately found.})) 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {Interestingly, in modern years, this move has become the mainline by a wide margin, and this may be what is prompting White players to look in other directions. However, I somehow have trouble believing Black can fracture their pawn structure this early and that we have no hope of fighting for an edge.} (4... Bf5 {I am not much of a Caro-Kann player these days, but I always chose this move in my own practice. Upon completing this course and having dug deep, I do still think it is the best move, and it is the one I had the most trouble with while preparing the course.}) (4... Nd7 {This old line is solid, but passive. It's easy for White to get a slightly better position, but it can be maddening to try to break Black down, and this is often the kind of thing that Caro-Kann players aim for. I like that I was able to take the game in an entirely new direction very early on.}) *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
