[Black "Abdrlauf, Elham vs. Houska"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B17"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "79"]
[GameId "2288537761702304"]

1. e4 {This game was one of my main inspirations for recommending the 6. Nc3 line against Black's ...Nd7 setup. Houska is a well-known Caro-Kann expert, and White made it look easy.} c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Nc3 e6 7. Bf4 b6 8. Bd3 (8. Ne5 {I gave this move in the course, with Qf3 and O-O-O to come next. The text is a little less accurate, but Elham handled the resulting middlegame very well.}) 8... Bb7 9. Qe2 Be7 $2 (9... Bb4 {This was an absolute must. Black is still worse, but at least White has to castle short.}) 10. O-O-O {[%cal Gf3e5,Gg2g4,Gh2h4]} ({Just like that, Black is in huge trouble.} 10. O-O-O Z0 11. Ne5 {,} Z0 12. g4 {,} Z0 13. h4 {. White's position plays itself.}) 10... a5 11. g4 (11. Ne5 {I would generally prefer to start with Ne5, but this is really a matter of taste.}) 11... Nd5 $2 {Black's position was already miserable, but now it is totally lost. The resulting structure with ...cxd5 is entirely unplayable.} 12. Nxd5 cxd5 (12... exd5 13. Rde1 $18 {Black faces an immediate disaster on the e-file.}) 13. g5 Qc8 14. h4 a4 15. a3 Ba6 16. h5 Bxd3 17. Rxd3 b5 18. Rc3 $1 Qa6 19. h6 $1 {White's play is so simple, yet so elegant. Black does not have a shred of counterplay, and now they will not be able to castle kingside without fearing for the king at all times.} g6 20. Ne5 Bd6 21. Kb1 Nxe5 22. Bxe5 O-O 23. Qf3 (23. Bxd6 Qxd6 24. Rc5 $18 {This looks easy enough.}) 23... f6 {Props to Houska for being resourceful; at least now Black can open some lines on the queenside. But of course, this should not save the game.} 24. Bxf6 b4 25. axb4 Rf7 ({▷} 25... Bxb4 26. Rc7 $1) ({▷} 25... Qb6 $1 {This would have somewhat kept the game going.} 26. b5 $1 Qxb5 27. Qe3 $16) 26. Qd3 Qb7 27. b5 {Now it's all over again. White is two pawns up for dead, nothing, and Black is not getting the counterplay they need.} Ra5 28. b6 $1 Ra6 (28... Qxb6 29. Rc8+ (29. Qxg6+ $1 {This would be a very pretty finish.} hxg6 30. h7+ Rxh7 31. Rc8+ Bf8 32. Rxh7 $18)) {My guess is this is what Elham had in mind, and it is more than good enough, but there was something even nicer...} 29. Bd8 $18 {The rest was easy.} a3 30. b3 a2+ 31. Ka1 Bb4 32. Rc7 Rxc7 33. Bxc7 Qc6 34. Rh3 Ra8 35. Re3 Ba3 36. Kxa2 Bc1+ 37. Kb1 Bxe3 38. Qxe3 Kf7 39. Qe5 Ke8 40. b7 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Caro-Kann: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Christiansen, Larry Mark vs. Norowitz"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B16"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "97"]
[GameId "2288537761706401"]

1. e4 {Much like the previous game, this one was an encounter that inspired me to recommend the setup I ended up giving. Norowitz plays this ...gxf6 Caro-Kann just about every game, so Larry knew it was coming, and he prepared very well.} c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 6. Nf3 Bg4 7. Be2 e6 8. O-O {You can train the line here.} Qc7 9. Nh4 $1 {Not the only good move, but it makes so much sense and is consistent with the general plan. We get rid of the light-squared bishops, and then push for c2-c4 and d4-d5.Note that since Black's queen is on c7, they are missing the ...f7-f5 resource.} Bxe2 10. Qxe2 Nd7 11. c4 O-O-O 12. Rd1 c5 $2 {This only forced White to do what they wanted to do anyway.} (12... Rg8 {In hindsight, Black could have tried maintaining the tension, but the position is still very unpleasant.} 13. Qh5 $5 {It is not easy to keep the kingside defended.}) 13. d5 Ne5 14. Bf4 Bd6 15. dxe6 $1 {Larry found a nice simplification.} fxe6 16. Bxe5 fxe5 $2 (16... Bxe5 {Black had to try this one. After} 17. Nf3 {, their position is horrible, and White can take on e5 at any moment, but at least they will have only a bad structure, and not have it compounded by a dreadful bishop against a gorgeous knight.}) 17. g3 $18 {White is strategically winning. Black never got any of the counterplay they desired in this line, and Larry made it look easy-Trade the bishops, get d5 through, and then clean up.} Qf7 18. Rd2 Rhf8 19. Kg2 Rd7 20. Rad1 Rfd8 21. Qe4 Kc7 22. Rd3 Qh5 23. Rb3 b6 24. Ra3 a5 25. Rad3 Rg7 26. h3 Rf7 27. R1d2 Rfd7 28. a4 Rf7 29. Rf3 Rfd7 30. Rfd3 Rf7 31. b3 Rfd7 32. Nf3 Qf5 33. Re2 Rf7 34. Rde3 Rdf8 35. Qxf5 exf5 36. Nxe5 Rg7 37. f4 h5 38. h4 Rfg8 39. Rd3 Re8 40. Rde3 Rge7 41. Kf3 Re6 42. Rd3 Bxe5 43. Rxe5 Rxe5 44. fxe5 Rxe5 45. Kf4 Re4+ 46. Kg5 Rd4 47. Re3 Kd6 48. Kxf5 Kd7 49. Kg5 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
