[White "Caro-Kann: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Mosadeghpour, Masoud vs. Maghsoodloo"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B15"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "67"]
[GameId "2288537761714595"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Qc2 Re8+ 9. Ne2 g6 {This definitely keeps Black's position more fundamentally sound, but the king will not appreciate the newly made hook. A young Parham Maghsoodloo went down hard.} 10. h4 Be6 11. h5 f5 12. Be3 Nd7 13. hxg6 $1 fxg6 14. g4 $1 f4 15. Bxg6 $1 fxe3 {You can train this position here.} 16. Bxe8 exf2+ 17. Kf1 Nf6 18. g5 $6 (18. Bg6 $1 {This was strongest, and Black has a difficult defense ahead. Even after the game move, when they should have been fine, in practice, it proved too hard to hold everything together.}) 18... Bd5 $2 (18... Qxe8 $1 {Black could have survived if they had found a long string of only moves in a very sharp and difficult position.} 19. gxf6 Qf7 $1 20. Rh6 Re8 $1 21. Kxf2 Bc4 $1 22. Rg1+ Kf8 $1 {It's not easy to play chess like this!}) 19. gxf6 $1 {After Black missed the one and only chance to find a bunch of tough moves and hang on for dear life, they were never able to put up any more resistance.} Bxh1 20. f7+ Kg7 21. Qf5 Qf6 22. Ng3 $1 {Well spotted. This may have been the move that Parham missed.} Bf3 23. Qxf6+ Kxf6 24. Kxf2 Bd5 25. Rf1 {White keeps the f7-pawn alive, and the rest is a piece of cake.} Bf8 26. Ke3+ Kg6 27. b3 c5 28. Ne2 cxd4+ 29. Kxd4 Bc6 30. Nf4+ Kf6 31. Nd5+ Kg6 32. Rg1+ Kh6 33. Rg8 Bg7+ 34. Kc4 1-0

