[PlyCount "102"]
[GameId "2288537761718692"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nd2 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Qc7 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O e6 13. Ne4 O-O-O {Black always has an unpleasant position in this line if they don't try to break out of it. But their only break is ...c6-c5, and as this game shows, that does not always mean happiness for the king!} 14. g3 Nxe4 15. Qxe4 Bd6 {You can train this line here.} 16. Kb1 Nf6 17. Qe2 c5 $6 {This was a bit rushed, and Ni Hua immediately pounces.} 18. dxc5 Bxc5 19. Rh4 $1 Nd5 20. Rc4 (20. b4 {I'm not sure what White missed here; the game is immediately over. The text does not spoil anything, but this would have gotten things over with quicker. Black loses a piece.}) 20... Nb6 21. Rg4 $1 {Black is coming under pressure from all angles.} Rhg8 22. a4 $1 Kb8 23. a5 Nd5 24. Rc4 {Now the rook is back. No more ...Nb6 for you!} Qe7 25. Ne5 $1 {As usual, the knight on e5 is very annoying for Black to deal with whenever they castles long. f7 is already loose.} a6 {This misses the threat, but Black was not going to save the game anyway.} (25... Rc8 26. a6 $16 {Black's position is full of endless weaknesses, and they have no counterplay at all.}) 26. Nxf7 $1 {Ni Hua's technique was not the best from here, but he ultimately did win the game.At this point, we are outside the scope of what I am hoping to discuss in the course. The game was played at rapid time controls, so I can only assume that time pressure contributed to the quality of the remaining moves.Rest assured, Black is absolutely done for here, and Ni Hua would have won the game easily if he had a bit more time.} Rdf8 27. Ne5 Rxf2 28. Qe1 Ba7 29. Bf4 Ka8 30. Qe4 Rd8 31. Nd3 Rh2 32. g4 Rh4 33. Be5 Rh3 34. Re1 Re3 35. Rxe3 Bxe3 36. Qg6 Rg8 37. Re4 Bg5 38. Bd4 Nf6 39. Bxf6 Bxf6 40. Rc4 Qd7 41. Qe4 Rd8 42. Rb4 Qd5 43. Qxd5 Rxd5 44. Ra4 Kb8 45. Kc1 Kc7 46. Re4 Kd7 47. b4 e5 48. Kd2 Kc6 49. c4 Rd4 50. Ke2 b6 51. axb6 Kxb6 1-0

