1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Qb6 {I never really believed in trying fancy move orders for Black in the mainline Caro-Kann. My sense is that their best chance to fight for equality is to bite the bullet, play ...Be7, castle short, and try not to get mated. As this game shows, the king in the center is hardly an improvement over the king on the kingside.} 13. Ne5 Rd8 $2 14. Ng6 $1 {Just like that, Black is done for. Some accuracy is needed though, and Macieja did not disappoint.} fxg6 (14... Rg8 {I would have tried to keep the king from taking a walk, but after} 15. Rhe1 $18 {Black is definitely still lost.}) 15. Qxg6+ Ke7 16. Nf5+ $1 exf5 17. Rhe1+ Ne4 (17... Kd6 18. Bf4+) 18. Qxf5 $1 (18. Qxf5 Z0 19. Rxe4+ {is coming next.You can train the line here.}) 18... Ndf6 {Shankland Method time!} 19. Rxe4+ $1 Nxe4 20. Qxe4+ Kd6 (20... Kf7 21. Qf5+ Kg8 {Black avoids mate. But...} 22. Ba5 $1 {White simply grabs the rook back, and remains two pawns up with a crushing position to boot.}) 21. Qe5+ Kd7 22. Qf5+ Ke8 23. Ba5 $1 Qb5 24. Re1+ Be7 25. Qg6+ {Iotov had seen enough. All it took was one oversight after he neglected his development, and then he got smashed.} 1-0

