1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 13. Ne4 Nxe4 (13... O-O 14. Nxf6+ Nxf6 15. Kb1 {This would be the move order we would use to get the position that was reached in the game.}) 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qd3 O-O 16. Kb1 c5 (16... Qd5 {I really think Black needed this one. White still has every chance to fight for an edge, but it won't be as straightforward.}) 17. g4 $1 Nxg4 18. Qe2 $1 Kh8 (18... cxd4 $1 {This was absolutely needed.} 19. Rhg1 d3 $1 20. cxd3 f5 $14 {White is a bit better, but the game goes on.}) 19. Rhg1 Nf6 20. dxc5 Qc7 21. Ne5 Bxc5 $2 {This loses on the spot, but Black's position was rotten anyway.} (21... Rad8 22. b4 $1 a5 23. a3 $16 {White's queenside pawn mass is very good at restricting Black's pieces, and they are much better. But the game goes on.}) 22. Bxh6 $1 gxh6 23. Rd7 $1 {Well calculated.} Qxd7 (23... Nxd7 24. Qd2 $1 Kh7 25. Ng4 $1 {The point of Rd7 is revealed. Black is missing ...Nxg4.}) 24. Nxd7 Nxd7 25. Qd2 $1 {White wins back his lost piece, and the rest was easy for Kamsky.} Kh7 26. b4 Rad8 27. bxc5 Nf6 28. Qf4 Ne8 29. Qe4+ Kh8 30. Qxb7 Ng7 31. Qxa7 Rc8 32. Rd1 Nf5 33. Rd7 Kg7 34. a4 Kf6 35. a5 1-0

