1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 13. Ne4 Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qe2 Qd5 16. Kb1 Nxh5 {Taking this pawn feels way too optimistic. Black loses so much time.} 17. Ne5 $1 Nf6 18. f3 $1 {Black can't castle on either side. Their king might survive in the middle for a little while, but it always feels like White inevitably finds a way to break through.You can train the line here.} Rf8 19. c4 Qd6 20. Bf4 Qb4 21. Rhe1 {Certainly not bad, but White could have been more direct.} (21. d5 {Already, this one was quite strong too.} cxd5 22. cxd5 Nxd5 23. Rxd5 $1 exd5 24. Re1 $18 {It is not so easy to stop} Z0 25. Nd3 {from coming next.}) 21... Qa5 22. a3 Qa6 23. g4 {Even though White missed the most direct knockout, Black's position is still unplayable. Their extra pawn does absolutely nothing for them; their pieces are passive, and White has ample ways to blow up the center.} Rd8 24. g5 $1 hxg5 25. Bxg5 Ng8 26. f4 $1 {Sooner or later, f4-f5 will end things.} Rd6 27. Qe4 Nf6 28. Qf3 Qa5 29. Ka2 Kd8 30. f5 $1 {Bang. It was time.} Kc8 31. c5 $1 Rd5 32. fxe6 fxe6 33. Qh3 {White takes on e6, restoring material parity, and their initiative rages on. It's all over.} Kb8 34. Qxe6 Re8 35. Bxf6 gxf6 36. Nxc6+ bxc6 37. Qxc6 Qd8 38. Rd3 Rxc5 39. Rb3+ 1-0

