{This is a game from a student of mine, played after I had finished my work on the present course, but long before it was officially published. As such, it was a very good test to see how it would fare against an opponent who does not know what is coming. The answer is: very well!} 1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 Re8 9. Qc2 h5 10. O-O h4 11. h3 Nd7 12. Bd2 Nf8 13. Rae1 Bd7 {Up to here, Logan has followed the course perfectly. At this moment, he forgot the specific move, but he knew a lot of the key ideas. Watch how he handles the resulting middlegame.} 14. f4 (14. c4 {According to my analysis, this move should be best, and it is given in the course.A few days later, Logan faced the Caro-Kann again and reached this exact position. His opponent clearly had prepared for f2-f4, and knew that ...c6-c5 was the best move then. Here, this is not the case. He went for ...c6-c5 anyway, and Black's position immediately became miserable.} c5 $2 15. dxc5 Bxc5 16. b4 $16 {Logan got a big advantage almost immediately in his second game as well.His play in the resulting middlegame was not as clean, so I chose not to fully annotate the game, but this is how it went.} Bb6 17. Nf4 Bd4 18. Rxe8 Qxe8 19. Re1 Qb8 20. Nd5 Qg3 21. Be3 Bxe3 22. Rxe3 Qd6 23. Ne7+ Kh8 24. c5 Qd4 25. Re4 Qa1+ 26. Kh2 g5 27. Qd2 Be6 28. Rxh4+ Kg7 29. Re4 Rd8 30. c6 bxc6 31. Nxc6 Rd6 32. b5 Nd7 33. Qc2 Qxa2 34. Qxa2 Bxa2 35. Re3 Ne5 36. Nxe5 fxe5 37. Be2 Kf6 38. Ra3 Rd2 39. Bf3 Bd5 40. Ra6+ Kg7 41. Rd6 {[%emt 0:00:08] 1-0 Brain,L 2138-Mede,I 2185 Marshall IM-B New York 2025}) 14... g6 (14... c5 $1 {This should have been played. Black is fine, but they really need to know what they are doing and play very energetically.} 15. d5 c4 $1 16. Bxc4 Qc8 $1 17. Bb3 a5 $1 18. a4 Bf5 19. Qd1 Bd3 {The machine claims that Black has enough counterplay for a pawn here. This is far from an obvious sequence, and I find it unsurprising that Black did not even play the first move.}) 15. f5 $1 {This was an absolute must. White makes sure not to allow Black to go for ...f6-f5.} g5 16. c4 $1 Rc8 17. Nc3 (17. c5 {I would have been tempted to throw c4-c5 directly, but the text is fine. One look at the d7-bishop and f8-knight should be enough to know who is on top.}) 17... Nh7 18. Ne4 $1 Bb8 19. c5 $1 {Already, Black is totally busted. He tried giving an exchange to play for an attack, but Logan was well aware of the idea of running the king to d1.} Rxe4 20. Rxe4 Qc7 21. Rg4 $1 Qh2+ 22. Kf2 Re8 23. Qc1 $1 {Very nice. Next up will be Rh1, expelling the active queen.} Kh8 24. Rh1 Qc7 25. Re1 $1 Rf8 26. Ke2 $1 {Other moves win, but I like this one. It shows a good understanding of the middlegame. White's king isn't crushed on the kingside or anything, but there are always nagging threats. So, they make a beeline for d1 and the c-file, where they will be forever safe.} Qc8 27. Qc2 Bg3 28. Rf1 Re8+ 29. Kd1 $18 {The rest was cleaning up.} Nf8 30. Kc1 Nh7 31. Re4 Rg8 32. Qb3 Bxf5 33. Rxf5 $1 {Far from necessary, but a nice way to finish the game.} Qxf5 34. Rxh4 Qxd3 35. Qxd3 gxh4 36. Qb3 {Black called it a day here. It has to be said, Logan did not have a great tournament. He did not show his best form and finished towards the bottom of the crosstable.It is a testament to how well the Caro-Kann repertoire fared that even when he wasn't playing up to his normal standards, he beat the Caro-Kann twice in his only two victories of the event.} 1-0

