1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 g6 4. e5 Bg7 5. h3 f6 6. f4 Nh6 {I had not reviewed the Caro-Kann at all before the game, and I was a little foggy on the details.I had remembered that White is supposed to play h2-h3, meet f7-f6 with f2-f4, and always take back d4xe5 when possible. But I had forgotten that I was supposed to play g2-g4 here, gaining even more space and restricting the h6-knight.} 7. Bd3 ({My point with this move was that I wanted to be able to meet} 7. Bd3 Nf5 {with} 8. Nce2 {, not blocking the bishop while doing so. Then,} Z0 9. Nf3 {,} Z0 10. c3 {would solidify everything and leave me with a huge position thanks to the extra space. But I somewhat underestimated Black's resources.}) (7. g4 $1 {As seen in the movetrainer, this was a stronger option.}) 7... O-O 8. Nf3 fxe5 $6 {This gives me exactly what I want. I already knew that we are supposed to meet ...fxe5 with dxe5, thanks to having written the present course.} (8... c5 {Black should have started with this one. The point is that they need to undermine my d4-pawn before taking on e5. They are definitely still worse, but it won't be as bad as the game continuation was.} 9. exf6 {This position nicely highlights another reason that an earlier g2-g4 would have been well advised. Had the pawn been on g4, Black would not be able to take back with a piece on pain of g4-g5.} Rxf6 10. dxc5 Qa5 11. O-O Qxc5+ 12. Kh2 $14 {White has a pleasant edge thanks to the superior pawn structure, but the game goes on.}) 9. dxe5 $1 c5 {At this point, we sort of see another reason that it was not great to commit to Bf1-d3 so soon. Black is able to play ...c6-c5 to counterattack the center, and they do not have to worry about their loose d5-pawn.Here, White is still much better, though. I had multiple simple options to consolidate a big advantage, and honestly, that is what I should have done. But instead, I went a bit nuts.} 10. h4 {This was not bad, but a bit too brash.} (10. b3 {Of White's many good moves, this is the one that appeals to me most. First off, we make the primitive threat of Nxd5. But then, when Black prevents it, we can safely regroup with Nc3-e2 without worrying about c5-c4 trapping the bishop.} Nf7 11. Ne2 $1 Nc6 12. c3 $16 Z0 13. Be3 {comes next.All of White's pieces will be fully developed, Black's central pawns will be well under control, and then and only then, h4-h5 will come to end the game on the kingside.}) 10... Qa5 (10... Bg4 11. h5 gxh5 (11... Bxh5 12. Rxh5 $1 gxh5 13. Ng5 {Black will be demolished on the kingside light squares.}) 12. Nxd5 $1 {I was counting on this resource, pointing out that the g6-pawn moving to h5 means the knight cannot be captured on pain of Bxh7+. But the computer thinks they are almost fine if they keep developing normally.} Nc6 13. c3 e6 $1 14. Ne3 Rxf4 $1 15. Nxg4 Rxg4 16. Rxh5 {Black's position looks a bit loose, but objectively, they are not in such bad shape.}) 11. h5 {In for a penny, in for a pound. I did not play h3-h4 to not play h4-h5 next. White loses a piece, but gets a vicious attack.} d4 12. hxg6 dxc3 13. gxh7+ (13. b4 {This was my original intention, and it's actually the best move. I thought I could stop ...cxb2+ with a gain of tempo, hit the queen, and then execute the devastating threat of gxh7+, followed by Nh4. However, once ...dxc3 was on the board, I realized an issue with this idea.} Qb6 $1 {The queen on the sixth rank will cover the g6-square.} 14. bxc5 Qc6 $1 15. gxh7+ $2 (15. Qe2 $1 {White should prefer this move, when a complicated game arises. Obviously, I retain a lot of compensation for the piece, and the computer even gives White a slight edge. But I don't really regret the move I chose instead.}) 15... Kh8 16. Nh4 Nf5 $1 {Ng6 has been prevented, and Black is good to go.}) 13... Kh8 14. Nh4 $1 {In for a penny, in for a pound... again. I am not a great attacking player by any stretch, but I do know that when you are attacking, to be careful about which pieces you sacrifice. Here, I was quite confident that the c3-knight and the a1-rook would not help my kingside endeavors, so they were non-essential and not worth saving.} cxb2+ 15. Bd2 bxa1=Q $2 {This does not lose yet, but Black's defense becomes incredibly difficult.} (15... Qb6 $1 16. Ng6+ Qxg6 $1 {Giving the queen was the best chance.} 17. Bxg6 bxa1=Q 18. Qxa1 $13 {I was a bit surprised that the machine called this equal, but apparently, it is. Evaluating irrational positions like this one is not easy!}) 16. Ng6+ $1 {With this in between move, I make sure that Black does not have the chance to sacrifice their queen for the knight.} Kxh7 17. Qxa1 {And here, Black needed to find a very hard only move to hang on. Whenever you are attacking or defending, keeping a clear material count is important.Black is currently up a rook and a knight for a pawn. Undoubtedly, this is a lot of material. But it is not so much that simply letting me take their queen is an option. As such, they need to save her, and do so in the best way to play against the potential discovered checks.} Qa4 $2 ({▷} 17... Qd8 {During the game, I was most worried about this move. I had seen a good line, but there was an even nicer one.} 18. Nxe7+ $1 Kh8 19. Rxh6+ $1 {This variation is straightforward enough that I think I would have found it if ...Qd8 came on the board. But, from afar, I had only thought of f4-f5.} (19. f5 $5 Qxe7 20. Bxh6 $1 Qxe5+ 21. Qxe5 Bxe5 22. Bxf8+ Kg8 23. Bh6 $1 {Threatening Bc4 with mate.Black needs to move their king immediately out of the kill zone.} Kf7 24. g4 $44 {White's initiative continues deep into the endgame. The computer swears this is equal, but I would certainly prefer white.}) 19... Bxh6 20. e6+ Bg7 (20... Qd4 21. Bc3 $18) 21. Qd1 $1 {Silent but deadly. Qh5 is on the way.} Rf6 (21... Qxe7 22. Qh5+ Bh6 23. Bc3+ $1 Kg8 24. Qg6+ $1 Bg7 25. Qh7#) 22. Qh5+ Rh6 23. Ng6+ $1 Kg8 24. e7 $1 Qe8 25. Qd5+ Kh7 26. Ne5+ $1 Kh8 27. Nf7+ {At long last, White wins. This is a really nice line!}) ({▷} 17... Qb6 $1 {This was Black's best chance. The point is that after} 18. Nxe7+ Bf5 $1 19. Nxf5 Rxf5 $1 20. Bxf5+ (20. g4 $5 {This one is also quite interesting.}) 20... Kg8 {Black's queen is in a good spot on b6, stopping any hope of a sacrifice on h6. The position remains very complex, and White is probably a bit better objectively, but Black is not crushed on the spot.}) 18. Nxe7+ Kh8 19. f5 $1 {I was really happy with this move. Despite being down a rook and a piece for just a pawn, I played a calm, simple move, foregoing any direct checks or captures.White opens up the d2-h6 diagonal to pressure the knight, f5-f6 is on the way, and Black is crushed despite his extra material. At this point, Robert felt the need to start giving pieces back, and funnily enough, before long, I was the one with the material advantage!} (19. Rxh6+ $2 {Now this one does not work.} Bxh6 20. e6+ Bg7 21. Qd1 Rf6 $1 22. Qh5+ Rh6 23. Ng6+ Kg8 {With Black's queen on d8, e7 would be a winner. Now, I have to settle for a perpetual.}) 19... Bxf5 ({▷} 19... Qd4 {A queen trade does not save Black.} 20. Qxd4 $1 cxd4 21. Bxh6 Bxh6 22. f6 $1 Z0 23. Rxh6# {will be mate next move.}) ({▷} 19... Nc6 {I was expecting this one, though Black certainly still loses.} 20. Bxh6 Nxe7 21. f6 $1 Kg8 22. Bxg7 {It will take a bit more work to finish off the king, but it should be very doable.}) 20. Nxf5 Rxf5 21. Bxf5 Kg8 22. Bxh6 Bxh6 {Now, thanks to the foresight of playing ...Kh8-g8, I don't have Qc1, mating immediately. Still, I have a continuing attack, and funnily enough, a material advantage with a clean pawn up. Black cannot count on a queen exchange as a means to save the game.} 23. Rxh6 Qb4+ (23... Qd4 24. Qxd4 $1 cxd4 25. Bc8 $18 {The simplest of many winning moves. White easily wins the endgame.}) 24. Ke2 $1 {The best square. Now White's queen has access to f1 or g1.} Nc6 25. Rg6+ $18 Kf8 26. Qf1 (26. Qh1 $1 {This would have won faster, but the text did not spoil anything.} Nd4+ 27. Kd3 $1 Qb5+ {I somehow missed c4 here.}) 26... Ke7 (26... Nxe5 {This was the last chance to delay the inevitable, but it doesn't change the result.} 27. Be6+ $1 Ke8 28. Rg8+ Ke7 29. Rxa8 $18 {Black has no perpetual, and the extra exchange should win easily enough.}) 27. Rg7+ Kd8 28. Bd3 $1 Nd4+ 29. Ke3 Nxc2+ 30. Bxc2 Qd4+ (30... Qc3+ 31. Qd3+ $1 {Check!}) 31. Kf3 Qd5+ 32. Be4 {A wild game! Of course, I could have played more cleanly in the early middlegame, but I definitely think I managed to highlight how dangerous Black's position is in this line where they go with ...fxe5 and we can play dxe5 back.} 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "Caro-Kann: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Lee, Megan vs. Fus"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "B19"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "131"]
[GameId "2288537761751468"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 e6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Nf6 11. Bd2 Nbd7 12. O-O-O Be7 13. Ne4 Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qe2 Qd5 {At this point, Megan forgot the concrete moves I had recommended, and she made a couple of mistakes. But she remembered some very important themes – namely, that White should avoid a queen exchange and be in great shape if she can maroon Black's queen on h7.} 16. c4 (16. Kb1 {This is the move I recommend in the course.} Qe4 17. Be3 $1 {And this is the best way to avoid the queen exchange.}) 16... Qe4 17. Qf1 {The machine is not a fan of White's last 2 moves, and Black is already comfortably equalizing. But this is for very concrete reasons, and White will soon end up much better, highlighting the strategic danger in Black's position.} b5 $2 {This is a typical idea when White pushes c2-c4 something I try to avoid doing in the course whenever possible, pitching a pawn to control the d5-square and open some queenside lines. But Megan reacted well.} (17... Ng4 $1 {This should have been preferred. After} 18. Re1 Qf5 $10 {Black's queen will not be marooned, and O-O-O can come next.}) 18. Re1 $1 Qh7 (18... Qf5 19. Ne5 $1 {The queen is only temporarily given access to the f5-square. f2-f3 and g2-g4 will come in short order.}) 19. c5 $2 {This was the right idea, but ill-timed. Now, if White is able to play Ne5, Black will be in a world of pain – something Black did not properly appreciate.} (19. Ne5 $1 {This was the way. White needed to get the knight to e5 before locking the position.} Rc8 20. c5 $1 {Now and only now, c5 comes. White is ready for a kingside expansion next.}) 19... O-O $2 ({▷} 19... Ng4 $1 {This would have been okay. Black prevents Ne5.}) ({▷} 19... Rd8 {This also would be fine, once more preventing Ne5 this time for tactical reasons, as d4 is hanging.}) 20. Ne5 $1 {Just like that, Black is in big trouble, and it all comes down to the horribly misplaced queen on h7. They will be able to make no counterplay while White slowly advances f2-f4, g2-g4, and g4-g5.  It would be a very different story without queens, or if Black's queen were on a square like d5. Then, Black's better structure and superior bishop would be a nice asset. As is, playing without your biggest piece is not a recipe for success.} Rac8 21. Qe2 Nd5 22. f4 $1 a5 (22... Bf6 {This was the absolute last chance.} 23. g4 Bxe5 $1 24. dxe5 $14 {Black is passive and has to sit around and wait for something like Rhf1, followed by f4-f5, but at least the king is not immediately crushed.}) 23. g4 $1 {g4-g5 will come next, and it will end the game.} Nb4 {What else? At least now Black makes a threat. But White can also dribble the ball into the goal.} 24. Bxb4 axb4 {The machine claims Ng6 is crushing. It is not wrong, but I've always liked simple solutions when they exist.} 25. Qf3 $18 {White wins the c6- and b4-pawns while Black's queen is out of play. The game is over. Megan allowed a little more counterplay than I would have liked, but the result was never in doubt, and Black never had a chance to actually get back into the game.} Bf6 26. Nxc6 Ra8 27. Qb3 Ra6 28. Nxb4 Ra4 29. d5 Rc8 30. c6 Be7 31. Nc2 Rxf4 32. Qxb5 Bg5 33. Kb1 Rf2 34. Re2 Rxe2 35. Qxe2 exd5 36. Re1 Kh8 37. b4 Qg8 38. b5 Qd8 39. Rd1 Bf6 40. Qd2 Qc7 41. a4 Qg3 {Around here I would start to worry a little, but Megan kept it all under control.} 42. Qb4 Qf3 43. Rd4 $1 {I like this move a lot. The lost exchange is of no consequence, White's queenside pawn mass will easily win the game. She just has to make sure not to get mated, and clogging the long diagonal is a good way to ensure her king's safety.} Qh1+ 44. Ka2 Qc1 45. Qd2 Qf1 46. Rf4 $1 {Other moves win routinely too, but I like this one. White gets rid of the problematic f6-bishop.} Qg1 47. Rxf6 $1 gxf6 48. Qxh6+ Kg8 49. Qf4 {Without the dark-squared bishop, Black has no hope to look for counterplay against the king, and the rook will never be a valuable attacking unit. The pawns should crash through in short order.} Qc5 50. Kb2 Re8 51. Qb4 Qf2 52. Qd4 Qh2 53. Qxd5 Re2 54. Qc5 Re5 55. Qc4 Re8 56. c7 {Finally, the pawns go through!} Rc8 57. b6 Qg2 58. Qd4 Qb7 59. Qxf6 Kh7 60. Qxf7+ Kh8 61. Qf6+ Kh7 62. a5 Rg8 63. g5 Ra8 64. g6+ Kh6 65. g7+ Kh7 66. Qf7 1-0

