1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 (2... d6 {this transposes to 3...c6 in the Pirc section.}) 3. Nc3 ({Now, anything other than} 3. Nc3 dxe4 {would be quite surprising. Still, it's good to have some sense of what to do if Black tried to mix things up.}) 3... g6 4. e5 Bg7 {Now, all White needs to know is not to rush with f2-f4. This will give Black the Gurgenidze setup, which is not that easy to break down.} 5. h3 $1 {This seems quite sufficient. White wants Nf3 next without running into ...Bg4.} f6 (5... c5 $2 {just loses a pawn after} 6. dxc5 Bxe5 7. Nxd5 $16) 6. f4 $1 {Now and only now, we can play f2-f4. Black is unable to set up the Gurgenidze once they have committed their pawn to f6.} Nh6 7. g4 c5 8. exf6 $18 ({Black is already busted. Taking back with the bishop,} 8. exf6 Bxf6 {, loses a piece directly to} (8... exf6 {, allows} 9. dxc5 {, with a clean pawn up and a better structure to boot.}) 9. g5 {, and taking back with the pawn,}) *

