[PlyCount "27"]
[GameId "2288537761792441"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {This was an old favorite of Karpov, and he won some nice games, but I think it is definitely the worst of Black's three moves.} 5. Nf3 Ngf6 {[%cal Gf6e4,Gd7f6,Gc8f5,Gf5g4] Black's point is obvious enough. They want to exchange off one pair of knights, bring their other knight to f6, and then get their bishop outside the pawn chain to either f5 or g4. If they pull this off, they should be totally fine. For some time, White was trying a quick 5.Bd3 to play Ne4-g5 and not allow h7-h6, or trying something like 5.Ng3 here. I have a different idea.} 6. Nc3 $5 {This move looks a bit weird, but it has some important strategic points.We avoid the knight exchange; this is obvious enough. White has more space, so avoiding piece exchanges should be in his favor. But I specifically like bringing the knight back to c3 rather than g3, which is played nearly 100 times more often!First of all, if Black wants to develop with ...g7-g6, our knight no longer looks ridiculous.Secondly, Black will have a much harder time achieving the ...c6-c5 break, which is how they would ideally like to break down the space advantage and activate their c8-bishop.} Nb6 $1 {This is far from an obvious move, but I think Black's best development scheme is to get the bishop outside the pawn chain and then play for e6.} 7. a4 $1 {Before anything else, we make sure to provoke ...a7-a5, which weakens the b5-square.} a5 ({If} 7... Bg4 {, then} 8. a5 {.}) 8. h3 $1 {As usual, I think is it important not to let Black get the bishop to g4.} Bf5 {This is the only sensible way for Black to try to activate the light-squared bishop. But, now we can chase it a bit.} 9. g4 $1 Bg6 ({After} 9... Be6 10. Bg2 $14 {, White can confidently castle short next with no fear of their king coming under fire, then Rf1-e1 will threaten a strategic exchange sacrifice on e6. Black still has very awkward pieces, and the f8-bishop will not develop too easily.}) 10. Ne5 $1 {[%cal Gh3h4]} (10. Ne5 Z0 11. h4 {is already a big threat that needs to be respected.}) 10... e6 $6 {A nonchalant move like this one cannot be recommended.} 11. h4 $1 {Black is lucky not to lose immediately.} Bb4 $1 12. h5 Be4 13. f3 Bd5 14. Bd3 $14 {[%csl Rd5] Black is not dead, but their position is quite poor. The bishop on d5 looks nice, but it does not actually do that much, and it can randomly be trapped by c2-c4 at any moment. It is hard to imagine them ever finding freedom with ...e6-e5 or ...c6-c5.} *

