1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {This was an old favorite of Karpov, and he won some nice games, but I think it is definitely the worst of Black's three moves.} 5. Nf3 Ngf6 {[%cal Gf6e4,Gd7f6,Gc8f5,Gf5g4] Black's point is obvious enough. They want to exchange off one pair of knights, bring their other knight to f6, and then get their bishop outside the pawn chain to either f5 or g4. If they pull this off, they should be totally fine. For some time, White was trying a quick 5.Bd3 to play Ne4-g5 and not allow h7-h6, or trying something like 5.Ng3 here. I have a different idea.} 6. Nc3 $5 {This move looks a bit weird, but it has some important strategic points.We avoid the knight exchange; this is obvious enough. White has more space, so avoiding piece exchanges should be in his favor. But I specifically like bringing the knight back to c3 rather than g3, which is played nearly 100 times more often!First of all, if Black wants to develop with ...g7-g6, our knight no longer looks ridiculous.Secondly, Black will have a much harder time achieving the ...c6-c5 break, which is how they would ideally like to break down the space advantage and activate their c8-bishop.} g6 {This is a very human move, and one I would expect to see a lot. Black aims to play what looks like an excellent version of the Modern. However, I think White has an interesting development scheme that should leave them better off.} 7. g3 $5 ({The bishop coming to g2 will make it very hard for Black to execute the c5-break. It is very important that White always meets} 7. g3 Nb6 {with} 8. h3 {, stopping} Bg4 {, which will help Black trade off some pieces.}) 7... Bg7 (7... a5 8. a4 $1 {should promptly transpose} Nb6 9. h3 $1) 8. Bg2 O-O 9. O-O ({Now, how does Black develop the bishop on c8?} 9. O-O b6 {will never be a thing with} 10. Ne5 {, hanging over their head.If Black ever moves the knight on d7 to try to develop it that way, then after h2-h3, the bishop is still not great, the knight on b6 is awkward, and ...c6-c5 will be all but impossible to pull off.}) 9... Nb6 {This offers no independent value, as Black should really meet a2-a4 with ...a4-a5.} 10. a4 a5 11. h3 *

