1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {This was an old favorite of Karpov, and he won some nice games, but I think it is definitely the worst of Black's three moves.} 5. Nf3 Ngf6 {[%cal Gf6e4,Gd7f6,Gc8f5,Gf5g4] Black's point is obvious enough. They want to exchange off one pair of knights, bring their other knight to f6, and then get their bishop outside the pawn chain to either f5 or g4. If they pull this off, they should be totally fine. For some time, White was trying a quick 5.Bd3 to play Ne4-g5 and not allow h7-h6, or trying something like 5.Ng3 here. I have a different idea.} 6. Nc3 $5 {This move looks a bit weird, but it has some important strategic points.We avoid the knight exchange; this is obvious enough. White has more space, so avoiding piece exchanges should be in his favor. But I specifically like bringing the knight back to c3 rather than g3, which is played nearly 100 times more often!First of all, if Black wants to develop with ...g7-g6, our knight no longer looks ridiculous.Secondly, Black will have a much harder time achieving the ...c6-c5 break, which is how they would ideally like to break down the space advantage and activate their c8-bishop.} e6 $6 ({This is the most common move by a fairly wide margin, but I actually think it is not the best.Black is aiming to develop with} 6... e6 7. Z0 Be7 {,} 8. Z0 O-O {, and then push for} 9. Z0 c5 {later, this is all natural enough. However...}) 7. Bf4 $1 {This is definitely the best place to begin. White prevents Black from playing ...Bf8-d6, and they also are heavily discouraging ...c6-c5 with Nc3-b5 or d4-d5 always hanging over Black's head.} c5 $2 8. d5 $18 {Just like that, the game is over. Black will be demolished in the center.} *

