1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {This was an old favorite of Karpov, and he won some nice games, but I think it is definitely the worst of Black's three moves.} 5. Nf3 Ngf6 {[%cal Gf6e4,Gd7f6,Gc8f5,Gf5g4] Black's point is obvious enough. They want to exchange off one pair of knights, bring their other knight to f6, and then get their bishop outside the pawn chain to either f5 or g4. If they pull this off, they should be totally fine. For some time, White was trying a quick 5.Bd3 to play Ne4-g5 and not allow h7-h6, or trying something like 5.Ng3 here. I have a different idea.} 6. Nc3 $5 {This move looks a bit weird, but it has some important strategic points.We avoid the knight exchange; this is obvious enough. White has more space, so avoiding piece exchanges should be in his favor. But I specifically like bringing the knight back to c3 rather than g3, which is played nearly 100 times more often!First of all, if Black wants to develop with ...g7-g6, our knight no longer looks ridiculous.Secondly, Black will have a much harder time achieving the ...c6-c5 break, which is how they would ideally like to break down the space advantage and activate their c8-bishop.} e6 $6 ({This is the most common move by a fairly wide margin, but I actually think it is not the best.Black is aiming to develop with} 6... e6 7. Z0 Be7 {,} 8. Z0 O-O {, and then push for} 9. Z0 c5 {later, this is all natural enough. However...}) 7. Bf4 $1 {This is definitely the best place to begin. White prevents Black from playing ...Bf8-d6, and they also are heavily discouraging ...c6-c5 with Nc3-b5 or d4-d5 always hanging over Black's head.} Be7 {This definitely feels the most natural to me, but I don't think it is best. Black does have other development schemes available to them.} 8. Bd3 O-O 9. Qe2 $1 {With the not so subtle intention of castling long. We are already in fresh territory.} b5 {This was the best I could find for Black, and it is a very human move considering that White is advertising that he wants to castle long. Still, I don't think Black will equalize.} 10. Ne5 $1 (10. a3 $5 a6 11. Rd1 Bb7 12. O-O {This is also decent for White.}) 10... Bb7 11. O-O-O $1 {Black now has to be absurdly precise to get a slightly worse position. It is very easy for them to totally collapse with normal-looking moves, either strategically in the center or violently on the kingside.} Qa5 {This feels very human to me, but in fact, the queen is just misplaced.} 12. Kb1 {Now, the computer insists on ...b5-b4, followed by ...c6-c5 all the same. When this happens, the inclusion of ...Qd8-a5 and Kc1-b1 means that White will have a very nice Ne5-c4 resource at an opportune moment.} b4 13. Ne4 c5 14. Nc4 $1 Qa4 15. Nxf6+ Nxf6 16. dxc5 Bxc5 17. Be5 ({Black's kingside is looking very tender, and the queen is offside. Already, the machine claims} 17. Be5 Be7 {is the only move not to lose immediately.}) 17... Be7 18. Rhe1 $16 (18. Rhe1 Z0 19. g4 {,} Z0 20. g5 {comes next, and Black has a really lousy position.}) *

