1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 $2 {A move that is the Yaacov Norowitz blitz special. It is a bad move, and Black is already quite a bit worse, but I do have to admire the fact that Yaacov plays it every single game regardless of how much damage modern engines can do.Over the years, it had some other adherents, but he has been so much more devoted to it that I think it is apt to name the line after him.} 6. Nf3 Bf5 {This has been played as well, but it strikes me as cooperative. Black now cannot get ...f6-f5 in.} 7. Be2 e6 8. O-O Bd6 9. c4 $16 {As usual. White is ready to slam forward with d4-d5.} *

