1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 $2 {A move that is the Yaacov Norowitz blitz special. It is a bad move, and Black is already quite a bit worse, but I do have to admire the fact that Yaacov plays it every single game regardless of how much damage modern engines can do.Over the years, it had some other adherents, but he has been so much more devoted to it that I think it is apt to name the line after him.} 6. Nf3 Bg4 (6... Rg8 7. g3 {It does not help Black at all, and even gives White's bishop access to the g2-square before castling short. The king will be totally safe, and the same c2-c4 and d4-d5 plan should be very powerful.}) 7. Be2 {Now, a couple of key points:  ▷ First off, Black's supposed kingside attack is a lot less scary than it might seem. They can often set up with ...Qc7, ...e7-e6, ...Bd6, ...Rg8; we don't care. Castle short anyway, you won't get mated.  ▷ Secondly, White's best plan is to slam forward with c2-c4 and d4-d5, blowing up the center and leaving Black's king in a tough spot. In general, I think Black should be trying to stop this from happening by playing with ...e7-e6, ...Qc7, ...Nd7, and ...O-O-O as quickly as possible, foregoing moves like ...Bd6 or ...Rg8 that don't do anything to stop d5.} e6 (7... Nd7 8. O-O {Black has nothing better than ...e7-e6 anyway.}) 8. O-O $1 {As mentioned, we don't care about any supposed attack Black might claim to have. Next up is c2-c4 and d4-d5.} Bd6 {Looks aggressive, but I think it actually just makes White's life easier. By spending a tempo on a move that does not help Black fight for the d5-square, White should break through right away.} 9. c4 $1 Rg8 10. g3 $16 {I like making this preparatory move, stopping any hope of Bg4-h3.Now, Black will eventually be able to tear our kingside apart with ...h7-h5, ...f6-f5, and then the pawns can come to h2-h4 and f2-f4. Luckily for us, it takes them three moves even to try to get one pawn ready to pressure the g-file. In the meantime, we are slamming d4-d5 next move.} (10. d5 $5 {The machine thinks we can even just give an exchange here, and that White is much better. This is true, but also unnecessary.} Bh3 11. g3 $1 Bxf1 12. Bxf1 $16) *

