1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 $1 {This is important. White wants to develop with Bd3 and Ne2, and they need to protect the d4-pawn in order to do so.} Bf5 {...has been tried too, hoping to trade light-squared bishops and not allowing us to put the bishop on d3.I don't really buy it even if we do just play Bd3 and call the bluff, but we can kick the bishop away first instead.} 7. Ne2 $1 Bd6 8. Ng3 Bg6 (8... Be6 9. Bd3 {White gets a tempo up on the main line. The knight got to g3 very nicely.}) 9. Qe2+ $1 Qe7 10. Qxe7+ Kxe7 11. f4 $1 h5 12. h4 {[%cal Ge1f2,Gf1e2] Black has an unpleasant endgame ahead. The g6-bishop is really awkward.} *

