1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 $1 {This is important. White wants to develop with Bd3 and Ne2, and they need to protect the d4-pawn in order to do so.} Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 Re8 9. Qc2 $1 {We make a very blunt threat to the h7-pawn, and we do so before Black can defend with ...Nd7-f8, which is part of their plan. Black now needs to make up their mind on how to deal with the threat to h7, and we will play very different plans depending on what they do.In general, I really do not believe in castling short if Black can ever successfully play ...f7-f5. The d3-bishop will be blunted, Black will be able to play ...g7-g6 and ...Nd7-f6 as a means to get their knight to a much more active square than what they normally get to, the queen has access to h4 to poke at our kingside, and I want no piece of any of that.If Black touches their h-pawn here, castling short makes a lot more sense. It will be much, much harder for them to get the f6-f5 advance in because we will have the square under control, the h6-square will be weaker in some cases, and ...g7-g6 will be very hard to pull off when Bxg6 is always there.If Black plays with ...g7-g6 directly instead, then castling short would allow Black to reach the exact kind of position that I want to avoid. But at the same time, it would give us much greater attacking chances in an opposite side castling scenario due to the possibility of h4-h5.} g6 10. h4 $1 ({Of course, White goes for it. Now, Black has two serious setups to consider. One is to play with} 10. h4 Be6 {here and ...f5, and the other is with} (10... Nd7 {here and ...Nf8. They are both reasonably solid, but still very dangerous for Black.})) 10... Bg4 $2 11. f3 $1 {Of course. The bishop is kicked away, and Black was kind to give us a free tempo.} Be6 12. h5 f5 13. hxg6 $1 fxg6 14. g4 $1 ({Just like that, Black is smashed. It's nice that} 14. g4 Bd5 {does not hit the rook on h1!}) *

