rse, White goes for it. Now, Black has two serious setups to consider. One is to play with} 10. h4 Be6 {here and ...f5, and the other is with} (10... Nd7 {here and ...Nf8. They are both reasonably solid, but still very dangerous for Black.})) 10... h5 $2 {...would be a very surprising mistake to see, but my friend Josiah played it once, so you never know.} 11. Bxg6 $1 {Of course.} fxg6 12. Qxg6+ Kh8 13. Qxh5+ Kg8 14. Bh6 $16 {Black faces a vicious long-term attack. The machine wants to take on e2, immediately giving back material and leaving us ahead in brute force as well.} *

