rse, White goes for it. Now, Black has two serious setups to consider. One is to play with} 10. h4 Be6 {here and ...f5, and the other is with} (10... Nd7 {and ...Nf8. They are both reasonably solid, but still very dangerous for Black.})) 10... Nd7 $6 11. h5 Nf8 12. Bh6 $1 Be6 13. O-O-O {Black's position is no fun here either. White can combine threats in the center with c3-c4 with direct ideas on the kingside. For example, the machine gives ...b7-b5, which feels like a very human move to me, stopping us from pushing c3-c4.} b5 14. hxg6 $1 fxg6 {Now White has two good options.} ({If} 14... hxg6 {, then} 15. Kb1 $16 {Black isn't mated right away, but the open h-file should spell their doom.White has several attacking ideas available, such as} Z0 16. Nf4 {to sac on g6, or} Z0 17. Qd2 {, followed by} Z0 18. Bg7 {. Black should not survive.}) 15. d5 $5 Bxd5 16. Bxb5 $14 {It is quite good too.} *

