rse, White goes for it. Now, Black has two serious setups to consider. One is to play with} 10. h4 Be6 {here and ...f5, and the other is with} (10... Nd7 {here and ...Nf8. They are both reasonably solid, but still very dangerous for Black.})) 10... Be6 $1 11. h5 f5 $1 {Now, unfortunately, the most violent way does not work. But White can still build up an attack.} 12. Be3 Qf6 {This is necessary prophylaxis, as we were already threatening to take on g6 and slam g2-g4.} 13. O-O-O Nd7 ({Now, the machine wants to play} 13... Nd7 14. Nf4 {, which I am not too keen on. Black takes it,} Bxf4 {, plays} 15. Bxf4 b5 {, and claims they have good play on the light squares. I like doing something different, blending ideas on both sides of the board.}) 14. c4 $1 {If White is allowed to play Nc3 next, Black is strategically busted. They should be very direct with ...b7-b5, which I don't see a human failing to find.} b5 15. hxg6 $1 {The right moment. Black cannot take back with the f-pawn.} hxg6 {Now, the d4-d5 idea is not working any more, but we are very happy to have opened the h-file.} 16. c5 Bc7 17. Rh6 $1 {Now, Black needs a few 'only moves' to avoid losing immediately.} f4 $1 18. Bd2 Kg7 19. Rdh1 Rh8 20. Rxh8 Rxh8 21. Rxh8 Kxh8 {Black avoided getting mated. But after...} 22. Be4 $1 Bc4 23. Bf3 $1 {They face an uphill struggle to hang on. They have too many weak pawns, and is not happy at all that the b-pawn is on b5 rather than b7. The machine insists they should take on e2.} Bxe2 24. Bxe2 Qe6 $1 25. Bf3 Nf6 $1 26. a4 a6 27. axb5 axb5 28. Qd3 $14 {Maybe Black will hold this, maybe they won't. They definitely cannot pretend they are equalizing.White can aim to pitch a pawn with d4-d5 or try to contest the queen with Kc1-c2 and Qd3-b3. Black has a long way to go.} *

