[Black "15B | 5...exf6 6.c3 Bd6 - 9...h5 10.O-O f5"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "B15"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "37"]
[GameId "2288537761989097"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 $1 {This is important. White wants to develop with Bd3 and Ne2, and they need to protect the d4-pawn in order to do so.} Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 Re8 9. Qc2 $1 {We make a very blunt threat to the h7-pawn, and we do so before Black can defend with ...Nd7-f8, which is part of their plan. Black now needs to make up their mind on how to deal with the threat to h7, and we will play very different plans depending on what they do.In general, I really do not believe in castling short if Black can ever successfully play ...f7-f5. The d3-bishop will be blunted, Black will be able to play ...g7-g6 and ...Nd7-f6 as a means to get their knight to a much more active square than what they normally get to, the queen has access to h4 to poke at our kingside, and I want no piece of any of that.If Black touches their h-pawn here, castling short makes a lot more sense. It will be much, much harder for them to get the f6-f5 advance in because we will have the square under control, the h6-square will be weaker in some cases, and ...g7-g6 will be very hard to pull off when Bxg6 is always there.If Black plays with ...g7-g6 directly instead, then castling short would allow Black to reach the exact kind of position that I want to avoid. But at the same time, it would give us much greater attacking chances in an opposite side castling scenario due to the possibility of h4-h5.} h5 {This has become the most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} f5 $6 {In theory, their move should be a bit annoying—I just said that I don't want to let Black play ...f6-f5 if we are castling short, but here the situation is a bit different because of the h5-pawn, which not only costs Black a tempo, but also weakens their kingside and gives White access to the h6-square.} 11. Re1 $1 {Now we really are threatening to take on f5. Black's hand is forced.} g6 12. Bh6 $1 {This is a very important resource, and one that heavily changes the evaluation in White's favor. The bishop can go to h6 only because Black has played ...h7-h5. Otherwise, we would be immediately crushed by ...Qh4.} Nd7 13. Nf4 $1 Nf8 (13... Nf6 14. Bg5 $16 {Here we see another point of the pawn on h5 ruining Black's position.They will never have access to the move ...h7-h6, so the pin on the knight is much more permanent in its nature. They are already facing concrete problems on the e-file, which they are not well-equipped to fight for.}) 14. Rxe8 Qxe8 15. Qd2 {Black does not have their kingside dark squares under control, and White is definitely better with Ra1-e1 coming next.} Qd8 16. Re1 {Now, it is not a good sign for Black that the machine insists that Bc8-e6 is their best move. But if they don't do it, Bd3-c4 means we will get the diagonal first.} Be6 17. Nxe6 $1 Nxe6 18. Bc4 $1 Ng7 19. g3 $14 {Black doesn't have much to show for the bishop pair and the worse structure.} *

