[GameId "2288537761997291"]

1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 $1 {This is important. White wants to develop with Bd3 and Ne2, and they need to protect the d4-pawn in order to do so.} Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 Re8 9. Qc2 $1 {We make a very blunt threat to the h7-pawn, and we do so before Black can defend with ...Nd7-f8, which is part of their plan. Black now needs to make up their mind on how to deal with the threat to h7, and we will play very different plans depending on what they do.In general, I really do not believe in castling short if Black can ever successfully play ...f7-f5. The d3-bishop will be blunted, Black will be able to play ...g7-g6 and ...Nd7-f6 as a means to get their knight to a much more active square than what they normally get to, the queen has access to h4 to poke at our kingside, and I want no piece of any of that.If Black touches their h-pawn here, castling short makes a lot more sense. It will be much, much harder for them to get the f6-f5 advance in because we will have the square under control, the h6-square will be weaker in some cases, and ...g7-g6 will be very hard to pull off when Bxg6 is always there.If Black plays with ...g7-g6 directly instead, then castling short would allow Black to reach the exact kind of position that I want to avoid. But at the same time, it would give us much greater attacking chances in an opposite side castling scenario due to the possibility of h4-h5.} h5 {This has become the most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} Nd7 $6 {Their innocent looking move is actually a notable mistake.} 11. Ng3 $1 {Just like that, White is forcing a concession of some kind. The h5-pawn is hit, and ...g7-g6 is a non-starter.} Nf8 {It is not a good sign for Black that the computer wants this one, giving up a pawn on the spot.} 12. Nxh5 g6 13. Ng3 f5 14. Bd2 Ne6 15. Rfe1 {This position is not that easy for White—the extra pawn is a long way from counting, and Black's f5-pawn restricts the pieces quite a bit. It is not that hard to imagine something like ...Kg7, ...Rh8, and ...Qh4 coming, with very obvious counterplay.Indeed, I actually think Black is totally fine here if White tries to consolidate and eventually make their extra pawn play. But I have other plans. We are going to sacrifice on f5 pretty soon. When we do, having the third pawn means that we aren't really going to be down material, and Black's king will never really find safety.} Kg7 (15... Bd7 16. Re2 {.}) 16. h3 Rh8 17. Bxf5 $1 gxf5 18. Nxf5+ (18. Qxf5 $5 {This is not bad either, with Ng3-e4 coming next. The machine calls it equal, but I would be worried with Black.}) 18... Kf8 {Black faces a powerful initiative in both the short and long term. The computer now wants to be materialistic with Bh6+, grabbing an exchange and quickly forcing an endgame.It's definitely a little better for White, but I like being more aggressive.} 19. d5 $5 cxd5 20. c4 $1 {Black will not enjoy the center being blasted open. The computer only gives White a small edge, but I think a human would be quite unhappy with Black here, far beyond a simple +0.2.} *

