he most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} Nd7 $6 {Their innocent looking move is actually a notable mistake.} 11. Ng3 $1 {Just like that, White is forcing a concession of some kind. The h5-pawn is hit, and ...g7-g6 is a non-starter.} Bxg3 12. hxg3 $14 {[%cal Gc1e3,Gf1e1,Gf2f3,Gg1f2]} ({Black is none too happy about giving up their bishop. Now, White has an easy plan with} 12. hxg3 Z0 13. Be3 {and} Z0 14. Rfe1 {, and sometimes even f2-f3 and Kg1-f2.}) *

