he most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} h4 $1 {This move is the most common by far. Black gains kingside space, taking advantage of their far advanced pawn, and stops any hope of Ne2-g3. I have no interest in allowing the pawn to reach h3.} 11. h3 Na6 {I don't think the knight makes a whole lot of sense here. The standard plan works fine.} 12. Bd2 Nc7 13. Rae1 Nd5 {What else?} 14. c4 $1 Ne7 15. Nf4 {Not the only good move, but definitely the best one. The d4-pawn is immune thanks to a nice tactic.} Bxf4 16. Bxf4 Be6 (16... Qxd4 $2 17. Bh7+ $1 Kh8 18. Rxe7 $1 {Well spotted!} Rxe7 19. Rd1 {Black is facing a problem on the back rank. They might be able to gain some material for the queen and are not worse by force on the board, but White's activity will carry the day.} Qxd1+ 20. Qxd1 Kxh7 {. With one more move, Black would be totally fine after ...Be6 or ...Bf5. Alas...} 21. Qd8 $1 $16 {Black's queenside will not properly develop.}) 17. Rd1 $16 (17. Rd1 Z0 18. Rfe1 {comes next, and White can count on a nice edge thanks to their much better structure and healthy bishop pair.}) *

