1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 $1 {This is important. White wants to develop with Bd3 and Ne2, and they need to protect the d4-pawn in order to do so.} Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 Re8 9. Qc2 $1 {We make a very blunt threat to the h7-pawn, and we do so before Black can defend with ...Nd7-f8, which is part of their plan. Black now needs to make up their mind on how to deal with the threat to h7, and we will play very different plans depending on what they do.In general, I really do not believe in castling short if Black can ever successfully play ...f7-f5. The d3-bishop will be blunted, Black will be able to play ...g7-g6 and ...Nd7-f6 as a means to get their knight to a much more active square than what they normally get to, the queen has access to h4 to poke at our kingside, and I want no piece of any of that.If Black touches their h-pawn here, castling short makes a lot more sense. It will be much, much harder for them to get the f6-f5 advance in because we will have the square under control, the h6-square will be weaker in some cases, and ...g7-g6 will be very hard to pull off when Bxg6 is always there.If Black plays with ...g7-g6 directly instead, then castling short would allow Black to reach the exact kind of position that I want to avoid. But at the same time, it would give us much greater attacking chances in an opposite side castling scenario due to the possibility of h4-h5.} h5 {This has become the most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} h4 $1 {This move is the most common by far. Black gains kingside space, taking advantage of their far advanced pawn, and stops any hope of Ne2-g3. I have no interest in allowing the pawn to reach h3.} 11. h3 Nd7 {This is the main move by far.} 12. Bd2 $1 Nf8 13. Rae1 $1 {I like this coordination for White. One of the main points is that I want to slam f2-f4, f4-f5, and we can't allow Black to respond with ...f6-f5. So, overprotecting the knight on e2 makes a lot of sense, as does leaving the other rook behind the f-pawn.Now, the machine is fairly agnostic for Black, claiming that ...Bc7, ...Bd7, and ...g7-g6 are all of roughly equal value. I strongly disagree; I think ...g7-g6 is absolutely Black's best move, and that their other two give us a lot more chances.} Bc7 $6 {In human practice, the one has been the most common. It makes perfect sense to try to slam ...Qd6 next, and in fact, Erwin L'Ami recommended this move in their Caro-Kann course right here on Chessable. But White can be very direct.} 14. f4 $1 {If we can play f4-f5 next, we will be in fantastic shape. The f8-knight will look ridiculous, and we will never have to worry about the b8-h2 diagonal with the f4-square so heavily under our control. Black should be very direct here.} g6 {This is Black's main move. Their point is that they want to play ...f6-f5 next, and then our position will totally suck once their knight reaches f6. Of course, we beat them to the punch.} ({If} 14... Ng6 $2 {, then} 15. f5 $16) 15. f5 g5 $1 {And now we see Black's point. By playing ...g7-g6, they managed to get their pawn to g5 without allowing for en passant. Now, ...Qd8-d6 looks scary, but I refuse to be intimidated.} 16. c4 $1 {White gains space in the center and sets their healthier majority in motion. It's quite nice that ...Qd6, which looks like by far Black's most natural move, is a big mistake!} b6 {This strange move is Black's best. I guess the point is that they want ...Qd6 next and not to face c4-c5, but now, White has another interesting idea.} 17. g3 $1 {As of now, this is a novelty.} hxg3 {And here, White has a bunch of interesting options. Be4, Kg2, and h3-h4 all require very precise responses for many moves down the line. I liked Be4 the most.} 18. Be4 {One of many testing moves White can try.} Qd7 $1 {This is far from an obvious move. The point is that now the generally desirable Kg1-g2 will be rudely met with ...Nf8-g6, and the h3-pawn hanging is a major problem.} 19. Qd3 $1 Ng6 $2 {If Black figured out that this move is the point of playing the queen to d7, I could easily imagine them playing it here. Now, they are crushed immediately.} 20. Bxc6 $1 {Oops!} Qxc6 21. fxg6 {Black is demolished on the kingside.} *

