he most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} h4 $1 {This move is the most common by far. Black gains kingside space, taking advantage of their far advanced pawn, and stops any hope of Ne2-g3. I have no interest in allowing the pawn to reach h3.} 11. h3 Nd7 {This is the main move by far.} 12. Bd2 $1 Nf8 13. Rae1 $1 {I like this coordination for White. One of the main points is that I want to slam f2-f4, f4-f5, and we can't allow Black to respond with ...f6-f5. So, overprotecting the knight on e2 makes a lot of sense, as does leaving the other rook behind the f-pawn.Now, the machine is fairly agnostic for Black, claiming that ...Bc7, ...Bd7, and ...g7-g6 are all of roughly equal value. I strongly disagree; I think ...g7-g6 is absolutely Black's best move, and that their other two give us a lot more chances.} Bd7 {This has been tried too, but I don't really get it.} 14. c4 $1 {This might not be the most obvious move because ...Qd8-c8 looks scary, but we have it all under control.} g6 {The machine gives this one, but I am not convinced.} 15. Bc3 f5 16. Qc1 $1 {[%cal Gd4d5,Gc1h6] White already has ideas of d4-d5 and Qh6 in the air.} Nh7 17. c5 $1 {Now this works very nicely.} Bc7 {The big appeal of ...Nh7 is that now Black can play their move without being mated by Qh6. They are in time with ...Qg5.} 18. Nf4 {Still, Black needs to be precise and play some only moves.} Qg5 19. Bd2 Qf6 20. Bc4 $1 {It seems like in this line, we can often just go Nf4 and Bc4, not worrying about the d4-pawn. Whenever Black takes it, they tend to be hit with some tactic.} g5 $1 ({If} 20... Qxd4 $2 {, then} 21. Nxg6 {.}) 21. Nd3 Be6 $1 22. Bxe6 Rxe6 23. Bc3 Qg6 24. d5 cxd5 25. Rxe6 Qxe6 26. Re1 Qd7 $14 {I don't care what the machine says. White is better here.} *

