4-d5 and Qh6 in the air.} Rc8 $1 ({A typical move in the present line, but also not an obvious one here. I'd think that a human might go for} 16... Ne6) ({here or} 16... Nh7 {here.}) 17. Bc2 $1 {But, on 18...Be7 , there is some forcing line with 19.Nf4 Bg5 20.Rxe8 Bxe8 , and here it will be very nice if, after 21.Bb3 , Black will not be able to play ...b7-b5 to gain time. Then once d4-d5 comes, White will go with Qe3 and play on the long diagonal, while Black is not able to get their a- and b-pawns moving to harass the bishops.} ({This is subtle, but very clever. White wants to play} 17. Bc2 Z0 18. c5 {next, forcing Black to make up their mind about the d6-bishop. If it goes back to c7,} Bc7 19. Qh6 {should mate.}) (17. c5 {This is not terrible, but White is not quite ready to fully bring the heat just yet.} Be7 (17... Bc7 18. Qh6 Qf6) 18. Nf4 Bg5 $1 19. Rxe8 Bxe8 20. Bc4 b5 $1 {This tempo is a bit annoying for us.}) 17... b5 $2 18. c5 Be7 19. Nf4 Bg5 20. Rxe8 Bxe8 21. b4 $16 {This structure all comes down to the d5-square. If Black can control it firmly, they are always at least fine and sometimes better.} ({If we get d4-d5 through safely, we should basically just win. And, the latter seems much more likely than the former at this point. It does not take a genius to play} 21. b4 Z0 22. Bb3 {and} Z0 23. Rd1 {.}) *

