1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Nf6 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. It does make some sense. Black quickly develops, ends up with four pawns around their king, and then can use that to their advantage when it comes to king safety, no matter which side White castles on.There's no doubt, this position is not that hard to play for the next five moves. They take back ...exf6, develops with ...Bd6, ...O-O, ...Re8, ...Nd7-f8. And they can do this against almost anything. But once this happens, we can definitely still put their position under a lot of pressure.} 5. Nxf6+ exf6 6. c3 $1 {This is important. White wants to develop with Bd3 and Ne2, and they need to protect the d4-pawn in order to do so.} Bd6 7. Bd3 O-O 8. Ne2 Re8 9. Qc2 $1 {We make a very blunt threat to the h7-pawn, and we do so before Black can defend with ...Nd7-f8, which is part of their plan. Black now needs to make up their mind on how to deal with the threat to h7, and we will play very different plans depending on what they do.In general, I really do not believe in castling short if Black can ever successfully play ...f7-f5. The d3-bishop will be blunted, Black will be able to play ...g7-g6 and ...Nd7-f6 as a means to get their knight to a much more active square than what they normally get to, the queen has access to h4 to poke at our kingside, and I want no piece of any of that.If Black touches their h-pawn here, castling short makes a lot more sense. It will be much, much harder for them to get the f6-f5 advance in because we will have the square under control, the h6-square will be weaker in some cases, and ...g7-g6 will be very hard to pull off when Bxg6 is always there.If Black plays with ...g7-g6 directly instead, then castling short would allow Black to reach the exact kind of position that I want to avoid. But at the same time, it would give us much greater attacking chances in an opposite side castling scenario due to the possibility of h4-h5.} h5 {This has become the most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} h4 $1 {This move is the most common by far. Black gains kingside space, taking advantage of their far advanced pawn, and stops any hope of Ne2-g3. I have no interest in allowing the pawn to reach h3.} 11. h3 Nd7 {This is the main move by far.} 12. Bd2 $1 Nf8 13. Rae1 $1 {I like this coordination for White. One of the main points is that I want to slam f2-f4, f4-f5, and we can't allow Black to respond with ...f6-f5. So, overprotecting the knight on e2 makes a lot of sense, as does leaving the other rook behind the f-pawn.Now, the machine is fairly agnostic for Black, claiming that ...Bc7, ...Bd7, and ...g7-g6 are all of roughly equal value. I strongly disagree; I think ...g7-g6 is absolutely Black's best move, and that their other two give us a lot more chances.} Bd7 {This has been tried too, but I don't really get it.} 14. c4 $1 {This might not be the most obvious move because ...Qd8-c8 looks scary, but we have it all under control.} g6 {The machine gives this one, but I am not convinced.} 15. Bc3 f5 16. Qc1 $1 {[%cal Gd4d5,Gc1h6] White already has ideas of d4-d5 and Qh6 in the air.} Rc8 $1 ({A typical move in the present line, but also not an obvious one here. I'd think that a human might go for} 16... Ne6) ({here or} 16... Nh7 {here.}) 17. Bc2 $1 {But, on 18...Be7 , there is some forcing line with 19.Nf4 Bg5 20.Rxe8 Bxe8 , and here it will be very nice if, after 21.Bb3 , Black will not be able to play ...b7-b5 to gain time. Then once d4-d5 comes, White will go with Qe3 and play on the long diagonal, while Black is not able to get their a- and b-pawns moving to harass the bishops.} ({This is subtle, but very clever. White wants to play} 17. Bc2 Z0 18. c5 {next, forcing Black to make up their mind about the d6-bishop. If it goes back to c7,} Bc7 19. Qh6 {should mate.}) (17. c5 {This is not terrible, but White is not quite ready to fully bring the heat just yet.} Be7 (17... Bc7 18. Qh6 Qf6) 18. Nf4 Bg5 $1 19. Rxe8 Bxe8 20. Bc4 b5 $1 {This tempo is a bit annoying for us.}) 17... Ne6 {Now and only now, Black should go for this one. Seems a bit too subtle.} 18. d5 cxd5 19. Qh6 d4 20. Nxd4 Bf8 ({Now White can get an endgame with either} 20... Bf8 21. Nxe6 ({or} 21. Qh8+ {. They might look the same, but in fact, Black has to defend differently in each case.})) 21. Nxe6 Bxh6 22. Nxd8 Rexd8 23. Bf6 {[%csl Gc4] And c4 is loose at the end of the line, so White does not end up winning a pawn. Still, after...} Re8 24. b3 {It doesn't look that obvious to me that Black is fine. Indeed, they need some precise moves still.} Rc6 $1 25. Rxe8+ Bxe8 26. Bxh4 {And now I like that the very tempting ...g6-g5 and ...f5-f4 will not equalize.} g5 27. Bg3 f4 28. Bh2 Re6 29. Rc1 $14 {A good move! This is important. White overprotects the c2-bishop so that he can meet ...Re2 with Kf1. Eventually, Kf1 comes anyway, and f2-f3 will allow the h2-bishop to rejoin the game from g1.} *

