he most common move by a wide margin, even though the machines are fairly agnostic as to what Black should play. While it may look unusual at first glance, upon closer examination, it does make some sense.Black gains kingside space, and if White castles short, throwing the pawn further forward could cause some discomfort around the white king.And normally, the biggest weakness of playing ...h7-h5, particularly in an opposite side castling situation, is the weakness of the g5-square.Here, this is a non-issue for Black thanks to their doubled f-pawns, so they can give this move a shot even while White still has the option to castle long.} 10. O-O {I think White can and should castle short all the same. Black's hope was that ...h7-h5 discourages this move, but I think we can do it anyway. It is very nice that Black cannot really hope to get ...f6-f5 or ...g7-g6 in without going through a lot of convulsions first.} h4 $1 {This move is the most common by far. Black gains kingside space, taking advantage of their far advanced pawn, and stops any hope of Ne2-g3. I have no interest in allowing the pawn to reach h3.} 11. h3 Nd7 {This is the main move by far.} 12. Bd2 $1 Nf8 13. Rae1 $1 {I like this coordination for White. One of the main points is that I want to slam f2-f4, f4-f5, and we can't allow Black to respond with ...f6-f5. So, overprotecting the knight on e2 makes a lot of sense, as does leaving the other rook behind the f-pawn.Now, the machine is fairly agnostic for Black, claiming that ...Bc7, ...Bd7, and ...g7-g6 are all of roughly equal value. I strongly disagree; I think ...g7-g6 is absolutely Black's best move, and that their other two give us a lot more chances.} g6 $1 {Definitely should be Black's best move. I think they have to take the strategic threat of f2-f4-f5 very seriously, and now we won't be able to do it anymore because ...f6-f5 will come first. But we can switch gears.} (13... Be6 $2 {This is human, but poor. We wanted f4-f5 anyway, and Black walks straight into it.} 14. f4 $14) 14. c4 $1 {It's time to mobilize the majority.} Be6 15. Kh1 $1 {This is an important resource. Black potentially had the idea of ...Qd7 and sacrificing on h3. Now, White can defend with Ng1, and eventually the knight might look nice on f3.} Bc7 16. Bc3 $1 {Making sure to keep d4 protected.} Qd6 17. f4 f5 18. Ng1 $1 {[%cal Gc2f2] White is ready for Qf2 next, and Black is too slow to get their knight to e4 first.} b5 $1 19. b3 {[%cal Gc2f2,Gd4d5,Gf2d4] And now the machine claims Black is just about equalizing. But it also claims that the best move is ...Rac8!, which is weird. The position is not easy at all for Black to play, and for White, it is much simpler.We go Qf2, hitting the pawn on h4, and also keeping the idea of d4-d5 and Qf2-d4 in mind. Despite the computer's insistence on equality, I think White has everything to play for here.} *

