1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 e6 {[%cal Gc6c5,Gb8c6] Black can also try to keep more flexibility by avoiding ...Nb8-d7. This way, they can hope to play ...c7-c5 and ...Nb8-c6 instead.} 8. h5 {I like staying consistent. We play the same plan, and just have to make sure to be ready in case Black tries to play without ...Nd7.} Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Nf6 11. Bd2 {[%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Gd8d5]} ({If Black wants to avoid ...Nbd7, I think their best option is} 11. Bd2 Be7 {and} 12. Z0 O-O {, hoping for} 13. Z0 Qd5 {. They can also try for an earlier c5 to get the knight to c6, but those lines feel very shaky.}) 11... c5 ({If Black wants to play with} 11... c5 {, I think they should play this move order instead of the more common}) (11... Be7 {, followed by} 12. Z0 c5 {. Still, White should be better.}) 12. Ne4 $1 Nc6 13. Bc3 $1 {Usually, we want to put the bishop on e3 rather than c3, but here, we choose c3, for two reasons:  ▷ First off, we are not concerned about ...Qd5, so we do not need to have c2-c4 at the ready.  ▷ And secondly, because we have not castled yet, Bd2-e3 can be met with an annoying check on a5.} cxd4 (13... Qd5 $2 {would work with ...Be7 and ...O-O-O flicked in; as it is, it does not quite work.} 14. Nxf6+ $1 gxf6 15. dxc5 $16) 14. Nxd4 Nxd4 15. Nxf6+ Qxf6 16. Qxd4 $1 Qxd4 17. Bxd4 $14 {White gets to the open d-file first, and Black has problems to solve deep into the endgame.} *

