1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 e6 {[%cal Gc6c5,Gb8c6] Black can also try to keep more flexibility by avoiding ...Nb8-d7. This way, they can hope to play ...c7-c5 and ...Nb8-c6 instead.} 8. h5 {I like staying consistent. We play the same plan, and just have to make sure to be ready in case Black tries to play without ...Nd7.} Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Nf6 11. Bd2 {[%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Gd8d5]} ({If Black wants to avoid ...Nbd7, I think their best option is} 11. Bd2 Be7 {and} 12. Z0 O-O {, hoping for} 13. Z0 Qd5 {. They can also try for an earlier c5 to get the knight to c6, but those lines feel very shaky.}) 11... Be7 (11... Nbd7 {transposes to the 7...Nd7 lines.}) 12. O-O-O c5 $6 13. Ne4 $1 ({As usual, I like playing} 13. Ne4 {.}) 13... Nc6 14. Be3 $146 ({Surprisingly, this move is a novelty, although strong machines show it immediately. Thus far, people have only gone for} 14. Bc3 {instead.In general, I think that more often than not, e3 is a better square for the bishop than c3, and this is especially true if White's pawn is still on c2.}) 14... cxd4 (14... Qd5 $2 15. Nxf6+ gxf6 16. c4 $1 $18 {We see the point of putting the bishop on e3 rather than c3. Black is busted.}) 15. Nxd4 Qd5 16. Nxf6+ Bxf6 17. c4 $1 Qd7 18. Nb5 $14 {White keeps good pressure well into the endgame. They will assume full control of the d-file.} *

