1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 e6 {[%cal Gc6c5,Gb8c6] Black can also try to keep more flexibility by avoiding ...Nb8-d7. This way, they can hope to play ...c7-c5 and ...Nb8-c6 instead.} 8. h5 {I like staying consistent. We play the same plan, and just have to make sure to be ready in case Black tries to play without ...Nd7.} Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 Nf6 11. Bd2 {[%cal Gf8e7,Ge8g8,Gd8d5]} ({If Black wants to avoid ...Nbd7, I think their best option is} 11. Bd2 Be7 {and} 12. Z0 O-O {, hoping for} 13. Z0 Qd5 {. They can also try for an earlier c5 to get the knight to c6, but those lines feel very shaky.}) 11... Be7 (11... Nbd7 {transposes to the 7...Nd7 lines.}) 12. O-O-O O-O 13. Ne4 {This is Black's last opportunity to avoid transposing to the main lines.} Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Qd5 {Magnus went for this position in the second round of their first ever World Championship match. The position might hold together, but it definitely feels shaky for Black.} 15. Qg4 ({For reasons unknown to me,} 15. Qg4 Kh8 {has been more popular than} (15... Kh7 {.}) ({I don't think the position changes too much, but} 15... Kh7 {feels like a much more natural move to me.})) 15... Kh8 16. Kb1 Nd7 17. Qh3 {I like playing the same way against ...Kh8.} Nf6 18. Be3 $1 Rad8 19. g4 Ne4 20. Ne5 *

