is sort of hanging in there, but there are a lot of ways that White can apply practical pressure. I like taking the knight directly.} 21. Bxg5 $1 Bxg5 22. c3 ({This is a subtly tricky move. White wants to play} 22. c3 Z0 23. Rhe1 {next, and they will have a huge position specifically because they can meet} Bf4 {with} 24. Nd3 {.}) (22. Rhe1 Bf4 $1 $10 {is a bit annoying.}) 22... c5 {This feels the most human to me by far. Black attacks the center.} 23. Rhe1 $1 Bf6 {Now, the machine claims Black is more or less fine here, but I think we can apply some pressure with a practical idea.} 24. c4 $1 Qd6 25. f4 $1 cxd4 26. Nd3 $1 {[%cal Ge1g1]} ({White gives a pawn, but shuts down Black's pieces. Now, all we need to do to launch a vicious attack is play} 26. Nd3 Z0 27. Rg1 {and then} Z0 28. g5 {. It is not so easy for Black to prevent!}) 26... Be7 $1 ({Black's best move is not an easy one. They need to be able to meet} 26... Be7 27. Rg1 {with} f6 {.}) ({▷} 26... Kg8 27. Rg1 $1 Z0 28. g5 {is coming.}) ({▷} 26... Rc8 $2 27. Rg1 $16 {just like that, Black is in huge trouble.}) 27. Rg1 f6 28. Qg2 {The machine calls this equal. To me, this is very strange. White has a clear plan to stack their rooks on the e-file and target the e6-pawn, and it is not easy to prevent. In human terms, I think there is a lot to play for.} *

