1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 (7... Nf6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2) ({transposes to the} 7... e6 {lines.}) 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Bd6 {I don't think the bishop makes much sense here.} 13. Ne4 Nxe4 14. Qxe4 Qe7 {The queen on e7 doesn't seem all that fantastic, but there's no other reason to play this line and put the bishop on d6 instead of e7.} (14... Qc7 15. Qg4 $1 {It is surprisingly annoying to deal with the very direct threat of taking on g7.}) 15. Kb1 O-O-O 16. Qe2 {Typical. White gets the queen off the loose square before anything else.} Rhe8 17. a3 $14 ({Nice and simple. Next up is} 17. a3 Z0 18. Rhe1 {, and we will not have to worry about ...Bb4 anymore. Black does not have a great way to challenge our space advantage, which will eventually grow more once c2-c4 comes.}) *

