1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 (7... Nf6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2) ({transposes to the} 7... e6 {lines.}) 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O a5 {Ivanchuk tried this one once. I suppose it makes some sense to try to get the bishop to b4, and gaining space on the queenside is not ridiculous. Still, it's hard to believe that Black will equalize playing a move like ...a7-a5 with their king still in the center. We can proceed along with the prescribed plan.} 13. Ne4 Bb4 {The consistent follow-up. Now, White is better in many ways, but I like avoiding the bishop trade.} 14. c3 Be7 15. Nxf6+ $1 {As usual, whenever Black's bishop is on e7 and their knight is on d7, we resolve the tension.} Bxf6 16. Kb1 $16 {Black's position looks a bit silly to me. They can't castle short because of g2-g4 and g4-g5, and castling long looks sketchy too, thanks to the weakened queenside.So, what do they do? I have no idea. White, on the other hand, can start gaining space on both sides with c3-c4 and g2-g4.} *

