1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 (7... Nf6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2) ({transposes to the} 7... e6 {lines.}) 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Qb6 ({This one is actually quite clever. Black's point is that by bringing the queen to b6, they can meet} 12... Qb6 13. Ne4 {with} Rd8 {, and we will not be able to take on f6 and put the other knight on e5. But, while it prevents us from playing Ne4, it allows another option instead.}) 13. Ne5 $1 {This move becomes possible because Black cannot play ...Nxe5, followed by ...Qxd3.} Rd8 $2 {This feels very human to me, but it is met with a nice refutation.} 14. Ng6 $1 fxg6 (14... Rg8 15. Rhe1 $18 {Black can barely move.}) 15. Qxg6+ Ke7 16. Nf5+ $1 exf5 17. Rhe1+ Ne4 18. Qxf5 $18 {Black is demolished.You can follow an illustrative game here.} *

