1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 (7... Nf6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2) ({transposes to the} 7... e6 {lines.}) 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Qb6 ({This one is actually quite clever. Black's point is that by bringing the queen to b6, they can meet} 12... Qb6 13. Ne4 {with} Rd8 {, and we will not be able to take on f6 and put the other knight on e5. But, while it prevents us from playing Ne4, it allows another option instead.}) 13. Ne5 $1 {This move becomes possible because Black cannot play ...Nxe5, followed by ...Qxd3.} Qb5 {My computer insists that this rare move is best. I am sure it's right, but I am equally sure that White is better.} 14. Nxd7 Nxd7 $2 15. Qxb5 $1 cxb5 16. d5 $16 {Black is ill-prepared for the center to open like this. The ...cxb5 structure is only tenable for them if they can prevent the d4-d5 advance, which they have not done here.} *

