1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 (7... Nf6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2) ({transposes to the} 7... e6 {lines.}) 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Qc7 {This move is usually played with the plan of castling long. Karpov used to like this kind of thing for Black back in the day, but in modern times, I do not hold the O-O-O plans in high regard.} 13. Ne4 Be7 {In general, if Black ever leaves the knight tension with their bishop on e7, we want to resolve it and take on f6. This is because we want to ask Black which piece they wants to recapture with, and then alter our plan accordingly.} 14. Nxf6+ $1 Bxf6 {By taking with the bishop, obviously Black disallows Ne5. But, their position is still tough. They can never castle short because g2-g4 is too dangerous.} 15. Rhe1 $1 O-O-O {It's hard to suggest another move.} ({▷} 15... O-O $2 16. g4 $1 {even with a burned tempo on Rh1-e1, this looks very dangerous for Black. g4-g5 is coming.}) ({▷} 15... Rd8 16. Kb1 $16 {Black can't ever castle, g2-g4 is always too painful. So, they basically play without their rook on h8. White can play g2-g4 all the same, Qe4, Bf4, and break through at some point with either d4-d5 or g4-g5.}) 16. Qa3 $1 ({If} 16. Qe4 {, then} c5 {.}) 16... a6 17. Re4 $1 {Now, Black should be very careful not to allow Bf4, with total devastation.} Rhe8 $1 ({This is far from the most obvious move. Black's point is that after} 17... Rhe8 18. Bf4 Qb6 {, they will be able to play ...Be7 later.}) 18. Bb4 $1 ({I like this one, disallowing any hope of ...Be7. Black has a very tough position, and} 18. Bb4 Z0 19. Bd6 {might be coming next.}) *

