1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 (7... Nf6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2) ({transposes to the} 7... e6 {lines.}) 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Qc7 {This move is usually played with the plan of castling long. Karpov used to like this kind of thing for Black back in the day, but in modern times, I do not hold the O-O-O plans in high regard.} 13. Ne4 Be7 {In general, if Black ever leaves the knight tension with their bishop on e7, we want to resolve it and take on f6. This is because we want to ask Black which piece they wants to recapture with, and then alter our plan accordingly.} 14. Nxf6+ $1 Nxf6 15. Ne5 $1 {A strong multi-purpose move. The knight finds an active square, and also prevents Black from castling long.} O-O $2 16. g4 $1 {Just like that, Black is smashed on the kingside.} *

