1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 ({Finally, we reach} 4... Bf5 {.}) ({I consider this to be Black's best move, even though it has been largely eclipsed by} 4... Nf6 {in recent years.}) 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 (7... Nf6 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2) ({transposes to the} 7... e6 {lines.}) 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Qc7 {This move is usually played with the plan of castling long. Karpov used to like this kind of thing for Black back in the day, but in modern times, I do not hold the O-O-O plans in high regard.} 13. Ne4 O-O-O (13... Nxe4 14. Qxe4 O-O-O 15. g3) ({transposes to} 13... O-O-O {.}) 14. g3 $1 {[%cal Gd2f4] White wants to go Bf4 next.} Nxe4 15. Qxe4 Nf6 16. Qe2 {If Black had a position like this one and had castled short instead, they would be quite happy to be playing ...c6-c5 and pressuring the center; as is, this will not happen.} Rd5 $2 {This is way too optimistic.} 17. c4 $1 Rxh5 18. Rhe1 $1 (18. Rhe1 Z0 19. Ne5 {is coming next.}) 18... Bd6 (18... Rf5 19. Ne5 {, after g3-g4 in the next move, White will trap the rook.}) 19. c5 $1 Be7 20. Bf4 Qd8 21. Ne5 {We see a key example of the knight on e5 being annoying. If Black had castled short, f7 would not be a concern. As is, it most definitely is a concern and it cannot be defended.} Qe8 22. Rd3 $18 (22. Rd3 Z0 23. Ra3 {is coming, and Black will be mated.}) *

