1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 {This is Black's main move in the modern era, and in my opinion, their best one by a wide margin. They plan on castling short and immediately slamming ...c6-c5, counting on being able to open the center as a means to defend against a direct kingside assault.} 13. Ne4 $5 (13. Kb1 {is the most common move here, but I think Black equalizes, and they have lots of options. They can castle immediately and play for ...c6-c5, or they can play with ...Qc7, ...Qb6, or even ...Rc8 and argue that Kb1 was not the most useful move.The whole point of putting the king on b1 is to prepare for Ng3-e4 to come next, and when Black's queen reaches d5, the a2-pawn will not be hanging. I think we can be blunt and play Ne4 right away.}) 13... Nxe4 (13... Qc7 14. Nxf6+ $1 {transposes to the 12...Qc7 line.}) 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qe2 O-O $6 {This would be incredibly naive.} 16. g4 $1 Qd5 (16... Nxg4 17. Rhg1 $18 {Black is crushed.}) 17. g5 $1 {Black can take on a2, but they will not give mate with the queen alone. White's attack is much more powerful.} Qxa2 18. c4 $1 {White's king is quite safe for the moment. When I am thinking about how Black could generate attacking chances, something like ...b7-b5, ...b5xc4, and ...Ra8-b8 comes to mind.Undoubtedly, this would lead to mate if Black had the time to pull it off. As is, they lose the race, and lose it by a lot.} b5 19. gxf6 Bxf6 20. c5 $1 {White keeps everything closed and is totally winning with an extra piece.} *

