1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 {This is Black's main move in the modern era, and in my opinion, their best one by a wide margin. They plan on castling short and immediately slamming ...c6-c5, counting on being able to open the center as a means to defend against a direct kingside assault.} 13. Ne4 $5 (13. Kb1 {is the most common move here, but I think Black equalizes, and they have lots of options. They can castle immediately and play for ...c6-c5, or they can play with ...Qc7, ...Qb6, or even ...Rc8 and argue that Kb1 was not the most useful move.The whole point of putting the king on b1 is to prepare for Ng3-e4 to come next, and when Black's queen reaches d5, the a2-pawn will not be hanging. I think we can be blunt and play Ne4 right away.}) 13... Nxe4 (13... Qc7 14. Nxf6+ $1 {transposes to the 12...Qc7 line.}) 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qe2 Qd5 {This position is supposedly the reason Kb1 is a good prophylactic move. It's a bit annoying that we need to spend a tempo to make sure that a2 does not get captured.} 16. Kb1 Qb5 {When I was playing the Caro-Kann, I always felt that putting the queen on b5 was a cleaner way to fight for equality. It is also the main recommednation of Erwin L'Ami here on Chessable, and should be taken seriously.} 17. Qe3 $146 {I like this one, avoiding the exchange of queens without putting our pawn on c4. L'Ami does not mention it.} c5 $1 {The computer insists on this move, which is not so easy against a blitzing opponent. Anything else would immediately land Black in a difficult position.} (17... O-O 18. Ne5 $1 {g2-g4 and g4-g5 are on the way. White got exactly what they wanted.}) 18. Ne5 $1 cxd4 19. Qg3 $1 Ne4 {Black can also try to force matters, but this will not fully equalize either.} 20. Qxg7 Rf8 21. Nd3 Nxd2+ $1 22. Rxd2 Qg5 {Black manages to get the queens off, but they still will struggle a bit in the endgame.} 23. Qxg5 Bxg5 24. f4 Bf6 25. g4 $14 {[%cal Gd2g2,Gh1g1,Gg4g5] Black's position should be holdable, but we can definitely press. The bishop is far from spectactular, and stacking the rooks on the g-file to advance g4-g5 looks like a dangerous plan.} *

