1. e4 c6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 4. Nxe4 Bf5 5. Ng3 Bg6 6. h4 h6 7. Nf3 Nd7 8. h5 Bh7 9. Bd3 Bxd3 10. Qxd3 e6 11. Bd2 Ngf6 12. O-O-O Be7 {This is Black's main move in the modern era, and in my opinion, their best one by a wide margin. They plan on castling short and immediately slamming ...c6-c5, counting on being able to open the center as a means to defend against a direct kingside assault.} 13. Ne4 $5 (13. Kb1 {is the most common move here, but I think Black equalizes, and they have lots of options. They can castle immediately and play for ...c6-c5, or they can play with ...Qc7, ...Qb6, or even ...Rc8 and argue that Kb1 was not the most useful move.The whole point of putting the king on b1 is to prepare for Ng3-e4 to come next, and when Black's queen reaches d5, the a2-pawn will not be hanging. I think we can be blunt and play Ne4 right away.}) 13... Nxe4 (13... Qc7 14. Nxf6+ $1 {transposes to the 12...Qc7 line.}) 14. Qxe4 Nf6 15. Qe2 Qd5 {This position is supposedly the reason Kb1 is a good prophylactic move. It's a bit annoying that we need to spend a tempo to make sure that a2 does not get captured.} 16. Kb1 Qe4 {This is Black's main move by far, hoping to trade queens. I definitely think the endgames after Qxe4 are not as easy for Black as theory regards them to be, and that White can absolutely still press. That being said, I have no intention of bailing out so soon.} 17. Be3 $1 ({Now we want to play} 17. Be3 Z0 18. Nd2 ({or} 18. Rh4 {next to kick the queen away from the annoying e4-square.})) 17... Ng4 $1 (17... O-O-O $2 18. Ne5 {This should be second nature. Ne5 works great when Black castles long!}) 18. Nd2 Qf5 {It looks like Black has things more or less under control. The queen is good on f5; they can castle short next without g2-g4 coming, or they can castle long without Ne5. But we can still apply pressure.} 19. Nf1 $1 ({Now,} 19. Nf1 Z0 20. Ng3 {is threatening to win a piece. Black should take on e3.}) 19... Nxe3 20. Nxe3 ({Now,} 20. Nxe3 Qa5 {has been the most common, but I don't think it is the best.}) 20... Qf4 $1 {This has not been played yet, but my computer is adamant that it is best. And it feels very natural to me to give the queen access to c7 without allowing Nc4 to come with tempo.} 21. g3 $1 Qc7 22. f4 O-O $1 {The right decision. The knight on e5 will not be so great when f7 is firmly guarded. Still, things are not easy.} 23. Qf3 $1 {Perhaps I am not objective, but I think White has good chances here despite the computer's indifference. We never have to worry about ...c6-c5; we can slam d4-d5 in response. And now, g3-g4 is a real idea.The machine claims that Black is fine, but that their only equalizing way is to play ...Rad8 and ...b7-b5 in one move order or another in the next two moves.This feels very strange to me. Why play ...b7-b5 and then bring the a-rook to d8 instead of leaving it behind the a-pawn for a pawn storm? I don't doubt that if you dig a lot, Black can equalize. But with respect to finding ideas in the opening and knowing when to ignore the computer, I think this is a very good moment.} Rad8 (23... Rfd8 $2 24. g4 $1 {Just like that, Black is in big trouble.} b5 25. Rhg1 Qb6 26. Qe4 Bf6 27. c3 b4 28. g5 {.}) 24. g4 b5 $1 25. Rhg1 Qb6 $1 26. Qe4 Bf6 27. c3 b4 28. g5 bxc3 29. Nc4 Rxd4 30. Nxb6 Rxe4 {And now we see the point. No hanging rook on a8. This is way too subtle, and nobody will find it.} 31. Nd7 $13 {We reach a very messy endgame. The computer thinks Black is okay, and objectively, they are. But, anything can still happen from here, and this represents them playing perfect chess and finding a bunch of very difficult moves after already finding a novelty on move 20.Against serious openings, you can never show an edge with White against good play. But, I am very satisfied with how much pressure I was able to put Black under up to this point.} *

