1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 ({Much like the Caro-Kann, Black has three serious options here, all of which take on different characteristics. There are some sidelines to be aware of, but for the most part, you can expect to face} 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({,} 3... Bb4) ({or} 3... dxe4 {, just about every single game.})) 3... Bb4 {The most common option for Black after Nc3 is the Winawer variation. Here, play takes on a very different character than either of the previous two positions.} ({▷} 3... Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 {The Steinitz tends to lead to more strategic positions, but they can also end up very dynamic.In general, White can count on an advantage thanks to the extra space if Black does not challenge the center directly. You will see in a lot of these lines that White gets a huge position the second Black does something that does not dissuade you from completing development with simple moves like Nf3, Nce2, and c2-c3.Additionally, any structure involving the c7 and d4-pawns being exchanged tends to favor White as long as Black does not get concrete counterplay and cannot trade off their light-squared bishop.}) ({▷} 3... dxe4 4. Nxe4 {This is the Rubinstein. The position is much more open than what you will see in the Steinitz or the Winawer.Black is hoping that they can eventually exchange off one set of minor pieces, most desirably by playing ...Nd7 and ...Ngf6, and then getting the ...c7-c5 advance into challenge White's center.Long term, they should be totally fine if they can accomplish these goals, and White should be aiming to maximize their lead in development, play for an initiative, and they often want to castle long.}) ({▷} 3... a6 {If Black plays a non-move, in general we want to follow up with Bd3.}) 4. e5 {It is very likely that White will play a2-a3, and Black will take on c3. When this happens, White has a free hand on the kingside to try to make trouble, and Black is badly missing the dark-squared bishop.Generally, the most direct plans involve h2-h4, h4-h5, Qd1-g4, or both. Black has two general ideas they can try to play, both of which can be done in different ways.The slower, strategic idea is to try to play ...b7-b6 and Bc8-a6, trading off this bad bishop. In general, we usually cannot stop them from doing so, but this means they are not attacking the center, and Qd1-g4 tends to cause a lot of problems on the kingside, and White can play very aggressively for the initiative.Alternatively, Black can try to maximize development with moves like...c7-c5, ...Nb8-c6, and...Qd8-c7. Such moves do pressure the center, and then White often is unable to play Qd1-g4 without allowing more counterplay than I am comfortable with.Against such plans, White generally is happier playing a solid game with simple developing moves like Nf3, pointing out that Black is missing the best strategic idea of ...Bc8-a6 once their knight has been committed to the a6-square.} *

