1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Qb6 8. Na4 Qa5+ 9. c3 c4 10. b4 Nxb4 11. cxb4 Bxb4+ 12. Kf2 b5 13. Nb2 Bc3 14. Qc2 {Thus far, everything has proceeded normally, and White has played the exact way recommended in the course. Their subsequent efforts in the ensuing middlegame were exemplary.} b4 15. Rc1 Nb6 16. Nd1 Na4 17. Qb1 Bd7 18. h4 $1 {This is far from the only good move, but I am awarding it an exclamation mark for the depth of understanding.White does not try to question Black's pawn mass somehow, and instead works on opening the kingside where they can make use of the extra piece.} Qb6 19. h5 h6 20. g3 (20. g4 {I would have preferred to be a bit more direct, but the text does not spoil anything.}) 20... Rb8 21. Bh3 O-O 22. Kg2 {This position perfectly highlights the problem with Black's strategy. Their two queenside pawns are connected, and they form a very nice barrier that will prevent any White pieces from making any trouble on the queenside.That's all they really serve as, a barrier. They are not much of a threat and unlikely to advance any further. In the meantime, White is smashing the kingside.} Qa5 23. f5 $1 exf5 24. Bxf5 Bxf5 25. Qxf5 Qa6 26. a3 {Excellent awareness. This move was not necessary, but it is very professional and very convincing. Black's pawn mass becomes undermined.} Bb2 27. Nxb2 Nxb2 28. axb4 {Petkov cleaned up easily from here.} Rxb4 29. Ra1 Qb6 30. Qd7 Rb3 31. Bc1 Nd3 32. Qxd5 Rc3 33. Bd2 Rc2 34. Rhb1 Qe6 35. Qxe6 fxe6 36. Ra3 Rxd2+ 37. Nxd2 Rf2+ 38. Kg1 Rxd2 39. Rxa7 Nb2 40. Rf1 Rd1 41. Rc7 Rxf1+ 42. Kxf1 Nd1 43. Ke2 Nc3+ 44. Kf3 Nb1 45. Ke3 Na3 46. Kd2 c3+ 47. Rxc3 Nb1+ 48. Kd3 1-0

