1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Qb6 8. Na4 Qa5+ 9. c3 c4 10. b4 Qc7 {Unlike the previous game, here we see Black choosing not to give a piece on b4. Najer put on a clinic on how to make use of the subsequent space advantage.} 11. Be2 {Train the line here.} Be7 12. O-O Nf8 13. Qe1 Ng6 14. a3 Bd8 15. Bd1 $1 {I like this move. White aims to regroup the bishop towards the c2-square, where it can one day help prepare the f4-f5 advance.} Nce7 16. Bc2 b6 17. Nb2 a5 18. Qe2 (18. b5 {I would have preferred keeping the queenside locked. g2-g4 and f4-f5 will come soon enough.}) 18... Bd7 19. Bd2 O-O {This strikes me as optimistic, castling right into a potential kingside pawn storm, but how else could Black get their a8-rook into play or hope for any counterplay?} (19... axb4 {Just because Black can open the queenside doesn't mean they want to.} 20. axb4 {White will be in much better shape to fight for the open a-file.}) 20. Ng5 axb4 21. axb4 Rxa1 22. Rxa1 Nc6 {Now, White controls the a-file, but there is nothing to do with it. For now, we can be satisfied that Black won't be able to make any queenside counterplay, and Najer regrouped the worst piece to the more important part of the board.} 23. Nd1 $1 Bxg5 {This was not a happy decision, but the knight was hardly tolerable long-term.} 24. fxg5 Qb7 25. Ne3 Ra8 26. Rf1 $1 {Full credit to Najer for avoiding the rook exchange. The open a-file won't do much for Black, and White's kingside attack is coming.} Ra2 27. h4 $1 {Shankland Method time!} Be8 (27... Nxh4 28. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 29. Qh5+ {Black is mated on the spot.} Kg8 30. Qxf7+ Kh7 31. Rf4 $1 Ng6 32. Rf3 {.}) 28. h5 Nf8 29. Qf2 Qe7 30. Bb1 Rb2 31. Ng4 Nd7 32. Qe1 Rb3 {White's queenside is way too secure for them to have to worry about anything.} 33. Qc1 Qd8 34. Bc2 Qa8 35. Nf6+ $1 gxf6 36. gxf6 Nf8 37. Be1 ({Black resigned with} 37. Be1 Z0 38. Qh6 {on the way.}) 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "French: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Leko, Peter vs. Cori"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C11"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "109"]
[GameId "2288537762382427"]

1. e4 {I was playing in this tournament as well, and while my own play was quite dreadful, I did very much enjoy Peter Leko's effort in his game with Jorge Cori.} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 8. Qd2 O-O 9. dxc5 Nxc5 10. O-O-O {This structure does not look like it should be so bad for Black at first glance. ...a7-a6, ...b7-b5, and ...b5-b4 looks like a clear plan, and then ...a7-a5 can come. The queenside pawn storm looks potentially dangerous, and Black could ultimately exchange off their bad light-squared bishop. But Leko knew the right idea for White.} a6 11. h4 $1 b5 12. Bd3 $1 {Already, the sacrifice on h7 is a big threat. But how should Black stop it? either ...g7-g6 or ...h7-h6 would allow a hook to be made on the kingside.} Nxd3+ {Cori felt compelled to take the bishop. But this means another problem will arise.} ({▷} 12... h6 13. g4 $1 {g4-g5 comes next, and Black's kingside will be torn open.}) ({▷} 12... g6 13. Bxc5 Bxc5 14. h5 $18 {Black will not survive once the h-file opens.}) ({▷} 12... b4 $2 {Ignore the threat at your own risk.} 13. Bxc5 Bxc5 14. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 15. Ng5+ Kg8 16. Qd3 $1 g6 17. h5 $1 {Black will be mated.}) 13. Qxd3 {You can train the line here.} Nb4 {A sad necessity. Black does prevent the immediate threat, but the knight on b4 will leave their position badly uncoordinated. Now, b5-b4 will obviously not be a thing.} (13... b4 {Black would much prefer to do something like this, aiming to accelerate their queenside play. But, with the queen on d3, there will be an issue...} 14. Na4 a5 15. Ng5 $1 {Mate is threatened on h7. Black is forced to play ...g7-g6, but this means we will have a hook to open the kingside.} g6 16. Bb6 $1 {Before anything else, White makes sure that the queen has access to h3.} Qd7 17. h5 $1 Ba6 18. Qh3 $18 {Black will be demolished.}) 14. Qd2 Bb7 15. Kb1 Rc8 16. f5 $5 {Very direct. This is a typical idea in the Steinitz, where White castles long; f4-f5 often can be justified by the weakness of the d5-pawn. This position is no exception.} (16. Nd4 {The machine prefers simple strategic chess and claims White is much better here with Nce2 and g2-g4 to follow. It's not wrong, but I don't mind Leko's choice at all in human terms.}) 16... exf5 17. a3 d4 $2 (17... Nc6 $1 {This would have been a better defensive try, with the important point that after} 18. Nxd5 {Black has an 'only move' to hang on.} Nb8 $1 {Black pressures the d5-knight. Still, after} 19. Bg5 Bxd5 20. Qxd5 Qxd5 21. Rxd5 $14 {White remains better thanks to his activity.}) 18. Nxd4 Nd5 19. Nxd5 Bxd5 20. Nxb5 $1 Be4 21. Nc3 Qc7 $2 (21... Bc6 {Black absolutely needed to keep the bishop, but their position is still miserable.} 22. Nd5 $16) 22. Nxe4 $1 fxe4 23. Bd4 $1 Rb8 24. Rh3 $1 {Peter is keeping everything nicely under control, and eventually his extra pawn should be decisive.} Rfd8 25. Qe2 Qc6 26. Rc3 $2 {The only blemish in an otherwise fantastic game.} Qd5 (26... Qb5 $1 27. Qxb5 Rxb5 $10 {This would have kept Black in the game. I suspect that Peter saw it and thought White wins with Re3, but missed that Black can then take on e5. Whatever he missed, Cori did as well.}) 27. Re3 a5 28. Ka1 a4 29. Rxe4 $18 {The rest was not too interesting.} Qb7 30. c4 Qc6 31. e6 f6 32. Re3 Rb3 33. Bc3 Rxd1+ 34. Qxd1 Qxc4 35. Qd7 Kf8 36. g4 Rb8 37. Bb4 Qc1+ 38. Ka2 Qxe3 39. Qxe7+ Kg8 40. Qf7+ Kh8 41. e7 h6 42. g5 Qb3+ 43. Qxb3 axb3+ 44. Kxb3 fxg5 45. hxg5 Kh7 46. Kc4 Kg6 47. Bd6 Ra8 48. Kb5 Kf7 49. gxh6 gxh6 50. a4 Ke6 51. Kc6 Rc8+ 52. Kb7 Kd7 53. a5 Rc2 54. b4 Re2 55. Bc5 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
[Round "?"]
[White "French: Introduction & Model Games"]
[Black "Andreikin, Dmitry vs. Pelletier"]
[Result "1-0"]
[ECO "C11"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "69"]
[GameId "2288537762386524"]

1. e4 {I was playing on the board next to this game, and I remembered being very impressed with how well Andreikin squeezed his opponent to death.Pelletier played very quickly deep into the endgame, showing that he had prepared well and was confident that he could hold the position, but it was always a bit more pleasant for White.} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 {You can train the line here.} cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 Bxd4 10. Bxd4 Nxd4 11. Qxd4 Qb6 12. Qxb6 Nxb6 13. Nb5 Ke7 14. b3 $6 {This feels a bit off to me, and the machine is not a fan. I don't think we needed to worry about ...Na4 or ...Nc4, and now Black is able to contest the center directly.} (14. Kd2 {I would have preferred immediately bringing the king to its ideal post. White has Ra1-e1 ready, so they are not as worried about g5.} g5 $2 15. fxg5 $1 Nd7 16. Re1 $16) 14... f6 {Not a bad move, but Black could have done better.} (14... g5 $1 {This looks more natural to me, striking at the center from the side. White cannot safely take on g5, as then e5 would fall.} 15. fxg5 Nd7 $10) 15. Kd2 Bd7 16. Nd4 fxe5 17. fxe5 Raf8 {The computer claims Black is totally fine here. Indeed, Andreikin's b2-b3 was not fantastic, and Black is probably fine. But even in supposedly equal Steinitz endgames, Black's position often feels unpleasant thanks to the bad bishop.} 18. g3 g5 $6 (18... Be8 $1 {According to the machine, this was the only move that kept the balance. It looks a bit weird to a human, though – doesn't it walk right into Bh3? I can certainly understand why Yannick did not play it.}) 19. h4 $1 Rhg8 (19... g4 20. Be2 h5 21. a4 $14 {White will push forever. Black's pawns, all of which are on light squares, will cause them endless headaches.}) 20. hxg5 Rxg5 21. Rxh7+ Rf7 22. Rxf7+ Kxf7 23. Bd3 {Even in such a simplified position, Black still has a lot of problems to solve.} Rxg3 $2 {This was a misunderstanding. Pelletier definitely underestimated the long-term danger in his position and should have taken on e5 instead to try to shake things up a bit.} (23... Rxe5 $1 24. g4 Rg5 {Forced, as otherwise Nf3 would win the rook immediately.} 25. Nf3 Rg7 26. Ke3 $14 {White has good winning chances, and the game goes on.}) 24. Rf1+ (24. Rh1 {I'm not sure why Andreikin chose to give the check on f1 first. Rh1 looks more natural to me; Black's king wanted to run to a square like c7 anyway.}) 24... Ke7 25. Rh1 a6 26. Rh7+ Kf8 $2 (26... Kd8 {Black absolutely had to get the king to the queenside to keep their pawns safe.} 27. Rh8+ Kc7 28. Rh6 {White will win the e6-pawn, but Black will retain drawing chances thanks to the simplified nature of the position. They can hope to exchange the d5-pawn for White's e5-pawn and reach a drawn 3 vs 2 on the queenside.}) 27. a4 $1 Rg7 (27... a5 28. Bb5 $1 {Black collapses on e6, b7, or both.}) 28. Rh8+ Rg8 29. Rxg8+ Kxg8 30. a5 Nc8 31. Kc3 Kf7 32. Kb4 Ke7 33. Kc5 Be8 {Finally... Black's bishop moved. It really did not do much for them this whole game. And now it is far too late.} 34. c4 dxc4 35. bxc4 ({Pelletier resigned in view of} 35. bxc4 Z0 36. Be4 {coming next.}) 1-0

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
