1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 Nxd4 10. Bxd4 Bxd4 11. Qxd4 Qb6 12. Nb5 (12. Qxb6 {In the course I gave this move}) ({but} 12. Nb5 {is fine too. They reach very similar positions.}) 12... Qxd4 13. Nxd4 Ke7 14. Bd3 {Black does not want to be slowly squeezed, and looking for a way to immediately break down White's central pawn mass seems logical.} g5 (14... f6 15. exf6+ Nxf6 16. O-O-O $16 Z0 17. Rhe1 {comes next, and White has good play against Black's hanging pawns.}) 15. O-O $1 gxf4 16. Rae1 $1 {White can take on f4 next, and their center has been perfectly secured.} Nc5 17. Rxf4 Bd7 18. Ref1 Raf8 19. Rh4 Nxd3 {I don't love this move, but I definitely get it. It's not like Black can just give up h7.} 20. cxd3 {Now, long term, White is totally winning thanks to their good knight against the bad bishop and all the weakened dark squares.Black made the absolutely necessary decision to play ...f7-f6 to try to change the course of the game, but this will not mean the end of his troubles.} f6 21. Rxf6 Rxf6 22. exf6+ Kxf6 23. Nf3 $1 {Stopping ...e6-e5. White can hope to blockade on the dark squares.} Be8 (23... e5 $2 24. Rh6+ $1 Kg7 25. Rd6 $18 {White wins a pawn, and the game.}) 24. Rf4+ Ke7 25. Rb4 b6 26. Ne5 {My computer thinks Black is okay here. In human terms, this feels bizarre.When White pushes d3-d4 next, Black will be left with an ineffective central pawn mass long term, and White's kingside pawns will matter.Of course, nobody can beat the machine from a position it claims is zero, but it is not telling the full story of how tough Black's position is. Indeed, after a single bad move, which looks like a totally normal decision, the game is almost over.} Kd6 $2 ({Apparently, the king is misplaced here, and} 26... Kf6 {would have been better. To my human eye, this would not be obvious.}) 27. d4 Bg6 28. Rb3 $1 Rc8 29. Rc3 Rxc3 30. bxc3 $18 {White is totally winning in the resulting endgame. Black's 4 vs 3 central/queenside majority is firmly blockaded thanks to the weak dark squares, and they are left with a bad bishop that cannot fight for them. By contrast, White will win on the kingside.} Bb1 31. a3 b5 32. Kf2 Kc7 33. Ke3 Be4 34. g4 Kb6 35. Kd2 Ka5 36. Kc1 Ka4 37. Kb2 a5 38. g5 b4 39. axb4 axb4 40. h4 bxc3+ 41. Kxc3 Kb5 42. h5 Kb6 43. h6 ({Black resigned rather than face the music with} 43. h6 Z0 44. g6 {coming next. A great effort from Dragnev!}) 1-0

