1. e4 {It was nearly 20 years ago that I played this game! A lot has changed since then, and my play in the early middlegame was far from perfect, but I was able to show White's plan in action very nicely.} e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O a6 11. Nb3 b6 12. Na4 $2 {It was not necessary to provoke an immediate resolution of the tension, and my knight is misplaced here.} (12. h4 $1 {I gave this simple move in the course, and White is clearly better. It is hard to suggest a move for Black.}) 12... Bxe3 (12... Be7 $1 {I think Black should have avoided the bishop exchange. Now} 13. h4 {can hope for} b5 {next, with decent counterplay.}) 13. Qxe3 Qc7 $6 {This was a bit slow.} (13... b5 {I don't think there was a reason not to play the most direct move.}) 14. Bd3 b5 15. Nac5 Na5 16. Kb1 Nc4 {Now I was able to demonstrate White's main attacking plan in the Steinitz with opposite side castling.} 17. Qh3 $1 {I threaten mate on h7. And no matter how Black stops it, they will make a hook for me to open the kingside.} g6 (17... h6 {This was no better.} 18. Nxd7 Bxd7 19. g4 $1 {g4-g5 comes next, and Black will be crushed.}) 18. Nxd7 Bxd7 19. Qh6 $1 {h2-h4, h4-h5 is coming, and there is not a thing that Black can do about it.} Rfc8 20. h4 Qd8 21. h5 Qf8 22. hxg6 (22. Qg5 $18 {If I was hell bent on playing for mate and only mate, I could have kept the queens on, but taking on g6 is more than good enough.}) 22... Qxh6 23. Rxh6 hxg6 24. Rdh1 $1 {Threatening Rh8 with a mate in 2. Black's king has to run, but they are definitely not escaping.} Kf8 25. Bxg6 $1 {The bishop is immune from capture. Black should be checkmated soon enough, even without queens. And if by some miracle they avoid mate, they are down a clean pawn and have a strategically lost position to boot.} Be8 26. Rh8+ Ke7 27. R1h7 Ne3 28. Nd4 Nxg2 29. Rxe8+ Rxe8 30. Bxf7 $1 {Well calculated. I had a nice idea in mind.} Kf8 (30... Rh8 31. Nc6+ $1 Kd7 32. Be8+ $1 {The double check seals Black's fate. White takes everything.}) 31. Nxe6+ Ke7 {Now, to my knowledge, this was the only time in my entire career that I had a checkmate in one move in a reasonably long game, and did not find it. Rest assured, my chosen move did not spoil anything.} 32. Bxe8+ (32. Bg8# {.}) 32... Kxe6 (32... Kxe8 33. Nc7+) 33. Bd7# 1-0

