1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Qb6 9. Qd2 Qxb2 10. Rb1 Qa3 11. Bb5 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 a6 13. Bxd7+ Bxd7 14. Rb3 Qe7 15. Rxb7 Qh4+ 16. Qf2 Qxf2+ 17. Kxf2 Be7 18. Rhb1 Bd8 19. Rb8 Rxb8 20. Rxb8 O-O 21. Rb7 Bc6 22. Ra7 Ba5 23. Rxa6 Bxc3 24. Bxc3 {Up to here, we are following the course. Black gets an unpleasant position with a pawn less.While I am fairly confident that it should objectively be holdable and he should make a draw with good defense, this is not so easy in human practice. Indeed, even a player as strong as Murzin went down, and surprisingly quickly.} Rc8 25. Ke3 Bb5 $6 26. Ra5 $1 {Well calculated.} Bf1 $2 (26... Rxc3+ 27. Kd2 $1 {Black loses the piece back right away.} Rc8 28. Rxb5 {The a-pawn is coming, and White should be winning.}) (26... g5 $1 {Already, this was an only move.} 27. Kd2 Bf1 28. fxg5 Rc4 $1 29. g3 Bh3 $1 {I suppose Black probably can still hold here, but they obviously have a lot of work to do.}) 27. Kf2 $16 {Black's bishop lacks a good square.} h5 (27... Bc4 28. Bd4 $1 {White will be able to set up with c2-c3, as they did in the game.}) 28. Bd4 Bc4 29. a4 {The a-pawn is coming. Black has no counterplay, as the bishop is sort of stuck on c4, blocking their rook from joining the game, and White can always play c2-c3 at a moment's notice to keep everything defended.} Kh7 30. Ra7 Kg6 31. a5 Rb8 32. Bb6 Rc8 33. Bd4 Rc6 {For the moment, the a-pawn is stopped. But Black's pieces are forced to pay attention to it at all times, and they have to be aware that even a pure opposite bishops endgame might not save them if White's king gets to the queenside first.} 34. Re7 Rc8 35. g3 Ba6 36. c3 Rb8 37. Bb6 Rb7 (37... Rc8 38. Bc7 $1 {Black's rook is dominated, and the king is coming to b6.}) 38. Re8 (38. Rxb7 $5 {In fact, White could already exchange rooks. Of course, the text does not spoil anything.} Bxb7 39. Ke3 Kf5 40. Bd8 $1 {The cleanest. White stops both ...h5-h4 and ...g7-g5, and their king is ready to charge into b6.}) 38... Kf5 39. Ke3 Kg4 40. Kd4 Rd7 41. Rg8 g6 42. Ra8 Bb7 43. Ra7 Kh3 44. a6 Bc6 45. Rxd7 Bxd7 46. Bd8 $1 {Other moves win, but this one is totally idiot proof. Stick the bishop on h4, and no counterplay.} Kxh2 47. Kc5 Bc8 48. a7 Bb7 49. Bh4 Kg2 50. Kd6 Kf3 51. Ke7 {I like this one. White goes after the kingside pawns, taking advantage of Black's bishop being stuck defending against the a-pawn.} Ke4 52. Kxf7 Kf5 53. Ke7 Bc6 54. Kd6 Ba8 55. Bd8 Bb7 56. Kc7 Ba8 57. Kb8 Bc6 58. a8=Q Bxa8 59. Kxa8 Ke4 60. Kb7 Kd3 61. Ba5 Ke3 62. Bd8 Kd3 63. Kc6 Kxc3 64. Kd6 d4 65. Kxe6 d3 66. Ba5+ {Excellent effort from Madaminov. While Black's position should be defensible at the start of the endgame, White made sure that it did not look easy!} 1-0

