1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 8. Ne2 b5 $6 {A slow move like this one will give us exactly what we want.} (8... Qb6 {Black has to go for the direct complications. We still can fight for an edge, but it won't be nearly as easy as it was for Sasi in the game.}) 9. c3 b4 10. cxb4 $1 cxd4 11. Nexd4 Bxb4+ 12. Kf2 {You can train the line here.} Nxd4 13. Bxd4 $1 {Full credit for taking with the bishop on d4. This means Black will have a harder time using the c5-square. Now, Black is in big trouble because White will get to the c-file first, the bishop will be awesome on d3, and Black's bishop on c8 will remain a problem for the rest of the game.} O-O 14. g3 $2 {Black now had one moment and only one moment to get a tenable position.} (14. Rc1 {I would have preferred this move, being a bit more direct. Black keeps all unwanted guests off of c5, and next up is Bd3.}) 14... Bc5 (14... Nc5 $10 {The knight gets to e4, and Black is back in the game. I'm not sure what Rasmus disliked here.}) 15. Rc1 Qa5 16. a3 Bb7 $2 {Black's position is so strategically suspect that it only takes one innocent-looking move for it all to completely fall apart.} 17. b4 $1 {Well calculated by Sasi.} Bxd4+ 18. Qxd4 Qa4 (18... Qxa3 19. Rc7 Bc8 20. Bd3 {Black is a pawn up, but they can barely move, and Ra1 is not messing around.}) 19. Rc7 Bc6 20. Bd3 (20. b5 $5 {Already, this was working.} Bxb5 21. Bxb5 Qxb5 22. a4 {White wins a piece, and the game.}) 20... Rfc8 21. Rxc8+ $1 Rxc8 22. Rc1 {Black is completely ill-equipped to fight on the weak dark squares.} Nf8 (22... Bb7 23. Rxc8+ Bxc8 24. Qc3 $18 {The queen gets to c7, and that is that.} Bb7 25. Qc7 Qc6 26. Qxc6 Bxc6 27. Bxa6 {.}) 23. b5 $1 {Well calculated.} axb5 24. Qxa4 bxa4 25. Ba6 Bb7 (25... Rc7 26. Nd4 $18) 26. Rxc8 Bxa6 27. Rb8 {Sasi did have a hiccup in the early middlegame, but with that one poor decision aside, the rest of the game really showed how questionable Black's position was.} 1-0

