1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 8. Ne2 Be7 (8... Qb6 {Again, I think Black should have gone for this one.}) 9. c3 cxd4 10. Nexd4 $14 {White gets a very pleasant and very typical Steinitz middlegame advantage. One bad piece makes a bad position, and the c8-bishop is Black's bad piece.} Nc5 11. Nb3 b6 {In general, I think Black is not too happy with having a pawn on b6 bolstering a piece on c5. It means they will not be able to play b5.} 12. Be2 O-O 13. O-O Bb7 14. Qe1 a5 15. Nbd4 Ne4 $6 {I don't get why Black let my bishop reach d3.} (15... Nxd4 {This should have been preferred.} 16. Nxd4 Qc7 $1 {Black prevents f5. They are definitely worse, but the game goes on.}) 16. Bd3 Bc5 {Now, I felt it was time to strike.} (16... Nxd4 17. Nxd4 $14 {It's not so easy for Black to prevent f4-f5.}) 17. f5 $5 {If White can ever successfully play f4-f5 in the Steinitz, Black usually is not long for this world. Here, they can still fight back, but are rapidly going to find themselves in only move territory.} (17. Rd1 {My computer wants to wait, but I think f5 is not bad from a human point of view.}) 17... exf5 (17... Nxd4 $1 18. Nxd4 exf5 $1 19. Nxf5 Qc7 $1 20. Bxc5 Qxc5+ 21. Kh1 Bc8 $1 {After a lot of only moves, Black ends up only a little worse.}) 18. Nxf5 Qd7 {At this point, I burned 20 minutes, and calculated out a forced win. It should not come as a big surprise that there is a direct win when Black allows the knight to remain on f5 like this.} 19. Bxc5 $1 bxc5 20. Bxe4 $1 dxe4 21. Ng5 $1 Nxe5 22. Rd1 $1 {I like this one a lot. I immediately poke the queen and ask Black how they want to coordinate.} (22. Nxh7 {This works too, but the text is even stronger.}) 22... Qc7 (22... Nd3 23. Qh4 h6 {Now anything wins, but I had a very nice idea in mind.} 24. Nxh6+ gxh6 25. Rf6 $1 {Black is mated. It's nice that without the knight on e5, they cannot take on g5 and block with the knight on g6.}) 23. Qg3 $1 {The knight does not move now, and White has huge threats on the kingside. Black is collapsing immediately.} Ra6 {Shankland Method time!} 24. Ne6 $1 Nf3+ 25. Rxf3 Qxg3 26. Nh6+ $1 Kh8 (26... gxh6 27. Rxg3+ Kh8 28. Nxf8 $18) 27. Nxf7+ Kg8 28. Nh6+ gxh6 29. Rxf8# {Black did not have to go down quite this hard, but I do think their position was very suspect early on. Once I got f5, they already needed to find a bunch of difficult moves to not die on the spot.} 1-0

