1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 8. Nxd4 Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O a6 11. Nb3 {After the game, Logan mentioned to me that he was very surprised to see the French, but happy to have remembered the idea of playing 11. Nb3. From here, White got a winning position almost immediately.} Bxe3 $6 {As mentioned in the theory section, this is the first inaccuracy. But it is such a human move! And just a couple moves later, the game is more or less over.} 12. Qxe3 b5 13. Bd3 Qb6 14. Qh3 $1 g6 (14... f5 {This was the lesser evil, though after} 15. g4 $1 {, Black's position still looks awful.}) 15. Qg3 (15. Qh6 {I might have stuck with the plan I played against Craig Jones all those years back. h2-h4, and h4-h5 is coming, and Black seems very lost.}) 15... Nc5 16. Nxc5 Qxc5 17. h4 $1 Nb4 18. h5 $18 {All too easy. Logan basically got a free point without having to work at all. Black's king will not survive in the upcoming moves.} Nxd3+ 19. Rxd3 Bb7 20. Qh4 Qc7 21. Rg3 Rac8 {White could have continued playing for mate here and won in fine style, but I like Logan's pragmatic approach.} 22. Qf6 {Now both hxg6 and h5-h6 are mating threats. Black needs to get the queens off immediately.} (22. f5 $1 exf5 23. e6 $1 {This would have been the end of Black's king.}) 22... Qd8 {Now Black avoids mate, but they have to allow a completely lost endgame instead – not exactly much hope of salvation.} 23. hxg6 Qxf6 24. gxh7+ $1 Kh8 25. exf6 $18 {White is obviously winning with two pawns up. Logan had no trouble putting the game away.} d4 26. Ne2 d3 27. Rxd3 Bxg2 28. Rh2 Be4 29. Rd2 Rfd8 30. Rxd8+ Rxd8 31. Nc3 Bxh7 32. Ne4 Kg8 33. Nc5 Bg6 34. Nxa6 Rd4 35. Rf2 Ra4 36. Nc7 b4 37. Kb1 Ra5 38. b3 Be4 39. Ne8 Rd5 40. Kc1 Kh7 41. Rd2 Ra5 42. Nd6 Bd5 43. Nxf7 Kg6 44. Ne5+ Kxf6 45. Kb2 Kf5 46. Rd4 Be4 47. Nc4 Rd5 48. Ne3+ 1-0

