1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 ({The main moves, by far, are} 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({,} 3... Bb4) ({and} 3... dxe4 {. But Black does have a couple of extraneous tries that you do want to be ready for.})) 3... Nc6 $6 {This one looks really dumb to me.} 4. Nf3 {If you want to look for maximum punishment, e4-e5 is probably a bit stronger. But I like keeping things simple against the sidelines.} Nf6 5. e5 Ne4 6. Ne2 $1 {[%cal Ge2g3,Gf1d3]} ({White has a very easy plan to play} 6. Ne2 Z0 7. Ng3 {and} Z0 8. Bd3 {, when the knight on e4 will be pressured. It looks quite unpleasant for Black to me.}) 6... f6 {Nothing else makes any sense. At least here, Black is pretending to attack the center.} 7. Ng3 $1 fxe5 8. dxe5 Be7 9. c3 $1 ({A final preparatory move gets us ready for} 9. c3 Z0 10. Bd3 {. Now, we will be able to keep the bishop on its best diagonal in the event of ...Nc5.}) 9... Nc5 ({At least now,} 9... Nc5 10. Bb5 {is not a total disaster for Black anymore. Still, the position is quite bad for them.}) 10. h4 $1 O-O 11. Be3 $1 {[%cal Gd1c2,Ge1c1]} ({White has a simple plan:} 11. Be3 Z0 12. Qc2 {, castle long,} Z0 13. O-O-O {, attack. Black still has a lot of problems to solve.}) *

