1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 ({The main moves, by far, are} 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({,} 3... Bb4) ({and} 3... dxe4 {. But Black does have a couple of extraneous tries that you do want to be ready for.})) 3... Nc6 $6 {This one looks really dumb to me.} 4. Nf3 {If you want to look for maximum punishment, e4-e5 is probably a bit stronger. But I like keeping things simple against the sidelines.} Nf6 5. e5 Ne4 6. Ne2 $1 {[%cal Ge2g3,Gf1d3]} ({White has a very easy plan to play} 6. Ne2 Z0 7. Ng3 {and} Z0 8. Bd3 {, when the knight on e4 will be pressured. It looks quite unpleasant for Black to me.}) 6... f6 {Nothing else makes any sense. At least here, Black is pretending to attack the center.} 7. Ng3 $1 fxe5 8. dxe5 Nc5 {This is Black's main move, hoping to stop our bishop from reaching d3. Now, White has an idea worth remembering.} 9. Bb5 $1 {We are very happy to take the knight on c6. Then, when Nd4 comes, Black will be feeling the pain of the weak e6-pawn.} Bd7 10. Bxc6 $1 Bxc6 11. O-O $16 {Black will really struggle to get castled.} ({▷} 11. O-O Be7 {always runs into} ({▷ If Black plays with} 11... Qd7 {instead, again,} 12. Nd4 {becomes an issue, and White will soon launch their pawns with a2-a4 and b2-b4.}) 12. Nd4 {, and} Z0 13. b4 {becomes a huge threat that Black has to respond to. Then, White can get the queen to f3 and disallow castling.}) *

