1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 ({The main moves, by far, are} 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({,} 3... Bb4) ({and} 3... dxe4 {. But Black does have a couple of extraneous tries that you do want to be ready for.})) 3... a6 {This is Black's most credible sideline, and I actually lost a painful game to Emilio Cordova from this position in 2024.That said, it had everything to do with my blundering a pawn in the center, and nothing to do with the danger of Black's opening choice.} 4. Bd3 {This seems easy enough to me. In general, I like avoiding Nf3 when possible because then Black can play ...Nf6 and aim to reach a Steinitz where we can no longer play f4.} Bb4 5. e5 $16 {White has a huge version of the Winawer. a6 is really out of place, and Bd3 is obviously a very useful move.} *

