1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 ({The main moves, by far, are} 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({,} 3... Bb4) ({and} 3... dxe4 {. But Black does have a couple of extraneous tries that you do want to be ready for.})) 3... a6 {This is Black's most credible sideline, and I actually lost a painful game to Emilio Cordova from this position in 2024.That said, it had everything to do with my blundering a pawn in the center, and nothing to do with the danger of Black's opening choice.} 4. Bd3 {This seems easy enough to me. In general, I like avoiding Nf3 when possible because then Black can play ...Nf6 and aim to reach a Steinitz where we can no longer play f4.} Nf6 $2 5. e5 $1 Nfd7 6. Nce2 $1 {White gets a fantastic version of the 7...a6 Steinitz. Not having to play Be3 is super helpful.} c5 7. c3 Nc6 8. f4 Qb6 {This is the exact kind of position where we are happy the bishop is still on c1, and White can develop in other ways. Black is not hitting b2!} 9. Nf3 $16 ({White castles next,} 9. Nf3 Z0 10. O-O {, and Black's position is miserable.}) *

