1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 ({The main moves, by far, are} 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({,} 3... Bb4) ({and} 3... dxe4 {. But Black does have a couple of extraneous tries that you do want to be ready for.})) 3... a6 {This is Black's most credible sideline, and I actually lost a painful game to Emilio Cordova from this position in 2024.That said, it had everything to do with my blundering a pawn in the center, and nothing to do with the danger of Black's opening choice.} 4. Bd3 {This seems easy enough to me. In general, I like avoiding Nf3 when possible because then Black can play ...Nf6 and aim to reach a Steinitz where we can no longer play f4.} c5 {At least now Black can play this move without losing a pawn directly because we no longer have a check on b5.} 5. dxc5 $1 Bxc5 6. exd5 exd5 7. Nxd5 $1 {The knight is immune to capture thanks to the check on b5.} Bxf2+ $1 {Now Black can safely take back on d5, as Bb5 will not be a thing.} 8. Kxf2 Qxd5 9. Qf3 (9. Nf3 {is good too, but this strikes me as simpler. The queens are exchanged, and White retains a nice position thanks to the bishop pair and imbalanced structure.}) 9... Nf6 (9... Qd4+ 10. Qe3+ $14 {Queens come off all the same, with similar consequences.}) 10. Qxd5 Nxd5 11. a3 $14 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gc2c4,Gb2b4,Gc1b2] Stopping any hope of ...Nb4. White has an easy plan with Nf3, c2-c4, b2-b4, and Bb2.} *

