1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 ({The main moves, by far, are} 3. Nc3 Nf6 ({,} 3... Bb4) ({and} 3... dxe4 {. But Black does have a couple of extraneous tries that you do want to be ready for.})) 3... a6 {This is Black's most credible sideline, and I actually lost a painful game to Emilio Cordova from this position in 2024.That said, it had everything to do with my blundering a pawn in the center, and nothing to do with the danger of Black's opening choice.} 4. Bd3 {This seems easy enough to me. In general, I like avoiding Nf3 when possible because then Black can play ...Nf6 and aim to reach a Steinitz where we can no longer play f4.} c5 {At least now Black can play this move without losing a pawn directly because we no longer have a check on b5.} 5. dxc5 $1 Bxc5 6. exd5 exd5 7. Nxd5 $1 {The knight is immune to capture thanks to the check on b5.} Bxf2+ $1 {Now Black can safely take back on d5, as Bb5 will not be a thing.} 8. Kxf2 Qxd5 9. Nf3 (9. Nf3 Z0 10. Re1+ {comes next, and Black doesn't get castled that easily.}) 9... Nf6 10. Re1+ $1 Be6 11. Ng5 $1 {Nxe6 is already hanging over Black's head.} Nc6 (11... O-O 12. Nxe6 $1 fxe6 13. Kg1 $16 {Black has nothing to show for the bishop pair and the lousy structure.}) 12. Bf1 $1 ({I like being a little patient with respect to taking on e6. Now,} 12. Bf1 Qf5+ {is less of an issue.}) (12. Nxe6 fxe6 13. Bf1 Qf5+ 14. Kg1 Ng4 15. Qf3 Qxf3 16. gxf3 Nge5 17. Bg2 O-O 18. f4 Ng6 {.}) 12... Qxd1 (12... Qf5+ $6 13. Kg1 Ng4 $2 {This would almost equalize if White had already taken on e6. As is, the knight covering f3 becomes very relevant.} 14. Qf3 $1 Qxf3 15. gxf3 Nge5 16. f4 $1 {White wins a piece on the e-file with f4-f5 coming next.}) 13. Rxd1 Bf5 14. Re1+ Kf8 15. c3 $14 {White has pleasant long term pressure thanks to the bishop pair, the imbalanced pawn structure, and the difficulty of including the h8-rook in the game.} *

