1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nf6 {This one feels strange to me. I don't get the point behind wanting not take back with the knight on f6.} 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 {I was surprised to find that there are some 800 games in the Mega DB with this move. It looks so bad! But it's not that straightforward to knock out of the park. White should at least have some sense of what they are doing.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Bc4 $1 {I like this one. d4-d5 is always hanging over Black's head, much like we saw in the ...gxf6 Caro-Kann.} Rg8 {This one is mildly tricky, but White is good to go if they know one key idea.} 8. Bf4 $1 {We don't allow ...e6-e5. Black is not able to take on g2, as then Bf4-g3 will leave their rook trapped.} ({After} 8. O-O e5 $1 {, this would fall for Black's trap. White is still better here, but it's not as clean as I would like.}) 8... Bd6 9. Bg3 Ne7 {Now, the only point White should be aware of is that once we have committed to Bf4-g3, castling short is too slow.Black will get his counterplay, and we won't be able to force ...c7-c6. But Black's last several moves have all clearly been geared against us castling short. We can simply go the other way.} 10. Qe2 Ng6 (10... b6 $2 {It's already too late for this one.} 11. Qe4 {White wins a pawn.}) 11. O-O-O $16 {Good move! Black has no counterplay at all and is clearly worse thanks to White's extra space in the center and lead in development.} *

