[PlyCount "25"]
[GameId "2288537762456183"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nf6 {This one feels strange to me. I don't get the point behind wanting not take back with the knight on f6.} 5. Nxf6+ gxf6 {I was surprised to find that there are some 800 games in the Mega DB with this move. It looks so bad! But it's not that straightforward to knock out of the park. White should at least have some sense of what they are doing.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Bc4 $1 {I like this one. d4-d5 is always hanging over Black's head, much like we saw in the ...gxf6 Caro-Kann.} b6 ({This is the main, but I think that} 7... Rg8 {is actually a better move.}) (7... Ne7 8. O-O b6 9. Qe2 {transposes to 7...b6.}) 8. O-O {Now, d4-d5 is a real idea, and Black should respect it. White's plan is simple: Qe2, Rd1, slam d4-d5.Black will probably have to play ...Ne7 at some point. When they do, it is important to know that we will want to bring the bishop to b5 before Black can castle long, as provoking ...c7-c6 to shut down the b7-bishop is very important.} a6 9. Qe2 {For simplicity's sake, I like playing the same move here as well.} b5 10. Bd3 Bb7 11. c3 $16 {While Black managed not to play ...c7-c6, it came at a high cost. If they ever castle long, a2-a4 will always be there to ruin their day.} Qd5 12. Bf4 Bd6 13. Bg3 $1 (13. Bg3 Z0 14. a4 {is coming next, and Black has nothing to show for their badly loosened pawn structure.}) *

