1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nf6 {This one feels strange to me. I don't get the point behind wanting not take back with the knight on f6.} 5. Nxf6+ Qxf6 6. Nf3 Bd7 7. Bg5 $1 Qg6 8. Bd3 $1 {White continues developing at an alarming rate.} Qh5 9. h3 $16 {[%cal Gf7f6] And now Black even has to weaken their position with ...f7-f6 to avoid getting their queen trapped. They are much worse.} *

