1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nf6 {This one feels strange to me. I don't get the point behind wanting not take back with the knight on f6.} 5. Nxf6+ Qxf6 6. Nf3 h6 ({A sad necessity to prevent Black from falling even further behind in development.} 6... Bd6 7. Bg5 $16) 7. Bd3 Bd6 {Now, if Black is able to castle, play ...Bd7-c6 and ...Nb8-d7, they shouldn't be in such terrible shape. However, with a little precision, we can cause them a lot of headaches.} (7... c5 $2 {Black should not be trying to open the center with all of their pieces on the back rank} 8. Be3 $16) 8. Qe2 $1 {I like this one, preventing Black from castling.} Bd7 9. O-O Bc6 {If Black had one more move to play ...Nd7, they would be totally fine, but we get there first.} (9... Nc6 10. c3 {transposes to 8...Nc6 here.}) 10. Ne5 $16 {Black's position is miserable. They need to take on e5 right now to avoid getting crushed by f2-f4, but they are still much worse.} Bxe5 11. dxe5 Qe7 12. Bd2 {[%cal Gb2b4,Ga2a4] White is ready to expand on the queenside. Black's king will never find safety; if they castle long, they castle into a pawn storm. If they castle short, they castle into Qh5 and a rook life.The machine claims they are more or less lost, and I won't argue with it. It is not a good sign that it wants to play ...Na6, losing a pawn for nothing on the spot.} *

