1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nf6 {This one feels strange to me. I don't get the point behind wanting not take back with the knight on f6.} 5. Nxf6+ Qxf6 6. Nf3 h6 ({A sad necessity to prevent Black from falling even further behind in development.} 6... Bd6 7. Bg5 $16) 7. Bd3 Bd6 {Now, if Black is able to castle, play ...Bd7-c6 and ...Nb8-d7, they shouldn't be in such terrible shape. However, with a little precision, we can cause them a lot of headaches.} (7... c5 $2 {Black should not be trying to open the center with all of his pieces on the back rank} 8. Be3 $16) 8. Qe2 $1 {I like this one, preventing Black from castling.} Nc6 (8... O-O $2 9. Qe4 $18 {Oops.}) 9. c3 Bd7 10. O-O O-O {At long last, Black can castle, but it doesn't equalize, or even come close.} 11. Re1 $14 {[%cal Gf3e5]} ({Black is hard pressed to deal with} 11. Re1 Z0 12. Ne5 {next. They should be very precise to avoid an unpleasant position instead of being dead lost.}) *

