1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Bd7 {This is known as the Fort Knox variation. It is extremely popular in blitz due to its solid nature and the fact that Black's first several moves tend to be the same no matter what White does.They want ...Bc6, ...Nd7, and ...Ngf6. They will be quite happy if they can trade their light-squared bishop and one of their knights for our 2 knights. We'd have the bishop pair, but once Black goes ...c7-c6, their position becomes annoyingly solid.That said, it definitely does not equalize, and I can't remember ever seeing someone strong play it in classical chess. White is slightly better, more or less, no matter what they do, but I like that there is a way to get Black out of their comfort zone, and where automatic moves no longer work.} 5. Bd3 $1 Bc6 6. Ne2 $1 {This is my big idea. My point is that by choosing the e2-square for the knight, Black is unable to trade off two sets of minor pieces.} ({Of course,} 6. Nf3 {looks more natural, but after} Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Nxf6 {. In general, I think Black's position is annoyingly solid if they are allowed to play ...Bxf3 next, followed by ...c7-c6. I would much rather have the knight on e2 instead, to not let Black get rid of the c6-bishop so easily.}) 6... Nd7 7. O-O Bxe4 $2 {Giving up this bishop does not work for Black unless they can also swap a pair of knights. Here, they will fail to do so.} 8. Bxe4 c6 9. g3 $1 Ngf6 10. Bg2 Be7 11. c4 $16 {[%cal Ge2c3,Gd4d5] White is ready for Ne2-c3 and d4-d5 to open the position. The extra space means that an extra pair of knights on the board is heavily in our favor.We can bring the e2-knight to c3 to help orchestrate the d4-d5 advance, but Black's d7-knight cannot also use the f6-square.} *

