1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Bd7 {This is known as the Fort Knox variation. It is extremely popular in blitz due to its solid nature and the fact that Black's first several moves tend to be the same no matter what White does.They want ...Bc6, ...Nd7, and ...Ngf6. They will be quite happy if they can trade their light-squared bishop and one of their knights for our 2 knights. We'd have the bishop pair, but once Black goes ...c7-c6, their position becomes annoyingly solid.That said, it definitely does not equalize, and I can't remember ever seeing someone strong play it in classical chess. White is slightly better, more or less, no matter what they do, but I like that there is a way to get Black out of their comfort zone, and where automatic moves no longer work.} 5. Bd3 $1 Bc6 6. Ne2 $1 {This is my big idea. My point is that by choosing the e2-square for the knight, Black is unable to trade off two sets of minor pieces.} ({Of course,} 6. Nf3 {looks more natural, but after} Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Nxf6 {. In general, I think Black's position is annoyingly solid if they are allowed to play ...Bxf3 next, followed by ...c7-c6. I would much rather have the knight on e2 instead, to not let Black get rid of the c6-bishop so easily.}) 6... Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Qxf6 $2 9. c4 $1 {Black's bishop on c6 is becoming a real problem. This whole line is dependent upon Black being able to trade them off, and when they is still on the board, things get tough for the second player.} Bd6 10. c5 $1 Be7 11. b4 $1 {Just imagine this position without the c6-bishop and e2-knight, Black would be more than fine. As is, they have major problems with the bishop.} a6 12. a4 O-O 13. Be3 $16 {[%cal Gb4b5,Ge2c3]} (13. Be3 Z0 14. b5 {, and Nc3 will come sooner rather than later.}) *

