1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Bd7 {This is known as the Fort Knox variation. It is extremely popular in blitz due to its solid nature and the fact that Black's first several moves tend to be the same no matter what White does.They want ...Bc6, ...Nd7, and ...Ngf6. They will be quite happy if they can trade their light-squared bishop and one of their knights for our 2 knights. We'd have the bishop pair, but once Black goes ...c7-c6, their position becomes annoyingly solid.That said, it definitely does not equalize, and I can't remember ever seeing someone strong play it in classical chess. White is slightly better, more or less, no matter what they do, but I like that there is a way to get Black out of their comfort zone, and where automatic moves no longer work.} 5. Bd3 $1 Bc6 6. Ne2 $1 {This is my big idea. My point is that by choosing the e2-square for the knight, Black is unable to trade off two sets of minor pieces.} ({Of course,} 6. Nf3 {looks more natural, but after} Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Nxf6 {. In general, I think Black's position is annoyingly solid if they are allowed to play ...Bxf3 next, followed by ...c7-c6. I would much rather have the knight on e2 instead, to not let Black get rid of the c6-bishop so easily.}) 6... Nd7 7. O-O Ngf6 8. Nxf6+ Nxf6 9. c4 Be7 10. Qc2 O-O 11. Be3 h6 12. h3 $14 {[%cal Ge2c3,Ga1d1,Gf1e1] I think White has a really nice position here. The plan will be Nc3, and rooks to d1 and e1. Eventually, we can even drop back with Qc1 and look for a sacrifice on h6.In the meantime, Black's inability to exchange their light-squared bishop makes their position much harder to play than it is if we were to have developed with Ng1-f3 instead.They can't play ...c7-c6. So, they can't play ...Qd8-a5 or ...b7-b6. Where does the queen go? It's not easy to see what Black is doing.} *

