1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Be7 {This move is legal, but it feels a bit strange to me, like a poor cousin of ...Nbd7.} 5. Nf3 Nf6 (5... Nd7 6. Bd3 Ngf6 7. Nxf6+ Nxf6 {transposes to the main line.}) 6. Nxf6+ Bxf6 7. c3 O-O 8. Bd3 $14 {[%cal Gc1f4,Gd1e2,Ge1c1] I don't really get what the bishop is doing on f6. Black clearly will not get ...e6-e5 in, and otherwise, it is not harder for them to defend his kingside or to play ...c7-c5. I would think ...Nd7-f6 was a better coordination, and there is a reason it is the main line.White can follow up with Bf4, Qe2, and O-O-O, and I would be very worried already if I were Black here.} *

