1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Be7 {This move is legal, but it feels a bit strange to me, like a poor cousin of ...Nbd7.} 5. Nf3 Nf6 (5... Nd7 6. Bd3 Ngf6 7. Nxf6+ Nxf6 {transposes to the main line.}) 6. Nxf6+ Bxf6 7. c3 Nd7 8. Bf4 $1 {I like this move order the most. Black will not play ...e6-e5, and if they play ...c7-c5, we will be glad to castle long and not lose a tempo with the bishop on d3.} (8. Bd3 c5 $1 {This one is a bit annoying. White is probably a little better, but they will have to accept an isolani. I prefer keeping things simpler and more risk-free.}) 8... c5 9. dxc5 (9. g4 $6 {I am not totally convinced by this one, but it is an interesting try for White if you don't want to allow an endgame so fast.}) 9... Nxc5 10. Qxd8+ Bxd8 11. O-O-O $14 {White maintains pressure well into the endgame. Black will have a hard time completing his development.} *

