1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Nxf6+ {Much like we saw in the Caro, I often want to resolve the tension as soon as possible on f6.} Nxf6 7. Bd3 {Now, I like that Black gets a very dangerous position if they continue with ...Be7 and ...O-O, which looks the most natural if someone is freestyling. They are provoked into a confrontation right away.} Bd7 {If Black does not want to enter the sharp positions after ...c7-c5, this one makes some sense. They are hoping for ...Bc6 next, to give the bishop, and play a Fort Knox structure. I will not allow it.} 8. Ne5 $1 {No ...Bc6 for you!} c5 9. dxc5 Bxc5 10. O-O Qc7 11. Qe2 $14 {White can take the bishop pair whenever they want it, and it is very important that we have an imbalanced and more open pawn structure.As a result, Black is not going to be able to sit tight and let you bash your head against the wall as you struggle to open the game. The machine only gives 0.2 or so, but it looks very unpleasant for Black to me, and in practice, his results have been dreadful.} *

