1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Nxf6+ {Much like we saw in the Caro, I often want to resolve the tension as soon as possible on f6.} Nxf6 7. Bd3 {Now, I like that Black gets a very dangerous position if they continue with ...Be7 and ...O-O, which looks the most natural if someone is freestyling. They are provoked into a confrontation right away.} b6 ({This move exists, but it strikes me as a bad version of} 7... Bd7 {.If Black's plan is ...Bb7 and to take on f3, I think they want their pawn back on b7. Regardless, I won't allow it.}) 8. Qe2 Bb7 9. Ne5 $1 {No taking on f3 for you!} a6 10. Be3 $14 ({White is ready for} 10. Be3 Z0 11. O-O-O {next, with a typical 'attacking' position.}) *

