1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 dxe4 {The Rubinstein French has never been that popular, but it's a tough nut to crack. Black's position is definitely under pressure and there are a lot of lines to know for them, but it's not like he is automatically crushed or anything.In my own experience, I have had trouble showing much of anything the few times it has been played against me. That said, for this course, I found some stuff that I liked.} 4. Nxe4 ({Now, Black's main move by far is} 4. Nxe4 Nd7 {, with the plan of playing} 5. Z0 Ngf6 {next. But there are some extraneous options that White should be aware of.}) 4... Nd7 5. Nf3 Ngf6 6. Nxf6+ {Much like we saw in the Caro, I often want to resolve the tension as soon as possible on f6.} Nxf6 7. Bd3 {Now, I like that Black gets a very dangerous position if they continue with ...Be7 and ...O-O, which looks the most natural if someone is freestyling. They are provoked into a confrontation right away.} c5 $1 8. Be3 Nd5 {This was Black's main move for a while, but not so much anymore. Still, White should be precise to show an edge. If Black can complete his development without any incidents, they will definitely be fine, so energetic play is allowed.} 9. O-O $1 {White pays no heed to the bishop on e3. By all means, please take it and open the f-file.} Be7 10. dxc5 $1 {An important move. White goes pawn-grabbing and will remain a clean pawn up if they are allowed to play Be3-d4 next. As such, Black is forced to take on e3.} Nxe3 11. fxe3 Qa5 $2 {This one is met in the same manner.} 12. Ne5 $1 {White is smashing through. Black needs to give the f7-pawn to avoid losing on the spot.} Qxc5 13. Nxf7 O-O 14. Qg4 $1 {An important resource. White gets their queen in a good attacking position, counting on ...Rxf7 failing to tactics.} *

