1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Ng8 {This move is hard to take seriously. Nc3-b1 would lead to the Advance French, which is a serious attempt to fight for an advantage. And think about how bad Nb1 looks here! It is probably White's 10th best move or something like this.} 5. Nf3 ({I like this more than} 5. f4 {. As we will see in the Winawer with Bb4-f8, f2-f4 makes a bit less sense if Black's knight is on g8 rather than d7.}) 5... c5 {I can scarcely imagine Black playing anything else.} 6. Be3 cxd4 {Black is not thrilled about resolving the tension, but they did not have much of a choice.} (6... Nc6 7. dxc5 $16 {Tell me why the knight is on g8 instead of e7? White is a clean pawn up already.}) 7. Qxd4 $1 Nc6 8. Qf4 $16 (8. Qf4 Z0 9. O-O-O {comes next, and White has a huge position.}) *

