1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 a6 {This is Black's next most common move.} 6. Nf3 ({And now they play} 6. Nf3 c5 {.}) ({We can go} 6. Be3 {, transposing to the main lines, but}) (6. Nce2 {is also worth a shot.}) 6... c5 7. Ne2 $5 (7. Be3 {transposes to the main lines.}) 7... Nc6 8. c3 $14 {White is arguing that Bc1-e3 was not the most useful move in this structure, and that is a very fair argument to make. They should be better with h2-h4 coming next, much like we see in the mainline with 7...a6.} *

