1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 b6 $2 {It makes some sense for Black to try to exchange off the light-squared bishop, but this is way too optimistic.} 7. f5 $1 {Surprisingly, this move has only been played twice. The machine claims that Black is borderline lost!} exf5 8. Nxd5 $18 {[%cal Gc1f4,Ge5e6,Gc1g5] Black will be smashed by Bc1-f4, followed by e5-e6, or simply Bc1-g5.} *

