1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Be7 {This move is unlikely to have independent value, but if Black wants a plan with ...b7-b6, this is the best move order.} 7. Be3 a6 {Black can also try this one. As usual, whenever they make a move that does not develop a new piece or influence the center, we go for Nc3-e2.} 8. Ne2 ({Black has nothing better than} 8. Ne2 Nc6 {, transposing back to the mainline with 7...a6.}) 8... b5 {I suppose this could be an attempt at independant value, but it doesn't do much.} 9. c3 $14 {I really don't see Black avoiding ...Nc6 here. Our plan is the same as always, h2-h4, and gain kingside space.} *

