1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Be7 {This move is unlikely to have independent value, but if Black wants a plan with ...b7-b6, this is the best move order.} 7. Be3 b6 ({Now, at least Black is not immediately smashed by ...f7-f5. But they are definitely still worse.} 7... Nc6 {transposes to the mainline with 7...Be7.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {I find that a good rule of thumb is that whenever Black plays ...a7-a6, ...b7-b6, or some move that generally does not contest the center, it tends to be a good idea to follow up with Nc3-e2.Nce2 is a very good coordination for White, but it can run into concrete issues with ...Qb6 and attacking the center. Once Black plays a move that does not mesh well with a direct central assault, Nc3-e2 makes a lot of sense.} Ba6 9. c3 Nc6 10. h4 {As usual, once we play with Nce2, the best plan will be to go for h2-h4 next to gain kingside space.} Rc8 {The machine gives this move as best, basically just waiting. I get it, but I also can't believe they can seriously fight for equality.} 11. h5 h6 12. g4 $16 {Black is suffering badly with less space, and f4-f5 could be coming next.} *

