1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Qb6 8. Be3 $1 ({Black can't take on b2. So, they should play} 8. Be3 Nc6 {and transpose to the main line.}) 8... Qxb2 $2 9. Ndb5 $18 {[%cal Gb5c7,Ga1b1] White wins material thanks to the twin threats of Nc7+ and Rb1.} *

