1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 cxd4 7. Nxd4 Bc5 8. Be3 ({I don't see Black avoiding} 8. Be3 Nc6 {here.}) 8... a6 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O b5 {I guess if they are hell-bent on not playing ...Nc6, this could be a sensible way to continue. But it is not intimidating.} 11. Bd3 $1 {It's nice that we can play this move without having to bother with Qf2 first! Black should be regretting the lack of pressure on the d4-square.} b4 12. Na4 Bxd4 13. Bxd4 a5 14. c4 $5 {I like that White can pressure both sides of the board. They are better after simple moves like Kb1 or g2-g4 as well, but this one appeals to me a lot.} bxc3 15. Qxc3 Ba6 16. Kb1 $14 {Black has a lot of queenside weaknesses and will have a hard time fighting for the c-file. And we are absolutely not afraid of being checkmated.} *

