1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Rb8 {I once faced this strange-looking move against Zhou Weiqi in 2013. In the meantime, it has not picked up much popularity, although Bluebaum has made it work in some blitz events. It is not totally ridiculous; Black wants ...b7-b5 someday, and without having to play ...a7-a6.} 8. h4 $5 ({I like responding in kind with a waiting move. If Black plays} 8. h4 a6 {, we know what to do.}) 8... a6 9. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we meet ...a7-a6 with Ne2.} b5 10. c3 b4 11. Kf2 $14 {...a7-a6 is more than a wasted move; the pawn is affirmatively worse on a6 than it would be on a7. Black is missing ...Ba6 as a resource. White is better with the space advantage.} *

