1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Rb8 {I once faced this strange-looking move against Zhou Weiqi in 2013. In the meantime, it has not picked up much popularity, although Bluebaum has made it work in some blitz events. It is not totally ridiculous; Black wants ...b7-b5 someday, and without having to play ...a7-a6.} 8. h4 $5 ({I like responding in kind with a waiting move. If Black plays} 8. h4 a6 {, we know what to do.}) 8... cxd4 {The machine wants this one.} 9. Nxd4 Bc5 10. Qd2 O-O 11. Be2 {I like keeping it simple. White makes a useful move and will decide where to castle next.} (11. O-O-O {Now, Black can at least argue that they tricked us into playing h2-h4 in the main line of the 8...cxd4 9. Nxd4 Bc5 variation. Indeed, this is not ideal, but White should be better all the same.} Bxd4 12. Bxd4 Qa5 13. Kb1 (13. Nb1 Qxa2 14. Rh3 Qa4 15. Bg1 a5 16. Ra3) {.} 13... b5 14. Ne2 b4 {.}) 11... a6 12. O-O-O $1 {Now Black is missing the ...Bxd4 and ...Qa5 plan.} Nxd4 13. Bxd4 b5 14. Bxc5 Nxc5 15. b4 $5 {Why not? I am not too worried we will get mated here, and I like dominating the dark squares.} Nd7 16. h5 Bb7 17. h6 g6 18. Rh3 $14 {White has good play on the dark squares, and there is no universe where Black will ever make us regret the slightly opened queenside.} *

