1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Qb6 $6 {This is the poor cousin of ...cxd4, followed by ...Qb6. Now, there is a major difference on clear display.} 8. Na4 $1 Qa5+ 9. c3 $1 {If ...cxd4 and Nxd4 had already been included, Black could swap on d4 and play ...b7-b5, winning material. As is, we will have an important move in between moves.} c4 10. b4 $1 Qc7 11. Be2 $16 {Black is strategically busted. They have no space, can never open the queenside, and can only wait for the kingside disaster to come.We'll see a nice effort from Najer in the illustrative games section.} *

