1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Qb6 $6 {This is the poor cousin of ...cxd4, followed by ...Qb6. Now, there is a major difference on clear display.} 8. Na4 $1 Qa5+ 9. c3 $1 {If ...cxd4 and Nxd4 had already been included, Black could swap on d4 and play ...b7-b5, winning material. As is, we will have an important move in between moves.} c4 10. b4 $1 Nxb4 {I think Black really has to try giving the piece to make some kind of counterplay, but it definitely will not save them.} 11. cxb4 Bxb4+ 12. Kf2 b5 13. Nb2 {[%cal Gd1c2,Gf4f5] Now, the key part is to understand what White is trying to do, and what they are not trying to do.It is very important to realize that the plan is NOT to try to contain Black's queenside pawns and consolidate the extra piece, which might be the first instinct that some would have. It is to smash the kingside with f4-f5 at the right moment, and point out that White has one more piece to fight on that side of the board.For the most part, Black's queenside pawn mass should be ignored. The b5- and c4-pawns make a nice chain that restricts our pieces to a degree, but they are never a danger to advance further. So, we just beat them on the side of the board where they don't have a pawn mass.} Bc3 {This was been most common in the past.} 14. Qc2 {Of course the queen goes to c2.} b4 15. Rc1 $18 {[%cal Gb2d1]} (15. Rc1 Z0 16. Nd1 {is coming, and Black is done for. We'll see a nice example of how this position can play out in the illustrative games section.}) *

