1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Qb6 $6 {This is the poor cousin of ...cxd4, followed by ...Qb6. Now, there is a major difference on clear display.} 8. Na4 $1 Qa5+ 9. c3 $1 {If ...cxd4 and Nxd4 had already been included, Black could swap on d4 and play ...b7-b5, winning material. As is, we will have an important move in between moves.} cxd4 10. b4 $1 (10. Nxd4 $2 {This would fall for Black's trap.} Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 $1) 10... Nxb4 {Some time back, this was a real line for Black. But in recent times, modern engines have crushed it.} 11. cxb4 Bxb4+ 12. Bd2 Bxd2+ 13. Nxd2 b6 ({This was historically the most common move. Black is obviously hoping for} 13... b6 14. Z0 Nc5 {next, pressuring our knight on a4 and trying to build a big center. We do not care.}) 14. Bd3 Nc5 15. Nxc5 bxc5 16. O-O {Black's three pawns might look intimidating, but they are actually not great. ...c5-c4 loses on the spot, but it's not easy for Black to prepare it either because castling will get mated.} O-O 17. Rc1 $1 {This one is quite clever. White compels Black to play ...c5-c4 by threatening Nb3, and then we can go for the Greek gift.} (17. Bxh7+ {This is also good for White, but the text is even stronger.} Kxh7 18. Qh5+ Kg8 19. Nf3 g6 20. Qh6 Qc7 {.}) 17... c4 18. Bxh7+ $1 Kxh7 19. Qh5+ Kg8 20. Nf3 g6 (20... f6 21. exf6 $18 {Black's king still does not survive.}) 21. Qh6 Qc7 ({Now, the machine claims that} 21... Qc7 22. f5 {crushes. Of course it does, but there are a lot of messy lines not worth memorizing. I like keeping things simple.}) 22. Nxd4 $1 {[%cal Gc1c3,Gf1f3] White grabs one of the dangerous pawns, and now is ready for a rook life with either rook. The machine claims Black is mated on the spot if they do not touch the f-pawn, but touching it will not offer much relief.} f6 23. Qxg6+ Qg7 24. Qb1 $16 (24. Qb1 Z0 25. Rc3 {is coming. Black should lose.}) *

