[PlyCount "31"]
[GameId "2288537762624183"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Qb6 $6 {This is the poor cousin of ...cxd4, followed by ...Qb6. Now, there is a major difference on clear display.} 8. Na4 $1 Qa5+ 9. c3 $1 {If ...cxd4 and Nxd4 had already been included, Black could swap on d4 and play ...b7-b5, winning material. As is, we will have an important move in between moves.} cxd4 10. b4 $1 (10. Nxd4 $2 {This would fall for Black's trap.} Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 $1) 10... Nxb4 {Some time back, this was a real line for Black. But in recent times, modern engines have crushed it.} 11. cxb4 Bxb4+ 12. Bd2 Bxd2+ 13. Nxd2 g5 $2 {This has been played a bunch of times, but it is the easiest of all of Black's moves to disarm.} 14. Rb1 $1 {No ...b7-b5 for you! And Bb5 is on the way.} a6 15. Bd3 gxf4 16. O-O $18 {[%cal Ga4b6,Gd7e5] The inclusion of Ra1-b1 and ...a7-a6 has weakened the b6-square. As such, Black cannot take on e4. So, they are done for.} *

