1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Qb6 $6 {This is the poor cousin of ...cxd4, followed by ...Qb6. Now, there is a major difference on clear display.} 8. Na4 $1 Qa5+ 9. c3 $1 {If ...cxd4 and Nxd4 had already been included, Black could swap on d4 and play ...b7-b5, winning material. As is, we will have an important move in between moves.} cxd4 10. b4 $1 (10. Nxd4 $2 {This would fall for Black's trap.} Nxd4 11. Bxd4 b5 $1) 10... Nxb4 {Some time back, this was a real line for Black. But in recent times, modern engines have crushed it.} 11. cxb4 Bxb4+ 12. Bd2 Bxd2+ 13. Nxd2 O-O ({Now, Black is ready for counterplay with either} 13... O-O 14. Z0 b6 {and} 15. Z0 Nc5 {or 14...f6. White should be energetic.}) 14. Bd3 $1 b5 15. Nb2 Nb6 {Now, if Black is able to successfully play Nc4, they will get a lot of counterplay. We do not want to have to take on c4 and give them a huge pawn mass. White does need to be precise here, but they also are winning.} 16. O-O $1 Nc4 17. Bxh7+ Kxh7 18. Qh5+ Kg8 ({Now, the computer finds mate with} 18... Kg8 19. Nbxc4 {, but once more, there are a lot of messy moves that I think are not worth memorizing. A simple solution is quite fine.}) 19. Nf3 $1 g6 20. Qh6 Qc7 21. Nd3 $1 {Nice and simple. White saves the piece and does not allow Black to get a pawn mass.} a5 {This was my attempt at a human move. Black might want to go ...b5-b4 next to play ...Ba6, and they can consider ...Ra7 as a way to defend along the 7th rank. White has no shortage of winning continuations, but I like a funny one.} 22. Nc5 $1 ({Very simple. I want} 22. Nc5 Z0 23. Ng5 {next, and after} f6 {and} 24. Z0 Qg7 {, I want to have Nxe6+ at the end. And there's not much Black can do about it.}) 22... b4 23. Ng5 $1 f6 24. Qxf8+ (24. Qxg6+ {works too, of course, but this is even more to the point.}) *

