1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Qb6 $6 {This is the poor cousin of ...cxd4, followed by ...Qb6. Now, there is a major difference on clear display.} 8. Na4 $1 Qa5+ 9. c3 $1 {If ...cxd4 and Nxd4 had already been included, Black could swap on d4 and play ...b7-b5, winning material. As is, we will have an important move in between moves.} b6 10. Bd2 $1 {Making the not so subtle threat of c3-c4, and forcing Black to lock the position immediately.} c4 11. b4 $1 Qa6 12. Be2 $16 {We get a position similar to the one with c4 instead of b6. Black is in massive trouble strategically, and eventually the kingside will open.} *

