[PlyCount "41"]
[GameId "2288537762648769"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose the dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Now, in recent times,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think that the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way of playing, and I was happy to have an opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 (6... a6 {transposes to 5...a6 .}) 7. Be3 Qb6 $6 {This is the poor cousin of ...cxd4, followed by ...Qb6. Now, there is a major difference on clear display.} 8. Na4 $1 Qa5+ 9. c3 $1 {If ...cxd4 and Nxd4 had already been included, Black could swap on d4 and play ...b7-b5, winning material. As is, we will have an important move in between moves.} b6 10. Bd2 $1 {Making the not so subtle threat of c3-c4, and forcing Black to lock the position immediately.} c4 11. b4 $1 Nxb4 $1 {This is the best way for Black to sacrifice a piece.} 12. cxb4 Bxb4 13. Qc2 $1 {This is an important move. You want to make sure to have Nc3 available.} Bxd2+ 14. Nxd2 b5 15. Nc3 $1 {Nice that this move is legal! Thanks Qc2.} b4 (15... O-O 16. g3 {same plan.}) 16. Ne2 {[%cal Gf4f5,Gd2f3,Ge2f4,Gf1h3,Gg2g3,Gf3g5] Now, this is very important. Your plan is NOT to try to slowly but surely contain Black's pawns and consolidate the extra piece. You will not be able to challege Black's queenside pawn mass. Your plan is to immediately smash the kingside and use your extra piece to go straight for the king. White's attack should be very dangerous here.} O-O 17. g3 Qb6 18. Nf3 a5 {Black's pawns actually do start moving here, and they get some counterplay. But certainly not enough.} 19. Bh3 a4 {Black tries to make counterplay on the queenside because what else?} 20. a3 $1 {I like this one. You were not going to stop ...b5-b4 no matter what you do, but you can stop ...a4-a3.} (20. O-O b3 21. Qd2 a3 {This is a bit less clear. The pawn reaches b2 without getting captured, which could be a bit much.}) 20... b3 21. Qd2 $16 {Black's pawns are forever blockaded, and the kingside assault is coming.} *

