1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose their dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Recently,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way to play, and I was happy to have the opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 {Back when I played the French a little, this move was very popular, and I chose it twice, against Robson and Salem, reaching good positions both games. White should be a bit careful here, but they are definitely better if they know what they are doing.} 8. Qd2 (8. Ne2 $2 {This only ever works if Black has burned a tempo on ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6.} Qb6 9. Qc1 O-O 10. c3 f6 {Black has way too much counterplay here. A tempo is a huge deal in such a sharp position.}) 8... a6 {This is a slightly tricky move order that I once used to beat Wesley So. Black says that they want to play an ...a7-a6 plan without allowing Nc3-e2, as Qd2 would be totally out of place then. Still, White should have a nice position.} 9. dxc5 {White has other good moves, but I like sticking with the main plan.} Nxc5 10. h4 $1 {This is a clever move order. We don't necessarily want to castle long right into ...Qa5 and ...b7-b5, when Black can keep the king in the center.} b5 {This feels like the natural follow-up, but now White shows their point.} 11. Qf2 $1 Na4 12. Nxa4 bxa4 13. c3 $16 {Black's queenside structure would make total sense if we had castled long, as they would have a lot of attacking chances. If we castle short, their structure is ridiculous.} *

