1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose their dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Recently,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way to play, and I was happy to have the opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 {Back when I played the French a little, this move was very popular, and I chose it twice, against Robson and Salem, reaching good positions both games. White should be a bit careful here, but they are definitely better if they know what they are doing.} 8. Qd2 (8. Ne2 $2 {This only ever works if Black has burned a tempo on ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6.} Qb6 9. Qc1 O-O 10. c3 f6 {Black has way too much counterplay here. A tempo is a huge deal in such a sharp position.}) 8... a6 {This is a slightly tricky move order that I once used to beat Wesley So. Black says that they want to play an ...a7-a6 plan without allowing Nc3-e2, as Qd2 would be totally out of place then. Still, White should have a nice position.} 9. dxc5 {White has other good moves, but I like sticking with the main plan.} Nxc5 10. h4 $1 {This is a clever move order. We don't necessarily want to castle long right into ...Qa5 and ...b7-b5, when Black can keep the king in the center.} Qa5 {This is Black's best attempt to stay consistent with the plan of delaying castling. Now, we cannot play Qf2.} (10... O-O 11. O-O-O {Back to the main line.}) 11. a3 $146 {I like this one. a2-a3 is generally a useful move for White in the Steinitz, and now it is even more so because of the possibility of b2-b4 coming next.} (11. h5 {is also a good try. I ultimately preferred the text.} h6 12. a3 b5 13. Nd4 Nxd4 14. b4 Qc7 15. Qxd4 Nd7 16. Bd3 {.}) 11... O-O $2 12. Nd4 $1 {This is an even worse version for Black. They will have real trouble getting ...b7-b5 in, and we will not be forced to play b2-b4.} Nxd4 13. Bxd4 $1 {No need for b2-b4 this time! White is much better thanks to the weak dark squares.} *

