1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose their dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Recently,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way to play, and I was happy to have the opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 {Back when I played the French a little, this move was very popular, and I chose it twice, against Robson and Salem, reaching good positions both games. White should be a bit careful here, but they are definitely better if they know what they are doing.} 8. Qd2 (8. Ne2 $2 {This only ever works if Black has burned a tempo on ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6.} Qb6 9. Qc1 O-O 10. c3 f6 {Black has way too much counterplay here. A tempo is a huge deal in such a sharp position.}) 8... O-O 9. dxc5 $1 ({This is a very necessary exchange to make. If you castle long first,} 9. O-O-O {, Black will be able to close the position with} c4 {and launch the b-pawn, with a very dangerous attack.}) 9... Qa5 {This one is a bit tricky, but not too strong. White is good to go, but they should be careful.} 10. a3 $1 {I like this one, immediately prompting Black to take on c5.} (10. O-O-O $2 b6 $1 {This actually gives Black a lot of counterplay.}) 10... Nxc5 11. Nd4 $14 {White has a great position. They are already threatening Nxc6 and b2-b4, forcing Black's queen to retreat.} Qc7 12. h4 $14 *

