1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 {The Steinitz is one of Black's two main tries after Nc3. In general, I think it is a more promising option for them than the Winawer because they do not lose their dark-squared bishop.} 4. e5 Nfd7 ({Recently,} 4... Nfd7 5. Nce2 {has been gaining some popularity.} ({I even played it myself the last time I faced the Steinitz, winning a nice game against Ray Robson.However, I ultimately think the main line with} 5. f4 {is a much more testing way to play, and I was happy to have the opportunity to dig deep while writing this course.})) 5. f4 c5 {Anything else would be utterly shocking.} 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 Be7 {Back when I played the French a little, this move was very popular, and I chose it twice, against Robson and Salem, reaching good positions both games. White should be a bit careful here, but they are definitely better if they know what they are doing.} 8. Qd2 (8. Ne2 $2 {This only ever works if Black has burned a tempo on ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6.} Qb6 9. Qc1 O-O 10. c3 f6 {Black has way too much counterplay here. A tempo is a huge deal in such a sharp position.}) 8... O-O 9. dxc5 $1 ({This is a very necessary exchange to make. If you castle long first,} 9. O-O-O {, Black will be able to close the position with} c4 {and launch the b-pawn, with a very dangerous attack.}) 9... Nxc5 {In my opinion, this is Black's most testing move, and it is the one I chose twice in my own practice.} 10. O-O-O {[%cal Gh2h4,Gf1d3,Gf3g5] Back when I was playing this line in 2016, engines were not as good at sacrificing stuff as they are now, and would often underestimate attacking plans.Modern machines don't suffer this shortcoming, and they quickly point out the best plan: h2-h4 and Bd3, when Ng5 is on the way.} b6 {This is probably a better move. Now, the plan with h2-h4 and Bd3 is not bad, but the Greek Gift is harder to pull off with the pawn on ...b7-b6. The point is that we will need Bxc5 at some point to drag away the e7-bishop, and that will not work as well with ...bxc5 as a possible recapture.} 11. h4 {We can start the same way.} Bb7 {But now, Bd3 is less convincing. I like another plan.} 12. Qe1 $1 Rc8 13. Nb5 $14 {White can play simple strategic chess. Not only is the b5-square available for him now to regroup the knight because Black played ...b7-b6 instead of ...a7-a6, but we are also not nearly as concerned about counterplay when Black's queenside pawns have not mobilized.If Black ever plays ...Ba6, we can comfortably leave the knight on b5, where it will be endlessly annoying for Black. And if they ever play ...a7-a6, of course, we will be all too happy to put the knight on d4 instead.} *

