[PlyCount "33"]
[GameId "2288537762714326"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} b5 {I don't think that Black can hope to fight for equality if they do not contest the center right away.} 9. c3 b4 {This would normally be the move Black would want to play to continue pressuring the center, but here they are too slow.} ({If} 9... Bb7 {, then} 10. Ng3 $16 {. White is ready for} Z0 11. Bd3 {next, and they should easily crash through on the kingside.}) 10. cxb4 $1 Nxb4 $2 11. a3 $1 {All of a sudden, Black is facing immediate problems with the knight. They cannot take on d4 without it being trapped.} Nc6 (11... cxd4 12. Nexd4 $18 {White wins a piece, and the game.}) 12. dxc5 $1 Bxc5 {This runs into tactical problems, but Black certainly could not allow White to play b2-b4 or Ra1-c1 next and remain a clean pawn up.} 13. Bxc5 Nxc5 14. Rc1 $1 {Black loses material on the spot.} Qa5+ (14... Qb6 15. Qc2 $1 $18 {White wins a piece.}) 15. b4 $1 Nxb4 16. axb4 Qxb4+ 17. Kf2 $1 {Avoiding the last trick. White's king will be totally safe on g1, and they win with the extra piece.} (17. Qd2 $2 Nd3+ $1 {Don't be that guy.}) *

