1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} b5 {I don't think that Black can hope to fight for equality if they do not contest the center right away.} 9. c3 Qb6 {This has been the most common move, but it doesn't provide much help. Black is blending plans in a bad way; ...Qb6 is the move you play to pressure the center. It does not mesh well with ...b7-b5, an attempt to gain space, because it loses time and means we don't have to bother about defending b2.} 10. a3 $16 {[%cal Gh2h4] Nice and simple. Black cannot play ...b5-b4, so what do they do? Basically nothing. In the meantime, White can play their standard h4-h5 plan.} *

