1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} Be7 $6 {This should lead to a bad version of the main line for Black.} 9. c3 O-O 10. h4 $14 {It's quite nice that after the inevitable ...Qd8-b6, we can choose a more active square for our queen.} Qb6 11. Qc2 $16 {The queen is much better here than on c1, as now Ng5 can become a real idea. In some cases in the main line, White plays Qc1-c2, spending a full tempo to improve her. Getting her there in one move gives White a huge position.} *

