1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} Qb6 $1 {Even though Black has spent a tempo on ...a7-a6, making it harder for them to attack the center quickly, I still think that trying to do so represents the best chance to equalize.Things get a bit messy here, and White is clearly better with easy moves after anything else Black can try. It should come as no surprise that this is Black's most common move by far.} 9. Qc1 {This is not the happiest square for the queen, but there was no choice.} cxd4 {The computer wants to take on d4, and claims Black is only a little worse. That's definitely true, but also not something we should be unhappy with.} 10. Nexd4 Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Bc5 12. c3 $14 {White has a typical Steinitz edge. Black did not get any counterplay, and did not manage to activate the light-squared bishop.} *

