1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} Qb6 $1 {Even though Black has spent a tempo on ...a7-a6, making it harder for them to attack the center quickly, I still think that trying to do so represents the best chance to equalize.Things get a bit messy here, and White is clearly better with easy moves after anything else Black can try. It should come as no surprise that this is Black's most common move by far.} 9. Qc1 {This is not the happiest square for the queen, but there was no choice.} g5 $6 {This is a bit too brazen.} 10. c3 {If Black is not going to take on d4 and explicitly win the pawn, I can't believe that ...g7-g5 is justified. It creates way too big a weakness on the kingside, which we were hoping to attack anyway.} g4 $2 {This is not the point of ...g7-g5. Black needs to be taking on f4 as a means to make it harder for us to take back with the e2-knight when ...Nxd4 inevitably comes.} 11. Nd2 $16 {Black has no way to increase the pressure on our center, and h2-h3 will come sooner rather than later.} *

