1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} Qb6 $1 {Even though Black has spent a tempo on ...a7-a6, making it harder for them to attack the center quickly, I still think that trying to do so represents the best chance to equalize.Things get a bit messy here, and White is clearly better with easy moves after anything else Black can try. It should come as no surprise that this is Black's most common move by far.} 9. Qc1 {This is not the happiest square for the queen, but there was no choice.} f6 {Though the machine prefers taking on d4 and accepting a slightly worse position right off the bat, I definitely think that ...f7-f6 should be the most testing move. Black wants to contest our center directly.} 10. c3 fxe5 $2 {[%cal Ge1f2,Gf2g1] Black should not release the tension unless they have a very clear reason to do so. Now, White is able to regroup with Kf2-g1.} 11. fxe5 {White in great shape. They can go Kf2-g1 next, and Black will have no counterplay.} Be7 12. Kf2 $1 {With f-pawns still on the board, ...g7-g5 would make a lot of counterplay. Without them, Black's position is wretched.} O-O 13. Kg1 $16 ({Next up is} 13. Kg1 Z0 14. h4 {and} Z0 15. Rh3 {. Black is out of luck.}) *

