1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} Qb6 $1 {Even though Black has spent a tempo on ...a7-a6, making it harder for them to attack the center quickly, I still think that trying to do so represents the best chance to equalize.Things get a bit messy here, and White is clearly better with easy moves after anything else Black can try. It should come as no surprise that this is Black's most common move by far.} 9. Qc1 {This is not the happiest square for the queen, but there was no choice.} f6 {Though the machine prefers taking on d4 and accepting a slightly worse position right off the bat, I definitely think that ...f7-f6 should be the most testing move. Black wants to contest our center directly.} 10. c3 Be7 11. h4 $1 {As usual, once we go Nce2, h2-h4 is our next move if we don't need to defend against a direct threat. It gains important kingside space, and it stops any hope of g5.Now, if we are allowed to play Kf2 next with Kg1 to follow, we will have a huge position.} (11. Kf2 $6 O-O 12. Kg1 {This would be the right plan for White, but unfortunately, Black is just in time to make counterplay with} g5) 11... O-O ({▷} 11... Na5 $2 {Arjun tried this move once in a rapid game. He must have been freestyling, as Black's position is horrible. But if someone as strong as him can play this move when thinking on their own, anyone can! So, we should be ready.} 12. dxc5 $1 Bxc5 13. Ned4 $18 {, and} Z0 14. b4 {comes next; Black is already busted.}) ({▷} 11... a5 {This is a typical motif for Black, but the wrong time. It allows White to execute the main plan.} 12. Kf2 $1 a4 13. Kg1 $16 {White managed to get the king to g1 safely, and they are much better with moves like} Z0 14. Rh3 {, and} Z0 15. h5 {coming next.}) 12. h5 $1 {This move might look simple in its nature; White gains space on the kingside, what could be more natural? But it also has a hidden point. Now, Kf2 is a real idea.} (12. Kf2 $2 fxe5 13. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 {Black gets a lot of counterplay here.} 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Kg1 Ng4 16. c4) ({.} 12. Rh3 {This is a very strategically ambitious move. White is getting ready for Kf2, but Black is not in such bad shape.} Qa5 $1 {No more preparatory moves for you! Go to f2 right now, and then Black has just enough counterplay once they take on d4 and e5.} 13. Kf2 (13. Bd2 b5 14. Rg3 Qb6) 13... cxd4 $1 14. cxd4 fxe5 15. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Kg1 Ng4 {, and now we see the point; White would win on the spot with Bd4, but here, Black has ...e6-e5 because the rook is loose on h3.} 18. Rg3 (18. Bd4 e5 $1) 18... e5 19. Rxg4 Bxg4 20. Nxe5 {.}) 12... a5 $2 {A do nothing move like this one is not cutting it.} 13. Kf2 $1 {It's now or never to take on e5.} (13. Rh3 $1 {This is an interesting move, but it's not my main recommendation. The point is that Black's normal way of dealing with Rh3, which helps prepare Kf2, is to go ...Qa5. Now, this is obviously not a thing.}) 13... fxe5 14. fxe5 Ncxe5 15. dxe5 Nxe5 16. Kg1 $1 Ng4 {If White's pawn was back on h4 here, Black might be okay. As is, the space around the king will matter.} 17. h6 $1 g6 18. c4 $1 {Black is unable to utilize the central pawn mass effectively. Now that ...c5-c4 has been forever prevented, White is ready for Be3-g5 next. They are more or less winning with the extra piece.} *

