xt; Black is already busted.}) ({▷} 11... a5 {This is a typical motif for Black, but the wrong time. It allows White to execute the main plan.} 12. Kf2 $1 a4 13. Kg1 $16 {White managed to get the king to g1 safely, and they are much better with moves like} Z0 14. Rh3 {, and} Z0 15. h5 {coming next.}) 12. h5 $1 {This move might look simple in its nature; White gains space on the kingside, what could be more natural? But it also has a hidden point. Now, Kf2 is a real idea.} (12. Kf2 $2 fxe5 13. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 {Black gets a lot of counterplay here.} 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Kg1 Ng4 16. c4) ({.} 12. Rh3 {This is a very strategically ambitious move. White is getting ready for Kf2, but Black is not in such bad shape.} Qa5 $1 {No more preparatory moves for you! Go to f2 right now, and then Black has just enough counterplay once they take on d4 and e5.} 13. Kf2 (13. Bd2 b5 14. Rg3 Qb6) 13... cxd4 $1 14. cxd4 fxe5 15. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Kg1 Ng4 {, and now we see the point; White would win on the spot with Bd4, but here, Black has ...e6-e5 because the rook is loose on h3.} 18. Rg3 (18. Bd4 e5 $1) 18... e5 19. Rxg4 Bxg4 20. Nxe5 {.}) 12... Rf7 $2 {This is the right idea, but Black should not be allowing h5-h6 first.} 13. h6 $1 g6 14. Rh3 $16 {White has a fantastic version of the main line.} *

