1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 a6 ({Finally, we reach Black's main and most common move. In recent times, it has seemed like} 7... cxd4) ({and} 7... Qb6 {has a better chance of equalizing, but only by reaching unpleasant positions with a pawn less that are probably holdable.}) ({With} 7... a6 {, Black is aiming to keep a bit more tension.}) 8. Ne2 $1 {As usual, we want to meet either ...a7-a6 or ...b7-b6 with Nce2. Black will not be able to pressure the center as much as they would if they had played a developing move instead.} Qb6 $1 {Even though Black has spent a tempo on ...a7-a6, making it harder for them to attack the center quickly, I still think that trying to do so represents the best chance to equalize.Things get a bit messy here, and White is clearly better with easy moves after anything else Black can try. It should come as no surprise that this is Black's most common move by far.} 9. Qc1 {This is not the happiest square for the queen, but there was no choice.} f6 {Though the machine prefers taking on d4 and accepting a slightly worse position right off the bat, I definitely think that ...f7-f6 should be the most testing move. Black wants to contest our center directly.} 10. c3 Be7 11. h4 $1 {As usual, once we go Nce2, h2-h4 is our next move if we don't need to defend against a direct threat. It gains important kingside space, and it stops any hope of g5.Now, if we are allowed to play Kf2 next with Kg1 to follow, we will have a huge position.} (11. Kf2 $6 O-O 12. Kg1 {This would be the right plan for White, but unfortunately, Black is just in time to make counterplay with} g5) 11... O-O ({▷} 11... Na5 $2 {Arjun tried this move once in a rapid game. He must have been freestyling, as Black's position is horrible. But if someone as strong as him can play this move when thinking on their own, anyone can! So, we should be ready.} 12. dxc5 $1 Bxc5 13. Ned4 $18 {, and} Z0 14. b4 {comes next; Black is already busted.}) ({▷} 11... a5 {This is a typical motif for Black, but the wrong time. It allows White to execute the main plan.} 12. Kf2 $1 a4 13. Kg1 $16 {White managed to get the king to g1 safely, and they are much better with moves like} Z0 14. Rh3 {, and} Z0 15. h5 {coming next.}) 12. h5 $1 {This move might look simple in its nature; White gains space on the kingside, what could be more natural? But it also has a hidden point. Now, Kf2 is a real idea.} (12. Kf2 $2 fxe5 13. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 {Black gets a lot of counterplay here.} 14. dxe5 Nxe5 15. Kg1 Ng4 16. c4) ({.} 12. Rh3 {This is a very strategically ambitious move. White is getting ready for Kf2, but Black is not in such bad shape.} Qa5 $1 {No more preparatory moves for you! Go to f2 right now, and then Black has just enough counterplay once they take on d4 and e5.} 13. Kf2 (13. Bd2 b5 14. Rg3 Qb6) 13... cxd4 $1 14. cxd4 fxe5 15. fxe5 Ndxe5 $1 {Of course.} 16. dxe5 Nxe5 17. Kg1 Ng4 {, and now we see the point; White would win on the spot with Bd4, but here, Black has ...e6-e5 because the rook is loose on h3.} 18. Rg3 (18. Bd4 e5 $1) 18... e5 19. Rxg4 Bxg4 20. Nxe5 {.}) 12... h6 {This is best, and the most common move by far. We still cannot successfully play Kf2, because Black will once more sacrifice on e5 and we won't have h5-h6 hanging over the head.} 13. Rh3 {This is generally a very useful move.} ({Now, the normal response for Black would be to play} 13. Rh3 Qa5 {, forcing our king to f2 right away.}) 13... Qa5 $6 {This would be the best move for Black if we had started with Rh3 instead of h4-h5. Now, the inclusion of h4-h5 and ...h7-h6 changes the position in White's favor in a subtle way. We can definitely count on an edge, but you really should know what you are doing.} 14. Bd2 $1 ({If} 14. Rg3 {, then} cxd4 15. Nexd4 fxe5 {.}) 14... b5 {Without h4-h5 and h7-h6 included, Black's queenside play would be coming, and there wouldn't be that much proactive that White could do. Now, things are different.} 15. Rg3 $1 {White is threatening f4-f5, with total devastation. As a result, Black absolutely must play ...Rf8-f7. This would not be the case if h5 and h6 were not thrown in.} Rf7 (15... Qb6 $2 16. f5 $18) 16. c4 $1 {And now White starts a tactical operation.} Nb4 $1 17. cxd5 $1 Nd3+ 18. Kd1 Qb6 (18... Nf2+ 19. Kc2 $16 {White's king is safe enough, and they should win material.}) 19. Qc2 {[%cal Gd3f2] And we see how flicking in Rh3-g3 was helful. Black does not have a fork on f2!} c4 {This position is obviously super messy. White still has to be precise to show an edge.} 20. f5 $1 {Black should check our king to be sooner or later. They might as well do it now.} Nf2+ (20... fxe5 21. dxe6 {Black needs ...Nf2+ anyway.}) 21. Kc1 Nd3+ 22. Kb1 {Now, Black is obviously about to lose material. But, they can get a hell of an initiative. We should be careful.} fxe5 23. dxe6 $1 Rxf5 {And now an important point. Don't take the piece!} 24. Bxh6 $1 (24. exd7 $2 Bxd7 {Black's activity is very impressive here, and White should be careful not to lose on the spot.}) 24... Bf6 25. Bxg7 $1 Bxg7 26. h6 e4 27. Rxg7+ Kh8 28. Ng3 $16 {The position remains very messy, but the machine does confirm White is much better.In general, I don't like having to memorize forced lines where the moves are all sort of random, and cannot be explained with principles. It makes it so much harder to remember! But here, I don't think there was a choice.} *

