ecessarily given up on sacrificing on e5, but they want to get the bishop to g5 first. And because the b2-pawn is loose, we supposedly cannot play Kg1.} 16. Kg1 $1 {I will not be dissuaded so easily.} Bxe3+ 17. Qxe3 Qxb2 18. Re1 {Black has won a pawn, but the kingside attack will be very dangerous. They have to be precise here.} Ne7 $1 {Only move. Otherwise, Rg3 would cause massive problems.} 19. Nh4 $1 {So we renew the threat.} cxd4 $1 {This is far from an obvious move-allowing the knight to d4 accelerates White's development. Now, Black probably holds if it knows absolutely everything, but White has a lot of tries.} 20. Nxd4 {Of course, we take with the knight to bring more pieces into the game.} (20. cxd4 Nb6 $1 {This one is okay for Black.} 21. Rg3 Nc4 22. Qxh6 Bd7 23. Qg5 Nd2 $1 {Black is fine. The point is that without c-pawns, they can meet} 24. Nf4 {with} Qxd4+ {.}) 20... Nc5 $1 {Black cannot allow Bd3.} (20... Nf8 $2 21. Bd3 $16 {Black faces a vicious attack in the upcoming moves.}) 21. Rf3 $1 {We start trading off Black's defensive pieces.} Rxf3 22. gxf3 $1 {[%cal Ge1e2,Ge2g2] Re2-g2 is on the way. Perhaps Black can survive here, but it looks very scary to me.} *

