1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 Nxd4 {Sometimes Black tries forcing the endgame right away, hoping that the king will be nicer on e7 than it would be if they castled.} 10. Bxd4 Bxd4 11. Qxd4 Qb6 12. Qxb6 Nxb6 13. Nb5 $14 {Endgames like this are typical in the Steinitz, and White is always a little better. There's no way to avoid them; if Black wants one, they can get it very easily, and this is a decent version for them. Still, White has good pressure.We'll see multiple endgames like this one in the illustrative games section, including an effort from Dmitry Andreikin in this exact position.} *

