[ECO "C11"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "25"]
[GameId "2288537762837237"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... Nxd4 11. Bxd4 Bxd4 $6 12. Qxd4 Qb6 13. Nb5 $14 {These endgames are generally not great for Black, but this one will be a particularly bad version. White will start with the knight already on d4, and Black will have committed to castling instead of putting the king on the e7-square.If Black wants something like this, they can get a much better version of the same endgame by other means.} *

