1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... Nxd4 11. Bxd4 a6 12. Qe3 $1 ({Nice and simple. White wants} 12. Qe3 Z0 13. Bd3 {next without losing a piece.}) 12... Qe7 13. Bd3 Bxd4 14. Qxd4 $14 ({Black is in a bad way. We are ready for} 14. Qxd4 Z0 ({Black cannot even play} 14... b5 {on pain of} 15. Ne4 {.}) 15. Rhe1 {and} Z0 16. f5 {to come next.}) *

