1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... Bxd4 {If I had to play this line tomorrow, I would probably play ...Bxd4. But it definitely does not equalize.} 11. Bxd4 Qa5 ({Now, Black actually has a plan. They want} 11... Qa5 12. Z0 Rb8 {,} 13. Z0 b5 {, and} 14. Z0 b4 {. When this happens, they get ...Ba6 in, trade off the bad bishop, and they should be fine. So, we need to be accurate.}) 12. Bf2 $1 Rb8 13. Kb1 $1 b5 14. Ne2 $1 {This is an important move, without which Black would be quite fine. Now, the endgame will be miserable for them, and I can't imagine ...b5-b4 not coming.} b4 15. Nc1 $1 {And the point is revealed. White has Nb3 as a possibility, and as a result, it is harder for Black to play ...Ba6 under good circumstances. It is still the best move, but not inspiring.} Qc7 16. Bd3 a5 17. h4 a4 18. h5 $14 {[%cal Gh1h3]} ({White is ready for} 18. h5 Z0 19. Rh3 {, which will both lift the rook into the attack and also help contain the ...b4-b3 break. Their attack is much more dangerous than Black's is.}) *

[Event "Lifetime Repertoires: Shankland's 1.e4 – Part 1 Caro-Kann, French & Sidelines"]
[Site "?"]
[Date "????.??.??"]
