[White "23) French Steinitz with 7...cxd4"]
[Black "23A | 8...Bc5 #6"]
[Result "*"]
[ECO "C11"]
[Annotator "SchackNuva"]
[PlyCount "41"]
[GameId "2288537762845433"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... Bxd4 {If I had to play this line tomorrow, I would probably play ...Bxd4. But it definitely does not equalize.} 11. Bxd4 Qa5 ({Now, Black actually has a plan. They want} 11... Qa5 12. Z0 Rb8 {,} 13. Z0 b5 {, and} 14. Z0 b4 {. When this happens, they get ...Ba6 in, trade off the bad bishop, and they should be fine. So, we need to be accurate.}) 12. Bf2 $1 Rb8 13. Kb1 $1 b5 14. Ne2 $1 {This is an important move, without which Black would be quite fine. Now, the endgame will be miserable for them, and I can't imagine ...b5-b4 not coming.} b4 15. Nc1 $1 {And the point is revealed. White has Nb3 as a possibility, and as a result, it is harder for Black to play ...Ba6 under good circumstances. It is still the best move, but not inspiring.} Ba6 {I think Black should do this anyway, but it does not equalize.} 16. Nb3 $1 {Now, a forced line begins.} Qa4 17. Nc5 $1 Nxc5 18. Bxc5 Rfc8 19. b3 $1 Qa5 20. Bxa6 Qxa6 {Strategically, Black is totally fine now. But their pieces are sidelined.} 21. f5 $14 {Black is ill equipped to deal with f5-f6 next. The machine avoids getting mated with good defense, but definitely does not equalize.} *

