1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... Qe7 {This one has been played before, but I don't think much of it.} 11. Nb3 {I like sticking with the main plan, and not allowing the knight exchange. Black definitely does not want to trade dark squared bishops.} b6 12. Qf2 $14 {I don't get what Black is playing for. They can't ever get ...b6-b5 in, they do not make any counterplay, they have less space, and the dark squares are weak.} *

