1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... a6 {This is the main move by far. Sooner or later, Black would like to play ...b7-b5. However, it is not so easy to do when the d4-knight keeps the c6-knight under pressure.} 11. Nb3 $5 {I like immediately denying Black the opportunity to trade on d4. Now, the c5-bishop is attacked, and Black should make up their mind right away about where they want to go.} b6 12. h4 $1 {White starts gaining kingside space. Black's position doesn't make sense. Playing ...b7-b6 to bolster the bishop is fine and good, but it means Black can't play ...b6-b5. So, I have no clue what they want next.} *

