1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Bc5 9. Qd2 O-O 10. O-O-O ({Now, if Black plays the main move with} 10. O-O-O a6 {, I like going} 11. Nb3 {, avoiding the piece exchange. As such, if Black wants to take anything on d4, now is the time.}) 10... a6 {This is the main move by far. Sooner or later, Black would like to play ...b7-b5. However, it is not so easy to do when the d4-knight keeps the c6-knight under pressure.} 11. Nb3 $5 {I like immediately denying Black the opportunity to trade on d4. Now, the c5-bishop is attacked, and Black should make up their mind right away about where they want to go.} Be7 {This would be my instinct with Black, getting the bishop out of the way and hoping to throw the b-pawn next, but it is also quite bad for them.} 12. h4 b5 13. h5 Nb6 {Now, White has a number of good continuations, but I like a simple one.} (13... b4 $6 {In general, Black cannot play ...b5-b4 in this line until they have the a4-square under control.} 14. Na4 $1 $16 {Black's queenside play is frozen. In some cases, White can even play with Kb1 and c2-c4.}) 14. Qf2 $1 {I like poking the knight right now. The point is that Black might have wanted to play ...b5-b4 next, when we can't play Na4. So, our knight would have been forced back to e2, and we'd lose the chance to play Qf2.} Rb8 15. Bd3 b4 16. Ne2 a5 17. Nbd4 $18 {White has a strategically winning position. Black's queenside play will never amount to anything, and eventually f4-f5 will come.} *

