1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Qb6 {In modern times, this has become the main move. Black goes pawn-grabbing in the style of the Bg5 Najdorf, and hopes they can get away with it.For once, they will not have a strategically suspect position early on; we will, with the pawn less. But with energetic play, we will quickly gain the pawn back and get ahead on development.} 9. Qd2 $1 Qxb2 {Nothing else makes any sense.} 10. Rb1 Qa3 11. Bb5 $1 Ndb8 $2 {This is way too greedy. Black falls even further behind in development.} 12. O-O a6 13. f5 $18 {Black is smashed.} *

