1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Qb6 {In modern times, this has become the main move. Black goes pawn-grabbing in the style of the Bg5 Najdorf, and hopes they can get away with it.For once, they will not have a strategically suspect position early on; we will, with the pawn less. But with energetic play, we will quickly gain the pawn back and get ahead on development.} 9. Qd2 $1 Qxb2 {Nothing else makes any sense.} 10. Rb1 Qa3 11. Bb5 $1 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 a6 13. Bxd7+ Bxd7 14. Rb3 $1 {Before we take the pawn back, we provoke Black's queen to a worse square.} Qe7 15. Rxb7 {Now, the battle lines are drawn. Black has the bishop pair, and their light-squared bishop is not nearly as bad as it is in a normal closed French structure.White has weaknesses down the c-file. If Black can complete the development and consolidate, they should be easily fine. But that is a big if!} Rc8 16. O-O Qa3 ({This one is a little tricky. Black wants} 16... Qa3 17. Z0 Bc5 ({or} 17... Bb4 {next. But White is good to go if they know what they are doing.})) (16... Qd8 {transposes to the 15...Qd8 line.}) 17. f5 $1 {Black's queen is offside, so there is no need to wait around. Smash time.} Bc5 18. Ne2 $1 exf5 {If Black's queen were back on d8 here, they would be okay. They'd be ready to castle next, and the d7-bishop would be protected. As is, the offside queen changes everything.} 19. Qg5 $1 {No castle for you!} g6 20. Rf3 $1 Qa4 21. Bxc5 Rxc5 22. Qh6 $36 {Black cannot stop an invasion on g7. Their king is stuck in the middle, and they are rapidly losing control of the game.} *

