1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Qb6 {In modern times, this has become the main move. Black goes pawn-grabbing in the style of the Bg5 Najdorf, and hopes they can get away with it.For once, they will not have a strategically suspect position early on; we will, with the pawn less. But with energetic play, we will quickly gain the pawn back and get ahead on development.} 9. Qd2 $1 Qxb2 {Nothing else makes any sense.} 10. Rb1 Qa3 11. Bb5 $1 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 a6 13. Bxd7+ Bxd7 14. Rb3 $1 {Before we take the pawn back, we provoke Black's queen to a worse square.} Qe7 15. Rxb7 {Now, the battle lines are drawn. Black has the bishop pair, and their light-squared bishop is not nearly as bad as it is in a normal closed French structure.White has weaknesses down the c-file. If Black can complete the development and consolidate, they should be easily fine. But that is a big if!} Qd8 16. O-O Rc8 {This feels like the wrong coordination for Black. They do not gain a tempo on the rook, so they will not get the bishop to c5.} 17. Qe3 $1 Rc4 18. Rfb1 Be7 19. f5 $1 {Black faces a dangerous attack. They need to be very precise to hang on.} Bc8 {This one is a bit tricky, but White is better as well.} 20. Ra7 (20. Bb6 $2 Bxb7 $1 21. Bxd8 Bc5 $1 {Oops!}) 20... Bg5 21. Qf2 $1 {Now we have to go to f2 instead of d3. Previously, this failed because of ...exf5, but now that is no longer possible, as Bb6 is a big threat.} (21. Qd3 O-O $1 {Now we cannot play f5-f6 so easily, since Black can take it. Getting the bishop to safety and off d7 with a gain of tempo was very helpful for him.}) 21... O-O (21... exf5 $2 22. Bb6 $1 $18 {That's a queen.}) 22. f6 $1 gxf6 23. exf6 Qd6 24. h4 $1 ({If} 24. Ne2 {, then} e5 {.}) 24... Bh6 25. Ne2 $1 ({Now and only now because} 25. Ne2 e5 {can be met with} 26. Qg3+ {.}) 25... Kh8 26. Re7 $1 {[%cal Gf2f3,Gf3h5] More than anything else, this is prophylaxis. No ...Rxd4 and ...Qc5 please! Plan is Qf3-h5 next, and Black is in big trouble.} ({After} 26. Qf3 {, Black plays} Rxd4 {.}) *

