1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Qb6 {In modern times, this has become the main move. Black goes pawn-grabbing in the style of the Bg5 Najdorf, and hopes they can get away with it.For once, they will not have a strategically suspect position early on; we will, with the pawn less. But with energetic play, we will quickly gain the pawn back and get ahead on development.} 9. Qd2 $1 Qxb2 {Nothing else makes any sense.} 10. Rb1 Qa3 11. Bb5 $1 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 a6 13. Bxd7+ Bxd7 14. Rb3 $1 {Before we take the pawn back, we provoke Black's queen to a worse square.} Qe7 15. Rxb7 {Now, the battle lines are drawn. Black has the bishop pair, and their light-squared bishop is not nearly as bad as it is in a normal closed French structure.White has weaknesses down the c-file. If Black can complete the development and consolidate, they should be easily fine. But that is a big if!} Qd8 16. O-O Rc8 {This feels like the wrong coordination for Black. They do not gain a tempo on the rook, so they will not get the bishop to c5.} 17. Qe3 $1 Rc4 18. Rfb1 Be7 19. f5 $1 {Black faces a dangerous attack. They need to be very precise to hang on.} Bg5 20. Qd3 $1 (20. Qf2 $2 exf5 {Black is fine now. It's a problem that the rook on c4 is not hanging after Nxd5.}) 20... O-O {[%cal Gd4b6]} ({▷} 20... exf5 $2 21. Nxd5 $18 {The hanging rook on c4 costs Black a tempo, and that tempo costs them the game. Bb6 is coming.}) ({▷} 20... Bc8 21. Bb6 $1 $18 {White wins the queen. The ...Bc8 resource only existed when ...Bxb7 and Bc5 would pin the queen on e3.}) 21. f6 Bc6 (21... Bc8 $2 {If White had to play Ra7 here, the position would not be great — this is what Black is aiming for if they choose ...Bc8 before ...Bg5. But now that they waited on ...Bc8, they lose immediately instead.} 22. Bb6 Qe8 23. Re7 Qc6 24. Rc7 $18 {Black loses heavy material, since retreating the queen will allow Rxc4, followed by Qg3.}) 22. Re7 {→ I won't mate the computer here, but Black's position definitely looks dangerous.} *

