1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Nf6 4. e5 Nfd7 5. f4 c5 6. Nf3 Nc6 7. Be3 cxd4 {This is the most direct move, and the most common one in modern times. Black immediately provokes a conflict and starts some forced lines.} 8. Nxd4 ({Now, the old line is for Black to play with} 8. Nxd4 Bc5 {and} 9. Z0 O-O {. This did not stand the test of time too well, and in more recent years, Black has almost exclusively been playing 8...Qb6, provoking an immediate fight.}) 8... Qb6 {In modern times, this has become the main move. Black goes pawn-grabbing in the style of the Bg5 Najdorf, and hopes they can get away with it.For once, they will not have a strategically suspect position early on; we will, with the pawn less. But with energetic play, we will quickly gain the pawn back and get ahead on development.} 9. Qd2 $1 Qxb2 {Nothing else makes any sense.} 10. Rb1 Qa3 11. Bb5 $1 Nxd4 12. Bxd4 a6 13. Bxd7+ Bxd7 14. Rb3 $1 {Before we take the pawn back, we provoke Black's queen to a worse square.} Qe7 15. Rxb7 {Now, the battle lines are drawn. Black has the bishop pair, and their light-squared bishop is not nearly as bad as it is in a normal closed French structure.White has weaknesses down the c-file. If Black can complete the development and consolidate, they should be easily fine. But that is a big if!} Qd8 16. O-O Qc8 17. Rfb1 Bc5 18. f5 $1 {Now, the position becomes very forcing. Black is crushed immediately if they do not go for a pawn down heavy piece endgame.} Bb5 (18... Bxd4+ 19. Qxd4 Bb5 20. Nxb5 axb5 21. R7xb5 {transposes to the main line.}) 19. Nxb5 axb5 20. R7xb5 Bxd4+ 21. Qxd4 O-O 22. R1b2 {White is a pawn up. The structure is not great, and Black obviously has drawing chances. However, they have not obviously equalized. In human practice, White has won 2 out of 4 games.I certainly don't think we can expect to score 75% from here, and I do think Black should make a draw more often than not. But I also think we have every right to press, and that we can't really expect better than this against such a forcing line.} *

