1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 Ne7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({Now, Black nearly always plays} 6. bxc3 c5 {here, but it's worth being ready for ...b7-b6 as well.}) 6... b6 {I don't hold ...b7-b6 in very high regard. It's obviously a natural idea to try to trade light-squared bishops, as that is one of Black's biggest strategic goals in the Winawer. But here, it gives White way too much kingside initiative.} 7. Qg4 $1 {In general, I am not recommending that we go for Qd1-g4 too often. But in this particular case, Black is not pressuring the center at all, and it makes perfect sense.} Ng6 8. h4 $1 h5 9. Qg3 ({Now} 9. Qg3 Z0 ({I can't imagine Black playing anything other than} 9... Ba6 {.}) 10. Bd3 {is a huge threat.}) 9... Ba6 10. Bxa6 $1 {We definitely want to take the bishop right away.} ({If you play} 10. Bg5 {first, Black can go for} Qc8 {, and take back on a6 with the queen, which is a much better coordination.}) 10... Nxa6 11. Bg5 Qd7 {White has some weak light squares, but luckily, this is not the most relevant factor. The misplaced knight on g6 will cause Black endless trouble, and the inclusion of h4 and h5 is heavily in White's favor. Black will have a very hard time castling on either side.} 12. Ne2 ({It is not a good sign for Black that the machine wants} 12. Ne2 Nb8 {here.}) 12... c5 $2 {This feels like a human move to me, but it only makes things worse.} 13. O-O $16 {[%cal Gf2f5] White is ready for f2-f4, and f4-f5 next, taking advantage of the misplaced knight on g6. The machine already claims ...Ne7 is more or less the only move, and it gives the g7-pawn directly. White is more or less winning.} *

