1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 Ne7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({Now, Black nearly always plays} 6. bxc3 c5 {here, but it's worth being ready for ...b7-b6 as well.}) 6... b6 {I don't hold ...b7-b6 in very high regard. It's obviously a natural idea to try to trade light-squared bishops, as that is one of Black's biggest strategic goals in the Winawer. But here, it gives White way too much kingside initiative.} 7. Qg4 $1 {In general, I am not recommending that we go for Qd1-g4 too often. But in this particular case, Black is not pressuring the center at all, and it makes perfect sense.} Ng6 8. h4 $1 h5 9. Qg3 ({Now} 9. Qg3 Z0 ({I can't imagine Black playing anything other than} 9... Ba6 {.}) 10. Bd3 {is a huge threat.}) 9... Ba6 10. Bxa6 $1 {We definitely want to take the bishop right away.} ({If you play} 10. Bg5 {first, Black can go for} Qc8 {, and take back on a6 with the queen, which is a much better coordination.}) 10... Nxa6 11. Bg5 Qd7 {White has some weak light squares, but luckily, this is not the most relevant factor. The misplaced knight on g6 will cause Black endless trouble, and the inclusion of h4 and h5 is heavily in White's favor. Black will have a very hard time castling on either side.} 12. Ne2 ({It is not a good sign for Black that the machine wants} 12. Ne2 Nb8 {here.}) 12... Nb8 $1 {This is a dual-purpose move.The first point is defensive. Our most direct attacking plan is castling and f4-f5. If Black gets the knight to c6, they will be able to then meet any f2-f4 plan with ...Ng6-e7, and ...Qxg7 will not be an issue anymore.The second point is proactive. Black can hope to bring the knight to a5 next, and then ...Qc6, ...Kd7, ...Kc8 to castle by hand. This all seems a bit artificial, though, and White can count on a big advantage if they play well.} 13. Qf3 $1 {White makes a very blunt threat of Ne2-g3. If we can take on h5, we will easily win the game on the kingside. As such, Nb8-c6 is not a thing, and Black must put the queen there instead.} (13. O-O $6 {This would play into Black's hands. White is still better, but I find their play a bit less convincing here.} Nc6 14. f4 Nge7 $1 {A key point of regrouping the knight to c6 is revealed. We are not really in great shape to take on e7 and g7.} 15. Bxe7 $2 Nxe7 $1 16. Qxg7 O-O-O $17 {Our queen is in trouble, and things are going badly wrong.}) 13... Qc6 $1 {This is not an ideal move for Black as now they cannot coordinate with ...Nc6-a5, but there was no choice. Ng3 was coming.} (13... Nc6 14. Ng3 $18 {White wins immediately.}) 14. O-O (14. Ng3 {was not working because we could not actually take on h5 with c3 loose. But now it is a very real threat.}) 14... Nd7 {Unfortunately, here, we are one tempo too slow to win the game on the spot with Ne2-g3. Black is just in time with ...f7-f6. But White has a very clever idea.} 15. Qg3 $16 {[%cal Gf2f4,Gf4f5] This looks a bit strange; the queen was on g3 before, she went to f3, and now back to g3? When we peel back the layers, it makes a lot more sense.With the queen on g3, White's best plan is definitely f4-f5. Black could deal with this plan nicely if they coordinated with ...Nb8-c6, allowing ...Ne7 to come next. But, by bringing the queen to f3 and having the Ng3 resource hanging over Black's head, we convinced them to coordinate their pieces differently to stop our other avenue of attack.Now that the knight is on d7, we can go back to the main plan. f2-f4-f5 is coming, it can't be easily stopped, and Black is in trouble.} *

