1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 b6 {This move is hard to take seriously, but it does have some adherents.} (4... Qd7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 {The only move Black ever plays here is ...b7-b6, which transposes to the}) (4... b6 {line.}) 5. a3 Bf8 {I guess with this one, Black is hoping to play ...c7-c5 next when we cannot bolster the pawn with c3. Now, the position resembles a Steinitz much more than a Winawer, so I will borrow an idea from the Steinitz section.} 6. Nce2 $1 c5 7. c3 $16 {[%cal Gg1f3,Gh2h4]} ({White has an easy plan with} 7. c3 Z0 8. Nf3 {and} Z0 9. h4 {. It's important to note that in this case, we actually want to leave the pawn back on f2. This is because in the Steinitz, Black's knight is on d7, and they are not in the best spot to stop an f4-f5 break. With the knight on g8 instead, f4-f5 is much harder to pull off.We'll see a game in this line from my own practice where I managed to make Black's position look fairly unplayable.}) *

