[GameId "2288537762906898"]

1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 b6 {This move is hard to take seriously, but it does have some adherents.} (4... Qd7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 {The only move Black ever plays here is ...b7-b6, which transposes to the}) (4... b6 {line.}) 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 (6. bxc3 Ne7 {would transpose to the 6...b6 line, but Black does have two extraneous options.}) 6... Qd7 {This is Black's main point. By avoiding ...Ne7 for the moment, they are ready to meet Qg4 with ...f7-f5. I will not be dissuaded.} 7. Qg4 $1 f5 8. Qg3 Nc6 {This is the next most common move, and it is ridiculous. Black's only serious plan is to try to get the light-squared bishops off the board — that way, at least they have something to play for.} 9. Nh3 ({If} 9. Ne2 {, then} Ba6 {.}) 9... Bb7 10. Nf4 O-O-O 11. a4 {Black's position is miserable. The bishop on b7 is comically bad, and they will have to constantly be looking out for threats on both sides of the board.} Na5 12. Ba3 $16 (12. Ba3 Z0 13. Bb4 {comes next to kick the knight out of the way, and then White will achieve the desired a4-a5.}) *

