1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 b6 {This move is hard to take seriously, but it does have some adherents.} (4... Qd7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 {The only move Black ever plays here is ...b7-b6, which transposes to the}) (4... b6 {line.}) 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 (6. bxc3 Ne7 {would transpose to the 6...b6 line, but Black does have two extraneous options.}) 6... Qd7 {This is Black's main point. By avoiding ...Ne7 for the moment, they are ready to meet Qg4 with ...f7-f5. I will not be dissuaded.} 7. Qg4 $1 f5 8. Qg3 Ba6 9. Bxa6 Nxa6 10. Ne2 {Black has some nice strategic assets; eventually, our bishop will be a bad piece, and the c4-square is weak. But their king will cause them no shortage of issues; this is often the case in the positional Winawer lines. Now, Black would love to castle long, but isn't quite able to.} Kf7 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. Now, it is a somewhat frustrating position to play for White. The computer always gives some big advantage, around +1, but it never really finds a plan to knock Black out.In the meantime, Black's moves are pretty easy. Ne7, some rook to c8, c5. In human terms, this is not so simple for White, and they should be well prepared and have a good understanding of the resulting middlegame if they want to put Black under pressure.Some key points to understand:1. White needs to start with h4. This will immediately ask Black how they want to coordinate.2. If Black does not play h6, we are always happy to meet Ne7 with Bg5. We don't necessarily want to take on e7 unprovoked, but if Black ever plays h7-h6, we are very happy to take and play h5!, cementing our control of the g6-square.3. If Black does play h6, obviously, we cannot bring the bishop to g5 anymore.} (10... O-O-O $2 11. a4 $1 $16 {Just in time before Black could regroup with ...Na6-b8-c6. Black cannot stop a4-a5, and the king will never find happiness.}) 11. h4 $1 h6 {This one is not so common, but my computer thinks it is no worse than ...Ne7. In human terms, I definitely think it is a worse move, though.} 12. Qf3 $16 {The machine likes other moves more, but this one appeals to me. When in doubt, please be sure to stick with moves you understand. Nice and simple. g4 comes next, and Black cannot stop it. The kingside will be blown open, and it will be White's choice how it opens.This all comes down to the pawns being on h4 and h6 as opposed to h2 and h7. Now, if Black were to try to play ...Ne7, ...Rhf8, and castle by hand, we have the flexibility to play g2-g4, and g4-g5 instead of gxf5. Black is in big trouble.} *

