1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 b6 {This move is hard to take seriously, but it does have some adherents.} (4... Qd7 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 {The only move Black ever plays here is ...b7-b6, which transposes to the}) (4... b6 {line.}) 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 (6. bxc3 Ne7 {would transpose to the 6...b6 line, but Black does have two extraneous options.}) 6... Qd7 {This is Black's main point. By avoiding ...Ne7 for the moment, they are ready to meet Qg4 with ...f7-f5. I will not be dissuaded.} 7. Qg4 $1 f5 8. Qg3 Ba6 9. Bxa6 Nxa6 10. Ne2 {Black has some nice strategic assets; eventually, our bishop will be a bad piece, and the c4-square is weak. But their king will cause them no shortage of issues; this is often the case in the positional Winawer lines. Now, Black would love to castle long, but isn't quite able to.} Kf7 {In modern times, this has emerged as the main move. Now, it is a somewhat frustrating position to play for White. The computer always gives some big advantage, around +1, but it never really finds a plan to knock Black out.In the meantime, Black's moves are pretty easy. Ne7, some rook to c8, c5. In human terms, this is not so simple for White, and they should be well prepared and have a good understanding of the resulting middlegame if they want to put Black under pressure.Some key points to understand:1. White needs to start with h4. This will immediately ask Black how they want to coordinate.2. If Black does not play h6, we are always happy to meet Ne7 with Bg5. We don't necessarily want to take on e7 unprovoked, but if Black ever plays h7-h6, we are very happy to take and play h5!, cementing our control of the g6-square.3. If Black does play h6, obviously, we cannot bring the bishop to g5 anymore.} (10... O-O-O $2 11. a4 $1 $16 {Just in time before Black could regroup with ...Na6-b8-c6. Black cannot stop a4-a5, and the king will never find happiness.}) 11. h4 $1 Ne7 12. Bg5 $1 {As stated before, we are always happy to meet ...h7-h6 with Bxe7. Now, White's plan should be to play h4-h5 themself, and then h5-h6 if allowed.} (12. h5 {This has been the main move in the past, but I don't like it.} h6 $1 {Black stops Bg5, and now White is too slow for Qf3 and g2-g4. I guess White is still better, but the position is a lot less clean.}) 12... h6 $2 13. Bxe7 Qxe7 14. h5 $18 {[%cal Ge2g6,Gg2g4] Black is strategically busted. White will bring the knight to g6, controlling the f8-square, and then blast through with g2-g4.} *

