1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 (5. Bd2 Ne7 $1 {without dxc5 here, White's position is not so special.}) 5... Ba5 {This tricky move is better than it is given credit for. The one time I ever faced it, my opponent did equalize. But if you know just a little bit, it is nothing to be scared of. I like a thematic response.} 6. Bd2 $1 {The computer prefers other moves, but I like this for its simplicity. Bc1-d2 instead of a2-a3 is a real variation, but Black should equalize with good play. Here, we have a much improved version.} Nc6 7. Qg4 $1 (7. dxc5 {This is also decent, but I don't love that Black can take on e5 directly and not allow the queen to g4.}) 7... Kf8 8. dxc5 $1 {Again, we see the same dxc5 theme come up. Black is sorely missing the bishop on b4.} Nxe5 9. Qg3 Ng6 $6 {This is most common, but not great. Now, White should know an important idea.} 10. Bd3 $1 {This move is best for two reasons: First off, it allows White to stay flexible with their king- they can still castle on either side.  Secondly, and more concretely, it means we will get Nge2 in time to stop Black from annoying us with ...Bc7.} Nf6 11. Nge2 $1 (11. O-O-O $2 Bc7 $1 {and it is quite annoying that} 12. f4 {can be met with} Nh5 {. Black is already at least fine.}) 11... Bc7 12. f4 $1 {[%csl Ge2][%cal Ge1c1,Gh2h5] Because the knight is already on e2, White does not need to worry about ...Nh5. Black is in bad shape here.} *

