1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 (5. Bd2 Ne7 $1 {without dxc5 here, White's position is not so special.}) 5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({Now, Black's two main moves, by a wide margin, are} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({and} 6... Qc7 {. They can often be transposed to one another. However, they do have a couple of extraneous tries that White should definitely be aware of.})) 6... Nc6 {This move has been championed by Richard Rapport, and even Magnus played it a bunch in the World Blitz one year. It definitely does not equalize, but it can be quite tricky to meet.} 7. Qg4 {I think White can and should slam Qg4 here. Black is not threatening to crash through on c3, so immediately attacking g7 makes a lot of sense.} Kf8 8. a4 $1 {This is an important move. White needs to make sure not to allow Qa5-a4, with a transposition to a line that I don't want there.} Nge7 9. Nf3 Bd7 10. h4 $16 {White has a powerful kingside initiative, and Black has nothing to show for it.} *

