1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 (5. Bd2 Ne7 $1 {without dxc5 here, White's position is not so special.}) 5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({Now, Black's two main moves, by a wide margin, are} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({and} 6... Qc7 {. They can often be transposed to one another. However, they do have a couple of extraneous tries that White should definitely be aware of.})) 6... Nc6 {This move has been championed by Richard Rapport, and even Magnus played it a bunch in the World Blitz one year. It definitely does not equalize, but it can be quite tricky to meet.} 7. Qg4 {I think White can and should slam Qg4 here. Black is not threatening to crash through on c3, so immediately attacking g7 makes a lot of sense.} g6 8. h4 $1 {Of course, we immediately target the new kingside weakness. h4-h5 is on the way.} h6 ({This was Black's big idea. They argue that it isn't so easy for us to open the kingside, because} 8... h6 9. h5 g5 {and} 10. f4 {will allow} f5 $1 {, and Black can keep the center closed. Still, I think White can count on a nice edge.}) 9. h5 {Calling the bluff.} g5 10. f4 $1 Qa5 $6 {This allows White to blow up more lines.} 11. Bd2 f5 12. Qg3 g4 13. c4 $1 (13. c4) ({is better than} 13. dxc5 Qxc5 {.}) 13... Qa4 14. dxc5 dxc4 {White has to be precise here, but they are almost winning if they are.} 15. Ne2 $1 {This knight needs to land on d6. With a little creativity, it gets there.} Nge7 16. Nc3 Qa6 17. a4 $1 (17. a4 Z0 18. Nb5 {is coming, and Black is done for.}) *

