1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 (5. Bd2 Ne7 $1 {without dxc5 here, White's position is not so special.}) 5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({Now, Black's two main moves, by a wide margin, are} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({and} 6... Qc7 {. They can often be transposed to one another. However, they do have a couple of extraneous tries that White should definitely be aware of.})) 6... Qa5 {This move is aimed at forcing a closed structure. Black argues that if they ever play ...c5-c4, a3-a4, and Bc1-a3 will make them regret their life's decisions. So, they occupy the a4-square first, and keep ...c5-c4 as a potential idea in their back pocket.} 7. Bd2 c4 $2 {This is inflexible. ...c5-c4 is one of Black's resources, but they do not want to do it against literally every single one of White's plans. Now that the center is permanently closed, we have a free hand on the kingside without having to worry about counterplay.} 8. Qg4 $1 g6 (8... Kf8 9. h4 $16 {White should easily crash through on the kingside.}) 9. h4 h6 10. Qg3 $16 ({I like this patient move. White wants} 10. Qg3 Z0 11. h5 {next, and after} g5 {, they will be able to advance f2-f4 without worrying about a tricky ...f7-f5 in response.}) *

