1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 (5. Bd2 Ne7 $1 {without dxc5 here, White's position is not so special.}) 5... Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({Now, Black's two main moves, by a wide margin, are} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({and} 6... Qc7 {. They can often be transposed to one another. However, they do have a couple of extraneous tries that White should definitely be aware of.})) 6... Qa5 {This move is aimed at forcing a closed structure. Black argues that if they ever play ...c5-c4, a3-a4, and Bc1-a3 will make them regret their life's decisions. So, they occupy the a4-square first, and keep ...c5-c4 as a potential idea in their back pocket.} 7. Bd2 Qa4 {This is the main move by far, and it is the most flexible. Black asks us what we want to do: ▷ If we go for some Qd1-g4, they will hope to open the game quickly, while we waste time with the queen.  ▷ And if we instead aim to maximize development and give up on Qg4, Black can feel more comfortable locking the position with ...c5-c4.} 8. h4 {I like slamming ahead with the h-pawn, consistent with the overall idea. We want to play a h2-h4 and h4-h5 plan if Black hits the center early with ...c7-c5.In this particular case, Qd1-g4 becomes a very good move if Black ever takes on d4, but it is not quite as convincing if they maintain the tension instead. So, while we generally can meet ...cxd4 with Qg4, we hold off for the moment.} (8. Qg4 {I am not recommending this move, and it is important to understand why.} Kf8 9. h4 Nc6 10. h5 h6 11. Z0 Nxe5 ({is a threat,} 11... Qxc2 {is a threat, and things are going wrong. But what Black does NOT want to do is ...cxd4. We will always have Nf3 in response. Here, though, it's not so easy to keep up the initiative, and the machine is recommending Qd1 back. I can't bring myself to play like that.})) 8... Nc6 9. h5 $1 {Again, White pays no heed to the d4-pawn. Now, we don't necessarily want to advance h5-h6 just yet; Black will be able to take with the knight. But if the knight ever moves, then h5-h6 becomes a huge problem for Black.} h6 {This position is the most critical one in the ...Qa5-a4 line. I think White is better, but they should be careful and show a good understanding.} 10. Nf3 {At long last, it is time to develop normally. If Black closes the position with ...c5-c4, we do not have Qg4 anymore. But the pawn on h5 gives White a lot more kingside potential than they might otherwise have.} Nge7 11. dxc5 ({At this point, I think it is appropriate to deny Black the ability to lock the game. e5 is safe, and if White can go} 11. dxc5 Z0 12. Bd3 {and} Z0 13. Rh4 {next, they are more or less winning. Black's only chance is to play ...Qe4 to equalize material.}) 11... Qe4+ 12. Qe2 $1 {Making sure to save the e5-pawn.} Qxc2 13. Qb5 $1 (13. Qb5 Z0 14. Bd3 {is not a small threat. Black should play ...a7-a6 to force the queens off, but this will critically weaken the b-file.}) 13... a6 (13... Qe4+ 14. Be2 Z0 15. Rh4 {is coming, and Black needs ...a7-a6 anyway.}) 14. Qb1 $1 Qxb1+ 15. Rxb1 Na5 ({The only sensible move. Now, at least Black can play} 15... Na5 16. Z0 Bd7 {.}) 16. Bd3 Bd7 17. Rh4 $1 {It's not so easy to keep this position under control for Black.} O-O 18. c4 $1 {Black is facing concrete problems on b7.} *

