es a huge problem for Black.} h6 {This position is the most critical one in the ...Qa5-a4 line. I think White is better, but they should be careful and show a good understanding.} 10. Nf3 {At long last, it is time to develop normally. If Black closes the position with ...c5-c4, we do not have Qg4 anymore. But the pawn on h5 gives White a lot more kingside potential than they might otherwise have.} Nge7 11. dxc5 ({At this point, I think it is appropriate to deny Black the ability to lock the game. e5 is safe, and if White can go} 11. dxc5 Z0 12. Bd3 {and} Z0 13. Rh4 {next, they are more or less winning. Black's only chance is to play ...Qe4 to equalize material.}) 11... Qe4+ 12. Qe2 $1 {Making sure to save the e5-pawn.} Qxc2 13. Qb5 $1 (13. Qb5 Z0 14. Bd3 {is not a small threat. Black should play ...a7-a6 to force the queens off, but this will critically weaken the b-file.}) 13... a6 (13... Qe4+ 14. Be2 Z0 15. Rh4 {is coming, and Black needs ...a7-a6 anyway.}) 14. Qb1 $1 Qxb1+ 15. Rxb1 Na5 ({The only sensible move. Now, at least Black can play} 15... Na5 16. Z0 Bd7 {.}) 16. Bd3 Bd7 17. Rh4 $1 {It's not so easy to keep this position under control for Black.} O-O-O 18. Rf4 Be8 19. Be3 $14 {[%cal Ge1e2,Gb1h1,Gh1h3,Gh3g3] White installs the bishop on d4, and they can count on some edge thanks to the space advantage.Black will not be able to target any of the pawns, and eventually, White can even look for Ke2 and Rb1-h1-h3-g3 to lift the other rook. Black definitely still has some problems to solve.} *

