1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({At long last, it is time to cover the main line; I consider this to be} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({here or} 6... Qc7 {. For the most part, these two moves will transpose. But each one has a couple of independent options that you should know how to handle. Understanding the resulting middlegame is extremely important, particularly with respect to when to play Qg4 and when not to.While you will need to memorize some specific moments, there are a few guidelines to help along the way.1. Black's best plan to meet Qg4 tends to be to immediately pressure the center. As such, if you ever see a move like ...b7-b6 or ...Bd7, Qg4 may well be the best move. You should not play it if Black is making moves that target the center.2. Black can aim to play ...b7-b6 and ...Ba6, trading off light-squared bishops at some point. If they do, it will be extremely important to have Qg4 available. Otherwise, you won't really be able to pressure the kingside.3. If Black ever plays ...Nc6, we can generally put the knight on f3 without too much concern. This rules out Qg4, but I don't think Qg4 is the right idea if Black is pressuring the center anyway. And, by playing ...Nc6, Black is showing that they will not be playing ...Bc8-a6 to trade light-squared bishops.4. Black tends to be fine if they can lock the position with ...c5-c4 and also prevent us from advancing a3-a4, clearing the a3-square for the bishop. In general, they would like to do this by playing Ba4 and not by playing Qa4. If they lock the position without stopping a3-a4, White should be better with a3-a4 and Ba3.5. We are advancing h2-h4, h4-h5 to gain space. If Black ever allows h5-h6 to come safely, they are probably just going to lose. The weakness of the g5-square will be a big problem. But, be careful not to advance h5-h6 if Black's knight remains on g8. They will then be able to take on h6 with the knight, and their position in the resulting structure will be much safer than it would be if we got to keep our dark-squared bishop.})) 6... Qc7 7. h4 ({Now, I would expect} 7. h4 Ne7 {to come, transposing to the mainline with 6...Ne7 . But Black does have some other options you need to be aware of.}) 7... Bd7 ({This move order is actually a bit tricky. Black is pointing out that they can meet} 7... Bd7 8. Qg4 {with} Ne7 {, quickly transposing into the 'Poisoned Pawn', which I want to avoid. But, if we proceed along with the obvious h4-h5, they can quickly get their bishop to a4.}) 8. h5 {I will not be dissuaded.} Ba4 {Nothing else makes any sense. Now, the machine is somewhat agnostic here, but I feel strongly that we should provoke a fight and not let Black lock the game.} 9. Qg4 $1 {Now and only now, White goes for Qg4. This is a relatively different position than the standard 'Poisoned Pawn' because Black has committed the bishop to a4, which is not normally part of their plan.And we once again see that Qg4 turns out to be the correct move if Black is not playing ...Nc6 or pressuring the center directly.} cxd4 10. Ne2 $1 {White's point is revealed. They are in time to get away with this move because now Qxg7 is about to win a full rook. So, Black cannot take on c3.} Qxe5 $2 11. cxd4 $1 Qf6 12. Nc3 $1 {Black is collapsing.} *

