it would be if we got to keep our dark-squared bishop.})) 6... Qc7 7. h4 ({Now, I would expect} 7. h4 Ne7 {to come, transposing to the mainline with 6...Ne7 . But Black does have some other options you need to be aware of.}) 7... Bd7 ({This move order is actually a bit tricky. Black is pointing out that they can meet} 7... Bd7 8. Qg4 {with} Ne7 {, quickly transposing into the 'Poisoned Pawn', which I want to avoid. But, if we proceed along with the obvious h4-h5, they can quickly get their bishop to a4.}) 8. h5 {I will not be dissuaded.} Ba4 {Nothing else makes any sense. Now, the machine is somewhat agnostic here, but I feel strongly that we should provoke a fight and not let Black lock the game.} 9. Qg4 $1 {Now and only now, White goes for Qg4. This is a relatively different position than the standard 'Poisoned Pawn' because Black has committed the bishop to a4, which is not normally part of their plan.And we once again see that Qg4 turns out to be the correct move if Black is not playing ...Nc6 or pressuring the center directly.} cxd4 10. Ne2 $1 {White's point is revealed. They are in time to get away with this move because now Qxg7 is about to win a full rook. So, Black cannot take on c3.} Ne7 11. cxd4 $1 Bxc2 {Black has to go for the complications.} 12. Qxg7 (12. Bd2 $5 {This is not bad either.}) 12... Rg8 13. Qh6 {[%cal Gh1h3]} ({The computer claims Black needs to play} 13. Qh6 Bd3 {right now and to chop the knight on e2. This feels unnatural to me. If they wait, White will play Rh3 and Black will lose their chance.}) 13... Bd3 14. Rh3 $1 {Now play takes on a forced character.} Bxe2 $1 15. Bxe2 Rxg2 $2 {I could easily see a human doing this.} 16. Kf1 $1 Rg8 17. Qxh7 {[%cal Gc1e3,Ga1c1,Gh3f3] Just like that, Black is busted. White keeps the center intact, is totally winning.} *

