1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({At long last, it is time to cover the main line; I consider this to be} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({here or} 6... Qc7 {. For the most part, these two moves will transpose. But each one has a couple of independent options that you should know how to handle. Understanding the resulting middlegame is extremely important, particularly with respect to when to play Qg4 and when not to.While you will need to memorize some specific moments, there are a few guidelines to help along the way.1. Black's best plan to meet Qg4 tends to be to immediately pressure the center. As such, if you ever see a move like ...b7-b6 or ...Bd7, Qg4 may well be the best move. You should not play it if Black is making moves that target the center.2. Black can aim to play ...b7-b6 and ...Ba6, trading off light-squared bishops at some point. If they do, it will be extremely important to have Qg4 available. Otherwise, you won't really be able to pressure the kingside.3. If Black ever plays ...Nc6, we can generally put the knight on f3 without too much concern. This rules out Qg4, but I don't think Qg4 is the right idea if Black is pressuring the center anyway. And, by playing ...Nc6, Black is showing that they will not be playing ...Bc8-a6 to trade light-squared bishops.4. Black tends to be fine if they can lock the position with ...c5-c4 and also prevent us from advancing a3-a4, clearing the a3-square for the bishop. In general, they would like to do this by playing Ba4 and not by playing Qa4. If they lock the position without stopping a3-a4, White should be better with a3-a4 and Ba3.5. We are advancing h2-h4, h4-h5 to gain space. If Black ever allows h5-h6 to come safely, they are probably just going to lose. The weakness of the g5-square will be a big problem. But, be careful not to advance h5-h6 if Black's knight remains on g8. They will then be able to take on h6 with the knight, and their position in the resulting structure will be much safer than it would be if we got to keep our dark-squared bishop.})) 6... Ne7 7. h4 Qc7 (7... Qa5 8. Bd2 {transposes to the 6...Qa5 line.}) 8. h5 cxd4 $6 {Again, this is not great.} 9. cxd4 Qc3+ 10. Bd2 Qxd4 11. Nf3 Qe4+ ({Now, it is a better version for Black than it was a move prior, because we cannot play} 11... Qe4+ 12. Be3 {on pain of} Nf5 {. Still, White should be better.}) 12. Be2 Nf5 {Absolutely forced, as otherwise Rh1-h4 would have been a huge problem.} 13. Kf1 $1 ({Nice and simple. White wants} 13. Kf1 Z0 14. Bd3 {next.}) 13... b6 $1 {The only way to fight back. Anything else would allow Bd3 and Black would have a horrible position. Now, White needs to make use of an important and typical motif.} 14. Bb5+ $1 {This check proves quite annoying for Black, as blocking it will allow our bishop back to d3 without running into ...Ba6.} Bd7 $2 15. Bd3 $1 {Now this move makes perfect sense. Black will not get the knight to c5.} Qg4 16. Qc1 $1 {[%cal Gd2f4,Gh1h3,Gf3h2]} ({White has a very simple plan,} 16. Qc1 Z0 17. Bf4 {,} Z0 18. Rh3 {,} Z0 19. Nh2 {. This will trap the queen. If Black needs to play a move like ...g7-g6 at some point to make room for her escape, White should be able to crush the kingside. The machine claims it is already over.}) *

