it would be if we got to keep our dark-squared bishop.})) 6... Ne7 7. h4 Qc7 (7... Qa5 8. Bd2 {transposes to the 6...Qa5 line.}) 8. h5 h6 {I consider this position to be the most critical tabiya of the Winawer.The machine gives White some edge, but things are not so simple. It often wants to go for some Qg4, and Qxg7, sacrificing the queenside. So, it starts with something like Ra1-b1, making sure that Qxc3 won't fork the rook on a1. It's definitely not that easy for White to show a clear edge, but I am happy with what I came up with.} 9. Rh3 $1 ({I like this flexible move a lot.First of all, by overprotecting the c3-square, White can play} 9. Rh3 Z0 10. Qg4 {much more easily.}) {And secondly, by not committing the f3-knight so soon, the queen still has access to g4. At this point, Black should make up their mind between Bd7 , Nbc6 and b6 .} 9... b6 {As is usually the case, whenever Black plays ...b7-b6, Qg4 becomes a much more attractive possibility since we are less concerned with direct counterplay on the queenside and in the center. I don't want to play it just yet, although I am glad to have it in reserve when the moment is right.} 10. a4 $1 ({This is a typical way to respond to ...b7-b6. White makes sure that they are ready to meet} 10. a4 Ba6 {with a check on b5,} 11. Bb5+ {, forcing Black to improve our structure if they want to get the bishops off the board.}) 10... Ba6 11. Bb5+ $1 Bxb5 12. axb5 {Black should be very precise here. I like that the human moves are not great.} a6 $6 ({This is somewhat better than} 12... a5 {here, but still ill-advised.}) 13. Qg4 $1 Rg8 14. c4 $1 {Black is ill prepared for the center to open like this.} dxc4 15. Ne2 $1 Qb7 16. Rha3 $14 {The position looks a bit artificial, but Black is in trouble. The e4-knight is not so far away from d6.} *

