it would be if we got to keep our dark-squared bishop.})) 6... Ne7 7. h4 Qc7 (7... Qa5 8. Bd2 {transposes to the 6...Qa5 line.}) 8. h5 h6 {I consider this position to be the most critical tabiya of the Winawer.The machine gives White some edge, but things are not so simple. It often wants to go for some Qg4, and Qxg7, sacrificing the queenside. So, it starts with something like Ra1-b1, making sure that Qxc3 won't fork the rook on a1. It's definitely not that easy for White to show a clear edge, but I am happy with what I came up with.} 9. Rh3 $1 ({I like this flexible move a lot.First of all, by overprotecting the c3-square, White can play} 9. Rh3 Z0 10. Qg4 {much more easily.}) {And secondly, by not committing the f3-knight so soon, the queen still has access to g4. At this point, Black should make up their mind between Bd7 , Nbc6 and b6 .} 9... b6 {As is usually the case, whenever Black plays ...b7-b6, Qg4 becomes a much more attractive possibility since we are less concerned with direct counterplay on the queenside and in the center. I don't want to play it just yet, although I am glad to have it in reserve when the moment is right.} 10. a4 $1 ({This is a typical way to respond to ...b7-b6. White makes sure that they are ready to meet} 10. a4 Ba6 {with a check on b5,} 11. Bb5+ {, forcing Black to improve our structure if they want to get the bishops off the board.}) 10... Ba6 11. Bb5+ $1 Bxb5 12. axb5 {Black should be very precise here. I like that the human moves are not great.} Qd7 $1 {This is definitely Black's best move, and it is far from an obvious decision. Black has not chosen it in any of the games up to this point. This is the moment where I am glad to have avoided Qg4, as any other move Black plays, Qg4 is coming.} 13. Qe2 $1 Qb7 14. Qg4 $1 {The queen is notably worse on b7. Now, we need not worry about the hanging b5-pawn.} Rg8 15. Ne2 $1 {Nice that Black does not take on b5 here!} a6 16. dxc5 $1 bxc5 17. Qa4 $1 Nd7 18. c4 $1 ({The computer prefers taking on a6,} 18. bxa6 {, but I wasn't so sure about the position after} Qc6 {. I like sticking with moves I understand. White maintains the tension, for now.}) 18... dxc4 19. Kf1 $16 {Black has trouble finding a decent plan; they are coming under pressure from all angles, and their king will never have a semblance of safety. White is clearly better.} *

