1. e4 e6 2. d4 d5 3. Nc3 Bb4 4. e5 c5 5. a3 Bxc3+ 6. bxc3 ({At long last, it is time to cover the main line; I consider this to be} 6. bxc3 Ne7 ({here or} 6... Qc7 {. For the most part, these two moves will transpose. But each one has a couple of independent options that you should know how to handle. Understanding the resulting middlegame is extremely important, particularly with respect to when to play Qg4 and when not to.While you will need to memorize some specific moments, there are a few guidelines to help along the way.1. Black's best plan to meet Qg4 tends to be to immediately pressure the center. As such, if you ever see a move like ...b7-b6 or ...Bd7, Qg4 may well be the best move. You should not play it if Black is making moves that target the center.2. Black can aim to play ...b7-b6 and ...Ba6, trading off light-squared bishops at some point. If they do, it will be extremely important to have Qg4 available. Otherwise, you won't really be able to pressure the kingside.3. If Black ever plays ...Nc6, we can generally put the knight on f3 without too much concern. This rules out Qg4, but I don't think Qg4 is the right idea if Black is pressuring the center anyway. And, by playing ...Nc6, Black is showing that they will not be playing ...Bc8-a6 to trade light-squared bishops.4. Black tends to be fine if they can lock the position with ...c5-c4 and also prevent us from advancing a3-a4, clearing the a3-square for the bishop. In general, they would like to do this by playing Ba4 and not by playing Qa4. If they lock the position without stopping a3-a4, White should be better with a3-a4 and Ba3.5. We are advancing h2-h4, h4-h5 to gain space. If Black ever allows h5-h6 to come safely, they are probably just going to lose. The weakness of the g5-square will be a big problem. But, be careful not to advance h5-h6 if Black's knight remains on g8. They will then be able to take on h6 with the knight, and their position in the resulting structure will be much safer than it would be if we got to keep our dark-squared bishop.})) 6... Ne7 7. h4 Qc7 (7... Qa5 8. Bd2 {transposes to the 6...Qa5 line.}) 8. h5 h6 {I consider this position to be the most critical tabiya of the Winawer.The machine gives White some edge, but things are not so simple. It often wants to go for some Qg4, and Qxg7, sacrificing the queenside. So, it starts with something like Ra1-b1, making sure that Qxc3 won't fork the rook on a1. It's definitely not that easy for White to show a clear edge, but I am happy with what I came up with.} 9. Rh3 $1 ({I like this flexible move a lot.First of all, by overprotecting the c3-square, White can play} 9. Rh3 Z0 10. Qg4 {much more easily.}) {And secondly, by not committing the f3-knight so soon, the queen still has access to g4. At this point, Black should make up their mind between Bd7 , Nbc6 and b6 .} 9... Bd7 $1 {In my opinion, this is Black's best move, although it is the least common of the three. They hope to be able to put the bishop on a4. Once that happens, locking the position with ...c5-c4 should not be so bad for them.} 10. a4 {I like remaining principled and stopping Black's main idea.} Nbc6 $1 {Now and only now, Black does for ...Nbc6. Their hope is that because we had to play a3-a4 so quickly, we are lagging a little on development.We cannot play Nf3 for concrete reasons, and as a result, Black has a very real plan to play with ...f7-f6. Normally, we would be relying on Bf4 in response, but this obviously will not work if the knight is not yet on f3.} 11. f4 $146 {I like this one. White takes all the space, and next up, Bc1-a3 could be a big threat because e5 is protected.} ({The machine insists on} 11. f4 f6 {here, which might not be that difficult. But then... castling short? I would be worried about Black.}) ({Not} 11. Nf3 $2 {because Black plays} cxd4 12. cxd4 Nb4 $1 {Oops!}) 11... O-O {This one allows White's bishop to find a great home.} 12. Ba3 $1 cxd4 13. cxd4 {[%cal Gf7f6] Black needs ...f7-f6 anyway.} f6 14. Bd6 $1 {Of course.} Qa5+ 15. Qd2 Qxd2+ 16. Kxd2 Rf7 17. Bxe7 $1 Rxe7 18. Nf3 $14 {White has a pleasant edge in the endgame. Eventually, they can look to expand on the kingside.} *

