o play Nf3 next, and not to allow ...cxd4, followed by ...Nb4. And, leaving the bishop on c1 means it can reach a3 later in some cases.} O-O $1 13. Nf3 {Black apparently can mostly equalize here with a very precise sequence.} fxe5 $1 14. fxe5 Rf7 $1 ({With} 14... Rf7 15. Z0 Raf8 {and} 16. Z0 Be8 {on the way, there is some counterplay.}) 15. Kf2 $1 {Borrowing an idea from the Steinitz.} Raf8 16. Kg1 Be8 $1 ({Black wants} 16... Be8 17. Z0 Rf5 {next, and then they are hitting h5 and will not have to be worried about Bd3.}) 17. Qd1 $1 ({White overprotects the knight on f3, and now is ready to meet} 17. Qd1 Rf5 {with} 18. g4 {. The queen did her duty on d2, stopping ...cxd4 and ...Nb4 when it was an issue. Again, Black needs more 'only moves.'}) 17... cxd4 $1 ({If} 17... Rf5 {, then} 18. g4 {.}) 18. cxd4 Nb4 $2 {This would only be a concern if White's king were still on f2.} 19. c3 $1 Qxc3 20. Bd2 $18 {Black loses a piece.} *

