ite wants to play Nf3 next, and not to allow ...cxd4, followed by ...Nb4. And, leaving the bishop on c1 means it can reach a3 later in some cases.} O-O $1 13. Nf3 {Black apparently can mostly equalize here with a very precise sequence.} fxe5 $1 14. fxe5 Rf7 $1 ({With} 14... Rf7 15. Z0 Raf8 {and} 16. Z0 Be8 {on the way, there is some counterplay.}) 15. Kf2 $1 {Borrowing an idea from the Steinitz.} Raf8 16. Kg1 Be8 $1 ({Black wants} 16... Be8 17. Z0 Rf5 {next, and then they are hitting h5 and will not have to be worried about Bd3.}) 17. Qd1 $1 ({White overprotects the knight on f3, and now is ready to meet} 17. Qd1 Rf5 {with} 18. g4 {. The queen did her duty on d2, stopping ...cxd4 and ...Nb4 when it was an issue. Again, Black needs more 'only moves.'}) 17... cxd4 $1 ({If} 17... Rf5 {, then} 18. g4 {.}) 18. cxd4 Qb6 $1 {And annoyingly, we cannot play c2-c3 because Black can take on e5.} 19. Be2 Rxf3 $1 {Otherwise, Black's preceding moves make no sense.} 20. Rxf3 Rxf3 {Even after the sacrifice, Black still has a lot of work to do.} 21. Bxf3 Nxd4 (21... Qxd4+ $2 22. Qxd4 Nxd4 23. Bd1 $18 {Just like that, Black is done for. The endgame is lost.}) 22. Be3 $1 Nxf3+ 23. Qxf3 (23. Qxf3 Z0 24. Rf1 {is on the agenda, and} Nf5 {is never that secure with} 25. g4 {in the air.Now, Black needs a lot more tough moves in a row to hang on.}) 23... Qb2 $1 ({This looks insane to me. What human walks right into} 23... Qb2 24. Rf1 $2) 24. Rf1 Nf5 $1 25. Bxa7 Qxc2 $1 26. g4 Qxa4 27. gxf5 Qxa7+ 28. Kh2 {After Black finds a lot of only moves all the way up to here, we reach this position.The computer can hold it together for Black, but I think a human would struggle a lot. The e-pawn is going to be very dangerous. And, this is 17 moves after my novelty. I don't think getting this far is super likely.} *

